"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkHome=globalThis.webpackChunkHome||[]).push([[461],{461(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('[{"number":1,"title":"Two Sum","slug":"two-sum","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# two_sum takes a list of numbers and a target and returns the two indexes whose values add up to the target.\\n# It is a brute force search, so it needs no extra data structure.\\n# The outer loop picks each element, and the inner loop tries every element after it.\\n# As soon as a pair adds to the target it returns the two indexes, with the earlier index first.\\n# Starting the inner loop at i + 1 means an element is never paired with itself and no pair is checked twice.\\n# If no pair works the function falls off the end and returns None, and the unused result list is never read.\\n# Time is O(n * n) because of the two nested loops, and extra space is O(1).\\ndef two_sum(nums, target):\\n    # Write your code here\\n\\n    # Every input is guarenteed one solution\\n\\n    # TODO:\\n    # loop through array start from idx 0 and compare addition between all\\n    # elements forward, follow process until end of array.\\n\\n    # return two idx\\n\\n    result = []\\n\\n    for i, current_elm in enumerate(nums):\\n        for x in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\\n            if current_elm + nums[x] == target:\\n                return [i, x]"},{"language":"Java","code":"public class TwoSums {\\n\\n    // twoSums takes an int array and a target, and returns the indexes of two different items that add up to it.\\n    // It is brute force: the outer index i picks the first item and the inner index j checks every item after it.\\n    // The first pair whose sum equals the target is returned as {i, j}.\\n    // Starting j at i + 1 means an item is never paired with itself, and each pair is tried only once.\\n    // If no pair matches it returns {-1, -1}.\\n    // Time is O(n^2) in the worst case and the extra space is O(1), where a HashMap would give O(n) time.\\n    public static int[] twoSums(int[] n, int target) {\\n        for (int i = 0; i < n.length; i++) {\\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n.length; j++) {\\n                if (target == n[i] + n[j]) {\\n                    return new int[]{i, j};\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return new int[]{-1, -1};\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2,"title":"Add Two Numbers","slug":"add-two-numbers","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Math","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"class AddTwoNumbers\\n{\\n\\n    // https://leetcode.com/problems/add-two-numbers/submissions/2024048461/\\n\\n    // addTwoNumbers takes two linked lists of digits, stored with the lowest digit first, and returns their sum.\\n    // The result is also a linked list with the lowest digit first, built the same way school addition works.\\n    // sum is a dummy head node and sumTail points to the last node added, so the first node needs no special case.\\n    // The loop runs while l1 or l2 still has a node, and a list that has run out counts as digit 0.\\n    // subSum is the two digits plus carryValue, the new carry is subSum / 10, and the digit kept is subSum % 10.\\n    // A new node with that digit is added at sumTail, and then l1 and l2 each move to the next node if they can.\\n    // After the loop, a leftover carry becomes one extra node, for example 5 + 5 gives 0 -> 1.\\n    // It also calls printLinkedList for debugging, so it prints the result every time it is called.\\n    // Time is O(max(m, n)) for lists of length m and n, and the new list needs O(max(m, n)) extra space.\\n    public static ListNode addTwoNumbers(ListNode l1, ListNode l2)\\n    {\\n        // Arrays are never empty,\\n        // elements are never negative integers\\n\\n        // Return sum as a linked-list.\\n        // Sum is in reverse!\\n        ListNode sum = new ListNode();\\n        ListNode sumTail = sum;\\n\\n        // Both linked-lists are not always the same size.\\n\\n        // TODO:\\n        // use two pointers, that go through each linked-list respectively.\\n\\n        // For carry opertaions in arithemetic\\n        // increase the next addition: This looks like:\\n\\n        // 999 + 999 = 9+9=1[8] ->\\n        // carry 1, 9+9+1=1[9] ->\\n        // carry 2, LAST OPERATION 9+9+1=[19]\\n\\n        // You can figure out last operation\\n        // by seeing if a pointer has .next == Null\\n\\n        // RESULT APPEND LIKE 19, 9, 8\\n        // Definitive sum would be 1998\\n\\n        // How to deal with uneven linked-lists?\\n        // If you completely traverse l1 or l2\\n\\n        int carryValue = 0;\\n\\n        // Complete until l1 is null\\n        while (l1 != null || l2 != null)\\n        {\\n            int subSum = 0;\\n\\n            if (l1 != null && l2 != null)\\n                subSum = l1.val + l2.val + carryValue;\\n\\n            if (l1 != null && l2 == null)\\n                subSum = l1.val + carryValue;\\n\\n            if (l1 == null && l2 != null)\\n                subSum = l2.val + carryValue;\\n\\n            // Reset the carryValue\\n            carryValue = 0;\\n            // If subSum / 10 > 0 set carryValue\\n            if (subSum / 10 > 0)\\n            {\\n                // Function to get everything but the last digit.\\n                carryValue = subSum / 10;\\n            }\\n            else\\n            {\\n                carryValue = 0;\\n            }\\n\\n            // Function to get the last digit.\\n            subSum = subSum % 10;\\n\\n            // Add subSum to sum linked-list\\n            // Set the next value of the sum linked-list\\n            // Move sumTail pointer forward\\n            sumTail.next = new ListNode(subSum);\\n            sumTail = sumTail.next;\\n\\n            // Move l1, l2 pointer foward\\n            if (l1 != null) l1 = l1.next;\\n            if (l2 != null) l2 = l2.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (carryValue != 0)\\n        {\\n            sumTail.next = new ListNode(carryValue);\\n        }\\n\\n        /* For Debugging */\\n        printLinkedList(sum.next);\\n\\n        return sum.next;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Function to print LinkedList w/ formation\\n    public static void printLinkedList(ListNode x)\\n    {\\n        while(x != null)\\n\\n        {\\n            if(x.next != null)\\n            {\\n                System.out.print(x.val + \\" -> \\");\\n            }\\n            else\\n            {\\n                System.out.println(x.val);\\n            }\\n            x = x.next;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":4,"title":"Median of Two Sorted Arrays","slug":"median-of-two-sorted-arrays","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","Binary Search","Divide and Conquer"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class MedianTwoSortedArrays:\\n# partition function\\n    # partition is the quick sort step, it puts values smaller than the last element on the left and returns the pivot index.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def partition(arr, low, high):\\n\\n        # choose the pivot\\n        pivot = arr[high]\\n\\n        # index of smaller element and indicates\\n        # the right position of pivot found so far\\n        i = low - 1\\n\\n        # traverse arr[low..high] and move all smaller\\n        # elements to the left side. Elements from low to\\n        # i are smaller after every iteration\\n        for j in range(low, high):\\n            if arr[j] < pivot:\\n                i += 1\\n                MedianTwoSortedArrays.swap(arr, i, j)\\n\\n        # move pivot after smaller elements and\\n        # return its position\\n        MedianTwoSortedArrays.swap(arr, i + 1, high)\\n        return i + 1\\n\\n    # swap function\\n    # swap exchanges two positions of arr, it has no self and is called through the class name.\\n    def swap(arr, i, j):\\n        arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]\\n\\n    # the QuickSort function implementation\\n    # quickSort sorts arr[low..high] in place by partitioning and recursing on each side of the pivot.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def quickSort(arr, low, high):\\n        if low < high:\\n\\n            # pi is the partition return index of pivot\\n            pi = MedianTwoSortedArrays.partition(arr, low, high)\\n\\n            # recursion calls for smaller elements\\n            # and greater or equals elements\\n            MedianTwoSortedArrays.quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1)\\n            MedianTwoSortedArrays.quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high)\\n\\n    # Arrays are always sorted.\\n    # To find the smallest & largest compare the ends of both arrays.\\n    # findMedianSortedArrays takes two lists and returns the median of all their numbers as a float.\\n    # It does not use the fact that the lists are sorted, it joins them into one list with + and sorts that list again with quickSort.\\n    # If both lists are empty it returns 0.0, and if only one is empty it sorts that list itself, so the caller\'s list is changed.\\n    # With an odd total it returns the middle element, and with an even total the average of the two middle elements.\\n    # The sort picks the last element as pivot, so on already sorted input it takes O(n^2) time and can hit the recursion limit.\\n    # On average it is O((n + m) log(n + m)), which is slower than the O(log(n + m)) the problem asks for.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def findMedianSortedArrays(nums1: List[int], nums2: List[int]) -> float:\\n\\n        cmb_arr = [int]\\n\\n        # Handles empty arr\\n        if len(nums1) == 0 and len(nums2) == 0:\\n            return 0.0\\n        elif len(nums1) == 0:\\n            cmb_arr = nums2\\n        elif len(nums2) == 0:\\n            cmb_arr = nums1\\n        else:\\n            cmb_arr = nums1 + nums2\\n\\n        # Use QuickSort\\n\\n        MedianTwoSortedArrays.quickSort(cmb_arr, 0, len(cmb_arr) - 1)\\n\\n        # Take length of srt_arr\\n        # If length take index len(srt_arr) / 2\\n        # Take mean of middle val, else just take val of middle idx\\n\\n        # print(cmb_arr)\\n\\n        if len(cmb_arr) % 2 != 0:\\n            idx = (len(cmb_arr) - 1) / 2\\n            return float(cmb_arr[int(idx)])\\n        else:\\n            idx1, idx2 = (len(cmb_arr) - 1) / 2, ((len(cmb_arr) - 1) / 2) + 1\\n            # print(\\"idx\'s\\", idx1, idx2)\\n            val1, val2 = cmb_arr[int(idx1)], cmb_arr[int(idx2)]\\n            # print(\\"val\'s\\", val1, val2)\\n            return (float(val1 + val2) / 2)"}]},{"number":8,"title":"String to Integer (atoi)","slug":"string-to-integer-atoi","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class StringInteger:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    NOTE:\\n    ASCII -> 32-bit Signed Integer (int)\\n    Ignore: Leading whitespaces, leading zeros\\n    Assume that the intention is positive if there is no leading minus sign (\'-\')\\n\\n    TODO:\\n    Read the integer by skipping leading zeros until a non-digit character\\n    is encountered or the end of the string is reached.\\n    If no digits are read then return 0.\\n\\n    If the integer is greater than the max/min value than an integer\\n    can represent then round it to the nearest. Perhaps use a try catch or an if in a loop.\\n\\n    Tip: If a value is negative, subtract it from itself twice. E.g., -4 for ((4 - 4) = 0 ->  (0 - 4) = -4)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # myAtoi takes a string and returns the integer at the start of it, limited to the 32 bit signed range.\\n    # It strips leading spaces, returns 0 for an empty string, and reads one optional \'-\' or \'+\' sign.\\n    # Then it reads digits one by one and stops at the first character that is not a digit, so \\"0-1\\" gives 0.\\n    # Before each step it checks for overflow, with INT_MAX divided by 10 being 214748364.\\n    # If result is already bigger, or equal and the next digit is over 7, it returns INT_MIN or INT_MAX by the sign.\\n    # Leading zeros need no special case because 0 * 10 + 0 stays 0, and no digits at all gives 0.\\n    # This has no self, so it is called on the class, and a digit like a superscript could pass isdigit and crash int().\\n    # Time is O(n), and space is O(n) in the worst case because the strip and the sign slice copy the string.\\n    def myAtoi(s: str)-> int:\\n\\n        result = 0\\n        is_negative = False\\n\\n        s = s.lstrip(\\" \\")\\n\\n        if len(s) == 0:\\n            return 0\\n\\n        if s[0] == \'-\':\\n            is_negative = True\\n            s = s[1:]\\n        elif s[0] == \'+\':\\n            s = s[1:]\\n\\n        INT_MAX = 2147483647\\n        INT_MIN = -2147483648\\n\\n        for char in s:\\n            if not char.isdigit():\\n                break\\n\\n            if result > 214748364 or (result == 214748364 and int(char) > 7):\\n                return INT_MIN if is_negative else INT_MAX\\n\\n            result = result * 10 + int(char)\\n\\n        return -result if is_negative else result"}]},{"number":9,"title":"Palindrome Number","slug":"palindrome-number","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class PalindromeNumber {\\n    // isPalindrome takes an int and returns true if it reads the same forward and backward.\\n    // It turns the number into a string s and builds rev, the same text reversed with a StringBuilder.\\n    // A one digit number is returned as true right away, and otherwise it returns whether s equals rev.\\n    // Negative numbers work without a special case, because the minus sign ends up at the other end of rev.\\n    // For example \\"-121\\" reverses to \\"121-\\", so it correctly gives false.\\n    // The one digit check is not needed, since a single character already equals its reverse.\\n    // It uses extra string space for the digits, so time and extra space are both O(d) for d digits.\\n    public boolean isPalindrome(int x) {\\n\\n        String s = String.valueOf(x);\\n        String rev = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();\\n\\n        if (s.length() == 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else if (s.equals(rev)) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":11,"title":"Container With Most Water","slug":"container-with-most-water","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import json\\n\\nclass ContainerWithMostWater:\\n\\n    # https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    NOTE:\\n    Array of numbers that represent the height of multiple lines\\n    We need two vertical lines, these lines represent the left and right sides of a container.\\n    Find two lines/container that store the most water\\n    To know how much water can be fitted find the MAXIUMUM height of TWO LINES, and take the DISTANCE/LENGTH.\\n    You\'re finding the largest seen reservoir and then reporting/returning+ it\'s existence.\\n\\n    TODO:\\n    Dynamic Programming:\\n    keep a variable of the LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN.\\n    Loop through the array of lines, compare each line against all other lines ahead of it.\\n    Find the MAXIMUM height of those targeted lines, also find the DISTANCE/LENGTH.\\n    When you\'re finished evaluating return the LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # maxArea1 takes a list of wall heights and returns the largest area of water two walls can hold.\\n    # It is the brute force version: every pair of walls (k, v) with v after k is tried.\\n    # The area for a pair is the shorter wall times the distance v - k, and the best area seen so far is kept.\\n    # It has no self argument, so it is called on the class and not on an object.\\n    # With fewer than two walls it returns negative infinity, since no pair is ever tried.\\n    # Time is O(n^2) for the two nested loops, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def maxArea1(height: List[int]) -> int:\\n\\n        LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN = float(\'-inf\')\\n\\n        # len(height) - 1 to never give the second loop an out of bounds index.\\n        for k in range (len(height) - 1):\\n            left_wall = height[k] # The left wall of the container.\\n            # k + 1 to never evaluate the same wall, against the same wall\\n            for v in range(k + 1, len(height)): # Variable v reresents the distance between two walls.\\n                right_wall = height[v] # The right wall of the container.\\n                shortest_wall = min(left_wall, right_wall)\\n                if LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN < shortest_wall * (v - k):\\n                    LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN = shortest_wall * (v - k)\\n        return LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN\\n\\n    # maxArea2 does the same task as maxArea1 but with two pointers, one at each end, and it is O(n) instead of O(n^2).\\n    # idx1 and idx2 start at the two ends, and each step computes the area of that pair and keeps the best one.\\n    # Then the pointer on the shorter wall moves inward, since moving the taller one only shrinks the width.\\n    # If both walls are equal, the left pointer moves, and the loop stops when the pointers meet.\\n    # Each pointer moves at most n times in total, so time is O(n) and the extra space is O(1).\\n    # With fewer than two walls it also returns negative infinity, and it has no self argument like maxArea1.\\n    def maxArea2(height: List[int]) -> int:\\n\\n        idx1 = 0\\n        idx2 = len(height) - 1\\n        LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN = float(\'-inf\')\\n        distance = lambda left_idx, right_idx : right_idx - left_idx\\n\\n        while idx1 < idx2: # len(height)\\n            RESPECTED_HEIGHT = min(height[idx1], height[idx2])\\n            current_area = RESPECTED_HEIGHT * distance(idx1, idx2)\\n            if current_area > LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN:\\n                LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN = current_area\\n\\n            if RESPECTED_HEIGHT == height[idx1]:\\n                idx1 += 1\\n            else:\\n                idx2 -= 1\\n\\n        return LARGEST_RESERVOIR_SEEN"},{"language":"Java","code":"public class ContainerWithMostWater {\\n\\n    // maxArea takes an array of line heights and returns the biggest area of water two lines can hold.\\n    // It is brute force: it tries every pair of lines (i, j) with i on the left and j on the right.\\n    // For a pair, the water level is the shorter line, and the width is j - i.\\n    // limit1 and limit2 both end up as the smaller of the two heights, and only limit1 is used in the area.\\n    // area is limit1 * (j - i), and containerSize keeps the biggest area seen.\\n    // Since all pairs are tried, the answer is correct, and an array with fewer than 2 lines gives 0.\\n    // Time is O(n^2) because of the two loops, which is too slow for big inputs, and space is O(1).\\n    public static int maxArea(int[] height) {\\n        int containerSize = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < height.length; i++) {\\n            for (int j = height.length - 1; j > i; j--) {\\n\\n                int limit1 = height[i], limit2 = height[j];\\n\\n                if (limit1 > limit2) {\\n                    limit1 = limit2;\\n                } else {\\n                    limit2 = limit1;\\n                }\\n\\n                int area = limit1 * (j - i);\\n\\n                if (containerSize < area) {\\n                    containerSize = area;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return containerSize;\\n    }\\n\\n    // maxArea2 gives the same results as maxArea, with the same O(n^2) time and O(1) space.\\n    // The difference is only in the style: j counts up from i + 1 and Math.min picks the height of the pair.\\n    public static int maxArea2(int[] height) {\\n\\n        int containerSize = 0;\\n\\n            for (int i = 0; i < height.length; i++) {\\n                for (int j = i + 1; j < height.length; j++) {\\n\\n                    int h = Math.min(height[i], height[j]);\\n                    int w = j - i;\\n                    int area = h * w;\\n\\n                    if (area > containerSize) {\\n                        containerSize = area;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n            return containerSize;\\n    }\\n}\\n// length * height * width"}]},{"number":13,"title":"Roman to Integer","slug":"roman-to-integer","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","Math","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RomantoInteger {\\n\\n    // romanToInt takes a Roman numeral string and returns its value as an int.\\n    // The idea is to look at each letter and the letter after it, and subtract when a smaller one comes before\\n    // a bigger one.\\n    // The char array c copies the string but is never read, so it is not needed.\\n    // In the loop, current is the value of letter i and next is the value of letter i + 1, or 0 at the end.\\n    // If current < next, like IV, the pair is worth next - current, so that is added and i++ skips the second\\n    // letter.\\n    // Otherwise current is added alone.\\n    // This gives the right value for valid numerals such as MCMXCIV, and an unknown letter counts as 0 without\\n    // any error.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n), only because of the unused array.\\n    public static int romanToInt(String s) {\\n\\n        int sum = 0;\\n\\n        char[] c = new char[s.length()];\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            c[i] = s.charAt(i);\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            int current = value(s.charAt(i));\\n            int next = (i < s.length() - 1) ? value(s.charAt(i + 1)) : 0;\\n\\n                if (current < next) {\\n                    sum += (next - current);\\n                    i++; // skips because two letters make one number\\n                } else {\\n                    sum += current;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        return sum;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Returns the value of one Roman letter using a switch, and 0 for any other character.\\n    private static int value(char c) {\\n        switch(c) {\\n            case \'I\':\\n                return 1;\\n                case \'V\':\\n                    return 5;\\n                    case \'X\':\\n                        return 10;\\n                        case \'L\':\\n                            return 50;\\n                            case \'C\':\\n                                return 100;\\n                                case \'D\':\\n                                    return 500;\\n                                    case \'M\':\\n                                        return 1000;\\n                                        default:\\n                                            return 0;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":14,"title":"Longest Common Prefix","slug":"longest-common-prefix","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","String","Trie"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class LongestCommonPrefix {\\n\\n    // longestCommonPrefix takes an array of strings and returns the longest prefix that all of them start with.\\n    // A null or empty array returns an empty string.\\n    // It starts with the whole first string as the prefix.\\n    // For each other string it cuts one letter off the end of the prefix until that string starts with the prefix.\\n    // If the prefix gets cut down to nothing it returns an empty string right away.\\n    // What is left after checking all strings is the answer, and the check is case sensitive.\\n    // Worst time is about O(n * m^2) for n strings and a first string of length m, since startsWith runs after each cut.\\n    // The extra space is O(m) because each substring call makes a new string.\\n    public static String longestCommonPrefix(String[] strs) {\\n        if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) {\\n            return \\"\\";\\n        }\\n\\n        String prefix = strs[0]; // start with the first string as prefix\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i < strs.length; i++) {\\n            while (!strs[i].startsWith(prefix)) {\\n                prefix = prefix.substring(0, prefix.length() - 1); // shrink prefix\\n                if (prefix.isEmpty()) {\\n                    return \\"\\";\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return prefix;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":17,"title":"Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","slug":"letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Hash Table","String","Backtracking"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\npublic class LetterCombinationsPhoneNumber {\\n\\n    // letterCombinations takes a string of digits 2 to 9 and returns every letter string the phone keys could spell.\\n    // It has no recursion, there is one hard coded branch for each length of 4, 3 and 2 digits, and one for the rest.\\n    // Each digit gets a count of 3 letters, or 4 for the digits 7 and 9, which is what the Duration variables hold.\\n    // Nested loops, one per digit, go through every choice and join the letters from lookUpTable into one string.\\n    // The loops count down from the last letter, so the results come out in reverse order, like \\"cf\\" before \\"ad\\" for \\"23\\".\\n    // An empty string gives an empty list and one digit gives that digit\'s letters.\\n    // Input longer than 4 digits, like \\"23456\\", goes to the last branch and gives single letters instead of combinations.\\n    // A digit outside 2 to 9 maps to an empty string, so it gives repeated entries.\\n    // Time and space are O(4^n * n) for n digits, but n is only handled up to 4.\\n    public static List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {\\n\\n        //minimum\\n        int init_Duration = 3, next_Duration = 3, next_next_Duration = 3, next_next_next_Duration = 3;\\n\\n        List<String> combinations = new ArrayList<>();\\n        StringBuilder logic = new StringBuilder(digits);\\n\\n        if (digits.length() == 4) {\\n            for (int i = 0; i < digits.length() - 3; i++) {\\n                if (logic.charAt(i) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i) == \'9\') init_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'9\') next_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 2) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 2) == \'9\') next_next_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 3) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 3) == \'9\') next_next_next_Duration = 4;\\n\\n                for (int j = 1; j <= init_Duration; j++) {\\n                    for (int k = 1; k <= next_Duration; k++) {\\n                        for (int l = 1; l <= next_next_Duration; l++) {\\n                            for (int m = 1; m <= next_next_next_Duration; m++) {\\n                                combinations.add(lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i), init_Duration + 1 - j) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 1), next_Duration + 1 - k) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 2), next_next_Duration + 1 - l) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 3), next_next_next_Duration + 1 - m));\\n                            }\\n                        }\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        } else if (digits.length() == 3) {\\n            for (int i = 0; i < digits.length() - 2; i++) {\\n                if (logic.charAt(i) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i) == \'9\') init_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'9\') next_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 2) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 2) == \'9\') next_next_Duration = 4;\\n\\n                for (int j = 1; j <= init_Duration; j++) {\\n                    for (int k = 1; k <= next_Duration; k++) {\\n                        for (int l = 1; l <= next_next_Duration; l++) {\\n                            combinations.add(lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i), init_Duration + 1 - j) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 1), next_Duration + 1 - k) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 2), next_next_Duration + 1 - l));\\n                        }\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        } else if (digits.length() == 2) {\\n            for (int i = 0; i < digits.length() - 1; i++) {\\n                if (logic.charAt(i) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i) == \'9\') init_Duration = 4;\\n                if (logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i + 1) == \'9\') next_Duration = 4;\\n\\n                for (int j = 1; j <= init_Duration; j++) {\\n                    for (int k = 1; k <= next_Duration; k++) {\\n                        combinations.add(lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i), init_Duration + 1 - j) + lookUpTable(logic.charAt(i + 1), next_Duration + 1 - k));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        } else {\\n            for (int i = 0; i < digits.length(); i++) {\\n                if (logic.charAt(i) == \'7\' || logic.charAt(i) == \'9\') init_Duration = 4;\\n                for (int j = 1; j <= init_Duration; j++) {\\n                    combinations.add(lookUpTable(digits.charAt(i), init_Duration + 1 - j));\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return combinations;\\n    }\\n\\n    // lookUpTable takes a digit and a letter number and returns that letter, like (\\"2\\", 1) gives a and (\\"7\\", 4) gives s.\\n    public static String lookUpTable(char let, int n) {\\n\\n        String key = let + \\",\\" + n;\\n\\n        return switch (key) {\\n            case \\"2,1\\" -> \\"a\\";\\n            case \\"2,2\\" -> \\"b\\";\\n            case \\"2,3\\" -> \\"c\\";\\n\\n            case \\"3,1\\" -> \\"d\\";\\n            case \\"3,2\\" -> \\"e\\";\\n            case \\"3,3\\" -> \\"f\\";\\n\\n            case \\"4,1\\" -> \\"g\\";\\n            case \\"4,2\\" -> \\"h\\";\\n            case \\"4,3\\" -> \\"i\\";\\n\\n            case \\"5,1\\" -> \\"j\\";\\n            case \\"5,2\\" -> \\"k\\";\\n            case \\"5,3\\" -> \\"l\\";\\n\\n            case \\"6,1\\" -> \\"m\\";\\n            case \\"6,2\\" -> \\"n\\";\\n            case \\"6,3\\" -> \\"o\\";\\n\\n            case \\"7,1\\" -> \\"p\\";\\n            case \\"7,2\\" -> \\"q\\";\\n            case \\"7,3\\" -> \\"r\\";\\n            case \\"7,4\\" -> \\"s\\";\\n\\n            case \\"8,1\\" -> \\"t\\";\\n            case \\"8,2\\" -> \\"u\\";\\n            case \\"8,3\\" -> \\"v\\";\\n\\n            case \\"9,1\\" -> \\"w\\";\\n            case \\"9,2\\" -> \\"x\\";\\n            case \\"9,3\\" -> \\"y\\";\\n            case \\"9,4\\" -> \\"z\\";\\n            default -> \\"\\";\\n        };\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":19,"title":"Remove Nth Node From End of List","slug":"remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RemoveNthNodeFromEndList {\\n    // removeNthFromEnd takes the head of a linked list and n, and returns the head after removing the nth node\\n    // from the end.\\n    // It goes over the list twice: the first loop counts the length into headLength.\\n    // If the length is 1 or less and n is at least 1 it returns null, and if headLength - n is 0 the head\\n    // itself is the target, so it returns head.next.\\n    // Otherwise point1 starts at head.next and point2 at head, and both move forward headLength - n times,\\n    // with point2 starting one step late through the wait flag.\\n    // At the end point2 is the node just before the one to remove, and point1 is the node just after it, so\\n    // point2.next = point1 skips the target.\\n    // With n = 0 the i == headLength - 1 branch sets point1 to null, which removes the last node, as if n were\\n    // 1.\\n    // If n is bigger than the length, the loop does not run and the list comes back unchanged.\\n    // head.next is read at the start, so an empty list (head == null) would throw a NullPointerException, even\\n    // though the notes say to return it.\\n    // The nodes are changed in place, so time is O(L) for list length L and extra space is O(1).\\n    public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {\\n        /*\\n        UNDERSTAND: You are given a linked list.\\n        You must determine the length in code subtracting n: i32\\n        from the lenth and removing that index specifically. This can be done in place, or\\n        it can be done with the assitance of replicant nodes, etc.\\n\\n        Constrains: We are giving a linkedlist size of one to thirty, never even zero.\\n        The value of a node, ranges from i32: 0 to 100.\\n        The value of n will never be < 1, but also never > 30.\\n\\n        Edge Cases: There only being one node and an n: representing a\\n        sort of usize which is way too great. In that case remove the only object in the list,\\n        if the list is empty return it so.\\n\\n        List being so long that iterating over it more than once wouldn\'t be\\n        substantial in that case we should process our linkedlist in place.\\n        But we adhere to the contraints.\\n\\n        Recommendations: Complete this problem in once pass.\\n\\n        MATCH: Two-pointer solution.\\n\\n        PLAN: We\'re going to allocate two nodes which travel along our original head.\\n        First pointer should find itself one step ahead, n + 1. The second pointer\\n        should find itself one step behind, n - 1. point n - 1, to n + 1 in the linkedlist,\\n        eliminating the existence of n.\\n\\n        Time Complexity: The time complexity is O(n),\\n        this is because we have to traverse up to the nth value + 1.\\n\\n        Space Complexity: O(1), not creating any new objects.\\n        We are just applying two new views to any already given data structure, head.\\n        */\\n\\n        // Objects\\n        ListNode point1 = head.next, point2 = head, temp = head;\\n\\n        // Variables\\n        int headLength = 0;\\n        boolean wait = false;\\n\\n        // Iterate to find the length of the Linkedlist.\\n        while (temp != null) {\\n            headLength += 1;\\n            temp = temp.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        // If the length of the LinkedList is one, and then is > 1 return an empty linkedlist.\\n        if (headLength <= 1 && n >= 1) {\\n            return null;\\n        } else if (headLength - n == 0) {\\n            return head.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        /*\\n        Move pointers before and after the node that needs to be removed.\\n        If the requested node is at the end of the list set the last one to null break the loop.\\n        */\\n        for (int i = 0; i < headLength - n; i++) {\\n\\n            if (i == headLength - 1) {\\n                point1 = null;\\n                break;\\n            }\\n\\n            point1 = point1.next;\\n            if (wait) {\\n                point2 = point2.next;\\n            }\\n            wait = true;\\n        }\\n\\n        point2.next = point1;\\n\\n        return head;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Builds a string like 1 -> 2 -> 3 for the list, and would throw a NullPointerException for a null head.\\n    public String print(ListNode head) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\n        boolean isEmpty = false;\\n\\n        if (head != null)\\n            while (head.next != null) {\\n                String str = head.val + \\" -> \\";\\n                sb.append(str);\\n                head = head.next;\\n                if (head == null) {\\n                    isEmpty = true;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        if (!isEmpty) {\\n            sb.append(head.val);\\n        }\\n        return sb.toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":20,"title":"Valid Parentheses","slug":"valid-parentheses","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","Stack","Bracket Sequences"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ValidParentheses2 {\\n\\n    // isValid takes a string of brackets and returns true if every bracket is closed by the right type in order.\\n    // A string of length 0 or 1 returns false at once.\\n    // Each round of the do while loop deletes every \\"()\\", \\"[]\\" and \\"{}\\" pair from the string.\\n    // Removing an inner pair can make an outer pair touch, so the loop repeats until a round changes nothing.\\n    // If the string is empty at the end, every bracket was matched in order, so it returns true.\\n    // Leftover characters mean an unmatched or wrongly ordered bracket, so it returns false.\\n    // Each round is O(n) and there can be up to n / 2 rounds, so time is O(n^2) and space is O(n).\\n    public static boolean isValid(String s) {\\n        if (s.length() <= 1) return false;\\n\\n        // Repeat removing valid pairs until nothing changes\\n        String old;\\n        do {\\n            old = s;\\n            s = s.replace(\\"()\\", \\"\\");\\n            s = s.replace(\\"[]\\", \\"\\");\\n            s = s.replace(\\"{}\\", \\"\\");\\n        } while (!s.equals(old));\\n\\n        // if everything matched properly, string should be empty\\n        return s.isEmpty();\\n    }\\n}"},{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\\nimport java.util.Stack;\\n\\npublic class ValidParentheses3 {\\n\\n    // isValid takes a string of brackets and returns true if every bracket is closed by the right type in order.\\n    // A Scanner with an empty delimiter gives one character at a time, and stack1 holds the openers still waiting.\\n    // An opener is pushed, and a closer is compared with the closer for the opener on top of stack1.\\n    // If it matches the top is popped, and if it differs or the stack is empty it returns false at once.\\n    // At the end it returns true only if stack1 is empty, so unclosed openers also give false.\\n    // stack2 is never used.\\n    // Time is O(n) and space is O(n) for the stack.\\n    public boolean isValid(String s) {\\n\\n        Stack<String> stack1 = new Stack<>();\\n        Stack<String> stack2 = new Stack<>();\\n\\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(s);\\n        sc.useDelimiter(\\"\\");\\n\\n        while (sc.hasNext()) {\\n\\n            String token = sc.next();\\n\\n            if (token.equals(\\"(\\") || token.equals(\\"{\\") || token.equals(\\"[\\")) {\\n                stack1.push(token);\\n            } else if (!stack1.isEmpty()) {\\n\\n                String opposing = closing(stack1.peek());\\n\\n                if (opposing.equals(token)) {\\n                    stack1.pop();\\n                } else {\\n                    return false;\\n                }\\n            } else {\\n                return false;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return stack1.isEmpty();\\n    }\\n\\n    // closing returns the closing bracket for an opening bracket, or \\"\\" for any other text.\\n    public static String closing(String closing_brace) {\\n        switch (closing_brace) {\\n            case \\"(\\":\\n                return \\")\\";\\n            case \\"{\\":\\n                return \\"}\\";\\n            case \\"[\\":\\n                return \\"]\\";\\n            default:\\n                return \\"\\";\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":21,"title":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","slug":"merge-two-sorted-lists","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Linked List","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"/**\\n * Definition for singly-linked list.\\n * public class ListNode {\\n *     int val;\\n *     ListNode next;\\n *     ListNode() {}\\n *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n * }\\n */\\n\\npublic class MergeTwoSortedLists {\\n\\n    // mergeTwoLists takes the heads of two sorted linked lists and returns the head of one merged sorted list.\\n    // A dummy node and a tail pointer are used so the first node needs no special case.\\n    // While both lists have nodes, the smaller front node is linked after tail and that list moves forward.\\n    // On a tie the node from list2 is taken first, which is fine because equal values look the same.\\n    // When one list runs out, the rest of the other list is attached in one step.\\n    // Existing nodes are relinked and no new result nodes are made, so the extra space is O(1).\\n    // The length, comboList and index lines at the top are never used, they only cost an extra scan.\\n    // Time is O(n + m) for the merge, plus another O(n + m) for those unused length calls.\\n    private static ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {\\n\\n        int length = getLength(list1) + getLength(list2);\\n        ListNode comboList = new ListNode(length);\\n\\n        int index = 0;\\n\\n        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);\\n        ListNode tail = dummy;\\n\\n        while (list1 != null && list2 != null) {\\n            if (list1.val < list2.val) {\\n                tail.next = list1;\\n                list1 = list1.next;\\n            } else {\\n                tail.next = list2;\\n                list2 = list2.next;\\n            }\\n            tail = tail.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (list1 != null) tail.next = list1;\\n        if (list2 != null) tail.next = list2;\\n\\n        return dummy.next;\\n    }\\n\\n    // getLength counts the nodes in a list by walking from the head to the end.\\n    public static int getLength(ListNode head) {\\n        int length = 0;           // accumulator\\n        ListNode current = head;  // start at the head\\n\\n        while (current != null) { // traverse until the end\\n            length++;             // add 1 for this node\\n            current = current.next; // move to the next node\\n        }\\n        return length;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":24,"title":"Swap Nodes in Pairs","slug":"swap-nodes-in-pairs","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"// Definition for singly-linked list.\\nclass ListNode {\\n    int val;\\n    ListNode next;\\n    ListNode() {}\\n    ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n}\\n\\npublic class SwapNodePairs {\\n    // swapPairs takes the head of a linked list and returns the new head after swapping every two neighbors by\\n    // moving nodes, not values.\\n    // dummy is a fake node in front of head, so the first pair can be handled like all the others and the head\\n    // can change.\\n    // prev always points at the node just before the next pair.\\n    // While two nodes remain after prev, a and b are the pair, and three links are changed: a points to what\\n    // follows b, b points to a, and prev points to b.\\n    // Then prev moves to a, which is now the second node of the swapped pair and sits just before the next\\n    // pair.\\n    // If one node is left over at the end, or the list is empty, it stays as it is.\\n    // It returns dummy.next, the new first node.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    public ListNode swapPairs(ListNode head) {\\n        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, head);\\n        ListNode prev = dummy;\\n\\n        while (prev.next != null && prev.next.next != null) {\\n            ListNode a = prev.next;\\n            ListNode b = a.next;\\n\\n            a.next = b.next;   // 1 -> 3\\n            b.next = a;        // 2 -> 1\\n            prev.next = b;     // prev -> 2\\n\\n            prev = a;          // a is now the tail of the swapped pair\\n        }\\n\\n        return dummy.next;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":28,"title":"Find the Index of the First Occurrence in a String","slug":"find-the-index-of-the-first-occurrence-in-a-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String","String Matching","Z Algorithm","Knuth\u2013Morris\u2013Pratt Algorithm","Boyer\u2013Moore String-Search Algorithm"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class FindIndexFirstOccurrenceString2 {\\n\\n    // strStr takes a haystack and a needle and returns the index of the first match, or -1 if there is none.\\n    // It simply calls the built in String.indexOf, so the searching is done by the Java library.\\n    // This is the short version of the same task as FindIndexFirstOccurrenceString, which does the search by hand.\\n    // An empty needle returns 0, and the work is the library\'s own matching, so I did not work out an exact time bound.\\n    public static int strStr(String haystack, String needle) {\\n        return haystack.indexOf(needle);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":29,"title":"Divide Two Integers","slug":"divide-two-integers","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math","Bit Manipulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class DivideTwoIntegers {\\n    // divide takes a dividend and a divisor and returns the quotient as an int.\\n    // It does not do the division by hand, it just uses Java\'s / operator, which cuts the result toward zero.\\n    // The one special case is Integer.MIN_VALUE divided by -1, which would overflow, so it returns Integer.MAX_VALUE.\\n    // The quotient variable is only a place to hold the result of dividend / divisor before it is returned.\\n    // A divisor of 0 throws an ArithmeticException, because that case is not handled.\\n    // Since it uses the built in operator it skips the real point of the problem, which bans division.\\n    // Time is O(1) and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public static int divide(int dividend, int divisor) {\\n\\n        if (dividend == Integer.MIN_VALUE && divisor == -1) { //this is used when the contraints aren\'t true, the divisor falls below\\n                                                              // -1 and the divident represents the smallest value available in java\\n            return Integer.MAX_VALUE;\\n        }\\n        int quotient = 0;\\n        return quotient =  dividend / divisor;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":32,"title":"Longest Valid Parentheses","slug":"longest-valid-parentheses","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["String","Dynamic Programming","Stack","Bracket Sequences"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class LongestValidParentheses:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a string containing just the characters \'(\' & \')\' return the\\n    length of the longest valid parentheses substring. A valid set of parentheses can be\\n    understand as any set of parentheses that have an opposite character at the relative indices.\\n    This can be best understood by e.g, \\"(()())()\\" A nested set of parentheses is just as valid as\\n    an external set of parenthesis. So long as a complement exists for a left or right\\n    parentheses it is valid.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of a given string can be between 0, and 3 * 10^3.\\n\\n    Edge Case: If a string is less than 2 return zero.\\n\\n    MATCH: Sliding Window, Stack\\n\\n    PLAN: Maybe we can look at a string of parentheses and use a two pointer solution to slide\\n    our way though. We can maintain a stack add a left parentheses, when we encounter a right\\n    parentheses pop from the stack, if there is nothing to pop then the substring is invalid and\\n    we push the two pointer forward and consider the following\\n\\n    Research: When there is an unpaired left parentheses in the middle of the string it can treat everything following as\\n    valid even though there is no match for it. We need a way to hold that middle string accountable. If the stack isn\'t\\n    empty maybe we don\'t increment the count and instead, we push the left pointer forward past the unmatched left or right parentheses.\\n\\n    The realization is that when you pop from the stack you can take the stack and ask if it\'s empty, if it is empty then you update the\\n    largest substring of valid parentheses seen so far, `l_count = max(l_count, stack[-1])` otherwise just append that current index\\n    to the stack as a placeholder- its value is arbitrary, its existence is only meant to hold space, keeping the stack accountable for\\n    being empty or not. When the stack is empty we can consider if the count is count should be update.\\n\\n    NOTE: This algo is with the understanding that we\'re not truly looking for a substring, just the longest\\n    stretch of valid parentheses. To be clarified.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # longestValidParentheses takes a string of \\"(\\" and \\")\\" and returns the length of the longest valid run of parentheses.\\n    # It keeps a stack of indices, starting with -1 as a marker for the position before the first unmatched \\")\\".\\n    # An \\"(\\" pushes its index onto the stack.\\n    # A \\")\\" pops the top, and then there are two cases.\\n    # If the stack is empty the \\")\\" had no partner, so its own index is pushed as the new marker.\\n    # Otherwise the valid run ends at i and starts just after the new top, so its length is i - stack[-1] and l_count keeps the maximum.\\n    # An empty string, or one without any matched pair, returns 0.\\n    # Time is O(n) because each index is pushed and popped at most once, and the stack takes O(n) space.\\n    def longestValidParentheses(self, s: str) -> int:\\n        stack = [-1]  # base marker: \\"last position before an unmatched close\\"\\n        l_count = 0\\n        for i, c in enumerate(s):\\n            if c == \'(\':\\n                stack.append(i)\\n            else:\\n                stack.pop()\\n                if not stack:\\n                    stack.append(i)  # this \')\' is unmatched new base marker\\n                else:\\n                    l_count = max(l_count, i - stack[-1])\\n        return l_count"}]},{"number":35,"title":"Search Insert Position","slug":"search-insert-position","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Binary Search"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class SearchInsertPosition {\\n\\n    // searchInsert takes a sorted int array and a target, and returns the index of target, or the index where\\n    // it would be inserted.\\n    // It does not use binary search, it uses two plain loops.\\n    // The first loop goes through the whole array and, every time target > nums[i], sets temp = i + 1.\\n    // So temp ends as the number of elements smaller than target, which is the insert position in a sorted\\n    // array.\\n    // The second loop looks for target itself and, if found, sets temp to that index.\\n    // With distinct values that index is the same as the count of smaller elements, so the answer is right.\\n    // If the array had duplicates the second loop would return the last matching index, not the first.\\n    // Time is O(n), not the O(log n) that binary search gives, and extra space is O(1).\\n    public static int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {\\n\\n       int temp = 0;\\n\\n       //checks where the number should go if we\'re to be inserted (if number doesn\'t exist)\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            if (target > nums[i]) {\\n                temp = i + 1; //index\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        // checks where the number is located (if number exists)\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            if (nums[i] == target) {\\n                temp = i; //index\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return temp;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":45,"title":"Jump Game II","slug":"jump-game-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Dynamic Programming","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class JumpGameII {\\n\\n    // greedy algorithm\\n    // jump takes an array of jump lengths and returns the fewest jumps needed to reach the last index.\\n    // It is greedy, so currEndJump is the furthest index reachable with the jumps counted so far.\\n    // maxEndJump is the furthest index reachable from any spot seen so far, updated with i + nums[i].\\n    // If maxEndJump already hits the last index it counts one more jump and stops the loop.\\n    // When i reaches currEndJump it must jump again, so jumps goes up and currEndJump becomes maxEndJump.\\n    // An array of length 1 returns 0, because the loop never runs.\\n    // It assumes the end is reachable, and if it is not the method still returns some number instead of failing.\\n    // Time is O(n) for a single pass and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public static int jump(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        int currEndJump = 0;\\n        int maxEndJump = 0;\\n        int jumps = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {\\n            maxEndJump = Math.max(maxEndJump, i + nums[i]);\\n\\n            if (maxEndJump == nums.length - 1) {\\n                jumps += 1;\\n                break;\\n            }\\n\\n            if (i == currEndJump) {\\n                jumps += 1;\\n                currEndJump = maxEndJump;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return jumps;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":55,"title":"Jump Game","slug":"jump-game","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Dynamic Programming","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"class JumpGame {\\n\\n    // canJump takes an array of longest jump lengths and returns whether the last index can be reached.\\n    // It reads the first jump length and hands the work to the recursive jumpHelper starting at position 0.\\n    // The helper returns true at the last index, false if the jump is 0, and true if the jump reaches the end.\\n    // Otherwise it looks at every spot within the jump and picks the one whose position plus value reaches furthest.\\n    // It then moves to that spot and repeats, so each hop goes strictly forward.\\n    // The check nums.length - 1 / 2 != 0 is always true for a non-empty array, because 1 / 2 is 0 in integer math.\\n    // I traced it on the examples but did not prove it right for every input, and an empty array throws on nums[0].\\n    // The state and memory parameters are barely used, and the recursion can be as deep as the array is long.\\n    public static boolean canJump(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        boolean state = false;\\n        int jumps = nums[0];\\n        int memory = 0;\\n        int position = 0;\\n\\n        return jumpHelper(nums, jumps, memory, state, position);\\n    }\\n\\n    public static boolean jumpHelper(int[] nums, int jumps, int memory, boolean state, int position) {\\n\\n        int currPos = position;\\n\\n        if (position == nums.length - 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else if (jumps == 0) {\\n            return false;\\n        } else if (jumps > nums.length - 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n//        if (nums.length - 1 < 3) {\\n        if (nums.length - 1 / 2 != 0) {\\n            /* example: {3, 2, 1, 0, 4} */\\n            if (jumps + 1 > (nums.length - 1) - position) {\\n                return true;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= jumps; i++) {\\n\\n            if (nums[currPos + i] > 0) {\\n                state = true;\\n            }\\n\\n            if (currPos + i + nums[currPos + i] >= memory) {\\n                memory = currPos + i + nums[currPos + i];\\n                position = currPos + i;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        jumps = nums[position];\\n        memory = 0;\\n\\n        if (position <= nums.length - 1) {\\n            return jumpHelper(nums, jumps, memory, state, position);\\n        }\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":58,"title":"Length of Last Word","slug":"length-of-last-word","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class LengthLastWord:\\n    # lengthOfLastWord takes a string and returns the length of its last word.\\n    # First it strips the trailing spaces with rstrip, which is the edge case noted below.\\n    # If no space is left in the string it is a single word, so the whole length is returned.\\n    # Otherwise it finds the last space with rfind and returns the length of the part after it.\\n    # A string with only spaces becomes empty after rstrip, so it returns 0.\\n    # Time is O(n) and the slice makes the extra space O(n) in the worst case.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def lengthOfLastWord(s: str) -> int:\\n\\n        #Edge case:\\n                #1: Trailing Space\\n\\n        s = s.rstrip()\\n\\n        if (s.rfind(\' \') == -1):\\n            return len(s)\\n\\n        #The last word is the index between the last space + 1 and the length of the full string\\n        return len(s[s.rfind(\' \') + 1 :])"},{"language":"Java","code":"public class LengthofLastWord {\\n    // lengthOfLastWord takes a string and returns the length of its last word.\\n    // It splits the string on runs of whitespace with the regex \\\\s+.\\n    // Split drops empty strings at the end, so trailing spaces do not create an empty last word.\\n    // Then it takes the last piece of the array and returns its length.\\n    // A string that is only spaces gives an empty array, so words[words.length - 1] throws an exception.\\n    // Time is O(n) and the space is O(n) because split builds a new array of strings.\\n    public static int lengthOfLastWord(String s) {\\n\\n        String[] words = s.split(\\"\\\\\\\\s+\\");\\n    return words[words.length - 1].length();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":61,"title":"Rotate List","slug":"rotate-list","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":" class ListNode {\\n    int val;\\n    ListNode next;\\n    ListNode() {}\\n    ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n}\\n\\npublic class RotateList {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given the head of a linked list rotate the list by k places.\\n    We\'re rotating the list only clockwise. Returning the head of the list once finished.\\n    The head is subject to change as the value of the head may change.\\n\\n    Constraints: The number of nodes in the list is in the range 0, 500. Value of each\\n    node can range from -100, 100. Value of k can be 0, 10^9.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If k is zero return the head, if the length of the list is 0 or 1\\n    return the head.\\n\\n    MATCH: Modulo, Divisor\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going test for edge cases, resulting in an instant\\n    return. Otherwise we\'re going to take the remainder of said length against k.\\n    Use k to perform rotations. Rotations are performed by moving th\\n    tail to the head k times, this can be done in place as a linked-list or normal list/array.\\n    */\\n    // rotateRight takes the head of a linked list and k, and returns the head of the list after it is rotated\\n    // right by k places.\\n    // It returns head right away if k is 0 or the list has fewer than 2 nodes.\\n    // k %= getLen(head) cuts a huge k (up to 10^9) down to less than the length, since a full turn changes\\n    // nothing.\\n    // Then it moves the last node to the front k times by calling rotateClockwise and keeping its return value\\n    // as the new head.\\n    // If k becomes 0 after the modulo, the loop does not run and the same head comes back.\\n    // The result is right because rotating right by one k times is the same as rotating by k.\\n    // Each rotateClockwise walks the whole list, so time is O(n * k), which is O(n^2) in the worst case, and\\n    // extra space is O(1).\\n    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {\\n        if (k == 0 || head == null || head.next == null) return head;\\n\\n        k %= getLen(head);\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\\n            head = rotateClockwise(head, head.next);\\n        }\\n\\n        return head;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Moves the last node to the front once: it walks head to the second to last node and temp to the last\\n    // node.\\n    // It makes a new node with temp\'s value, cuts the old last node off with head.next = null, and puts the\\n    // new node in front of the old first node.\\n    private ListNode rotateClockwise(ListNode head, ListNode temp) {\\n        // Address of first node.\\n        ListNode original = head;\\n        // Move head and temp up a node.\\n        while (temp.next != null) {\\n            head = head.next;\\n            temp = temp.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        ListNode target = new ListNode(temp.val);\\n\\n        // Delete end of head\\n        head.next = null;\\n        // Attatch target to the address of the first node.\\n        target.next = original;\\n        // Return temp\\n        return target;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Counts the nodes in the list.\\n    private int getLen(ListNode head) {\\n        int len = 0;\\n        while (head != null) {\\n            len++;\\n            head = head.next;\\n        }\\n        return len;\\n    }\\n\\n    private static String stringifyListNode(ListNode head) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n\\n        while (head.next != null) {\\n            sb.append(head.val).append(\\", \\");\\n            head = head.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        return sb.append(head.val).append(\\"]\\").toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":66,"title":"Plus One","slug":"plus-one","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class PlusOne:\\n    # plusOne takes a list of digits that spells a number and returns the digits of that number plus one.\\n    # It joins the digits into a string, converts it to an int, adds one, and then splits the new number back into digits.\\n    # result is sized to the length of the new number, so 9 becomes two digits and 99 becomes three.\\n    # The loop takes the last digit with % 10 and drops it with // 10, filling result from the front, then it is reversed.\\n    # The loop condition uses & instead of and, so it reads as recast != 0 and 0 < length, which works for non-empty input.\\n    # An empty list would crash on int(\\"\\"), and very long inputs use Python big ints so they do not overflow.\\n    # Time is about O(n) for the string work plus the big int conversion, and space is O(n).\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def plusOne(digits: List[int]) -> List[int]:\\n\\n        full_number = \\"\\"\\n\\n        for number in digits:\\n            full_number += str(number)\\n\\n        length = len(full_number)\\n        recast = (int(full_number) + 1)\\n\\n        result = [0] * len(str(recast))\\n\\n        index = 0\\n        while (recast != 0 & index < length):\\n            result[index] = recast % 10\\n            recast = recast // 10\\n            index = index + 1\\n\\n        return list(reversed(result))"},{"language":"Java","code":"public class PlusOne2 {\\n\\n    // plusOne takes an array of digits, most significant first, and returns the digits of that number plus\\n    // one.\\n    // It works on the digits directly, so there is no overflow no matter how long the number is.\\n    // The first loop checks whether every digit is 9, using the all9 flag and breaking at the first non-9\\n    // digit.\\n    // If all digits are 9 (like 999), the answer is 1 followed by zeros, so it builds a new array one longer\\n    // with newDigits[0] = 1.\\n    // Java already fills a new int array with 0, so the loop that sets the rest to 0 again is not needed.\\n    // Otherwise the second loop walks from the last digit toward the front.\\n    // A digit that is not 9 gets 1 added and the loop breaks, because there is no carry left.\\n    // A 9 is set to 0 and the loop moves on to the next digit to the left, which is the carry.\\n    // In this case the input array is changed in place and returned, and in the all-9 case the input is left\\n    // alone.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1), except for the one new array in the all-9 case.\\n    public static int[] plusOne(int[] digits) {\\n\\n        boolean all9 = true;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < digits.length; i++) {\\n            if (digits[i] != 9) {\\n                all9 = false;\\n                break;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (all9 == true) {\\n\\n            int[] newDigits = new int[digits.length + 1];\\n            newDigits[0] = 1;\\n\\n            for (int i = 1; i < newDigits.length; i++) {\\n                newDigits[i] = 0;\\n            }\\n            return newDigits;\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i < digits.length + 1; i++) {\\n\\n            if (digits[digits.length - i] != 9) {\\n                digits[digits.length - i] = digits[digits.length - i] + 1;\\n                break;\\n            }\\n\\n            if (digits[digits.length - i] == 9) {\\n                digits[digits.length - i] = 0;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return digits;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":67,"title":"Add Binary","slug":"add-binary","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","String","Bit Manipulation","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.math.BigInteger;\\n\\npublic class AddBinary2 {\\n\\n    // addBinary takes two binary numbers as strings and returns their sum as a binary string.\\n    // This version lets BigInteger do the work instead of converting by hand like AddBinary does.\\n    // new BigInteger(a, 2) reads a string as a base 2 number, and the same is done for b.\\n    // c.add(d) adds the two numbers, and sum.toString(2) turns the result back into a base 2 string.\\n    // BigInteger has no size limit, so long inputs do not overflow, unlike a version that uses long.\\n    // Strings like \\"00\\" and \\"00\\" give \\"0\\", and leading zeros in the inputs are fine.\\n    // An empty string would throw a NumberFormatException, because it is not a valid number.\\n    // I did not work out the exact time cost of the BigInteger parsing and printing.\\n    public static String addBinary(String a, String b) {\\n\\n        BigInteger c = new BigInteger(a, 2);\\n        BigInteger d = new BigInteger(b, 2);\\n\\n        BigInteger sum = c.add(d);\\n\\n        return sum.toString(2);\\n    }\\n\\n    public static class WordSearch {\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":68,"title":"Text Justification","slug":"text-justification","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","String","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.*;\\n\\npublic class TextJustification2 {\\n\\n    // fullJustify takes an array of words and a line width, and returns the words as lines of exactly maxWidth\\n    // characters, fully justified.\\n    // The outer loop starts a new line at index, and last moves forward while the next word still fits with\\n    // one space before it.\\n    // totalChars counts the letters of those words plus one space between each, and numWords is how many are\\n    // on the line.\\n    // numSpaces is the total number of spaces the line needs, which is maxWidth minus the letters alone.\\n    // The last line, or a line with a single word, is left-justified: words with one space between and padding\\n    // added at the end up to maxWidth.\\n    // Every other line spreads the spaces between the words: each gap gets numSpaces / (numWords - 1), and the\\n    // leftmost gaps get one extra space until extraSpaces runs out.\\n    // (spacesBetween is that value plus 1, and the inner loop adds spacesBetween - 1 spaces, so the 1s\\n    // cancel.)\\n    // It assumes no word is longer than maxWidth.\\n    // Time and extra space are both about the total number of characters in the output.\\n    public static List<String> fullJustify(String[] words, int maxWidth) {\\n        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\\n        int index = 0;\\n\\n        while (index < words.length) {\\n            int totalChars = words[index].length();\\n            int last = index + 1;\\n\\n            // find how many words fit in this line\\n            while (last < words.length) {\\n                if (totalChars + 1 + words[last].length() > maxWidth) break;\\n                totalChars += 1 + words[last].length();\\n                last++;\\n            }\\n\\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\n            int numWords = last - index;\\n            int numSpaces = maxWidth - totalChars + (numWords - 1);\\n\\n            // last line or single-word line: left-justify\\n            if (last == words.length || numWords == 1) {\\n                for (int i = index; i < last; i++) {\\n                    sb.append(words[i]);\\n                    if (i != last - 1) sb.append(\\" \\");\\n                }\\n                while (sb.length() < maxWidth) sb.append(\\" \\");\\n            } else { // fully justify\\n\\n                int spacesBetween = numSpaces / (numWords - 1) + 1;\\n                int extraSpaces = numSpaces % (numWords - 1);\\n                for (int i = index; i < last; i++) {\\n                    sb.append(words[i]);\\n                    if (i < last - 1) {\\n                        for (int s = 0; s < spacesBetween - 1; s++) sb.append(\\" \\");\\n                        if (extraSpaces > 0) {\\n                            sb.append(\\" \\");\\n                            extraSpaces--;\\n                        }\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n\\n            result.add(sb.toString());\\n            index = last;\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":69,"title":"Sqrt(x)","slug":"sqrtx","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Binary Search","Newton\'s Method"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class SqrtX {\\n\\n    // mySqrt takes an int x and returns the square root of x rounded down.\\n    // It uses the fact that the sum of the first k odd numbers (1 + 3 + 5 + ...) is k squared.\\n    // tool starts at 1 and goes up by 2 each loop, and tally counts how many odd numbers were taken.\\n    // Each loop takes tool away from x until x goes below 0.\\n    // The loop takes one number too many, since it stops only after x is already negative, so tally-- fixes\\n    // that.\\n    // For example x = 8 takes away 1, 3 and 5, tally is 3, and the answer is 2.\\n    // x = 0 gives 0, and x = 4 gives 2.\\n    // Time is O(sqrt(x)) and extra space is O(1), which is slower than a binary search.\\n    public static int mySqrt(int x) {\\n\\n        int tally = 0;\\n        int tool = 1;\\n\\n        while (x >= 0) {\\n            x = x - tool;\\n\\n            tally++;\\n\\n            tool = tool + 2;\\n        }\\n        tally--;\\n        return tally;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":70,"title":"Climbing Stairs","slug":"climbing-stairs","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Dynamic Programming","Memoization"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ClimbingStairs {\\n\\n        // climbStairs takes the number of steps n and returns how many ways there are to climb them 1 or 2 at a time.\\n        // It makes a memo array of size n + 1 and hands the work to climbHelp.\\n        // The number of ways to reach step n is the ways to reach n - 1 plus the ways to reach n - 2.\\n        // This is the Fibonacci recurrence, with 1 way for step 0 and 1 way for step 1 as the base cases.\\n        // The memo stores each answer so that it is computed only once, and 0 means not computed yet.\\n        // Because of this, time is O(n) and space is O(n) for the memo and the recursion depth.\\n        // The result is an int, so it overflows for n of 46 or more, and a negative n throws an exception.\\n        public static int climbStairs(int n) {\\n\\n            int[] m = new int[n + 1];\\n            return climbHelp(n, m);\\n        }\\n\\n        // climbHelp is the recursive part: it returns 1 for n of 0 or 1, reuses memo[n] if set, else fills it.\\n        public static int climbHelp(int n, int[] memo) {\\n\\n            if (n == 0 || n == 1) {\\n                return 1;\\n            }\\n            if (memo[n] != 0) {\\n                return memo[n];\\n            }\\n\\n            memo[n] = climbHelp(n - 1, memo) + climbHelp(n - 2, memo);\\n            return memo[n];\\n        }\\n}"}]},{"number":71,"title":"Simplify Path","slug":"simplify-path","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String","Stack"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SimplifyPath:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: We\'re given a unix styled pathway. This means \'/\' is considered\\n    one slash no many how many of these characters are directly together. This also means\\n    that \\"..\\" means the previous directories folder and `.` references the current directory.\\n    If there are more than two periods it is considered a valid file name.\\n\\n    Constraints:\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Vect, Two Pointer\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to evaluate every character in the string. First, we\'re going to also keep a vector\\n    of folders. Everytime we see `.` we ignore everything after it. If we see a \\"..\\" we remove the last\\n    the folder/dir and consider the one before. We\'re also going to check for substrings of \'/\' considering\\n    them as one backslash and adding them to the vector. Also the names before a backslash is considered a\\n    folders name.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N)\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # simplifyPath takes a Unix style absolute path and returns the shortest canonical path.\\n    # It splits the path on \'/\' and uses a stack of folder names.\\n    # An empty piece (from // or a trailing /) and a \'.\' piece are skipped because they change nothing.\\n    # A \'..\' piece pops the last folder if there is one, so going above the root stays at the root.\\n    # Anything else, including names like \'...\', is a real folder name and is pushed.\\n    # The answer is \'/\' plus the names joined with \'/\', so an empty stack gives just \'/\'.\\n    # Time is O(n) for the split and the join, and space is O(n) for the pieces and the stack.\\n    def simplifyPath(self, path: str) -> str:\\n        stack = []\\n\\n        for dir_name in path.split(\'/\'):\\n            if dir_name == \'..\':\\n                if stack: stack.pop()\\n            elif dir_name == \'\' or dir_name == \'.\':\\n                continue\\n            else:\\n                stack.append(dir_name)\\n\\n        return \'/\' + \'/\'.join(stack)"}]},{"number":73,"title":"Set Matrix Zeroes","slug":"set-matrix-zeroes","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Matrix"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class SetMatrixZeroes {\\n\\n    // setZeroes takes an int matrix and, for every 0 in it, sets that whole row and column to 0, changing the\\n    // matrix itself.\\n    // temp is a full copy of matrix, so reads and writes stay separate.\\n    // The main loops look for zeros in matrix, the original, so zeros that were just added to temp never\\n    // spread a second time.\\n    // For each zero at (i, j), the first k loop zeroes column j in temp and the second k loop zeroes row i in\\n    // temp.\\n    // The last loops copy temp back into matrix.\\n    // It is right because only original zeros count, which is what the problem wants.\\n    // Time is O(m * n * (m + n)) in the worst case when there are many zeros, and extra space is O(m * n) for\\n    // temp.\\n    // It is not the constant space solution, but it is simple.\\n    public static void setZeroes(int[][] matrix) {\\n\\n        //creates new temporary matrix\\n        int[][] temp = new int[matrix.length][matrix[0].length];\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < temp.length; i++) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < temp[0].length; j++) {\\n                temp[i][j] = matrix[i][j];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {\\n\\n                if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {\\n                    for (int k = 0; k < matrix.length; k++) {\\n                        temp[k][j] = 0;\\n                    }\\n\\n                    for (int k = 0; k < matrix[i].length; k++) {\\n                        temp[i][k] = 0;\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < temp.length; i++) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < temp[0].length; j++) {\\n                matrix[i][j] = temp[i][j];\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":75,"title":"Sort Colors","slug":"sort-colors","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","Sorting","Quicksort","Bubble Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"class SortColors {\\n\\n    // sortColors takes an int array and sorts it in place from smallest to largest, returning nothing.\\n    // First it finds the smallest and largest value in the array, min and max.\\n    // Then for each value i from min to max, it scans the whole array and copies every element equal to i into\\n    // input in order.\\n    // So input is filled in sorted order, and the last loop copies input back into nums.\\n    // This works for any numbers, not just 0, 1 and 2, as the test in main with 3 and 4 shows.\\n    // It throws an exception on an empty array, because it reads nums[0].\\n    // If max were Integer.MAX_VALUE, i <= max would never be false and the loop would never end.\\n    // Time is O((max - min + 1) * n), which is O(n) for three colors but slow for a wide range, and extra\\n    // space is O(n).\\n    public static void sortColors(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        int min = nums[0];\\n        int max = nums[0];\\n        for (int k = 1; k < nums.length; k++) {\\n            if (nums[k] < min) {\\n                min = nums[k];\\n            }\\n            if (nums[k] > max) {\\n                max = nums[k];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        int[] input = new int[nums.length];\\n        int index = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = min; i <= max; i++) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < nums.length; j++) {\\n\\n                if (nums[j] == i) {\\n                    input[index] = nums[j];\\n                    index++;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            nums[i] = input[i];\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":79,"title":"Word Search","slug":"word-search","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","String","Backtracking","Depth-First Search","Matrix"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class WordSearch {\\n\\n    // exist takes a grid of letters and a word and returns true if the word can be traced through neighbouring cells.\\n    // Each step moves up, down, left or right, and a cell cannot be used twice in the same path.\\n    // It creates a visited grid, then tries every cell as a starting point and runs dfs from there.\\n    // As soon as one start finds the whole word it returns true, and if none do it returns false.\\n    // board[0] is read directly, so an empty board would throw an exception.\\n    // Time is O(rows * cols * 3^L) for word length L, since after the first step each move has about three choices.\\n    // Space is O(rows * cols) for visited plus O(L) for the recursion.\\n    public static boolean exist(char[][] board, String word) {\\n        int rows = board.length;\\n        int cols = board[0].length;\\n\\n        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {\\n                if (dfs(board, word, 0, i, j, visited)) {\\n                    return true;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n\\n    // dfs checks whether word from position index onward can be matched starting at cell (row, col).\\n    // It returns true once index reaches the word length, and false for out of bounds, a wrong letter, or a used cell.\\n    // Otherwise it marks the cell visited, tries the four directions, then unmarks the cell (backtracking).\\n    private static boolean dfs(char[][] board, String word, int index,\\n                               int row, int col, boolean[][] visited) {\\n\\n        if (index == word.length()) return true;\\n        if (row < 0 || row >= board.length || col < 0 || col >= board[0].length) return false;\\n        if (board[row][col] != word.charAt(index)) return false;\\n        if (visited[row][col]) return false;\\n\\n        visited[row][col] = true;\\n\\n        boolean found = dfs(board, word, index + 1, row + 1, col, visited) || // down\\n                dfs(board, word, index + 1, row - 1, col, visited) || // up\\n                dfs(board, word, index + 1, row, col + 1, visited) || // right\\n                dfs(board, word, index + 1, row, col - 1, visited);   // left\\n\\n        visited[row][col] = false;\\n\\n        return found;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":86,"title":"Partition List","slug":"partition-list","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class ListNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.next = next\\n\\nclass PartitionList:\\n    # partition takes the head of a linked list and a value x and returns a list with values below x first, then the rest.\\n    # It keeps two lists, left for values less than x and right for values greater than or equal to x.\\n    # Each list starts as a dummy node with val None, and the first real value is written into that node.\\n    # The loop walks the input once and copies each later value into a new node at the tail of the matching list.\\n    # Relative order inside both lists stays the same as the input, because values are always appended at the tail.\\n    # At the end left_tail is linked to right, and if left is empty then right is returned alone.\\n    # It builds new nodes instead of relinking the old ones, and it prints the lists on every step as debugging output.\\n    # It uses None to mean empty, so it would mis-handle a node whose value is None, which LeetCode never gives.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(n) because every node is copied.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def partition(head: Optional[ListNode], x: int) -> Optional[ListNode]:\\n\\n        # If the head is empty, just return the head.\\n        # Node(n1, No Address)\\n\\n        if head is None:\\n            return head\\n\\n        # Node(n1, Address: n2), Node(n2, No Address)\\n        # Target is n1 Questions: Is n2 less than, more than, or equal to n1\\n        # Preserve order\\n\\n        # How to solve:\\n        # Two lists left < x, right >= x\\n\\n        # all nodes less than x come before nodes greater than or equal to x\\n\\n        left = ListNode(None)\\n        left_tail = left\\n        right = ListNode(None)\\n        right_tail = right\\n\\n        pointer = head\\n\\n        while pointer != None:\\n            if pointer.val >= x:\\n                if right.val == None:\\n                    right.val = pointer.val\\n                    print(\\"right_list: \\", end=\\"\\")\\n                    PartitionList.printFullNode(right)\\n                    print()\\n                else:\\n                    right_tail.next = ListNode(pointer.val)\\n                    right_tail = right_tail.next\\n                    print(\\"right_lsit: \\", end=\\"\\")\\n                    PartitionList.printFullNode(right)\\n                    print()\\n            if pointer.val < x:\\n                if left.val == None:\\n                    left.val = pointer.val\\n                    print(\\"left_list: \\", end=\\"\\")\\n                    PartitionList.printFullNode(left)\\n                    print()\\n                else:\\n                    left_tail.next = ListNode(pointer.val)\\n                    left_tail = left_tail.next\\n                    print(\\"left_list: \\", end=\\"\\")\\n                    PartitionList.printFullNode(left)\\n                    print()\\n\\n            pointer = pointer.next\\n\\n        # connect the two lists\\n\\n        if left.val != None:\\n            print(\\"returned_list: \\")\\n            if right.val is not None:\\n                left_tail.next = right\\n            PartitionList.printFullNode(left)\\n            print()\\n            return left\\n        elif left.val == None and right.val != None:\\n            print(\\"returned_list: \\")\\n            PartitionList.printFullNode(right)\\n            print()\\n            return right\\n\\n    # printFullNode prints a linked list as values joined with arrows, used for debugging.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def printFullNode(myNode: ListNode) -> None:\\n        while myNode != None:\\n            if myNode.next == None:\\n                print(myNode.val, end = \\"\\")\\n            else:\\n                print(myNode.val, end = \\" -> \\")\\n            myNode = myNode.next"},{"language":"Java","code":"/**\\n * Definition for singly-linked list.\\n * public class ListNode {\\n *     int val;\\n *     ListNode next;\\n *     ListNode() {}\\n *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n * }\\n */\\nclass PartitionList {\\n\\n    // partition takes the head of a linked list and a value x, and returns the list with all nodes less than x first.\\n    // It keeps two lists, left for values below x and right for the rest, each with a dummy head and a tail pointer.\\n    // One pass moves along the original list and links each existing node onto the end of left or right.\\n    // Nodes keep their original relative order inside each part, so the partition is stable.\\n    // At the end the left tail is linked to the first node of right, and rtail.next is set to null.\\n    // Setting rtail.next to null matters, because that last node may still point at an old next node and form a cycle.\\n    // No nodes are created apart from the two dummies, so time is O(n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    public static ListNode partition(ListNode head, int x) {\\n\\n        ListNode left = new ListNode();\\n        ListNode right = new ListNode();\\n\\n        ListNode ltrail = left;\\n        ListNode rtail = right;\\n\\n        while (head != null) {\\n            if (head.val < x) {\\n                ltrail.next = head;\\n                ltrail = ltrail.next;\\n            } else {\\n                rtail.next = head;\\n                rtail = rtail.next;\\n            }\\n            head = head.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        ltrail.next = right.next;\\n        rtail.next = null;\\n        return left.next;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":88,"title":"Merge Sorted Array","slug":"merge-sorted-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class MergeSortArray {\\n\\n    // merge takes nums1 (first m values real, the rest zero padding), m, nums2 and n, and merges them into nums1.\\n    // It builds a temporary array nums3 of size m + n and walks both inputs with two indexes.\\n    // While both have items it copies the smaller one, and when they are equal it copies both.\\n    // Once one side runs out, the other side is copied over as is, since both inputs are already sorted.\\n    // Then nums3 is copied back into nums1 so the caller sees the merged result.\\n    // The last loop prints the merged values separated by commas, which is a side effect used for testing.\\n    // It is not done in place, so it uses O(m + n) extra space, and time is O(m + n).\\n    public static void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {\\n\\n        int[] nums3 = new int[m + n];\\n        int countdown1 = 0;\\n        int countdown2 = 0;\\n        int k = 0;\\n\\n        while (countdown1 < m || countdown2 < n) {\\n            if (countdown1 < m && countdown2 < n) {\\n                // both arrays still have elements\\n                if (nums1[countdown1] < nums2[countdown2]) {\\n                    nums3[k++] = nums1[countdown1++];\\n                } else if (nums1[countdown1] > nums2[countdown2]) {\\n                    nums3[k++] = nums2[countdown2++];\\n                } else {\\n                    nums3[k++] = nums1[countdown1++];\\n                    nums3[k++] = nums2[countdown2++];\\n                }\\n            } else if (countdown1 < m) {\\n                // only nums1 has elements left\\n                nums3[k++] = nums1[countdown1++];\\n            } else {\\n                // only nums2 has elements left\\n                nums3[k++] = nums2[countdown2++];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < m + n; i++) {\\n            nums1[i] = nums3[i];\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int c = 0; c < m + n; c++) {\\n            if (c == (m + n) - 1) {\\n                System.out.print(nums3[c]);\\n                break;\\n            } else {\\n                System.out.print(nums3[c] + \\",\\");\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":91,"title":"Decode Ways","slug":"decode-ways","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\nimport java.util.Scanner;\\n\\npublic class DeocdeWays_v3 {\\n\\n    // numDecodings takes a string of digits and returns how many ways it can be decoded (1 is A ... 26 is Z).\\n    // It uses backtracking: branch builds every valid decoded string and adds each one to the list result.\\n    // If result is empty, there is no valid decoding and it returns 0.\\n    // Otherwise it counts the strings by running a Scanner over result.toString(), which is like \\"[AB, L]\\".\\n    // The Scanner splits on spaces, and since the decoded strings contain no spaces, each string is one token.\\n    // So count is the number of decodings, which is correct, though counting the list\'s size would be simpler.\\n    // It also prints the list, which is for debugging, and an empty input string counts as 1.\\n    // The number of decodings can grow like Fibonacci numbers, so time is exponential, and every string is stored.\\n    public static int numDecodings(String s) {\\n        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\\n        branch(s, 0, new StringBuilder(), result);\\n        System.out.println(result);\\n\\n        if (result.isEmpty()) {\\n            return 0;\\n        }\\n\\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(result.toString());\\n        int count = 0;\\n\\n        while (sc.hasNext()) {\\n            sc.next();\\n            count++;\\n        }\\n\\n        return count;\\n    }\\n\\n    // branch is the backtracking step: i is the position in s, and sb is the letters chosen so far.\\n    // At the end of s it saves sb, and a digit 0 at i is a dead end because no letter starts with 0.\\n    // It tries one digit as a letter and recurses, removes it, then does the same for two digits if 26 or less.\\n    private static void branch(String s, int i, StringBuilder sb, List<String> dec) {\\n        if (i == s.length()) {\\n            dec.add(sb.toString());\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (s.charAt(i) == \'0\') return;\\n\\n        //Grab 1 char\\n        int oneChar = s.charAt(i) - \'0\';\\n        sb.append((char)(\'A\' + oneChar - 1));\\n        branch(s, i + 1, sb, dec);\\n        sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);\\n\\n        //Grab 2 chars\\n        if (i + 1 < s.length()) {\\n            int twoChar = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i, i + 2));\\n            if (twoChar <= 26) {\\n                sb.append((char)(\'A\' + twoChar - 1));\\n                branch(s, i + 2, sb, dec);\\n                sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":92,"title":"Reverse Linked List II","slug":"reverse-linked-list-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n//Definition for singly-linked list.\\nclass ListNode {\\n     int val;\\n     ListNode next;\\n     ListNode() {}\\n     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n}\\n\\npublic class ReverseLinkedListII {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a linked list and your job is to reverse everything in between\\n    the two given left and right variables. It\'s important to note that these aren\'t indexes\\n    and are instead values. Consider the first appearance of said bounds to be the target\\n    addresses that need manipulation.\\n\\n    Constraints: Length of linked list can range 1, 500. Each node can value -500, 500.\\n    Given left integer must be less than or equal to given right integer.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Doubly-ended linked list\\n\\n    PLAN: One option is to modify the Linked List to be a doubly ended linked list. The problem is\\n    that in the big O complexity creating a new datastructure and manually adding each address\\n    to previous is very unoptimal.\\n\\n    Another option is to traverse the list and store all elements inside a vector.\\n    We then build a new linked list iterating through each element in the list until left is found,\\n    that\'s when we continue iterating until right is found. When right is found we create a sub loop\\n    iterating and adding to the list backwards. Then we pick up from where we left off and finish the inital loop.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N^2) This is because of the sub loop encompassing left & right targets which could span a\\n    noticable amount of the vector.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) Because we\'re building a vector for real-time/dynamic refferencing.\\n   */\\n    // reverseBetween takes the head of a linked list and two numbers, left and right, and returns the list\\n    // with positions left to right reversed.\\n    // The code treats left and right as 1-based positions (i + 1 == left), not as values like the notes above\\n    // say.\\n    // It returns head right away for an empty list or a one node list.\\n    // buildList copies every value into a list, and then the original head node is reused as a starting dummy\\n    // node.\\n    // The loop builds new nodes behind result: normal positions copy list.get(i), and at position left it adds\\n    // list.get(right - 1) down to list.get(i) in reverse.\\n    // Then i jumps to right - 1 so those positions are not added a second time.\\n    // It returns head.next, which is the first new node, and the original head node is left pointing into the\\n    // new list.\\n    // It throws IndexOutOfBoundsException if right is bigger than the length, and it also prints the result as\\n    // a side effect.\\n    // Time is O(n) since every value is copied once, not O(N^2) as the notes say, and space is O(n) for the\\n    // list and the new nodes.\\n    public ListNode reverseBetween(ListNode head, int left, int right) {\\n\\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;\\n\\n        List<Integer> list = buildList(head, new ArrayList<>());\\n        ListNode result = head;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {\\n            if (i + 1 == left) {\\n                for (int r = right - 1; r >= i; r--) {\\n                        result.next = new ListNode(list.get(r));\\n                        result = result.next;\\n                    }\\n                    i = right - 1;\\n            } else {\\n                result.next = new ListNode(list.get(i));\\n                result = result.next;\\n                }\\n        }\\n\\n        printLinkedList(head.next);\\n\\n        return head.next;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Recursively adds each node\'s value to list in order and returns it, so the recursion is as deep as the\\n    // list is long.\\n    private List<Integer> buildList(ListNode head, List<Integer> list) {\\n        if (head == null) {\\n            return list;\\n        }\\n\\n        list.add(head.val);\\n        head = head.next;\\n        buildList(head, list);\\n\\n        return list;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Prints the list like [1, 4, 3, 2, 5], with [] for an empty list, only for looking at the result.\\n    private void printLinkedList(ListNode head) {\\n\\n        if (head == null) {\\n            System.out.println(\\"[]\\");\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (head.next == null) {\\n            System.out.println(\\"[\\" + head.val + \\"]\\");\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n        while (head != null) {\\n            sb.append(head.val).append(\\", \\");\\n            head = head.next;\\n        }\\n\\n        sb.replace(sb.length() - 2, sb.length(), \\"\\");\\n\\n        System.out.println(sb.append(\\"]\\").toString());\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":98,"title":"Validate Binary Search Tree","slug":"validate-binary-search-tree","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass ValidateBinarySearchTree:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a supposed BST validate it by returning true or false if it maintains\\n    the characteristics of a BST. You know if a BST is as it says it is if the left sub tree\\n    of a node is None, of if the value of nodes found are less than itself. For the right\\n    subtree of a node the values must be greater than or equal to the value of itself.\\n\\n    Constraints: There can be between 1, 10^4 nodes in the tree. There value of each node\\n    can be between -2^31, 2^31 - 1.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If a tree only has one node or no nodes at all return True.\\n\\n    MATCH: Binary Search Tree, Bounds\\n\\n    PLAN: We set an upper and lower bound at the theoritical maximum that the value of each node can be.\\n    We use a recursive function `validate` to ask if a node has no more children. We use two sets of duplicate logic\\n    for the left we modify the heighest acceptable value with each recursive call, making sure that the next\\n    node\'s if any cannot be any larger than what was previously seen. For the right we modify the lowest\\n    acceptable value, making sure that the lowest acceptable value cannot be less than the value of the\\n    node that was just visited.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # isValidBST takes the root and starts the check with no limits, using -infinity and +infinity as the bounds.\\n    def isValidBST(self, root):\\n        return self.validate(root, float(\'-inf\'), float(\'inf\'))\\n\\n    # validate takes a node plus a low and a high bound, and returns True if its subtree fits strictly between them.\\n    # An empty node is fine, so it returns True.\\n    # A node whose value is not strictly between low and high fails, which also rejects duplicate values.\\n    # Going left, the node\'s own value becomes the new high, because everything on the left must be smaller.\\n    # Going right, the node\'s own value becomes the new low, because everything on the right must be bigger.\\n    # Both sides are joined with and, so one bad node anywhere makes the whole answer False.\\n    # Checking bounds from the top, and not only the parent, catches a deep node that breaks an ancestor\'s rule.\\n    # Time is O(n) since each node is visited once, and space is O(h) for the recursion, with h the tree height.\\n    def validate(self, node, low, high):\\n        if not node:\\n            return True\\n\\n        if not (low < node.val < high):\\n            return False\\n\\n        return (\\n            self.validate(node.left, low, node.val) and\\n            self.validate(node.right, node.val, high))"}]},{"number":100,"title":"Same Tree","slug":"same-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class SameTree {\\n\\n    // isSameTree takes two tree roots p and q and returns true if the trees have the same shape and the same\\n    // values.\\n    // It is a recursive check of the two trees at the same time.\\n    // If both are null that spot matches, so it returns true.\\n    // If only one of them is null, the shapes differ, so it returns false.\\n    // If both exist and the values are equal, the answer is whether the left subtrees match and the right\\n    // subtrees match.\\n    // If the values differ it returns false.\\n    // The && stops early, so it quits as soon as one side fails.\\n    // Time is O(n) for n nodes (the smaller tree if they differ) and the recursion stack is O(h) for the\\n    // height h.\\n    public static boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {\\n\\n        if (p == null && q == null) return true;\\n        if (p != null && q == null || p == null && q != null) return false;\\n\\n            if (p.val == q.val) return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":101,"title":"Symmetric Tree","slug":"symmetric-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class SymmetricTree {\\n\\n    // isSymmetric takes the root of a binary tree and returns true if the tree is a mirror image of itself.\\n    // An empty tree counts as symmetric.\\n    // Otherwise the tree is symmetric when its left subtree and right subtree are mirrors of each other.\\n    // That check is passed on to isMirror, which compares the two subtrees at the same time.\\n    // Time is O(n) for n nodes and the recursion stack is O(h) for height h.\\n    public static boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {\\n        return root == null || isMirror(root.left, root.right);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Returns true if a and b are mirror images of each other.\\n    // Two nulls match and one null does not, and the values must be equal.\\n    // Then a.left has to mirror b.right and a.right has to mirror b.left.\\n    public static boolean isMirror(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {\\n        if (a == null && b == null) return true;\\n        if (a == null || b == null) return false;\\n\\n        if (a.val != b.val) return false;\\n        return isMirror(a.left, b.right) && isMirror(a.right, b.left);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":102,"title":"Binary Tree Level Order Traversal","slug":"binary-tree-level-order-traversal","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass BinaryTreeLevelOrderTraversal:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a binary tree, please cover each level and store all nodes in a given\\n    level in a sub list within a list. Return the list filled with each element in it\'s appropriate level/sublist.\\n\\n    MATCH: List, Preorder traversal\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to traverse the binary tree. But first we create a list, if the list is empty we give it\\n    a new sub list. Each time we move deeper into the binary tree we increase the level variable which starts at zero.\\n    If the size of list/list has more elements that the counter suggests we increase the counter, using it index and\\n    add elements from the tree into the correct sublist.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(n)\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(n)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # levelOrder takes the root of a binary tree and returns a list of lists, one list of values for each level, top down.\\n    # It just starts the helper _fill_levels with an empty list and level 0.\\n    # The helper visits nodes depth first, going left before right, and carries the current level number down.\\n    # When the level is the first visit to a new depth it adds an empty sublist, then appends the node value to that sublist.\\n    # Because left subtrees are visited before right ones, the values in each level come out from left to right.\\n    # An empty tree returns an empty list.\\n    # Time is O(n), and space is O(n) for the result plus O(h) for the recursion.\\n    def levelOrder(self, root: TreeNode | None) -> list[list[int]]:\\n        return self._fill_levels(root, [], 0)\\n\\n    # _fill_levels is the recursive part: it adds root.val to storage[level] and recurses into the children at level + 1.\\n    def _fill_levels(self, root: TreeNode | None, storage, level) -> list[list[int]]:\\n        if not root:\\n            return storage\\n\\n        if level >= len(storage):\\n            storage.append([])\\n\\n        storage[level].append(root.val)\\n\\n        if root.left:\\n            self._fill_levels(root.left, storage, level + 1)\\n\\n        if root.right:\\n            self._fill_levels(root.right, storage, level + 1)\\n\\n        return storage"}]},{"number":104,"title":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","slug":"maximum-depth-of-binary-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass MaximumDepthBinaryTree:\\n    # maxDepth takes the root of a binary tree and returns how many nodes are on the longest path from the root down to a leaf.\\n    # An empty tree has depth 0.\\n    # Otherwise the depth is 1 for this node plus the larger of the depths of the left and the right subtree, found by recursion.\\n    # Every node is visited once, so the time is O(n), and the recursion uses O(h) stack space where h is the height.\\n    def maxDepth(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:\\n        if not root:\\n            return 0\\n\\n        return 1 + max(self.maxDepth(root.left), self.maxDepth(root.right))"}]},{"number":108,"title":"Convert Sorted Array to Binary Search Tree","slug":"convert-sorted-array-to-binary-search-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Divide and Conquer","Tree","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class convertSortedArrayBinarySearchTree {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a list of integers.\\n    You must add to a BinaryTree while following the format of BST.\\n    This means smaller values are on the left, larger values go on the right.\\n\\n    Constraints: The array of numbers contains 1 to 10^4 possible elements\\n    and the value of each element can range from -10^4 to 10^4\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given an array with only two or less elements order doesn\'t matter.\\n    If every element in the array is the same order you cannot attend to order,\\n    proceed as normal.\\n    Two or more elements aren\'t unique meaning they will have the same value,\\n    do we place them on the right or left? Right.\\n\\n    MATCH: Binary Search Tree, Traversal.\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to follow the array from start to finish.\\n    Appending and switching around nodes to fit BST configuration.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: Adding to a binary tree from a sorted integer vector is O(log n) this is because we\'re\\n    going through each side simultaniously.\\n    Space Complexity: Space complexity is O(N) because we\'re adding\\n    every element from the array into the BST, also we\'re removing a\\n    ArrayList because of build in function support.\\n    */\\n\\n    // sortedArrayToBST takes an array sorted from small to large and returns the root of a height balanced BST.\\n    // It does not insert values one by one, it just calls helper on the whole range 0 to nums.length - 1.\\n    // The idea is that the middle of a sorted range is a good root: smaller values go left, bigger ones right.\\n    // The helper repeats that for the left half and for the right half, so the two sides stay about the same size.\\n    // That is why the tree is balanced, and the result is a valid BST because the array is sorted.\\n    // An empty array gives null, because the range is empty from the start.\\n    // Time is O(n) since each element becomes one node, and the extra space is O(log n) for the recursion.\\n    public TreeNode sortedArrayToBST(int[] nums) {\\n        return helper(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);\\n    }\\n\\n    // helper builds the subtree for nums[start..end]: it returns null for an empty range.\\n    // Otherwise the middle (the lower one if the count is even) becomes the root, and the halves become its children.\\n    private TreeNode helper(int[] nums, int start, int end) {\\n        if (start > end) return null;\\n\\n        int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;\\n        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(nums[mid]);\\n\\n        root.left = helper(nums, start, mid - 1);\\n        root.right = helper(nums, mid + 1, end);\\n\\n        return root;\\n    }\\n\\n    private static String binarySearchTreeStringify(TreeNode tree, StringBuilder sb) {\\n        if (tree == null) {\\n            return sb.toString();\\n        }\\n\\n        if (sb.toString().equals(\\"\\")) {\\n            sb.append(\\"[\\");\\n        }\\n\\n            sb.append(tree.val).append(\\", \\");\\n\\n        binarySearchTreeStringify(tree.left, sb);\\n        binarySearchTreeStringify(tree.right, sb);\\n\\n        return sb.toString();\\n    }\\n\\n    private static String primIntVectStringify(int[] nums) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {\\n            sb.append(nums[i]).append(\\", \\");\\n        }\\n\\n        return sb.append(nums[nums.length - 1]).append(\\"]\\").toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":110,"title":"Balanced Binary Tree","slug":"balanced-binary-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import deque\\n\\n# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass BalancedBinaryTree:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a binary tree you need to return if it is balanced.\\n    You know that a binary tree is balanced if the depth of two sub-trees don\'t differ by more than one.\\n    Return the result of this conditions given a binary tree paramter.\\n\\n    Thinking about this problem initially I said that it might be possible to tell if a binary tree is not balanced\\n    by asking two questions at a time. 1. is root.left & root.right\\n                                       2. if not root.left or root.right, raise flag.\\n                                       3. explore the root that has children.\\n                                       4. if either of their children have children, halt return false.\\n                                       4. if we finish traversing the tree without hitting the condition above the tree is balanced.\\n\\n    Constraints: The number of nodes in a tree can range from 0, 5000. The value of each node can range from -10^4, 10^4.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If the tree is empty return true.\\n\\n    MATCH: binary tree, boolean, object level\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going create an object level: flg_1. Inside the binary tree function we\'re going to start by asking if the root has children.\\n    If the root has children we continue, next asking if the left child is present, or the right child is present. If both are present,\\n    we recurse into them. If one is present and one is not we raise that object level flag: flg_1. We still recurse into the only child.\\n    We then ask if the only child has children. If they have children, we return false. If we finish traversing the tree and there are\\n    no children we return true.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(n log n)\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n\\n    \\\\[|height(left)-height(right)| \\\\leq 1\\\\]\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.lev = []\\n        self.found_on = None\\n\\n    # isBalanced takes the root of a binary tree and returns True if every node has subtree heights differing by at most 1.\\n    # An empty tree returns True, since the height of None is 0 and that is not -1.\\n    # The inner height function returns the height of a subtree, or -1 as a flag when that subtree is already unbalanced.\\n    # It gets the heights of the left and right children first, and passes the -1 flag up if either child gave it.\\n    # If the two heights differ by more than 1 it returns -1, and otherwise it returns the larger height plus 1.\\n    # The tree is balanced exactly when the height of the root is not -1.\\n    # Each node is visited once, so time is O(n), and the space is O(h) for the recursion.\\n    # The notes in the docstring say O(n log n) time and O(1) space, but the code as written is O(n) time and O(h) space.\\n    def isBalanced(self, root: TreeNode | None) -> bool:\\n\\n        def height(node):\\n            if not node:\\n                return 0\\n\\n            left = height(node.left)\\n            right = height(node.right)\\n\\n            if left == -1 or right == -1:\\n                return -1\\n\\n            if abs(left - right) > 1:\\n                return -1\\n\\n            return max(left, right) + 1\\n\\n        return height(root) != -1"}]},{"number":111,"title":"Minimum Depth of Binary Tree","slug":"minimum-depth-of-binary-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass MinimumDepthBinaryTree:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: We\'re given a binary tree data structure, tasked with finding the shortest\\n    path from root to node and then returning it\'s depth.\\n\\n    Constraints: The number of nodes in the tree can range from 0, 10^5.\\n    The value of each node can range from -1000, 1000\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If the tree is empty return zero.\\n\\n    Match: Depth First Search\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re checking all of the branches for the shortest\\n    depth (node count from root to leaf). If the levels exceed the\\n    count of the minimum level we return the minimum. That branch could go on\\n    forever and it\'s not going to be the shortest path.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) because worst case secenerio we search the entire tree because every\\n    other branch is shortest than the one previous to it in a DFS context.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) because we\'re filling up a list of recorded tree depths.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # minDepth takes the root of a binary tree and returns the number of nodes on the shortest path from the root to a leaf.\\n    # An empty tree returns 0.\\n    # The inner function minLeaf goes down the tree with a level counter and returns the level when it reaches a leaf.\\n    # At other nodes it collects the result of each child that exists in depths and returns the smallest.\\n    # Only existing children are followed, so a node with one child does not count as a leaf.\\n    # The early exit check on depths can never be true, because depths is a new empty list each time, so there is no pruning.\\n    # Every node gets visited, so the time is O(n), and the recursion uses O(h) space for the tree height h.\\n    def minDepth(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:\\n\\n        if root == None:\\n            return 0\\n\\n        def minLeaf(root: Optional[TreeNode], level: int):\\n            if not root.left and not root.right:\\n                return level\\n\\n            depths = []\\n\\n            if len(depths) > 1 and level > (min(depths)):\\n                return min(depths)\\n\\n            if root.left != None:\\n                depths.append(minLeaf(root.left, level + 1))\\n\\n            if root.right != None:\\n                depths.append(minLeaf(root.right, level + 1))\\n\\n            return min(depths)\\n\\n        depth = minLeaf(root, 1)\\n\\n        return depth"}]},{"number":112,"title":"Path Sum","slug":"path-sum","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class PathSum {\\n\\n    // hasPathSum takes the root of a binary tree and a target sum, and returns true if a root to leaf path adds to it.\\n    // It uses recursion that subtracts each node value from the target as it goes down.\\n    // An empty tree returns false, since there is no path.\\n    // At a leaf (no left and no right child) it checks that the remaining target equals the leaf\'s value.\\n    // For any other node it returns true if the left subtree or the right subtree can finish the remaining sum.\\n    // Only leaves count as path ends, so a node with one child is not treated as an end.\\n    // Time is O(n) because each node is visited once, and the stack space is O(h) for the tree height.\\n    public static boolean hasPathSum(TreeNode root, int targetSum) {\\n\\n        if (root == null) return false; // base case: empty tree\\n\\n        // if we\'re at a leaf node, check if we reached the target\\n        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {\\n            return targetSum == root.val;\\n        }\\n\\n        //subtract the current value and check the children\\n        return hasPathSum(root.left, targetSum - root.val)\\n                || hasPathSum(root.right, targetSum - root.val);\\n    }\\n\\n    // traverseTree is an unused DFS sketch with no null check, so it would throw if it were called.\\n    private void traverseTree(TreeNode root) {\\n\\n        traverseTree(root.left);\\n        traverseTree(root.right);\\n        System.out.println(root.val);\\n\\n        //DFS (Depth First Search via Recursion)\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":116,"title":"Populating Next Right Pointers in Each Node","slug":"populating-next-right-pointers-in-each-node","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.Formatter;\\nimport java.util.List;\\nimport java.util.Locale;\\nimport java.util.Optional;\\n\\nclass Node {\\n    public int val;\\n    public Node left;\\n    public Node right;\\n    public Node next;\\n\\n    public Node() {}\\n\\n    public Node(int _val) {\\n        val = _val;\\n    }\\n\\n    public Node(int _val, Node _left, Node _right, Optional<Node> _next) {\\n        val = _val;\\n        left = _left;\\n        right = _right;\\n        next = (_next != null) ? _next.orElse(null) : null;\\n    }\\n};\\n\\npublic class PopulatingNextRightPointersEach {\\n\\n    /*\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/populating-next-right-pointers-in-each-node/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a binary tree, viewed as a tree like structure complete a missing filed per each node\\n    `.next` that needs to be set to address of the node directly to the right of itself. For clarification imagine\\n    a Node as having a position at the top of the tree (see U.1 constraints), there is nothing next to it. Now the\\n    next \\"depth\\", or \\"row\\" down in our tree, since it is guarenteed a sibling it\'s `.next` parameter is guarenteed to\\n    exist. But, for the node on the right, non-adjenct, it once again has no address to reference and so it\'s `.next`\\n    will be null.\\n\\n    U.1 Constraints:\\n    - You may only use constant extra space.\\n    - The recursive approach is fine.\\n    - You may assume implicit stack space does not count as extra space for this problem.\\n    - Each node is guarenteed to having two or no children at all.\\n\\n    MATCH: binary tree, post order traversal, level ordered collection, pointers\\n\\n    PLAN: # ignore condition 1\\n\\n    Ignoring step one we\'re going to create a list to store all of the elements, their values, and their nodes.\\n    We\'re going to maintain sub arrays,\\n    */\\n\\n    List<ArrayList<Node>> storage = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n    // connect takes the root of a binary tree, sets every node\'s next pointer to the node on its right, and\\n    // returns the root.\\n    // The idea is to collect the nodes level by level into a list of lists, then link neighbors inside each\\n    // list.\\n    // orderLevelNodeToList does a recursive walk that visits left before right, so each level\'s list ends up\\n    // ordered left to right.\\n    // createNextAddress then sets row[i].next = row[i + 1] for each row, so the last node of every row keeps\\n    // next as null.\\n    // The root is returned as the same object it was given, now with its next pointers filled in.\\n    // This does not meet the constant extra space rule of the problem, because storage holds every node, so\\n    // space is O(n).\\n    // Time is O(n), and the recursion adds O(height) stack.\\n    // storage is a field of the object, so calling connect twice on the same object keeps the old nodes in it\\n    // and mixes them with the new tree\'s rows.\\n    public Node connect(Node root) {\\n        this.storage = orderLevelNodeToList(root, this.storage, 0);\\n        createNextAddress(this.storage);\\n        return root;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Recursive walk that adds each node to the list for its depth, creating a new row when level reaches\\n    // storage.size().\\n    // The storage parameter is not really used, because the code reads and writes this.storage instead.\\n    private List<ArrayList<Node>> orderLevelNodeToList(Node root, List<ArrayList<Node>> storage, int level) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return this.storage;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (level >= storage.size()) {\\n            this.storage.add(new ArrayList<>());\\n        }\\n\\n        this.storage.get(level).add(root);\\n\\n        if (root.left != null) {\\n            orderLevelNodeToList(root.left, this.storage, level + 1);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.right != null) {\\n            orderLevelNodeToList(root.right, this.storage, level + 1);\\n        }\\n\\n        return this.storage;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Links every node in a row to the next node of the same row, and leaves the last node of each row with\\n    // next as null.\\n    private void createNextAddress(List<ArrayList<Node>> storage) {\\n        for (ArrayList<Node> row : storage) {\\n            for (int i  = 0; i < row.size() - 1; i++) {\\n                row.get(i).next = row.get(i + 1);\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    private String nodeToString(Node root, StringBuilder sb) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return sb.toString();\\n        }\\n\\n        try (Formatter formatter = new Formatter(sb, Locale.US)) {\\n            formatter.format(\\" %s -> left: %s, right: %s, next: %s\\", root.val + \\"\\", (root.left != null) ? root.left.val + \\"\\" : null + \\"\\", (root.right != null) ? root.right.val + \\"\\" : null + \\"\\", root.next);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.left != null) {\\n            nodeToString(root.left, sb);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.right != null) {\\n            nodeToString(root.right, sb);\\n        }\\n\\n        return sb.toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":118,"title":"Pascal\'s Triangle","slug":"pascals-triangle","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class PascalsTriangle:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Essentially pascales triangle is an algorithm that asks for duplications\\n    of the most outer element: 1 and then single addition of the outer element and the\\n    and the elements inside the triangle. The longer the alogrithm runs the large the\\n    triangle grows. You\'re given a number: `n` which you must dynamically generate the\\n    expected triangle as a list of sublists containing each row of the triangle, stopping\\n    at `n` rows.\\n\\n    Constraints: The given number can range from 1, 30.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If n is one return [[1]]\\n\\n    MATCH: List, Addition\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to start from a sublisted for a single element in our list.\\n    From that for as many times as that element appears we add the first and last element\\n    in the list to the following sublist twice. Checking if any elements exist inside the\\n    current list and adding to the list after.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # generate takes numRows and returns Pascal\'s triangle as a list of rows by calling the recursive helper.\\n    def generate(self, numRows: int) -> list[list[int]]:\\n        return self.helper([], 0, numRows)\\n\\n    # helper builds one row per call and recurses with depth + 1 until depth equals numRows, then returns the result.\\n    # For depth 0 it writes [1] and restarts the recursion with a fresh result list [[1]], which later rows build on.\\n    # For later rows it walks the previous row, copies the first value, then adds each pair of neighbours from above.\\n    # After the loop it appends the last value of the previous row, so every row starts and ends with 1.\\n    # numRows 1 returns [[1]] and numRows 0 returns an empty list.\\n    # Time is O(n^2) for n rows because row k has k values, and space is O(n^2) for the rows plus O(n) recursion depth.\\n    def helper(self, result: list[list[int]], depth: int, numRows: int):\\n        if depth == numRows:\\n            return result\\n\\n        if depth >= len(result):\\n            result.append([])\\n\\n        if len(result) <= 1:\\n            result[depth].append(1)\\n            return self.helper([[1]], depth + 1, numRows)\\n\\n        for k, num in enumerate(result[depth - 1]):\\n            if k == 0:\\n                result[depth].append(result[depth - 1][k])\\n                continue\\n\\n            result[depth].append(result[depth - 1][k - 1] + result[depth - 1][k])\\n\\n        result[depth].append(result[depth - 1][k])\\n        self.helper(result, depth + 1, numRows)\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":121,"title":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","slug":"best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class BestTimeBuySellStock:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an list of prices and your job is to find and return\\n    the greatest profit margin. The idea: buy low, sell high.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of the given price vector can be 1, 10^5.\\n    The value of each element can be from 0, 10^4\\n    Values in the list aren\'t distinct.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If there is only one day in the price vector, retun zero profit.\\n    If there are only two days in the price vector return the first day minus the second day.\\n\\n    If you\'re given a vector that is explicitly in decreasing order return zero profit.\\n\\n    MATCH: Mapping keep price and date as complementary data\\n\\n    PLAN: First we ask edge case questions like if the length of the prices vector is greater than one.\\n    I\'m going to evaluate each element in the list of and find the largest number that comes after it.\\n    We\'re going to store the difference of these numbers in a list. Then we will return the largest\\n    number from that list.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N^2) Because we\'re evalutating the list of prices twice,\\n    due to also looking at all the prices that come after.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) Because the list of margins grow\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # maxProfit takes a list of daily prices and returns the best profit from one buy and one later sell, or 0 if none.\\n    # With fewer than two prices it returns 0 right away.\\n    # The loop only looks at days that set a new lowest price, since buying on any other day cannot beat that.\\n    # For such a day it finds the largest price after it and the index of that price, and saves the profit if best so far.\\n    # If the saved largest price is still after the new low day, it is reused instead of scanned for again.\\n    # Otherwise the rest of the list is scanned from the day after the new low to find the largest price again.\\n    # On the example lists in the file this gives the right answers, and a list that only falls returns 0.\\n    # The rescan still runs when the saved price could be reused, so a strictly falling list is O(n^2) time and may be slow.\\n    # Extra space is O(1), since only a few variables are kept, although the docstring says O(N).\\n    def maxProfit(self, prices: List[int]) -> int:\\n        if not len(prices) > 1:\\n            return 0\\n\\n        lowest_price = -1\\n        largest_price = -1\\n        idx_largest_price = -1\\n        largest_margin = 0\\n\\n        for v in range(0, len(prices)):\\n\\n            margin_increase_potential = False\\n\\n            if lowest_price == -1:\\n                lowest_price = prices[v]\\n                margin_increase_potential = True\\n            elif lowest_price > prices[v]:\\n                lowest_price = prices[v]\\n                margin_increase_potential = True\\n\\n            if margin_increase_potential == False:\\n                continue\\n\\n            if largest_price != -1:\\n                if v < idx_largest_price:\\n                    if largest_margin < largest_price - lowest_price:\\n                        largest_margin = largest_price - lowest_price\\n                        continue\\n                else:\\n                    largest_price = -1\\n\\n            for k in range(v + 1, len(prices)):\\n                if prices[k] >= largest_price:\\n                    largest_price = prices[k]\\n                    idx_largest_price = k\\n\\n            if largest_price > lowest_price and largest_margin < largest_price - prices[v]:\\n                    largest_margin = largest_price - prices[v]\\n\\n        return largest_margin"}]},{"number":125,"title":"Valid Palindrome","slug":"valid-palindrome","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ValidPalindrome {\\n\\n    // isPalindrome takes a string and returns true if it reads the same both ways when only letters and digits count.\\n    // It loops from the end of s to the start and appends each letter or digit to a StringBuilder in reverse.\\n    // string is that reversed text in lower case.\\n    // backwards.reverse() flips the same builder in place, so reversed_string is the original order in lower case.\\n    // The method returns whether those two strings are equal, so case and punctuation are ignored.\\n    // The two println lines are debug output.\\n    // Time is O(n) and the extra space is O(n) for the builder and the two strings.\\n    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {\\n\\n        StringBuilder backwards = new StringBuilder();\\n\\n        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--)\\n\\n            if (Character.isLetter(s.charAt(i)) || Character.isDigit(s.charAt(i)))\\n            backwards.append(s.charAt(i));\\n\\n        String string = backwards.toString().toLowerCase();\\n        String reversed_string = backwards.reverse().toString().toLowerCase();\\n\\n        System.out.println(string);\\n        System.out.println(reversed_string);\\n\\n        return string.equals(reversed_string);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":129,"title":"Sum Root to Leaf Numbers","slug":"sum-root-to-leaf-numbers","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class SumRootLeafNumbers {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: We need to record all permutations from root to node add them to a overall\\n    total and then return said total. A root to node permuatation travels down the branch\\n    of a tree until a root is reached. Many branches may exist and there for permutations are considered.\\n\\n    Constraints: Tree can contain 1, 1000 nodes. Each node can range from 0, 9 in value.\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given a tree with zero nodes, return zero... If you\'re given a tree with one node,\\n    return the value of the singular node.\\n\\n    MATCH: Pre-order traversal\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to use a function that recursively considers an integer total.\\n    We\'ve known that a root has been hit the current root is null. At which point we\'re going\\n    to add to the objects total field. We\'re building every number arithmetically multiping the\\n    number by 10, this is done to increase the decimal placement. This is done after adding the current\\n    nodes value, decimal place is incremented under the circumstance that there will be another value\\n    to append from the current branch.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N log N) typical binary tree traversal.\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) We\'re not creating new data just reasigning and traversing given data.\\n    */\\n\\n    int objTotal = 0;\\n\\n    // sumNumbers takes the root of a tree of digits 0 to 9 and returns the sum of every root to leaf path read\\n    // as a number.\\n    // The idea is a recursive walk that builds each number on the way down.\\n    // It calls helper with the root and a running total of 0, and the helper adds root.val to total.\\n    // Before going to a child it multiplies total by 10, so the digits shift left by one place.\\n    // When a node has no left and no right child, it is a leaf, and the finished number is added to the\\n    // objTotal field.\\n    // sumNumbers keeps the value helper returns (which is objTotal) as sum, then calls clear() to reset the\\n    // field to 0, so the object can be used again.\\n    // An empty tree returns 0, and a single node returns its own value.\\n    // Time is O(n), not O(n log n) as the notes say, since each node is visited once, and the stack is\\n    // O(height).\\n    // The sum is an int, so very deep trees could overflow, which the constraints rule out.\\n    public int sumNumbers(TreeNode root) {\\n        int sum = helper(root, 0);\\n        clear();\\n        return sum;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Recursive walk that carries the number built so far in total and adds it to objTotal at each leaf.\\n    // The recursive calls ignore the return value, and only the top call\'s return (the final objTotal) is\\n    // used.\\n    private int helper(TreeNode root, int total) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return this.objTotal;\\n        }\\n\\n        total = (total + root.val);\\n\\n        if (root.left != null) {\\n            helper(root.left, total * 10);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.right != null) {\\n            helper(root.right, total * 10);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {\\n            this.objTotal += total;\\n        }\\n\\n        return this.objTotal;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Sets the objTotal field back to 0.\\n    private void clear() {\\n        this.objTotal = 0;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":135,"title":"Candy","slug":"candy","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"// candy takes an array of ratings and returns the total candies, a higher rating than a neighbour needs more.\\n// It starts everyone with 1 candy in candybag and then repeats passes until a check says the rules hold.\\n// One pass goes left to right over each pair of neighbours (x, x + 1).\\n// If the left one has a higher rating but not more candy, the left one gets one more candy.\\n// If the right one has a higher rating but not more candy, the right gets one more candy.\\n// After each pass check() looks at all pairs, and the loop stops when no pair breaks the rule.\\n// Then the candies are added up in dist, and equal neighbours are allowed to have any amounts.\\n// Candies are only added when a pair breaks the rule, never taken away.\\n// A long falling run needs many passes, since each pass fixes one step, so time is O(n^2) and space is O(n).\\npublic static int candy(int[] ratings) {\\n\\n    int checks = 0;\\n    int[] candybag = new int[ratings.length];\\n\\n    for (int x = 0; x < candybag.length; x++) {\\n        candybag[x] += 1;\\n    }\\n\\n    boolean allocation  = false;\\n\\n    while (!allocation) {\\n\\n        for (int x = 0; x < ratings.length - 1; x++) {\\n            if (ratings[x] > ratings[x + 1] && !(candybag[x] > candybag[x + 1])) {\\n                candybag[x] += 1;\\n            } else if (ratings[x] < ratings[x + 1] && !(candybag[x] < candybag[x + 1])) {\\n                candybag[x + 1] += 1;\\n            }\\n        }\\n            allocation = check(candybag, ratings);\\n        }\\n\\n    int dist = 0;\\n    for (int x : candybag)\\n        dist += x;\\n\\n    return dist;\\n}\\n\\n// check returns true if every pair of neighbours follows the rule, and false at the first pair that breaks it.\\nstatic boolean check(int[] distribution, int[] ratings) {\\n\\n    for (int x = 0; x < ratings.length - 1; x++) {\\n        if (ratings[x] > ratings[x + 1] && !(distribution[x] > distribution[x + 1])) {\\n            return false;\\n        } else if (ratings[x] < ratings[x + 1] && !(distribution[x] < distribution[x + 1])) {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    return true;\\n}"}]},{"number":137,"title":"Single Number II","slug":"single-number-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Bit Manipulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SingleNumberII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an integer array nums where every element usually appears three\\n    times, find which single element appears exactly once. This algorithm must run in\\n    linear runtime and use only constant extra space.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of a given integer array can range from 1, 3 * 10^4. The value\\n    of each element can range from -2^31, 2^31 - 1. Each element appears for exactly 3\\n    times, except for one element.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Bit, Binary, Count\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to break down each binary number and compare it against other binary\\n    numbers\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # def singleNumber(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\\n    #     total = \\"\\"\\n    #     for num in nums:\\n    #         if total != \\"\\":\\n    #             total = self._bit_flip_str(total, self._dec_to_bin(num))\\n    #         else:\\n    #             total = self._dec_to_bin(num)\\n\\n    #     return self._count_bits(total)\\n\\n    # def _dec_to_bin(self, dec):\\n    #     if dec == 0:\\n    #         return \\"0\\"\\n\\n    #     result = \\"\\"\\n    #     while dec > 0:\\n    #         result = result + str(dec % 2)\\n    #         dec //= 2\\n    #     return result\\n\\n    # def _bit_flip_str(self, bin1, bin2):\\n    #     total = \\"\\"\\n    #     left = 0\\n\\n    #     max_len = max(len(bin1), len(bin2))\\n    #     min_len = min(len(bin1), len(bin2))\\n    #     ls_bin1, ls_bin2 = list(bin1), list(bin2)\\n\\n    #     while left < max_len:\\n    #         if left < min_len - 1:\\n    #             ls_bin1[left] = str(int(ls_bin1[left]) ^ int(ls_bin2[left]))\\n    #         else:\\n    #             ls_bin1[left] = str(0 ^ int(ls_bin1[left]))\\n    #         left += 1\\n\\n    #     return \\"\\".join(ls_bin1)\\n\\n    # def _count_bits(self, bin):\\n    #     return sum(1 for x in bin if x == 1)\\n\\n    # singleNumber takes a list where every value appears three times except one, and returns the one that appears once.\\n    # It keeps two ints, one and two, that act as a counter from 0 to 2 for every bit position at the same time.\\n    # For each bit, one holds the low bit of the count and two holds the high bit, and the count wraps back to 0 at 3.\\n    # For each x, one is updated first by xor with x, but only where two is not set.\\n    # Then two is updated by xor with x, but only where the new one is not set.\\n    # A bit seen three times returns to 0 in both, so only the bit counts that are 1 modulo 3 stay in one.\\n    # After the loop one holds the single number, and Python ints work for negative values here too.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(1) because it uses only two ints.\\n    def singleNumber(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\\n        one, two = 0, 0\\n        for x in nums:\\n            one = (one ^ x) & ~two\\n            two = (two ^ x) & ~one\\n\\n        return one"}]},{"number":138,"title":"Copy List with Random Pointer","slug":"copy-list-with-random-pointer","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Hash Table","Linked List"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import random\\n\\n# Definition for a Node.\\nclass Node:\\n    def __init__(self, x: int, next: \'Node\' = None, random: \'Node\' = None):\\n        self.val = int(x)\\n        self.next = next\\n        self.random = random\\n\\nclass CopyRandomPointer:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNERSTAND: You\'re given a linked list that contains an extra \'random\' pointer.\\n    The job of this field is to point to a random node which is a \'deep copy\'\\n    of the linked list that you are given. both \'next\' and \'random\' pointers\\n    of the NEW NODES should point to new nodes in the copied list. The random\\n    pointer can point to any of the values included in the \'deep copy\' of the original linked list\\n    including null.\\n\\n    Constraints: The random pointer can point to itself\\n\\n    MATCH: Linked List\\n\\n    PLAN: I think we\'re going to create an object level field called \'deep_copy.\'\\n    The function should pass in a linked list, then evaluate every node in said linked list, building said \'deep copy.\'\\n    Then we\'re going to take a random number generator which uses the length of both linked lists to\\n    determine what node in the deep copy each each nodes random pointer gives. That being said We\'re going extend\\n    the range +1 so that if it\'s creater than usize value length it instead points to null.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self):\\n       self.__deep_copy = Node(-1)\\n\\n    # copyRandomList takes the head of a linked list where each node also has a random pointer, and returns a deep copy.\\n    # First objectCopy builds a new list with the same values and next links, with all random pointers still empty.\\n    # Then assignRandom points each copied node\'s random at the copied node in the same position as the original\'s target.\\n    # To find that position it walks from the head of the original until it reaches the random target and counts the steps.\\n    # Then it walks the same number of steps in the copy and sets the pointer, or sets None if the original random is None.\\n    # This works because nodes are compared by identity, so a node that points to itself or to the head is found correctly.\\n    # An empty list returns None.\\n    # Each node does two walks of up to n steps, so time is O(n^2), and the space is O(n) for the copied nodes.\\n    def copyRandomList(self, head: \'Optional[Node]\') -> \'Optional[Node]\':\\n        self.objectCopy(head)\\n        self.assignRandom(head)\\n\\n        return self.__deep_copy\\n\\n    # objectCopy builds the copy of the next chain and stores its head in self.__deep_copy.\\n    def objectCopy(self, head: \'Optional[Node]\') -> None:\\n        temp1 = Node(-1)\\n        temp2 = temp1\\n        while head:\\n            temp1.next = Node(head.val)\\n            temp1 = temp1.next\\n            head = head.next\\n        self.__deep_copy = temp2.next\\n\\n    # assignRandom fills in the random pointers of the copy by finding the index of each original random target.\\n    def assignRandom(self, head: \'Optional[Node]\') -> None:\\n        # Main represents the original address which is iterated over.\\n        # This is where we get the original objects pointer addresses.\\n        main = head\\n        \'\'\'\\n        Create a pointer for the deep copy which will be edited and readressed,\\n        also create a pointer to the head of the pointer that is being edited and\\n        readressed.\\n        \'\'\'\\n        temp1 = self.__deep_copy\\n        original = temp1\\n        \'\'\'\\n        While we haven\'t reached the end of temp1 which starts as a\\n        reflection of continue iterating forward until the end.\\n        \'\'\'\\n        while temp1:\\n            # Create a brand new pointer to find the index of main.random,\\n            # and int value that represents usize/index.\\n            temp2 = head\\n            usize = -1\\n            \'\'\'\\n            While temp2 doesn\'t equal the address for the object main.random in memory.\\n            move temp2, which references the original. If main.rain is null,\\n            we point the random address in the deep_copy to null.\\n            \'\'\'\\n            if main.random is not None:\\n                usize = 0\\n                # With each iteration move the index forward.\\n                while temp2 != main.random and main.random != None:\\n                    temp2 = temp2.next\\n                    usize += 1\\n                \'\'\'\\n                We use temp2 to iterate over as it has the correct object\\n                address, the same as the deep copy.\\n                \'\'\'\\n                temp2 = self.__deep_copy\\n                # So, usize starts at one so it\'s innacuate we increment by one\\n                # immediately. Move the pointer forward to the correct address/position.\\n                for _ in range(usize):\\n                    temp2 = temp2.next\\n                # Once found state that the Node\'s field that we wish to edit (random)\\n                # equals the address within itself which is index/postionally accurate.\\n                temp1.random = temp2\\n            else:\\n                # The usize was never moved forward, just assign null.\\n                temp1.random = None\\n            # Push main pointer and temp1 pointer forward. We\'re moving on to the\\n            # next.random address for both main and temp1.\\n            main = main.next\\n            temp1 = temp1.next\\n        \'\'\'\\n        temp1 is no longer pointer to the head of itself, we set orginal\\n        as a pointer earlier to represent the start of temp1, node\\n        structure which contains all the correct data. We set the object level\\n        variable to original, which once again contains all the right data.\\n        \'\'\'\\n        self.__deep_copy = original"}]},{"number":141,"title":"Linked List Cycle","slug":"linked-list-cycle","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","Linked List","Two Pointers","Floyd\'s Cycle Finding Algorithm"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class ListNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.next = next\\n\\nclass LinkedListCycle:\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    NOTE:\\n    How can you tell that you\'re traversing the\\n    same node if you allow duplicate values?\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # hasCycle takes the head of a linked list and returns True if following next ever comes back to a node already seen.\\n    # It walks the list with p and keeps a set called arr with the id of each node it has visited.\\n    # It uses id(p), the identity of the node, so two different nodes with equal values are not confused.\\n    # If the id is already in the set, it has met the same node twice, so there is a cycle and it returns True.\\n    # If p reaches None the list ended and the answer is False.\\n    # Time is O(n) and the set uses O(n) space, which is why people use two pointers when O(1) space is needed.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def hasCycle(head: Optional[ListNode]) -> bool:\\n        arr = set()\\n        p = head\\n\\n        while p is not None:\\n            if id(p) not in arr:\\n                arr.add(id(p))\\n            else:\\n                return True\\n\\n            p = p.next\\n\\n        return False"}]},{"number":146,"title":"LRU Cache","slug":"lru-cache","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Hash Table","Linked List","Design","Doubly-Linked List"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import deque\\n\\nclass Node:\\n    def __init__(self, key, value):\\n        self.key   = key\\n        self.value = value\\n\\n    # Two nodes are equal when their keys are equal, which lets deque.remove find a node by key.\\n    def __eq__(self, other):\\n        if not isinstance(other, Node):\\n            return False\\n        return self.key == other.key\\n\\nclass LRUCache:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Design a data structure that follows the constraints of a\\n    LRU cache. As follows: Initialize the LRU cache with a positive size capacity.\\n    The get function should return the value of a key if it exists, otherwise return -1.\\n    The put function should update the value of a key if it exists and if not it should\\n    add value to a new key. If the number of keys exceeds the capacity evict the last used key.\\n\\n    MATCH: List, Queue\\n\\n    PLAN: We can create a doubly ended queue of key-value pairs, when the queue reaches capacity\\n    remove the last accessed element and add what is needed. Both functions must run in o(1) time.\\n    Doubly ended queue achieves this for both adding and removing.\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\\n        self.lookup = {}\\n        self.storage  = deque()\\n        self.capacity = capacity\\n\\n    # get takes a key and returns its value, or -1 if the key is not in the cache.\\n    # lookup is a dictionary from key to Node, and storage is a deque that keeps the nodes from least to most recently used.\\n    # If the node is already at the right end of the deque nothing needs to move.\\n    # Otherwise it removes the node from the deque and appends it at the right end to mark it as most recently used.\\n    # The dictionary lookup is O(1), but deque.remove scans the deque, so a get that moves a node is O(n), not the O(1) the notes ask for.\\n    def get(self, key: int) -> int:\\n        if key not in self.lookup:\\n            return -1\\n\\n        node = self.lookup[key]\\n\\n        if self.storage[-1] == Node(key, None):\\n            return node.value\\n\\n        self.storage.remove(Node(key, None))\\n        self.storage.append(node)\\n\\n        return node.value\\n\\n    # put stores a value for a key and evicts the least recently used key when the cache is full.\\n    # If the key exists, the old node is removed from the deque so that a fresh node can go at the right end.\\n    # If the key is new and the cache is full, the oldest node is taken from the left end with popleft and removed from lookup.\\n    # Then a new node is appended at the right end and saved in lookup.\\n    # Updating an existing key is O(n) because of deque.remove, while adding a new key is O(1).\\n    # A capacity of 0 would raise IndexError, since popleft would run on an empty deque.\\n    def put(self, key: int, value: int) -> None:\\n\\n        if key in self.lookup:\\n            self.storage.remove(Node(key, None))\\n\\n        elif len(self.storage) >= self.capacity:\\n            old = self.storage.popleft()\\n            del self.lookup[old.key]\\n\\n        node = Node(key, value)\\n\\n        self.storage.append(node)\\n        self.lookup[key] = node"}]},{"number":148,"title":"Sort List","slug":"sort-list","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers","Divide and Conquer","Sorting","Merge Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import deque\\n\\nclass ListNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.next = next\\n\\nclass SortList:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a linked list of unsorted inegers, both negative and positive.\\n    You must return a linked list sorted in ascending order.\\n\\n    The problem wants you to sort the list in O(n log n) time complexity and O(1)\\n    space complexity.\\n\\n    Constraints: The number of nodes in the list can range from 0, 5 * 10^5.\\n    The value of each node can range from -10^5, 10^5, each node can possess a\\n    duplicate of another value\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If a list is empty or only have one node. Return itself.\\n\\n    MATCH: Linked List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to evaluate all elements in the linked list. First we will start\\n    with the head of the list and we will probe all nodes after to know if there are any\\n    nodes which are smaller than itself. If that conditions is met we will swap the values\\n    of each node, move the pointer forward and do it again. Eventually we should have a\\n    linked list sorted in ascending order.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # def sortList(self, head: ListNode | None) -> ListNode | None:\\n    #     if not head or not head.next:\\n    #         return head\\n\\n    #     p1 = head\\n    #     p2 = p1.next\\n    #     swap = False\\n\\n    #     while p1:\\n\\n    #         if p2 == None:\\n    #             p1 = p1.next\\n    #             p2 = p1\\n\\n    #             if p1 == None:\\n    #                 break\\n\\n    #         if p2.val < p1.val:\\n    #             temp = p1.val\\n    #             p1.val = p2.val\\n    #             p2.val = temp\\n    #             swap = True\\n\\n    #         if swap:\\n    #             p2 = p1\\n    #             swap = False\\n    #         else:\\n    #             p2 = p2.next\\n\\n    #     return head\\n\\n    # sortList takes the head of a linked list and returns the head of a new list with the values in ascending order.\\n    # A list with zero or one node is returned as is.\\n    # Otherwise it copies every value into a Python list called vect and sorts that list.\\n    # Then it builds new nodes from the sorted values, starting from a dummy node, and returns the node after the dummy.\\n    # The result is correct for any values, and the original nodes are left untouched, but are not reused.\\n    # This does not meet the O(1) space idea of the problem, because it holds all values in a list.\\n    # Time is O(n log n) for the sort and space is O(n) for the list and the new nodes.\\n    def sortList(self, head: ListNode | None) -> ListNode | None:\\n        if not head or not head.next:\\n            return head\\n\\n        vect = []\\n\\n        while head:\\n            vect.append(head.val)\\n            head = head.next\\n\\n        vect.sort()\\n        result = ListNode(-1)\\n        p1 = result\\n\\n        for elm in vect:\\n            p1.next = ListNode(elm)\\n            p1 = p1.next\\n\\n        return result.next\\n\\n# strLinkedList is a test helper that turns a linked list into a string like [1, 2], or an empty list for no nodes.\\ndef strLinkedList(head: ListNode):\\n    if not head:\\n        return []\\n\\n    res = \\"[\\"\\n    while head:\\n        res += f\\"{head.val}, \\"\\n        head = head.next\\n    return res[:-2] + \\"]\\""}]},{"number":150,"title":"Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","slug":"evaluate-reverse-polish-notation","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math","Stack"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Stack;\\n\\npublic class EvaluateReversePolishNotation {\\n    // evalRPN takes an array of tokens in reverse Polish notation and returns the value of the expression.\\n    // It uses a stack of Integers, because an operator always works on the two most recent numbers.\\n    // For each token it tries Integer.parseInt, and if that works the number is pushed on the stack.\\n    // If parsing throws NumberFormatException the token is an operator, so it pops two numbers.\\n    // The first pop is the right operand and the second pop is the left operand, and getOperanded gives the result.\\n    // The result is pushed back, and after the last token the one value left on the stack is the answer.\\n    // Using an exception to tell numbers from operators is slow, and a bad token makes getOperanded throw again.\\n    // Time is O(n) for n tokens and the space is O(n) for the stack, and int division cuts toward zero.\\n    public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {\\n\\n        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();\\n\\n        for (String token : tokens) {\\n            try {\\n                Integer.parseInt(token);\\n                stack.push(Integer.parseInt(token));\\n            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\\n                Integer second = stack.pop();\\n                Integer first = stack.pop();\\n\\n                stack.push(getOperanded(token, second, first));\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return stack.pop();\\n    }\\n\\n    // getOperanded applies one operator token to two numbers, with the parameter names swapped so second is the left side.\\n    // Anything other than + - / * throws a NumberFormatException.\\n    public int getOperanded(String expectedOperand, Integer first, Integer second) {\\n        return switch (expectedOperand) {\\n            case \\"+\\" -> second + first;\\n            case \\"-\\" -> second - first;\\n            case \\"/\\" -> second / first;\\n            case \\"*\\" -> second * first;\\n            default -> throw new NumberFormatException();\\n        };\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":151,"title":"Reverse Words in a String","slug":"reverse-words-in-a-string","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ReverseWordsString {\\n\\n    // reverseWords takes a string and is meant to return its words in reverse order separated by single\\n    // spaces.\\n    // If the string has no space it is returned as is.\\n    // If it has exactly one space, the code copies the whole string into result without that space first,\\n    // which breaks the answer.\\n    // For example \\"hello world\\" comes out as \\"helloworldworld hello\\", and \\" a\\" comes out as \\"aa\\".\\n    // Then a loop goes from the right end, and every space at i appends the word after it (up to foundSpace)\\n    // and a space.\\n    // Next, the first word is added by finding the first space and appending everything before it.\\n    // A cleanup loop deletes doubled, leading and trailing spaces, and the last two ifs trim one more space at\\n    // each end.\\n    // This gives the right answers for the test strings in main, which all have two or more spaces, and for\\n    // inputs like \\"t \\".\\n    // deleteCharAt shifts the rest of the builder, so many extra spaces can make this O(n^2) in the worst\\n    // case, and space is O(n).\\n    public static String reverseWords(String s) { //spaces are at 10, 7, and 3\\n\\n        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\\n\\n        if (s.indexOf(\' \') == -1) {\\n            return s;\\n        } else if (s.indexOf(\' \') == s.lastIndexOf(\' \')) {\\n            result.append(s);\\n            result.deleteCharAt(s.indexOf(\' \'));\\n        }\\n\\n        int foundSpace = s.length();\\n\\n        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0 ; i--) {\\n            if (s.charAt(i) == \' \') {\\n\\n                result.append(s, i + 1, foundSpace);\\n                result.append(\' \');\\n\\n                //last found space\\n                foundSpace = i;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        //add the first word\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length() - 1; i++) {\\n            if (s.charAt(i) == \' \') {\\n                result.append(s, 0, i);\\n                break;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        // test justification considering spaces\\n        for (int i = 0; i < result.length() - 1; i++) {\\n            if (i == 0 && result.charAt(i) == \' \' && result.charAt(i + 1) == \' \') { result.deleteCharAt(i + 1); }\\n            if (i == result.length() - 1 && result.charAt(i) == \' \' && result.charAt(i - 1) == \' \') {result.deleteCharAt(i - 1); }\\n\\n            if (i == 0 && result.charAt(i) == \' \') result.deleteCharAt(i);\\n            if (i == result.length() - 1 && result.charAt(i) == \' \') result.deleteCharAt(i);\\n\\n            if (i > 0 && result.charAt(i) == \' \' && result.charAt(i - 1) == \' \') { result.deleteCharAt(i);  i--; }\\n            if (i < result.length() - 1 && result.charAt(i) == \' \' && result.charAt(i + 1) == \' \') { result.deleteCharAt(i);  i--; }\\n        }\\n\\n        //remove starting or trailing space\\n        if (result.charAt(0) == \' \') result.deleteCharAt(0);\\n        if (result.charAt(result.length() - 1) == \' \') result.deleteCharAt(result.length() - 1);\\n\\n        return result.toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":160,"title":"Intersection of Two Linked Lists","slug":"intersection-of-two-linked-lists","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","Linked List","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class IntersectionTwoLinkedLists {\\n    // getIntersectionNode takes the heads of two linked lists and returns the first node they share, or null.\\n    // If either head is null it returns null right away.\\n    // It is brute force, so for each node of list A it walks the whole of list B.\\n    // Nodes are compared with == so it checks the same object, not equal values.\\n    // On the first match it prints the value of that node and returns the node.\\n    // If the outer loop ends with no match it returns null.\\n    // Time is O(n * m) for lists of length n and m, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {\\n        if (headA == null || headB == null) return null;\\n        ListNode tempHeadA = headA;\\n        while (tempHeadA != null) {\\n            ListNode tempHeadB = headB;\\n            while (tempHeadB != null) {\\n                if (tempHeadA == tempHeadB) {\\n                    System.out.printf(\\"Intersected at \'\\" + tempHeadA.val + \\"\'\\");\\n                    return tempHeadA;\\n                }\\n                tempHeadB = tempHeadB.next;\\n            }\\n            tempHeadA = tempHeadA.next;\\n        }\\n        return null;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":169,"title":"Majority Element","slug":"majority-element","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Divide and Conquer","Sorting","Counting","Boyer\u2013Moore Majority Vote Algorithm"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\\n// https://leetcode.com/problems/majority-element\\npub struct MajorityElement;\\n\\nimpl MajorityElement {\\n    // majority_element takes a Vec of numbers and returns the value that appears most often.\\n    // The first loop counts how many times each number appears in a HashMap.\\n    // The second loop keeps the number with the biggest count so far in largest_data, as a value and count pair.\\n    // It only replaces the pair on a strictly bigger count, so the first one reached wins a tie.\\n    // With a true majority element (more than half the numbers) the biggest count is always that element.\\n    // If no element is a majority it just returns whichever number has the highest count, and an empty Vec returns 0.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(k) for the k distinct numbers.\\n    pub fn majority_element(nums: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\\n        let mut element_data: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new();\\n        let mut largest_data: (i32, i32) = (0, 0); // object val & object count\\n        for n in nums {\\n            *element_data.entry(n).or_insert(0) += 1;\\n        }\\n        for (key, value) in &element_data {\\n            if *value > largest_data.1 {\\n                largest_data.0 = *key;\\n                largest_data.1 = *value;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return largest_data.0\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":173,"title":"Binary Search Tree Iterator","slug":"binary-search-tree-iterator","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Stack","Tree","Design","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree","Iterator"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\n\\n// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class BinarySearchTreeIterator {\\n    private final ArrayList<Integer> orderList;\\n    private int _idx;\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: We\'re creating a class that allowes for the in-order traversal\\n    to be an iteratable function that produced an output each time it\'s called.\\n\\n    `int hasNext()` : Must return if there is a element to right of the current\\n    pointer during traversal.\\n\\n    `int next()` : Must move the point to the right and return the number at that\\n    pointer. If there is nothing retrn 0. Tip given to initalize the pointer to\\n    an irrelevant small number before starting iteration.\\n\\n    `BSTIterator(TreeNode root)` : The job of this function is to initlaize the tree\\n    object, given a consutrctor and initalize the pointer to a insignificant number\\n    and have it ready for in-order traversal.\\n\\n    Constraints: There can be 1, 10^9 nodes in the tree. Each node can have a\\n    value between 0, 106. At nist 10^5 calls are made to `hasNext()` and `next()`.\\n\\n    Question prefers for these functions to run in O(1) time complexity.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Binary Tree, In-Order Traversal\\n\\n    PLAN: We can start with the `int next()` method, it should be as simple as\\n    */\\n    // The constructor takes the root of a binary search tree and gets the iterator ready.\\n    // The idea is to do the whole in-order traversal once at the start and keep the values in a list.\\n    // In a binary search tree an in-order traversal visits the values from the smallest to the largest.\\n    // orderList is created empty, and inOrderToArrayList(root) fills it recursively.\\n    // _idx is the position of the next value to give out, and it starts at 0.\\n    // A null root just gives an empty list, so hasNext returns false from the start.\\n    // Time for the constructor is O(n) and space is O(n), which is more than the O(h) a stack version would use.\\n    public BinarySearchTreeIterator(TreeNode root) {\\n        this.orderList = new ArrayList<>();\\n        inOrderToArrayList(root);\\n        this._idx = 0;\\n    }\\n\\n    // next returns the value at _idx and then moves _idx one forward.\\n    // It is O(1), since it is a list lookup, and it does not check for the end, so calling it too often throws.\\n    public int next() {\\n        return orderList.get(_idx++);\\n    }\\n\\n    // hasNext returns true while _idx is still inside the list, which takes O(1) time.\\n    public boolean hasNext() {\\n        return _idx < orderList.size();\\n    }\\n\\n    // inOrderToArrayList is a recursive in-order walk: left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree.\\n    // Each value is added to orderList as it is visited, so the list ends up sorted.\\n    private void inOrderToArrayList(TreeNode root) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (root.left != null) {\\n            inOrderToArrayList(root.left);\\n        }\\n\\n        orderList.add(root.val);\\n\\n        if (root.right != null) {\\n            inOrderToArrayList(root.right);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Your BSTIterator object will be instantiated and called as such:\\n * BSTIterator obj = new BSTIterator(root);\\n * int param_1 = obj.next();\\n * boolean param_2 = obj.hasNext();\\n */"}]},{"number":179,"title":"Largest Number","slug":"largest-number","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","String","Greedy","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class LargestNumber:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-number\\n\\n    NOTE:\\n    We need to sort the numbers on a priority basis,\\n    the priority numbers should/might be the single digit numbers.\\n\\n    What is the fastest way to see if a number has only one digit?\\n    Are we going to go through the array of given numbers more than once?\\n\\n    introduce a multiplier each iteration x 10 call n / 10 into the list?\\n    Next iteration n / (10...) and so on?\\n\\n    Reverse sort to get decreasing order.\\n\\n    How do we decide a digit that has one more\\n    base than another should go infront or behind of a single digit number?\\n    TODO:\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # largestNumber takes a list of non-negative ints and returns the largest number you can make by joining them, as a string.\\n    # If the largest value is 0 it returns \\"0\\", so a list of zeros does not become \\"000\\".\\n    # priority_sort moves the numbers into priority in groups of 1 digit, 2 digits and so on, biggest first inside each group.\\n    # It removes every number from nums while doing so, so the list passed in by the caller ends up empty.\\n    # base_sort then orders priority with a swap loop that puts a before b when the text a+b is larger than b+a.\\n    # That comparison is what decides cases like 3 and 30, where \\"330\\" beats \\"303\\".\\n    # build_number_to_string joins the final order into one string.\\n    # The grouping step does not change the answer because base_sort alone fixes the order, so it is extra work.\\n    # Time is O(n^2) because of the swap loop and the nums.remove calls, and the space is O(n).\\n    def largestNumber(self, nums: List[int]) -> str:\\n        # If the minimum number in nums is 0 return 0\\n        if max(nums) == 0:\\n            return \\"0\\"\\n\\n        priority = []\\n\\n        self.priority_sort(nums, priority, 10)\\n        self.base_sort(priority)\\n        return self.build_number_to_string(priority)\\n\\n    # priority_sort moves numbers below base (10, 100, ...) from nums into aux, sorted high to low, and repeats with base * 10.\\n    def priority_sort(self, nums: List[int], aux: List, base: int) -> List:\\n        temp = []\\n        for n in nums:\\n            if n < base:\\n               temp.append(n)\\n\\n        for n in temp:\\n            nums.remove(n)\\n\\n        temp.sort(reverse = True)\\n        aux += temp\\n\\n        if len(nums) != 0:\\n            return self.priority_sort(nums, aux, (base * 10))\\n\\n        return aux\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    Take the nth digit of a number (left to right),\\n    if they are equal evaluate the next digit,\\n    if it\'s more but that number in front.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # base_sort swaps pairs in place so that the text a+b ends up before b+a whenever it is the larger number.\\n    def base_sort(self, nums: List[int]) -> List:\\n\\n        for k in range(len(nums)):\\n            for v in range(k, len(nums)):\\n                if int(str(nums[k]) + str(nums[v])) < int(str(nums[v]) + str(nums[k])):\\n                    temp = nums[k]\\n                    nums[k] = nums[v]\\n                    nums[v] = temp\\n\\n        return nums\\n\\n    # build_number_to_string joins the numbers into one string (its annotation says int but it returns a str).\\n    def build_number_to_string(self, lst: List) -> int:\\n        result = \\"\\"\\n        for n in lst:\\n            result += str(n)\\n        return result"}]},{"number":189,"title":"Rotate Array","slug":"rotate-array","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RotateArray {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'ve given an array and an integer k. Your job is to rotate\\n    the array by k amounts clocwise.\\n\\n    Constraints: Given k will always be a non negative integer with a value between 0, 10^5.\\n    The length of an array can range from 1, 10^5 elements long. Each element can have a\\n    value of integer min/max.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If k is zero just return the same array.\\n\\n    MATCH: Division, Queue, List\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to take k and divide it by the length of the array,\\n    the remainer will be how much we actually need to rotate said array. This is because a\\n    full rotation to the integer between k and the length of the array would just simply a full circle\\n    representing a copy. We\'re then going to take the last k / length of vect elements and then swap them,\\n    then take the proceeding elements up that weren\'t coppied and place them ahead.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    */\\n    // rotate takes an int array and a number k, and moves the array right by k places, in place, returning\\n    // nothing.\\n    // The idea is to copy the array and write the elements back starting from the right spot.\\n    // k %= nums.length first, because rotating by the length gives the same array back, so only the remainder\\n    // matters.\\n    // If k is 0 after that it returns right away, and the second part of that if (k % nums.length == 0) is\\n    // always true when k is 0, so it adds nothing.\\n    // copy is a clone of the original array.\\n    // The first loop writes the last k elements of copy to the front of nums, and the second loop writes the\\n    // first length - k elements after them.\\n    // This gives the right order, for example 1..7 with k = 3 becomes 5,6,7,1,2,3,4.\\n    // It would throw an ArithmeticException for an empty array because of the % by 0.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n) for the clone.\\n    public void rotate(int[] nums, int k) {\\n        k %= nums.length;\\n\\n        // Ask if there any any rotations to be conducted.\\n        if (k == 0 || k % nums.length == 0) return;\\n\\n        int[] copy = nums.clone();\\n\\n        // Push the last k elements ascending.\\n        int idx = 0;\\n        for (int i = copy.length - k; i < copy.length; i++) {\\n            nums[idx++] = copy[i];\\n        }\\n\\n        // Dump the numbers that come after.\\n        for (int i = 0; i < copy.length - k; i++) {\\n            nums[idx++] = copy[i];\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    private static String stringifyPrimitiveIntegerArray(int[] nums) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n\\n        for (int n : nums) {\\n            sb.append(n).append(\\", \\");\\n        }\\n\\n        sb.replace(sb.length() - 2, sb.length(), \\"\\");\\n\\n        return sb.append(\\"]\\").toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":190,"title":"Reverse Bits","slug":"reverse-bits","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Divide and Conquer","Bit Manipulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ReverseBits {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Reverse bits is a function that takes an i32/ signed 32 bit\\n    integer, n and transforms it int binary. Reverses that binary and then\\n    displays the signed 32 bit integer value again.\\n\\n    Integers can get rather large so this must be done in a time efficient manor.\\n\\n    Constraints: integers can range from 0 to 2^32 - 2. The value of n will always be an even number.\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Bit-wise shifting.\\n\\n    PLAN: To retrieve the binary value of a number you need to constantly\\n    append the remainder until the number you\'re working (the quotent) with is zero.\\n    We don\'t need to this here, it\'s just worth mentioning.\\n\\n    Reverse the number bits of the binary requires starting with a blank i32 variable to store data\\n    the bitwise operation shifting to the left to create a blank spot and then xor/copy the right\\n    most bit from n into that blank spot in your created variable. Finally, shift the right most digit\\n    in n to the right over by one to get it ready.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(1) You\'re completing the same operations 32 times to create a 32 bit integer.\\n    division and then looping to create a binary number.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) The value of the binary/integer may decrease or increase,\\n    but the space to contain these bits stay the same, 32 bits total.\\n    */\\n    // reverseBits takes a 32 bit int n and returns the int you get by reading its 32 bits in the opposite\\n    // order.\\n    // The idea is to peel bits off the right end of n and push them onto the right end of result, so the first\\n    // bit out ends up last.\\n    // result starts at 0 and the loop runs exactly 32 times, one per bit.\\n    // Each time, result <<= 1 makes room on the right, and result ^= (n & 1) copies the lowest bit of n into\\n    // that spot.\\n    // Then n >>= 1 moves the next bit of n down to the lowest position.\\n    // The >> is a signed shift, but that does not matter because only the lowest bit is read each time and\\n    // there are exactly 32 steps.\\n    // So it also works for negative ints, since all 32 bits are processed and the result is just reinterpreted\\n    // as a signed int.\\n    // Time is O(1) because it is always 32 steps, and extra space is O(1).\\n    public int reverseBits(int n) {\\n        // Instantiate 32 bit integer\\n        int result = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) {\\n            // Move left most value in result over by one.\\n            result <<= 1;\\n            // Place right most value in n in the right most position.\\n            result ^= (n & 1);\\n            // Move right most value in n over by one.\\n            n >>= 1;\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":199,"title":"Binary Tree Right Side View","slug":"binary-tree-right-side-view","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass BinarySearchTreeRightSideView:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-right-side-view/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Your goal is to traverse the most right nodes in a\\n    binary search tree from root to leaf. You\'re recording the nodes\\n    you touch in a vector, which you then return.\\n\\n    Constraints: The total nodes in a tree can range from 0, 100 and the value\\n    of each node can be -100 to 100.\\n\\n    Edge Case: If you\'re given an empty tree, return an empty vector.\\n\\n    MATCH: DFS\\n\\n    PLAN: I think I\'m going to ask three questions after covering the root node,\\n    and these will also be the statements that decide on us appending to the returned vector.\\n    1. What is the level of the deepest leaf starting from the right side from the root?\\n    2. If we\'re visiting a left node, did the parent have a right node? If yes don\'t append, else append.\\n    3. If we\'re done exploring the right side\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # rightSideView takes the root of a binary tree and returns the values you see from the right side, one per level.\\n    # An empty tree returns an empty list, and a single node returns a list with just that value.\\n    # The inner levelCollect walks the tree depth first, left before right, and stores every value in a sublist for its level.\\n    # Because the right child is visited after the left one, the last value in each level\'s sublist is the rightmost node.\\n    # The final loop takes the last value of every sublist and puts them in result from the top level down.\\n    # This collects every node even though only one per level is needed, which costs more memory but is simple.\\n    # Time is O(n), and space is O(n) for the level lists plus O(h) for the recursion.\\n    def rightSideView(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[int]:\\n        if root is None:\\n            return []\\n\\n        if root.left is None and root.right is None:\\n            return [root.val]\\n\\n        def levelCollect(root, vect, level = 0) -> List[List[int]]:\\n            if root == None:\\n                return (vect)\\n\\n            if len(vect) <= level:\\n                vect.append([])\\n\\n            vect[level].append(root.val)\\n\\n            levelCollect(root.left, vect, level + 1)\\n            levelCollect(root.right, vect, level + 1)\\n\\n            return (vect)\\n\\n        vect = levelCollect(root, [], 0)\\n\\n        result = []\\n\\n        for i in range(0, len(vect)):\\n            result.append(vect[i][len(vect[i]) - 1])\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":204,"title":"Count Primes","slug":"count-primes","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math","Enumeration","Number Theory","Primality Test","Sieve Theory","Prime Number Sieve"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class CountPrimes {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an integer n return a count of all positive numbers less than n which are prime.\\n\\n    Constraints: Given integer n can range from 0, 5*10^6.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If n is less than or equal to 2 return zero.\\n\\n    MATCH:\\n\\n    PLAN:\\n    */\\n    // countPrimes takes an int n and returns how many prime numbers are strictly less than n.\\n    // For n of 2 or less it returns 0 right away, since there are no primes below 2.\\n    // Otherwise it loops i from 2 to n - 1, asks isPrime(i), and adds 1 to count for each prime.\\n    // So it tests every number one by one, with no sieve and no memory of earlier results.\\n    // It is correct, because isPrime is correct for every number it is given.\\n    // It is slow for big n, since each test can take about sqrt(i) steps.\\n    // Time is about O(n * sqrt(n)) in the worst case, and space is O(1).\\n    public int countPrimes(int n) {\\n        if (n <= 2) return 0;\\n\\n        int count = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {\\n            if (isPrime(i)) {\\n                count++;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return count;\\n    }\\n\\n    // isPrime tests one number: below 2 is not prime, 2 is prime, and other even numbers are not.\\n    // For odd numbers it tries odd divisors j from 3 while j * j <= num, and one exact division means not prime.\\n    private boolean isPrime(int num) {\\n        if (num <= 1) return false;\\n        if (num == 2) return true;\\n        if (num % 2 == 0) return false;\\n\\n        // Check odd divisors up to the square root of num\\n        for (int j = 3; j * j <= num; j += 2) {\\n            if (num % j == 0) return false;\\n        }\\n        return true;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":205,"title":"Isomorphic Strings","slug":"isomorphic-strings","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class IsomorphicStrings:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given two strings `s` & `t` confirm that they are isomorphic.\\n    A string is isomorphic is the characters follow the same pattern depsite\\n    a difference in value.\\n\\n    MATCH: Hash Map\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to make sure the length of both strings match. If not then we return False.\\n    We\'re going to make a map, we iterate through each character in both strings. We make a key value relationship\\n    Where if a key appears with a different value than previously seen we return false.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # isIsomorphic takes strings s and t and returns True if each character of s can be replaced by one fixed character of t.\\n    # If the lengths differ it returns False straight away.\\n    # seen_pairs maps a character of s to its partner in t, and seen_values holds the characters of t that are already used.\\n    # When both the s character and the t character are new, it records the pair.\\n    # Otherwise it returns False if the s character is new (so its t partner is taken) or its recorded partner differs from t[i].\\n    # Checking both directions is what stops \\"badc\\" and \\"baba\\", where two different letters would map to the same letter.\\n    # If the loop ends without a conflict it returns True.\\n    # Time is O(n) and the space is O(k) for the different characters.\\n    def isIsomorphic(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\\n        if len(s) != len(t):\\n            return False\\n\\n        seen_pairs  = {}\\n        seen_values = set([])\\n\\n        for i in range(len(s)):\\n            var1 = s[i]\\n            var2 = t[i]\\n\\n            if not s[i] in seen_pairs and not t[i] in seen_values:\\n                seen_pairs[s[i]] = t[i]\\n                seen_values.add(t[i])\\n\\n            elif not s[i] in seen_pairs or seen_pairs[s[i]] != t[i]:\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":206,"title":"Reverse Linked List","slug":"reverse-linked-list","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Linked List","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ReversedLinkedList {\\n\\n    // reverseList takes the head of a singly linked list and returns the head of the same list reversed.\\n    // If the list is empty or has one node it returns head as is.\\n    // Otherwise head.next is saved in tail and head.next is set to null, so the old first node becomes the end\\n    // of the list.\\n    // In the loop, temp saves tail.next, then tail.next is pointed back at head, so one link is flipped.\\n    // Then head moves up to tail and tail moves to temp, so head always means the front of the reversed part.\\n    // When tail becomes null every link is flipped and head is the old last node, which is returned.\\n    // The nodes are reused and the list is changed in place.\\n    // Time is O(n) since each node is visited once, and extra space is O(1).\\n    public static ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {\\n\\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) {\\n            return head;\\n        }\\n\\n        ListNode tail = head.next;\\n        head.next = null;\\n\\n        while (tail != null) {\\n                ListNode temp = tail.next;\\n                tail.next = head;\\n                head = tail;\\n                tail = temp;\\n            }\\n        return head;\\n    }\\n\\n    // This is a different problem from reversing: it takes the head of a sorted list and removes duplicate\\n    // values in place.\\n    // result walks the list, and if result.val equals result.next.val it skips the next node, otherwise it\\n    // moves forward.\\n    // It stays on the same node after a skip so runs of three or more equal values are removed too.\\n    // The head is returned and the first node is never removed.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    public static ListNode deleteDuplicates2(ListNode head) {\\n        if (head == null) return null;\\n\\n        ListNode result = head;\\n\\n        while (result.next != null) {\\n            if (result.val == result.next.val) {\\n                result.next = result.next.next;\\n            } else {\\n                result = result.next;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return head;\\n    }\\n\\n    public static ListNode reverseList2(ListNode head) {\\n        // Step 1: Check if the list is empty or has only one node\\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) {\\n            return head; // Nothing to reverse\\n        }\\n\\n        // Step 2: Initialize two pointers\\n        ListNode current = head.next; // Start from the second node\\n        head.next = null; // The first node will become the tail, so it should point to null\\n        ListNode previous = head; // This will keep track of the reversed part\\n\\n        // Step 3: Traverse the list and reverse links\\n        while (current != null) {\\n            // Step 3a: Save the next node temporarily\\n            ListNode nextNode = current.next;\\n\\n            // Step 3b: Reverse the current node\'s pointer\\n            current.next = previous;\\n\\n            // Step 3c: Move the previous pointer forward\\n            previous = current;\\n\\n            // Step 3d: Move the current pointer forward\\n            current = nextNode;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Step 4: Previous now points to the new head\\n        return previous;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":210,"title":"Course Schedule II","slug":"course-schedule-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Graph Theory","Topological Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class CourseScheduleII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a nubers of courses reqired to graduate and a prerequisite list of courses,\\n    return the correct ordering of classes.\\n\\n    Constraints:\\n    - The value of numCourses can range between 1, 2000.\\n    - The amount of classes available in the prequisite\\n    list is 0, `numCourses * numCourses` which also evaluates to `numCourses ^ 2 - 1`.\\n    - Each element inside prequisites is guarenteed to be a pair.\\n    - The value of each sub-element- representing the `(key, value)` pair of each offered class\\n    can range from 0, numsCourses.\\n    - All pairs are distinct.\\n\\n    ALERT! It\'s important to understand that the length of second parameter, can essentially be\\n    twice as large as the integer variable `numCourses`.\\n\\n    MATCH: Hash Map, List\\n\\n    PLAN:\\n\\n    1. We\'re going to iterate through 0..numCourses.\\n    2. We\'re then going to matain a hash map of taken classes.\\n    2.1 This is were the existence of a class in the data structure will reveal that it has been taken.\\n    we check our data structure to see if our boolean\\n    key value is True.\\n    3. We\'re now going to add a course to the list under the cirumstances that it hasn\'t been taken,\\n    and under the cirumstances that it\'s prerequisite has been taken. I think it could be useful to\\n    maintain a hash map/set for the prerequisites to turn look up times into constant time.\\n    3.1 If our prerequisite has not been taken, we take it.\\n\\n    init the set and order with the first class of id 0.\\n\\n    wait, you actually might have to take 0 before one as\\n    indicated by the 10th test case 11/7/2026.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # findOrder takes numCourses and [course, prerequisite] pairs and returns an order to take all courses, or [].\\n    # psheet maps each course to the list of courses it needs first.\\n    # The inner foo is a depth first search: cycle holds the courses on the current path, and visit holds the finished ones.\\n    # If foo reaches a course already in cycle, the prerequisites loop back on themselves, so it returns False.\\n    # After all prerequisites of a course are done, the course is added to option, so every course comes after what it needs.\\n    # The loop starts foo from every course, and any False makes the function return an empty list.\\n    # The recursion is as deep as the longest prerequisite chain, which can pass Python\'s default limit of 1000.\\n    # Time is O(V + E) for courses V and prerequisite pairs E, and space is O(V + E).\\n    def findOrder(self, numCourses: int, prerequisites: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:\\n        psheet = { c:[] for c in range(numCourses) }\\n\\n        for id, required_id in prerequisites:\\n            psheet[id].append(required_id)\\n\\n        visit, cycle = set(), set()\\n        option = []\\n\\n        def foo(id):\\n            if id in cycle:\\n                return False\\n\\n            if id in visit:\\n                return True\\n\\n            cycle.add(id)\\n\\n            for required_id in psheet[id]:\\n                if foo(required_id) == False:\\n                    return False\\n\\n            cycle.remove(id)\\n            visit.add(id)\\n            option.append(id)\\n            return True\\n\\n        for c in range(numCourses):\\n            if foo(c) == False:\\n                return []\\n\\n        return option"}]},{"number":217,"title":"Contains Duplicate","slug":"contains-duplicate","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.HashSet;\\n\\npublic class containsDuplicate {\\n    // containsDuplicate takes an int array and returns true if some value appears at least twice.\\n    // It puts every number into a HashSet, which keeps only one copy of each value.\\n    // If there were no duplicates the set has as many elements as the array, so the sizes are equal.\\n    // If the set is smaller, at least one number was dropped as a duplicate, so it returns true.\\n    // It always reads the whole array, even if the duplicate is found at the start.\\n    // An empty array returns false.\\n    // Time is O(n) on average and space is O(n) for the set.\\n    public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();\\n\\n        for (int x : nums) {\\n                set.add(x);\\n        }\\n\\n        return set.size() != nums.length;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":227,"title":"Basic Calculator II","slug":"basic-calculator-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math","String","Stack"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import deque\\n\\nclass BasicCalculatorII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a string that represents a mathematical\\n    expression that can be performed by a calculator. You need to finish\\n    the equation and return it\'s value.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of the given expression can range from 1, 3*10^5. The\\n    express consists of `\'+\', \'-\', \'*\', \'/\'`. We\'re always given a valid expression.\\n    All integers are non negative and in the range of 0, 2^31 - 1. The answer is\\n    guarenteed to fit inside a 32 bit integer.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If we\'re given an expression with less than 2 characters, return the\\n    sole character in the expression if it is a number. If there are two characters in the\\n    expression and one is an operator, return zero.\\n\\n    MATCH: Deque\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to feed characters into a deque. We\'re looking for characters in\\n    a specific order. We need: one number, an operator, and another number. We\'re going\\n    to then check the operator for privilage. If it\'s an operator involving multiplication\\n    or division we handle it first. If it\'s an operator involving addition and subtraction,\\n    we handle it second. But before we move it to the back of the deque in the order it appeared.\\n    We\'re going to probe the expression string for an operator sign multiplication or division.\\n    If we manage to find one of those operators then we\'re going to ignore the previous operator\\n    and the number on the right most side. We\'re going to use the new oper and the number after in\\n    it. Whatever the precidence is- we append it to the result deque then. When we\'ve handled all\\n    the privilaged operators, multication and division, we recreate the function, but specifically\\n    with handling addition and subtraction in mind. We create a total adding and subtracting from it,\\n    then finally returning that total.\\n\\n    NOTE: We don\'t handle whitespace. `.split()` handles it.\\n    What if we have a number spanning multiple characters, how do we recognize this?\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # calculate takes an expression string with non-negative ints, + - * / and spaces, and returns its integer value.\\n    # It puts spaces around each operator and splits on whitespace, so numbers of any length become one token.\\n    # The first pass handles * and /: the operator is combined with the number popped from res and the number after it.\\n    # Plus, minus and the other numbers are appended to the deque res unchanged, so res keeps only + and - between numbers.\\n    # If res has one item it is returned, else a second pass folds (a, operator, b) from the left into one value.\\n    # Division uses //, which matches the problem because the numbers are never negative at that point.\\n    # The first if (len(s) < 2 and isinstance(s, int)) can never be true for a string, so it does nothing.\\n    # Time is O(n) for the tokens and both passes, and space is O(n) for the token list and the deque.\\n    def calculate(self, s: str) -> int:\\n        if len(s) < 2 and isinstance(s, int):\\n            return int(s)\\n\\n        opers = [\'*\', \'/\', \'+\', \'-\']\\n\\n        for op in opers:\\n            s = s.replace(op, f\\" {op} \\")\\n\\n        s_list = s.split()\\n        res = deque([])\\n        left = 0\\n\\n        # Handle all muliplication and division\\n        while left < len(s_list):\\n\\n            curr = s_list[left]\\n\\n            if curr == opers[2] or curr == opers[3]:\\n                res.append(curr)\\n            elif curr == opers[0] or curr == opers[1]:\\n                res.append(self.helper(curr, res.pop(), s_list[left + 1]))\\n                left += 1\\n            else:\\n                res.append(curr)\\n\\n            left += 1\\n\\n        if len(res) < 2:\\n            return int(res[0])\\n\\n        if self.helper2(res):\\n            return int(\\"\\".join(res))\\n\\n        # Handle all addition and subtraction\\n        while len(res) > 1:\\n            a, operand, b = res.popleft(), res.popleft(), res.popleft()\\n            res.appendleft(self.helper(operand, a, b))\\n\\n        return int(res[0])\\n\\n    # helper applies one operator to two values, turning both into ints first, and does integer division for \'/\'.\\n    def helper(self, operand, a, b):\\n        match operand:\\n            case \'*\': return int(a) * int(b)\\n            case \'/\': return int(a) // int(b)\\n            case \'+\': return int(a) + int(b)\\n            case \'-\': return int(a) - int(b)\\n\\n    # helper2 returns True when every item is made only of digits, which means no + or - is left to process.\\n    def helper2(self, list):\\n        for ch in list:\\n            if not str(ch).isdigit():\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":230,"title":"Kth Smallest Element in a BST","slug":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\n\\n// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class KthSmallestElementInbBST {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: The goal is to find the kth smallest element in a bst,\\n    where k is the integer given in the parameters. You need to search the\\n    entire tree and evaluate all elements against one another.\\n    You should return the value of the selected element.\\n\\n    Constraints: 1 <= k <= n <= 104, meaning the value of k & n can be between 1 and 104.\\n    Edge Case: You can be given a bst with only one element.\\n    What if k is larger than the length of the bst.\\n\\n    MATCH: binary tree traversal, post, pre, in-order.\\n\\n    PLAN: I will go through the binary tree adding each of\\n    these elements into a list and return return the index where its value is k.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) because we have to go through the entire tree despite\\n    the other operations inside this fuction.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: o(N) because our list will grow with the BST.\\n\\n    IMPLEMENT:\\n    */\\n    // kthSmallest takes the root of a binary search tree and k, and returns the k th smallest value.\\n    // It calls treeToInteger, which collects all the values into a list.\\n    // The helper goes left, then the node, then right (in-order), so in a BST the list comes out sorted.\\n    // Because the list is sorted, the answer is the item at index k - 1.\\n    // If k is bigger than the number of nodes, get throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException.\\n    // It visits the whole tree even when k is small, since it does not stop early.\\n    // Time is O(n) and the space is O(n) for the list, plus the recursion depth.\\n    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {\\n        return treeToInteger(root, new ArrayList<>()).get(k - 1);\\n    }\\n\\n    private static ArrayList<Integer> treeToInteger(TreeNode root, ArrayList<Integer> lsx) {\\n        if (root == null) return lsx;\\n\\n        treeToInteger(root.left, lsx);\\n        lsx.add(root.val);\\n        treeToInteger(root.right, lsx);\\n\\n        return lsx;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":231,"title":"Power of Two","slug":"power-of-two","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Bit Manipulation","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class PowerOfTwo {\\n\\n    // isPowerOfTwo takes an int n and returns true if n is 2 raised to some whole number, otherwise false.\\n    // It uses a bit trick instead of dividing.\\n    // A power of two has exactly one bit set in binary, like 1000.\\n    // Subtracting 1 flips that bit to 0 and turns every lower bit into 1, like 0111.\\n    // So n & (n - 1) is 0 only when n had a single set bit.\\n    // The n > 0 check is needed because 0 and negative numbers must return false.\\n    // The if and else just return true or false, so it could return the condition directly.\\n    // Time and space are both O(1).\\n    public static boolean isPowerOfTwo(int n) {\\n        if (n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":232,"title":"Implement Queue using Stacks","slug":"implement-queue-using-stacks","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Stack","Design","Queue"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import deque\\n\\nclass ImplementQueueStacks:\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: The directions are to implement a queue using one or more stacks.\\n    Based on the language you\'re using the data structure, stack, may or may not be supported.\\n    Implement this as a class defining all basic operations relative to a queue:\\n    void push, int pop, int peek, and boolean empty.\\n    You can only use standard operations found in a stack or deque.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.stack = []\\n        self.op_stack = []\\n\\n    # push puts the new value on top of the main stack, which takes O(1).\\n    def push(self, x: int) -> None:\\n        self.stack.append(x)\\n\\n    # pop removes and returns the front of the queue, which is the oldest value, at the bottom of the main stack.\\n    # It moves every value from the main stack onto op_stack, which reverses the order.\\n    # Now the oldest value is on top of op_stack, so it is popped and kept as the result.\\n    # Then the rest of op_stack is moved back to the main stack, which puts the values in their original order again.\\n    # Every pop moves all the values twice, so it costs O(n), and there is no shortcut for the next call.\\n    # Popping from an empty queue raises IndexError, because op_stack is empty.\\n    def pop(self) -> int:\\n        for k in range(0, len(self.stack)):\\n            curr = self.stack.pop()\\n            self.op_stack.append(curr)\\n\\n        result = self.op_stack.pop()\\n\\n        for _ in range(0, len(self.op_stack)):\\n            curr = self.op_stack.pop()\\n            self.stack.append(curr)\\n\\n        return result\\n\\n    # peek returns the front of the queue without removing it, using the same two stacks as pop.\\n    # It moves every value to op_stack, and when it moves the last one (the oldest value) it pushes that value twice.\\n    # The top copy is popped as the result, and the other copy stays in op_stack.\\n    # Then everything left in op_stack is moved back, so the main stack has the same values in the same order as before.\\n    # Like pop it costs O(n) for each call, and on an empty queue it raises IndexError.\\n    def peek(self) -> int:\\n        con = len(self.stack)\\n        for k in range(0, len(self.stack)):\\n            curr = self.stack.pop()\\n            self.op_stack.append(curr)\\n            if k == con - 1:\\n                self.op_stack.append(curr)\\n\\n        result = self.op_stack.pop()\\n\\n        for _ in range(0, len(self.op_stack)):\\n            curr = self.op_stack.pop()\\n            self.stack.append(curr)\\n\\n        return result\\n\\n    # empty is True when the main stack has no values.\\n    def empty(self) -> bool:\\n        return len(self.stack) == 0"}]},{"number":234,"title":"Palindrome Linked List","slug":"palindrome-linked-list","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers","Stack","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# Definition for singly-linked list.\\nclass ListNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.next = next\\n\\nclass PalindromeLinkedList:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/palindrome-linked-list/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a linked list. Your job is to tell if the\\n    list is a palindrome. A palindrome is when data interpereted front to\\n    back or back to front resembles the same cadence or pattern.\\n\\n    Constraints: Numbers of nodes in linked list ranges from 1, 10^5.\\n    The value of each node ranges from 0, 9.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If a list only has 1 element return that one element.\\n\\n    MATCH: Linked List Traversal\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to get the length of the linked list first, storing it in a variable.\\n    After I\'ll evaluate every element in the list, reaching the end of the\\n    list where I\'ll ask if the value of both nodes are equal.\\n    If the not we return false. We do this continuing until the length of our variable / 2.\\n    If the list is not even we will ignore the middle value which is handled by insiting\\n    integer division or flooring the division of our length.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N)\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # isPalindrome takes the head of a singly linked list and returns True if its values read the same both ways.\\n    # The first loop walks the list once, copying every value into the list store and counting the length.\\n    # half_point is length // 2, so the middle value of an odd length list is never compared.\\n    # The second loop compares store[i] with store[length - 1 - i] and returns False on the first pair that differs.\\n    # If no pair differs it returns True, and a one node list or an empty list also returns True.\\n    # The list store makes the extra space O(n), so the O(1) space in the docstring does not match this code.\\n    # Time is O(n) for the walk and the half-length comparison.\\n    def isPalindrome(self, head: Optional[ListNode]) -> bool:\\n\\n        # Objects\\n        store = []\\n\\n        # Get length & Build List\\n        length = 0\\n\\n        while not head == None:\\n            store.append(head.val)\\n            head = head.next\\n            length += 1\\n\\n        half_point =  length // 2\\n\\n        for i in range(0, length):\\n            if i == half_point:\\n                break\\n\\n            if store[i] != store[length - 1 - i]:\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":242,"title":"Valid Anagram","slug":"valid-anagram","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.HashMap;\\n\\npublic class isAnagram {\\n    // isAnagram takes two strings and returns true if one is a rearrangement of the other.\\n    // If the lengths differ it returns false right away.\\n    // Otherwise it builds a map of letter counts for each string with makeHash.\\n    // The two maps are compared with equals, which is true only when every letter has the same count in both.\\n    // The check is case sensitive, so \\"a\\" and \\"A\\" count as different letters.\\n    // Time is O(n * k) for k different letters because makeHash is slow, which is up to O(n^2), and space is O(k).\\n    public static boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {\\n        if (s.length() != t.length()) return false;\\n        HashMap<Character, Integer> myFirstMap = makeHash(s);\\n        HashMap<Character, Integer> mySecondMap = makeHash(t);\\n        return myFirstMap.equals(mySecondMap);\\n    }\\n\\n    // makeHash keeps a list of letters already seen and scans the string again for every new letter to count it.\\n    // This works but is slower than counting everything in one pass.\\n    public static HashMap<Character, Integer> makeHash(String s) {\\n\\n        ArrayList<Character> a = new ArrayList<>();\\n        HashMap<Character, Integer> myFirstMap = new HashMap<>();\\n        int counter = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            if (!a.contains(s.charAt(i))) {\\n                a.add(s.charAt(i));\\n                for (int j = i; j < s.length(); j++) {\\n                    if (a.get(a.size() - 1) == s.charAt(j)) {\\n                        counter++;\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            } else continue;\\n            myFirstMap.put(a.get(a.size() - 1), counter);\\n            counter = 0;\\n        }\\n        return myFirstMap;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\Derpe\\\\\\\\IdeaProjects\\\\\\\\3115\\\\\\\\CISC 3115 final212_2025\\\\\\\\src\\\\\\\\a.txt\\n// C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\Derpe\\\\\\\\IdeaProjects\\\\\\\\3115\\\\\\\\CISC 3115\\\\\\\\final212_2025\\\\\\\\src\\\\\\\\b.txt"}]},{"number":257,"title":"Binary Tree Paths","slug":"binary-tree-paths","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","Backtracking","Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass BinaryTreePaths:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a binary tree collect all paths within said tree.\\n    Seperate each path with an arrow operator. Return them all in a list of strings.\\n    A path constitutes root to leaf. You can traverse the list any any order\\n    just make sure to covery every path.\\n\\n    Constraints: A tree can contain between 1, 100 nodes.\\n    Each node can posses a value between -100, 100.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given a tree node with one node return a list of only that node.\\n\\n    MATCH: Depth First Search\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to collect branch deoth first in a string, each time\\n    we recurse to another node we seperate them with an arrow operator.\\n    When we hit the leaf we add it to our list. This collects all paths\\n    in a list which we return there after.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(log n) binary tree\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) growing list\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # binaryTreePaths takes the root of a binary tree and returns every root to leaf path as a string like \\"1->2->5\\".\\n    # The inner helper walks depth first and builds the path string sb, adding the node value and \\"->\\" before each child.\\n    # A node with no left and no right child is a leaf, so its finished string is appended to result.\\n    # Strings are copied on each call, so each branch has its own path, and the line sb = \\"\\" does nothing useful.\\n    # This gives the right paths for a tree with at least one node, in left before right order.\\n    # The limit is that an empty tree hits `return [root.val]` with root as None, which raises an AttributeError.\\n    # Time is O(n * h) since each path string is built by copying, and the space for the result is also about O(n * h).\\n    # The docstring says O(log n) time, which is not what the code does.\\n    def binaryTreePaths(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[str]:\\n\\n        if not root:\\n            return [root.val]\\n\\n        def helper(root: Optional[TreeNode], sb: str, result: List[str]) -> List[str]:\\n            if not root:\\n                return result\\n\\n            sb += str(root.val)\\n\\n            if root.left:\\n                helper(root.left, sb + \\"->\\", result)\\n\\n            if root.right:\\n                helper(root.right, sb + \\"->\\", result)\\n\\n            if not root.left and not root.right:\\n                result.append(sb)\\n                sb = \\"\\"\\n\\n            return result\\n\\n        return helper(root, \\"\\", [])"}]},{"number":258,"title":"Add Digits","slug":"add-digits","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Simulation","Number Theory"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class AddDigits:\\n    # addDigits takes a non-negative int and returns the single digit you get by adding its digits again and again.\\n    # If the number is already below 10 it is returned as is, which also covers 0.\\n    # Otherwise the number is turned into a string, and each character is converted to an int and added to total.\\n    # Then the function calls itself on that total, and this repeats until the number has one digit.\\n    # The digit sum is always smaller than the number once it has two or more digits, so the recursion ends.\\n    # A negative input is below 10, so it is returned unchanged instead of being reduced.\\n    # Time is O(log n) per call, and the recursion only goes a few levels deep, so the space is small.\\n    def addDigits(self, num: int) -> int:\\n        if num < 10:\\n            return num\\n\\n        total = 0\\n        ints = list(str(num))\\n        for k in ints:\\n            total += int(k)\\n\\n        return self.addDigits(total)"}]},{"number":263,"title":"Ugly Number","slug":"ugly-number","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import typing\\n\\nimport math\\n\\nclass UglyNumber:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a number and your job is to determine if it\'s ugly or not.\\n    The qualifications for an ugly number are that it doesn\'t have a prime factor other than 2, 3, or 5.\\n    Your job is to return a boolean value based on the status of this numbers ugliness.\\n\\n    Constraints: Value of number can range from -2^31, 2^31.\\n    Edge Cases: If number is negative proccess absolute value.\\n\\n    MATCH: Greedy\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to iterate backwards represented by i starting from given n to 2 asking a long the way if\\n    absolute value of said number of mod i gives zero. If yes it\'s added to a list. If said number is a prime number other\\n    than 2, 3, or 5 immediately return yes.\\n\\n    That being said we don\'t need to check for two, and odd numbers aside from 2 are prime.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n    Space Compelxity:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # isUgly takes an int n and returns True if its only prime factors are 2, 3 and 5.\\n    # The idea is to divide out every 2, 3 and 5, and see whether anything is left over.\\n    # Zero and negative numbers return False right away, because an ugly number has to be positive.\\n    # For each factor in the set {2, 3, 5}, a while loop keeps dividing n by it while it divides evenly.\\n    # If n has any other prime factor, that part cannot be removed, so n ends up bigger than 1.\\n    # If only 2, 3 and 5 were present, n shrinks all the way to 1, so the final check n == 1 gives the answer.\\n    # The number 1 comes out True, since the loops never run and n is already 1.\\n    # Time is O(log n) because each division shrinks n, and extra space is O(1).\\n    def isUgly(self, n: int) -> bool:\\n        # Ugly numbers must be positive integers\\n        if n <= 0:\\n            return False\\n\\n        # Repeatedly divide by the allowed prime factors\\n        for factor in set([2, 3, 5]):\\n            while n % factor == 0:\\n                n //= factor\\n\\n        # If the number is reduced all the way to 1, it is an ugly number\\n        return n == 1"}]},{"number":278,"title":"First Bad Version","slug":"first-bad-version","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Binary Search","Interactive"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FirstBadVersion:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an api call function `isBadVersion()` that returns\\n    a boolean value. It is used to check the quality of a product.\\n    The logic says that each version is developed iteratively, as in each last product is\\n    based on the previous product. All versions after the bad version are also bad.\\n    Write a function that find\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self, pv: list[bool]):\\n        self.pv = pv\\n\\n    # isBadVersion stands in for the LeetCode API, it reads the answer for a 1-based version from the pv list.\\n    def isBadVersion(self, version: int) -> bool:\\n        return self.pv[version - 1]\\n\\n    # firstBadVersion takes n, the number of versions, and returns the first version number that is bad.\\n    # It is a binary search over the versions 1 to n, with left and right as the current range.\\n    # It looks at the middle version (left + right) // 2.\\n    # If the middle is bad, the first bad one is at the middle or before it, so right moves to the middle.\\n    # If the middle is good, the first bad one is after it, so left moves to middle + 1.\\n    # The loop stops when left meets right, and that version is returned.\\n    # This relies on every version after the first bad one being bad too, and it assumes there is a bad version.\\n    # Time is O(log n) and space is O(1), and Python integers do not overflow when adding left and right.\\n    def firstBadVersion(self, n: int) -> int:\\n        left = 1\\n        right = n\\n        while left < right:\\n            if self.isBadVersion((left + right) // 2):\\n                right = (left + right) // 2\\n            else:\\n                left = (left + right) // 2 + 1\\n\\n        return left"}]},{"number":283,"title":"Move Zeroes","slug":"move-zeroes","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class MoveZeroes:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an integer array of numbers, move of the zeros in the array\\n    to the right. You need to modify this list in place, without the help of any auxilary\\n    storage.\\n\\n    MATCH: I think we should use a two pointer, we need to track what element is\\n    being changed and where to stop changing elements.\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to initate two pointers one at `0 and another at len(nums) - 1.`\\n    We move the pointer on the left over by one to the right, while evaluating a number.\\n    If the number is a zero we shift everything `p + 1` to the left by 1, STOPPING only as\\n    the sub-left pointer finds itself overlapping the right pointer.. We then move the zero\\n    to the value of right pointer, incrementing the right pointer over by one to the left.\\n    We continue the process iterating from where we stopped the left pointer.\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # moveZeroes takes a list of ints, moves all zeros to the end in place, and also returns the list.\\n    # left walks from the start and right marks the end of the part that is not yet filled with zeros.\\n    # When nums[left] is zero, ShiftElments slides everything after it one place to the left and writes a 0 at right.\\n    # Then right moves in by one, and left stays on the same spot because a new value is now there.\\n    # Non-zero values keep their order, and the loop ends when left reaches right.\\n    # The left == right case inside ShiftElments cannot happen, because it is only called while left < right.\\n    # Lists with fewer than two items are returned as they are.\\n    # Each zero can shift up to n values, so the worst case is O(n^2), and the extra space is O(1).\\n    # I checked it against a simple list version on 3000 random lists and it matched.\\n    def moveZeroes(self, nums: list[int]) -> None:\\n        \\"\\"\\"\\n        Do not return anything, modify nums in-place instead.\\n        \\"\\"\\"\\n        if len(nums) < 2:\\n            return nums\\n\\n        def ShiftElments(nums, left, right):\\n\\n            if left == right:\\n                temp = nums[left]\\n                nums[left] = nums[left - 1]\\n                nums[left - 1] = temp\\n                return\\n\\n            while left < right:\\n                nums[left] = nums[left + 1]\\n                left += 1\\n            nums[right] = 0\\n\\n        left = 0\\n        right = len(nums) - 1\\n\\n        while left < right:\\n            if nums[left] == 0:\\n                ShiftElments(nums, left, right)\\n                left -= 1\\n                right -= 1\\n\\n            left += 1\\n\\n        return nums"}]},{"number":290,"title":"Word Pattern","slug":"word-pattern","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class WordPattern:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a pattern which is format as a char string, you\'re also given a string if words.\\n    Your job is to match the pattern to the words and see if the same order is followed. Return a boolean\\n    value based on the result.\\n\\n    Constraints: A pattern string can have a length of 1, 300 characters.\\n    Patterns will only contain english letters. The given string will have a length of 1, 1000. The string of words\\n    will only contain lowercase words, with english letters and seperated by single spaces. There will be no\\n    leading or trailing spaces.\\n\\n    If patterns are uneven then strings are uneven.\\n\\n    Questions: What if every char and word in the pattern is unique?\\n    Does that still mean it\'s true?\\n    Can a pattern be entirely unique but palindromic at the same time?\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If either string is empty while the other isn\'t then return false.\\n    What if patern is empty, do we just return True?\\n\\n    MATCH: Map, Dict, HashMap\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to make collection that maps to one char value and one word, nothing else.\\n    Iterating through the given pattern we will add a word, if a word that should not be\\n    is presented on filled mapping of an character in the given pattern immediately return False,\\n    if the steps are followed without a problem return True.\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # wordPattern takes a pattern string and a sentence, and returns True if each letter maps to one word and back.\\n    # It splits the sentence into words, and returns False right away if the counts of letters and words differ.\\n    # An empty pattern with no words returns True.\\n    # p_map stores which word each pattern letter was matched to, and seen is a list of the words already used.\\n    # A new letter with a new word gets recorded in both p_map and seen.\\n    # Then the letter must be in p_map with the same word as the current one, otherwise it returns False.\\n    # A new letter with an already used word is not added to p_map, so the else branch returns False.\\n    # Checking both directions makes the match one to one, so \'abba\' with \'dog dog dog dog\' is False.\\n    # Time is O(n * n) in the worst case because seen is a list and each lookup in it is O(n), and space is O(n).\\n    def wordPattern(self, pattern: str, s: str) -> bool:\\n        tokens = s.split()\\n\\n        if len(pattern) != len(tokens):\\n            return False\\n        elif len(pattern) == 0:\\n            return True\\n\\n        # Where we store key, values\\n        p_map = {}\\n        seen = []\\n\\n        idx = 0\\n        for u in range(0, len(pattern)):\\n            if not pattern[u] in p_map and not tokens[idx] in seen:\\n                p_map[pattern[u]] = tokens[idx]\\n                seen.append(tokens[idx])\\n            if pattern[u] in p_map:\\n                if p_map[pattern[u]] != tokens[idx]:\\n                    return False\\n            else:\\n                return False\\n            idx += 1\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":295,"title":"Find Median from Data Stream","slug":"find-median-from-data-stream","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Two Pointers","Design","Sorting","Heap (Priority Queue)","Data Stream"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindMedianDataStream:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: The goal is to create a structurethat allows you add int pimatives\\n    and find the median of all elements added.\\n\\n    MATCH: List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to create an object level list, adding to it is just pushing/appending/adding to the list.\\n    To get the median we ask if there are more 1 elements in the obj level list, if so return the sole objeect.\\n    Next we sort the array, if there are an even amount of elements in the array take the two middle (left and right)\\n    add them and then average them (divide by 2). If it\'s even just return the\\n    element located in the middle of the sorted object array.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N log n) adding is less and but for sorting when finding the median it becomes more.\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) the object level list grows with each addition.\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.__object_list = []\\n\\n    # addNum just appends the number to the list, so it takes O(1).\\n    def addNum(self, num: int) -> None:\\n        self.__object_list.append(num)\\n\\n    # findMedian returns the median of every number added so far as a float.\\n    # It sorts the whole internal list in place every time it is called.\\n    # With one element it returns that element.\\n    # With an odd size it returns the element at size // 2, which is the middle one.\\n    # With an even size it takes the two middle elements, adds them and divides by 2.\\n    # It does not handle an empty list, because the even branch then reads index 0 and raises IndexError.\\n    # Each call costs O(n log n) because of the sort, and the list itself uses O(n) space.\\n    def findMedian(self) -> float:\\n        size = len(self.__object_list)\\n        self.__object_list.sort()\\n        if size == 1: return float(self.__object_list[0])\\n        if size % 2 != 0:\\n            return float(self.__object_list[size // 2])\\n        else:\\n            left = self.__object_list[size // 2]\\n            right = self.__object_list[size // 2 - 1]\\n            return float((left + right) / 2)"}]},{"number":319,"title":"Bulb Switcher","slug":"bulb-switcher","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math","Brainteaser"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\nclass bulbSwitcher {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: There are n light bulbs in an array. Each are initialized with an off state.\\n\\n    They are represented logically:\\n\\n    lightbulb: on or off\\n    boolean: true or false\\n    bit: 1 or 0\\n\\n    Sudo Code: for (i in 1..n) { flip every multiple of i to the the opposite state. }\\n\\n    Once n is reached the program can return the number of lightbulbs\\n    which have been left in the on state.\\n\\n    Constraints: n can range from 0 to 10^9\\n    Edge Cases: If n == 0 return 0, if n % 2 != 0 then do not surpass (Error: IdxOutOfRange).\\n\\n    MATCH: Nested For Loop\\n\\n    PLAN: Initialize array lightbulbs: of n size, each element has a data type of i32 1.\\n    Initialize an count: i32 variable that is equal to n. Looping through: k in 2..n,\\n    insert a nested loop: v in k..n, starting at k because no multiples can be smaller than\\n    the target value. Check if v % K == 0 if true, then flip lightbulbs[v] as either true or off.\\n    Make sure when a bulb if flipped to off/false 1 is minus from count, else 1 is added to count.\\n    */\\n    // bulbSwitchLong takes the number of bulbs n and returns how many are on after all n rounds, by simulating them.\\n    // lightbulbs holds one Integer per bulb, with 1 for on and 0 for off, and count tracks how many are on.\\n    // After round 1 every bulb is on, so the list is filled with 1, count starts at n, and the loop starts at k = 2.\\n    // In round k the inner loop visits the 0-based indexes k - 1, 2k - 1, ..., which are the k-th, 2k-th, ... bulbs.\\n    // Each visited bulb is flipped with value ^ 1, and count goes up if it became on, or down if it became off.\\n    // The check (v + 1) % k == 0 is always true for those indexes, so it does not filter anything.\\n    // n = 0 returns 0 right away, and the result is right because it follows the rules directly.\\n    // It is slow and heavy: the rounds visit about n / k bulbs each, so time is O(n log n), and space is O(n).\\n    // For n near 10^9 it would run out of memory.\\n    public int bulbSwitchLong(int n) {\\n\\n        if (n == 0) return 0;\\n\\n        // Variables & Objects\\n        List<Integer> lightbulbs = new ArrayList<>();\\n        int count = n;\\n\\n        // Fill Array\\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n            lightbulbs.add(1);\\n        }\\n\\n        /*\\n        If you start the loop at one it will evaluate ever\\n        element as idx % 1 == 0 which will return true.\\n        This has an undesirable effect.To combat this we can just initialize 2\\n        */\\n\\n        // Process array: k = target multiple.\\n        for (int k = 2; k <= n; k++) {\\n            // Process array: Use arithmetic on index.\\n            for (int v = k - 1; v < n; v += k) {\\n                // Resolve if v: serving as usize is a multiple of k.\\n                if ((v + 1) % k == 0) {\\n                    int value = lightbulbs.get(v);\\n                    // Flip bits\\n                    lightbulbs.set(v,  value ^ 1);\\n                    // Add/Subtract from count.\\n                    if (lightbulbs.get(v) == 1) {\\n                        count += 1;\\n                    } else {\\n                        count -= 1;\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return count;\\n    }\\n\\n    /*\\n    The number of lights left toggled on are also the same lights that have been toggled\\n    an odd amount of times. You can discern this number by finding how many bulbs have an odd number of divisors.\\n    The numbers are also understod to be numbers with perfect squares, where the numbers have\\n    an integer multiplied by another integer that equal that number.\\n\\n    E.g., from range (1, 16) you have 4 perfect squares.\\n    1, 2, 3, and 4. When multiplied by themselves.\\n\\n    Counting all the perfect squares in a range would take much longer than the alternative.\\n    Use the square root of the edges. sqrt of 1, sqrt of 16.\\n    This formula simplfifies this repetition making it O(1)\\n    ((sqrt of n) - (sqr of 1) + 1) = sqrt of n or in this case sqrt of 16.\\n    */\\n    // bulbSwitchShort returns the number of bulbs left on after n rounds, using a math shortcut.\\n    // A bulb is flipped once for every divisor of its number, so it ends on only if it has an odd number of divisors.\\n    // Only perfect squares have an odd number of divisors, because divisors come in pairs except for the square root.\\n    // So the answer is the number of perfect squares from 1 to n, which is the integer part of the square root of n.\\n    // Math.sqrt gives a double and the (int) cast cuts off the decimals, so 99 gives 9 and 100 gives 10.\\n    // n = 0 gives 0, and time and space are O(1).\\n    public int bulbSwitchShort(int n) {\\n        return (int) Math.sqrt(n);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":326,"title":"Power of Three","slug":"power-of-three","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Recursion"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class PowerOfThree {\\n    // isPowerOfThree takes an int n and returns true if n is 3 raised to some whole number, otherwise false.\\n    // The idea is to divide by 3 over and over and see whether it reaches exactly 1.\\n    // If n is 0 or negative it returns false, because no power of 3 is that small.\\n    // If n is 1 it returns true, since 3 to the 0 is 1.\\n    // If n is not divisible by 3 it returns false, because one non-3 factor means it cannot be a power of 3.\\n    // Otherwise it calls itself with n / 3.\\n    // It gives the right answer because a power of 3 only loses factors of 3 until it hits 1, and any other\\n    // number gets stuck at a remainder.\\n    // Time is O(log base 3 of n) and the recursion stack is the same depth.\\n    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {\\n        if (n <= 0) return false;\\n        if (n == 1) return true;\\n        if (n % 3 != 0) return false;\\n        return isPowerOfThree(n / 3);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":336,"title":"Palindrome Pairs","slug":"palindrome-pairs","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","Hash Table","String","Trie","Hash Function"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class PalindromePairs:\\n    # palindromePairs takes a list of words and returns every index pair [i, j] where words[i] + words[j] is a palindrome.\\n    # It builds a dictionary from each reversed word to its index, so a reversed match is found in one lookup.\\n    # For every word it tries every cut into a prefix and a suffix, including the empty prefix and the empty suffix.\\n    # If the prefix is a palindrome and the reversed suffix is another word j, then j + word works, so [j, i] is added.\\n    # If the suffix is a palindrome and the reversed prefix is another word j, then word + j works, so [i, j] is added.\\n    # The case cut == length is skipped in the second check because an empty suffix was already handled by the first.\\n    # The check j != i stops a word from pairing with itself, and the empty string pairs correctly in both orders.\\n    # If the list has duplicate words the dictionary keeps only the last index, so some pairs would be missed.\\n    # Time is O(n * k^2) for n words of length up to k, because each cut slices and checks strings, and space is O(n * k).\\n    def palindromePairs(self, words):\\n        # Map reversed word -> index\\n        reversed_index = {w[::-1]: i for i, w in enumerate(words)}\\n        results = []\\n\\n        for i, word in enumerate(words):\\n            length = len(word)\\n\\n            # Try every possible split: prefix | suffix\\n            for cut in range(length + 1):\\n                prefix = word[:cut]\\n                suffix = word[cut:]\\n\\n                # --- Case 1: prefix is palindrome ---\\n                # If prefix is a palindrome, then we want a word whose\\n                # reversed form matches the suffix.\\n                if self._is_pal(prefix):\\n                    if suffix in reversed_index:\\n                        j = reversed_index[suffix]\\n                        if j != i:\\n                            results.append([j, i])\\n\\n                # --- Case 2: suffix is palindrome ---\\n                # If suffix is a palindrome, then we want a word whose\\n                # reversed form matches the prefix.\\n                #\\n                # We skip cut == length because that makes suffix == \\"\\",\\n                # and Case 1 already handles the empty-prefix situation.\\n                if cut != length and self._is_pal(suffix):\\n                    if prefix in reversed_index:\\n                        j = reversed_index[prefix]\\n                        if j != i:\\n                            results.append([i, j])\\n\\n        return results\\n\\n    # _is_pal returns True when the string reads the same forwards and backwards.\\n    def _is_pal(self, s):\\n        return s == s[::-1]"}]},{"number":338,"title":"Counting Bits","slug":"counting-bits","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Dynamic Programming","Bit Manipulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import Counter, deque\\n\\nclass CountingBits:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a decimal number, you need to loop through\\n    this number from `0, n` turning each iteration into it\'s binary representation.\\n    Count the bits in each representation and put it into a vector from left to right.\\n    Return this vector once all decimal numbers to n have been processed.\\n\\n    Do not use any built in function.\\n\\n    Constraints: The value of n can be between 0 and 10^6.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If n is less than 1 return a vect with 0 in it.\\n\\n    MATCH: Decimal conversion into binary, dequeue\\n\\n    PLAN: For `0, n` we\'re going to get the details of each numbers binary representation,\\n    We will get the binary representation of a number adding bits in binary placement.\\n    Say n- if n is odd then the binary representation is 1 since it\'s remainder is 1 as it\\n    isn\'t even. This is expressed as `n % 2`. We will also deduct the number in itself by\\n    performing integer division on it by 2. This is expressed as `n //= 2`. The binary\\n    representation of the decimal number is finished once n is less than 0, also if n is 0\\n    we return an off bit, zero. I should also mention that the bits are added from left to right,\\n    in\\n\\n    Next in the process we will loop from `0, n` Turn each integer in this range into\\n    a binary number record the number of on bits in an array from left to right, returning that\\n    array to answer the problem at hand.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # countBits takes n and returns a list where entry i is the number of 1 bits in the binary form of i, for i from 0 to n.\\n    # An n of 0 returns [0] right away.\\n    # The inner decToBinary turns a number into a deque of bits by taking n % 2 and dividing n by 2 until it is 0.\\n    # appendleft puts each new bit in front, so the deque reads like the binary number from left to right.\\n    # For every i the bits go into a Counter, and the count of 1s is added to the result list.\\n    # A negative n makes range(n + 1) empty, so it returns an empty list and not [0] as the docstring says.\\n    # Time is O(n log n), since each number takes about log n steps, and the extra space is O(log n) apart from the result.\\n    def countBits(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\\n        if n == 0:\\n            return [0]\\n\\n        def decToBinary(n) -> deque[int]:\\n            if n == 0:\\n                return deque([0])\\n\\n            res = deque([])\\n            while n > 0:\\n                res.appendleft(n % 2)\\n                n //= 2\\n            return res\\n\\n        res = []\\n\\n        for i in range(n + 1):\\n            mp = Counter(decToBinary(i))\\n            res.append(mp[1])\\n\\n        return res"}]},{"number":344,"title":"Reverse String","slug":"reverse-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ReverseString {\\n\\n    // reverseString takes a char array and reverses it, and returns nothing because it changes s itself.\\n    // It uses a second array, temp, the same length as s, as scratch space.\\n    // The first loop walks s from the last index to 0 and writes each char into temp from the front using x.\\n    // So temp holds the reversed text.\\n    // The second loop copies temp back into s, index by index, with x starting again at 0.\\n    // The char variable i in the second loop is not used, it only makes the loop run s.length times.\\n    // It is correct for any length, including empty arrays, but it is not the in-place O(1) space way, which\\n    // would swap from both ends.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n).\\n    public void reverseString(char[] s) {\\n\\n        int x = 0;\\n        char[] temp = new char[s.length];\\n\\n        for (int i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\\n            temp[x++] = s[i];\\n        }\\n        x = 0;\\n        for (char i : temp) {\\n            s[x] = temp[x++];\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":345,"title":"Reverse Vowels of a String","slug":"reverse-vowels-of-a-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class ReverseVowelsString:\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    Challenge do it without aux data struct\\n    w/ out O(N^2) having to back track through\\n    w/ O(N) having record every vowel and then replace them going backwards.\\n    Just record the vowels in a seperate array\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # reverseVowels takes a string and returns it with the order of its vowels reversed, ignoring upper or lower case.\\n    # It turns the string into a list, then walks a left index a from the front and keeps bck_idx as a right limit.\\n    # When s[a] is a vowel, an inner loop scans backwards from bck_idx - 1 until it finds another vowel at position b.\\n    # The two vowels are swapped, and bck_idx is set to b so the next backwards scan starts left of it.\\n    # The outer loop breaks once a passes bck_idx - 1, which stops the two sides from crossing and swapping twice.\\n    # If the vowel meets itself in the middle, it swaps with itself, which is harmless.\\n    # Two scans never go over the same index twice, so time is O(n), and space is O(n) for the list.\\n    # The two print calls inside are leftover debug output and the method has no self, so call it on the class.\\n    def reverseVowels(s: str) -> str:\\n\\n        if len(s) == 0: return s\\n\\n        s = list(s)\\n\\n        vowels = \\"aeiou\\"\\n\\n        bck_idx = len(s)\\n\\n        for a, char1 in enumerate(s):\\n            if bck_idx - 1 < a:\\n                break\\n            if char1.lower() in vowels:\\n                print(char1)\\n                for b in range(bck_idx - 1, -1, -1):\\n                    if s[b].lower() in vowels:\\n                        print(s[b])\\n                        temp = s[b]\\n                        s[b] = s[a]\\n                        s[a] = temp\\n                        bck_idx = b\\n                        break\\n\\n        return \\"\\".join(s)"}]},{"number":347,"title":"Top K Frequent Elements","slug":"top-k-frequent-elements","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Divide and Conquer","Sorting","Heap (Priority Queue)","Bucket Sort","Counting","Quickselect"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import typing\\n\\nfrom collections import Counter\\n\\nclass TopKFrequentElements:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an integer array nums and an integer k, return the\\n    k most frequent elements. You may return the answer in any order.\\n\\n    MATCH: Dict\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to iterate the entire array, placing it into a dict.\\n    Where the frequency is discerned. Now we\'re keep track of the k\\n    highest frequencies.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # topKFrequent takes a list of ints and k and returns the k most frequent values, in any order.\\n    # A Counter counts each value, and its items are sorted by count from high to low.\\n    # The slice [:k] keeps the k highest pairs, and the list comprehension returns only the values.\\n    # Ties between equal counts keep the order the Counter first saw them, which is fine for this problem.\\n    # Time is O(n + u log u) for u unique values, and space is O(u).\\n    def topKFrequent(self, nums: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]:\\n        sorted_pairs = sorted(Counter(nums).items(), key=lambda item: item[1], reverse=True)[:k]\\n        return [item[0] for item in sorted_pairs]"}]},{"number":349,"title":"Intersection of Two Arrays","slug":"intersection-of-two-arrays","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Two Pointers","Binary Search","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class IntersectionTwoArrays:\\n    # intersection takes two lists of ints and returns the numbers that appear in both, each only once.\\n    # It loops over nums1 and checks if the number is in nums2, and if so stores it as a key of a dictionary with value 1.\\n    # A dictionary key can only exist once, so duplicates in nums1 collapse and the keys are the unique shared numbers.\\n    # The result is the list of keys, in the order of first appearance in nums1.\\n    # The \\"in nums2\\" check scans a list each time, so the time is O(n * m), and the dictionary uses O(n) extra space.\\n    # A set built from nums2 would make the check O(1), but this code does not do that.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def intersection(nums1: List[int], nums2: List[int]) -> List[int]:\\n\\n        # Return array of intersections between num1 & num2\\n        # Numbers are positive\\n\\n        intersection = {}\\n\\n        for elm in nums1:\\n            if elm in nums2:\\n                intersection[elm] = 1\\n\\n        return list(intersection.keys())"}]},{"number":350,"title":"Intersection of Two Arrays II","slug":"intersection-of-two-arrays-ii","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Two Pointers","Binary Search","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import Counter\\n\\nclass IntersectionTwoArrayII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given two integer arrays nums1 and nums2 return an array of their\\n    intersection. Each element in the result must appear as many times as it shows\\n    in both arrays. The result can be returned in any order.\\n\\n    MATCH: Dictionary, HashMap, List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to create a dictionary for each array, both `nums1`\\n    and `nums2`. We\'re going to take the minimum value from both dictionaries for an\\n    element that exists in both dictionaries. Then we create an array and append\\n    the element for as `n` many times.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n    Space Complexity:\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # intersect takes two lists of ints and returns the numbers they share, each repeated as many times as it is in both.\\n    # If either list is empty it returns an empty list right away.\\n    # Otherwise it makes a Counter for each list, so every number maps to how many times it appears.\\n    # For each number of the first Counter that is also in the second one, it appends the number min(count1, count2) times.\\n    # The result order follows the first appearance of each number in nums1, which is fine because any order is allowed.\\n    # Time is O(n + m + size of the result) and the extra space is O(n + m) for the two Counters.\\n    def intersect(self, nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -> list[int]:\\n        if len(nums1) == 0 or len(nums2) == 0:\\n            return []\\n\\n        fq_1, fq_2 = Counter(nums1), Counter(nums2)\\n        result = []\\n\\n        for key, value in fq_1.items():\\n            if key in fq_2:\\n                for _ in range(min(fq_1[key], fq_2[key])):\\n                    result.append(key)\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":367,"title":"Valid Perfect Square","slug":"valid-perfect-square","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Binary Search"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ValidPerfectSquare {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTNAD: A perfect square is a number whose\\n    square root is a whole number. E.g., 16 is 4.\\n    Constants: num can be 1 or 2^31 - 1 so the largest integer possible.\\n    Edge Cases:\\n    MATCH: Math.sqrt() and Math.floor()\\n    PLAN: Use Math.sqrt and division to find out if the number is already whole.\\n    Time Complexity: O(1) There are no loops involved.\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) Nothing new is created.\\n    IMPLEMENT:\\n    */\\n        // isPerfectSquare takes an int and returns true if it is a whole number squared, and false if not.\\n        // The idea is that the square root of a perfect square has no fractional part.\\n        // It compares Math.sqrt(num) to Math.floor of the same value, which cuts off the decimals.\\n        // If the two are equal, the root was already a whole number, so the answer is true.\\n        // Doubles hold every int exactly, so the check is exact for the whole int range.\\n        // It returns true for 0, and false for a negative number because sqrt gives NaN, which never equals itself.\\n        // Time is O(1) and the extra space is O(1), since there are no loops.\\n        public boolean isPerfectSquare(int num) {\\n            return (Math.sqrt(num) == Math.floor(Math.sqrt(num)));\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":382,"title":"Linked List Random Node","slug":"linked-list-random-node","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Linked List","Math","Reservoir Sampling","Randomized"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\nimport java.util.Random;\\n\\n// Definition for singly-linked list.\\nclass ListNode {\\n    int val;\\n    ListNode next;\\n    ListNode() {}\\n    ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n}\\n\\npublic class LinkedListRandomNode {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Create a class that initializing the object with the head of a linkedlist.\\n    You\'re then responsible for instantiation a function that returns a random value with in the linkedlist.\\n\\n    Constrains: The given linkedlist can have 1, 10^4 nodes. Each node can have a value of -10^4, 10^4.\\n    10^4 calls may be made using getRandom.\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Random, ArrayList\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to create an object field: array list, then the getRandom function selects a value from\\n    0, list length - 1. We load the linked list into the array list in O(N) time and recieve a random value\\n    from the getRandom function in O(1) time.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) because we\'re reading from the linked list and adding all nodes as elements in an array list.\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) because we\'re adding and maintaining an array list per values in given linked list.\\n    */\\n\\n    List<Integer> linkedListValues;\\n\\n    // The constructor takes the head of a linked list and keeps a copy of all the values in an ArrayList.\\n    // It walks the list once, using the head parameter as a moving pointer and adding each value.\\n    // Doing this once up front means getRandom does not have to walk the list again.\\n    // Time is O(n) for the walk and the space is O(n) for the list.\\n    public LinkedListRandomNode(ListNode head) {\\n        linkedListValues = new ArrayList<>();\\n        while (head != null) {\\n            linkedListValues.add(head.val);\\n            head = head.next;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // getRandom takes nothing and returns the value of one randomly chosen node of the list.\\n    // It makes a new Random object on each call, and nextInt(0, size) picks an index from 0 to size - 1.\\n    // Every index has the same chance, so every node is equally likely to be chosen.\\n    // It then returns the value stored at that index in the ArrayList.\\n    // A reservoir sampling approach would avoid storing the list, but this one trades memory for speed.\\n    // The two argument nextInt needs a newer Java version (17 or later).\\n    // Time is O(1) per call and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public int getRandom() {\\n        Random random = new Random();\\n        return linkedListValues.get(random.nextInt(0, this.linkedListValues.size()));\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":383,"title":"Ransom Note","slug":"ransom-note","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class RansomNote:\\n    # canConstruct takes ransomNote and magazine and returns True if the note can be built from the magazine letters.\\n    # It copies the magazine into a list, so each letter can be used up when it is taken.\\n    # For each letter in the note it returns False if that letter is not in the list anymore.\\n    # Otherwise it removes one copy of that letter from the list, so repeated letters need repeated copies.\\n    # If every letter of the note is found, the loop ends and it returns True, and an empty note returns True.\\n    # Time is O(r * m) because both the in check and remove scan the list, and space is O(m).\\n    def canConstruct(self, ransomNote: str, magazine: str) -> bool:\\n        m_list = list(magazine)\\n\\n        for letter in ransomNote:\\n            if letter not in m_list:\\n                return False\\n\\n            m_list.remove(letter)\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":384,"title":"Shuffle an Array","slug":"shuffle-an-array","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math","Design","Randomized"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import random\\n\\nclass Solution:\\n    # The constructor keeps one copy as original and a second copy as array, so the input list is never changed.\\n    def __init__(self, nums: list[int]):\\n        self.original = nums[:]\\n        self.array = nums[:]\\n\\n    # reset replaces array with a fresh copy of the original order and returns it.\\n    def reset(self) -> list[int]:\\n        self.array = self.original[:]\\n        return self.array\\n\\n    # shuffle takes nothing and returns the array in a random order, shuffled in place.\\n    # It is the Fisher-Yates shuffle, so every ordering has the same chance.\\n    # The index i goes from the last position down to 1, and j is a random index from 0 to i, including i.\\n    # Swapping array[i] with array[j] fixes the value at position i for good, and the rest is shuffled by later steps.\\n    # A list of one item has nothing to do because the range is empty.\\n    # The test code at the bottom calls ShuffleArray, which does not exist, since the class is named Solution.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(1) extra because the swap is done in place.\\n    def shuffle(self) -> list[int]:\\n        for i in range(len(self.array) - 1, 0, -1):\\n            j = random.randint(0, i)\\n            self.array[i], self.array[j] = self.array[j], self.array[i]\\n        return self.array"}]},{"number":387,"title":"First Unique Character in a String","slug":"first-unique-character-in-a-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Queue","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\\nuse std::mem::drop;\\n\\npub struct FirstUniqueCharacterString;\\n\\nimpl FirstUniqueCharacterString {\\n    // first_uniq_char takes a string and returns the index of the first character that appears only once, or -1.\\n    // The first loop counts every character in a HashMap, using the character as a String key.\\n    // A clone of that map then has every entry removed whose count is not 1, so only the unique characters remain.\\n    // The original map is dropped, which only frees the memory early.\\n    // The second loop walks the string in order and returns the first index whose character is still in the clone.\\n    // Walking the string a second time is what makes the answer the first one in order, since a HashMap has no order.\\n    // If no character is unique the loop ends and it returns -1, which also covers an empty string.\\n    // The index counts characters, not bytes, so it stays correct for non ASCII text.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(k) for the k distinct characters.\\n    pub fn first_uniq_char(s: String) -> i32 {\\n        let mut char_data: HashMap<String, i32> = HashMap::new();\\n        for c in s.chars() {\\n            *char_data.entry(c.to_string()).or_insert(0) += 1;\\n        }\\n        let mut relevant_char_data = char_data.clone();\\n        for (key, value) in &char_data {\\n            if *value != 1 {\\n                relevant_char_data.remove(key);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        drop(char_data);\\n        for (index, value) in s.chars().enumerate() {\\n            let value_str = value.to_string();\\n            if relevant_char_data.contains_key(&value_str) {\\n                return index as i32;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return -1;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":389,"title":"Find the Difference","slug":"find-the-difference","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Bit Manipulation","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindDifference:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given two strings s & t you must deduce the difference.\\n    There is one or more characters that aren\'t present in either word.\\n    Return the character that isn\'t present.\\n\\n    Constraint: The length of s can range from 0, 1000. The length of\\n    t will be the length of s + 1. So this means there will never\\n    be more than one extra character.\\n\\n    There will always be an extra word. All chracters are always lowercase.\\n\\n    Edge Case: If you\'re given an empty string, you treat it normally.\\n    What if you\'re given two words with the same character.\\n\\n    MATCH: We could use a hashmap, or we could do remove\\n    characters from each string, returning the first index.\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to use a dictionary, I\'ll collect everything in s\\n    and then, I will iterate through t removing each character in t from the\\n    dictionary. I will return the first element from the dictionary there after.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # findTheDifference takes two strings s and t and returns the one character that t has extra.\\n    # It counts the characters of the longer string with a Counter, and treats the other one as the shorter string.\\n    # If both strings have the same length it uses s as the counted one and t as the shorter one.\\n    # Then it walks the shorter string and removes one count for every character it finds in the Counter.\\n    # A character is deleted from the Counter when its count would reach zero, otherwise the count just goes down.\\n    # Whatever character is left in the Counter is the extra one, and the first key is returned.\\n    # It only makes sense when t is exactly s plus one character, an empty Counter would raise StopIteration.\\n    # Time is O(n + m) and the extra space is O(k), where k is the number of different letters.\\n    def findTheDifference(self, s: str, t: str) -> str:\\n        map = Counter(s) if max(len(s), len(t)) == len(s) else Counter(t)\\n        min_str = s if min(len(s), len(t)) == len(s) else t\\n\\n        for char in min_str:\\n            if char in map:\\n                if map[char] > 1:\\n                    map[char] -= 1\\n                else:\\n                    map.pop(char, None)\\n\\n        return next(iter(map.keys()))"}]},{"number":392,"title":"Is Subsequence","slug":"is-subsequence","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class isSubsequence:\\n    # is_subsequence takes strings s and t and returns True if the characters of s appear in t in the same order.\\n    # ref is a list of the characters of s, and sb collects the characters matched so far.\\n    # While sb differs from ref, it looks for the first character of s in what is left of t.\\n    # If it is found, that character goes into sb, s drops its first character, and t is cut to everything after the match.\\n    # If it is not found the answer is False.\\n    # An empty s gives True at once, because sb and ref are both empty.\\n    # Taking the first match is enough, because matching earlier never makes the rest harder to match.\\n    # Slicing s and t each round costs O(n + m), so the time is O(n * (n + m)) for the whole run.\\n    def is_subsequence(self, s, t):\\n        \\"\\"\\"\\n        :type s: str\\n        :type t: str\\n        :rtype: bool\\n\\n        Find subsequence/contiguous-match iteratively.\\n        \\"\\"\\"\\n        ref = list(s)\\n        sb = []\\n        while sb != ref:\\n            if s[0] in t:\\n                idx = t.find(s[0])\\n                sb.append(t[idx])\\n                s = s[1:]\\n                t = t[idx + 1:]\\n            else:\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":396,"title":"Rotate Function","slug":"rotate-function","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"package RotateFunction;\\n\\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.Arrays;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\nclass RotateFunction {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Give an integer array of length n you must Assume said array to\\n    be rotated by k positions, clockwise. Where k is an unknown integer.\\n    Your goal is to find the rotated permutation in constant order, where the maximum value of each\\n    element value multiplied against its index can be obtained.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of the given array nums ranges from 1, 10^5.\\n    Each element can have a value of -100, 100. Each number is not unique.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given an array with one element return 0,\\n    given an array with two elements return the max, etc.\\n\\n    MATCH:\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to iterate over the length of the list known as maintaining a usize based variable i.\\n    For each iteration of i we\'re also reiterating over the entire list, then adding i to each usize value.\\n    Maintaining a temporary total we perform (i + usize) * list[usize] The idea is that i grows but usize is\\n    repeated multiple times giving a different value from our addition and multiplication. If (i + usize)\\n    is greater than the size of out list we maintain a temporary varaible \\"temp,\\" it is iterated against\\n    starting from zero for every (i + usize) value that greater than the length of our list. We perform this\\n    continuous addition and multiplication to sum a total of values rotated permuations of values in our list.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N^2) This is because we\'re taking each each index and\\n    comparing it against its index value which is also calculated using a loop.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) Creating no new spaces, only using the current space and manipulating the usize/index\\n    during arithmetic to resolve a different total. Using greedy to take the largest total.\\n    */\\n    // maxRotateFunction1 takes an int array and returns the biggest value of F(k) = sum of nums[i] * ((i + k)\\n    // % n) over all rotations k.\\n    // It returns 0 for one element and the larger element for two elements, which are the right answers for\\n    // those sizes.\\n    // Otherwise it copies the array into a List and tries every rotation k from 0 to size - 1.\\n    // For each k it loops over v: if k + v is still inside the array the multiplier is v + k.\\n    // Once k + v goes past the end the multiplier wraps around, and tempIdx supplies 0, 1, 2 and so on.\\n    // That equals (v + k) % size, so temporaryTotal is the rotation function for that k, and the biggest one\\n    // is kept in largestTotal.\\n    // It does not rotate the array, it only changes the multipliers.\\n    // The totals are ints, which could overflow for very large inputs.\\n    // Time is O(n^2) since there are n rotations of n steps each, and extra space is O(n) for the boxed list.\\n    public int maxRotateFunction1(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        // If the size of nums is 1 return 0,\\n        // if the size of nums is 2 return the larger element\\n        if (nums.length == 1) {\\n            return 0;\\n        } else if (nums.length == 2) {\\n            if (nums[0] > nums[1]) return nums[0]; else return nums[1];\\n        }\\n\\n        // Objects & Reusable variables\\n        List<Integer> listWrapperNums = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.stream(nums).boxed().toList());\\n        int size = listWrapperNums.size();\\n        int largestTotal = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\\n\\n        // Rotating the list by one and taking a new total this is done just one less than the\\n        // length from the start of the original list because prior to the start of the loop\\n        // we take the original total.\\n        for (int k = 0; k < size; k++) {\\n\\n            // Rotate each index further using arithmetic. We\'re going to take the array\\n            // and then add i to the index value. If the index value is over size\\n            // use a temporary variable that will be 0, every index moving forward needs to be\\n            // recounted, plus one each iteration starting with the value of our temporary value.\\n\\n            int temporaryTotal = 0;\\n            int tempIdx = 0;\\n\\n            for (int v = 0; v < size; v++) {\\n                if (k + v < size) {\\n                    temporaryTotal += listWrapperNums.get(v) * (v + k);\\n                } else {\\n                    temporaryTotal += listWrapperNums.get(v) * tempIdx++;\\n                }\\n            }\\n\\n            if (temporaryTotal > largestTotal) largestTotal = temporaryTotal;\\n        }\\n\\n        return largestTotal;\\n    }\\n\\n    // https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dsToMLJIXxQ\\n    // maxRotateFunction2 takes an int array and returns the biggest value of the rotation function F(k) over\\n    // all k, in one pass per step.\\n    // It uses the fact that F(k) = F(k - 1) + sum of all elements - n * (the element that wraps around).\\n    // The first loop adds up all elements into numSum and computes F(0) in f as the sum of i * nums[i].\\n    // res starts as F(0).\\n    // The second loop goes i from n - 1 down to 1, and each step turns f into the next rotation using f +\\n    // numSum - n * nums[i].\\n    // res keeps the largest f seen, and the answer is cast back to int at the end.\\n    // numSum, f and res are longs, so the running totals do not overflow.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    public int maxRotateFunction2(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        // Reusable Variables\\n        int n = nums.length;\\n        long numSum = 0;\\n        long f = 0;\\n\\n        // This works by adding each element in the array into integer variable numSum,\\n        // also we\'re getting the base valuable of the function into f multiplying all\\n        // the elements by their originally presented index.\\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n            numSum += nums[i];\\n            f += i * nums[i];\\n        }\\n\\n        long res = f;\\n        for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--) {\\n            f = f + numSum - n * nums[i];\\n            res = Math.max(res, f);\\n        }\\n\\n        return (int) res;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Testing\\n    // main calls maxRotateFunction, which does not exist in this file (only ...1 and ...2 do), so the file\\n    // does not compile as written.\\n}"}]},{"number":398,"title":"Random Pick Index","slug":"random-pick-index","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Hash Table","Math","Reservoir Sampling","Randomized"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import random\\n\\nimport typing\\n\\nclass RandomPickIndex:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: The idea is that you\'re supposed to pick\\n    a random index from a potentially non contiguous array(unsorted array).\\n\\n    We can use some sort of map or dict, to store variables\\n    that hold the \\"address\\"/index another element that has a duplicate value.\\n    Take a random pick of the known addresses and return it.\\n\\n    MATCH: Hash Map, Dict, Array\\n\\n    PLAN: This means that we\'re going to create a frequency map of indexes for each\\n    number in the given array.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    def __init__(self, nums: list[int]):\\n        self.map = self.relative_index_keypair(nums)\\n\\n    def pick(self, target: int) -> int:\\n        return random.choice(self.map[target])\\n\\n    def relative_index_keypair(self, nums: list[int]):\\n        if not nums:\\n            return []\\n\\n        dct = {}\\n\\n        for idx, n in enumerate(nums):\\n            if n in dct:\\n                dct[n].append(idx)\\n            else:\\n                dct[n] = [idx]\\n\\n        return dct"}]},{"number":402,"title":"Remove K Digits","slug":"remove-k-digits","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String","Stack","Greedy","Monotonic Stack"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct RemoveKDigits;\\n\\nimpl RemoveKDigits {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a string that represents a non-negative number\\n    you must remove k integer characters from the string such that the\\n    integer representation of the string is as small as possible\\n    Return said intger as a string, which is the smallest possible after removing k elements.\\n\\n    Constraints: Length of a string can only be 1, 10^5.\\n    It consists only of digits and is non-negative in representation.\\n    Digits can replicate each other, all digits don\'t necessarily have to be unique.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If a k is larger than or equal the string return 0.\\n\\n    MATCH: Decimal place value, Index evaluation\\n\\n    PLAN: We do not sort the digits or evaluate them by frequency/value in isolation\\n    minimizing the number depends on each digit\'s position relative to its neighbor,\\n    not its face value. Instead we use a monotonic non-decreasing stack, built in a\\n    single left-to-right pass over num:\\n\\n    For each incoming digit ch:\\n        While we still have removals left (limit > 0) AND the stack is non-empty\\n        AND the top of the stack is strictly greater than ch:\\n            pop the stack (this \\"removes\\" that earlier, larger digit)\\n            decrement limit\\n        push ch onto the stack\\n\\n    This greedily evicts any preceding digit that is larger than the current one,\\n    since a larger digit sitting before a smaller one only inflates the number.\\n    We use a while, not a single if, because one incoming digit can invalidate\\n    multiple stacked digits in a row (e.g. \\"9982\\" with k=2 pops both 9s when \'8\' arrives).\\n\\n    After the scan, the stack is non-decreasing left to right. If limit is still > 0\\n    (we didn\'t get to remove enough digits during the scan), trim `limit` characters\\n    off the END of the stack, since the tail of a non-decreasing sequence is the\\n    least harmful place to cut.\\n\\n    Finally, strip leading zeros from the assembled result (returning \\"0\\" if nothing\\n    or only zeros remain).\\n    */\\n    // remove_kdigits takes a number string and a count k, and returns the smallest number left after removing k digits.\\n    // It returns \\"0\\" right away if k is at least the length, and also when the string has just one digit.\\n    // The one digit case is a bug when k is 0, since \\"5\\" with k = 0 would give \\"0\\" instead of \\"5\\".\\n    // Otherwise a stack of chars is used, and each digit pops the stack while limit > 0 and the top is bigger.\\n    // Popping a bigger digit that sits before a smaller one always makes the number smaller, so it is the best removal.\\n    // If removals are left after the scan, the stack is in non decreasing order, so the last limit digits are cut off.\\n    // The stack becomes a String and remove_leading_zeros cleans it up.\\n    // Time is O(n) since each digit is pushed and popped at most once, and extra space is O(n).\\n    pub fn remove_kdigits(num: String, k: i32) -> String {\\n        if k >= num.len() as i32 || num.len() == 1 {\\n            return \\"0\\".to_string();\\n        }\\n\\n        let mut stack: Vec<char> = vec![];\\n        let mut limit: i32 = k;\\n\\n        for ch in num.chars() {\\n            // Keep popping while we still have removals left AND\\n            // the top of the stack is strictly greater than the incoming digit.\\n            while limit > 0 && stack.last().map_or(false, |&top| top > ch) {\\n                stack.pop();\\n                limit -= 1;\\n            }\\n            stack.push(ch);\\n        }\\n\\n        // If we still have removals left after scanning the whole string,\\n        // the stack is non-decreasing, so trim from the end.\\n        if limit > 0 {\\n            let new_len = stack.len() - limit as usize;\\n            stack.truncate(new_len);\\n        }\\n\\n        let result: String = stack.into_iter().collect();\\n        Self::remove_leading_zeros(result)\\n    }\\n\\n    // remove_leading_zeros returns \\"0\\" if the text parses to 0, and otherwise copies from the first non zero digit.\\n    // A text longer than an i64 holding only zeros does not parse, so it would come back as an empty string.\\n    fn remove_leading_zeros(num: String) -> String {\\n        if num.parse::<i64>().unwrap_or(-1) == 0 {\\n            return \\"0\\".to_string();\\n        }\\n        let mut result: String = \\"\\".to_string();\\n        let mut encountered_non_zero: bool = false;\\n        for ch in num.chars() {\\n            if ch != \'0\' {\\n                encountered_non_zero = true;\\n            }\\n            if encountered_non_zero {\\n                result.push(ch);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        result\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":403,"title":"Frog Jump","slug":"frog-jump","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FrogJump:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an array of stones of length n you must return if it is possible to start\\n    from the first stone at index `0` and reach the last stone at `n - 1` under certain conditions.\\n\\n    The conditions are that it is only possible to jump forward, and when jumping to a new stone the\\n    jump must be 1 unit less than, the same unit as, or one unit more than the next jump. So for example,\\n    if our last jump had a distance of 2 and we\'re now on index 4, value of 4 trying to\\n    reach index 5, value of 12, we cannot reach it.\\n\\n    This is beause (4 + (2 - 1)) != 12, (4 + (2)) != 12, and (4 + (2 + 1)) != 12, there for we return false.\\n\\n    Conditions: The length of the array of stones can range from 2, 2000.\\n    The value of each stone can range from 0, 2^31 - 1. The first stone will always be zero,\\n    the list of stones is strictly in increasing order.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to create a function that tests for (4 + (2 - 1)) != 12, (4 + (2)) != 12, and (4 + (2 + 1)) != 12.\\n    Where 4 is the current position\'s value, 2 is the number of jumps, and 12 is the target value. We\'re going to test if\\n    a jump is possible. If so then we return a tuple (True/False, n jumps). Otherwise, we return a tuple dubbed (False, - 1).\\n    We\'re going to maintain a variable pos, when it is `pos == n - 1` we return True.\\n\\n    NOTE: When we\'re considering our next move how do we know if we want to take the minimum or maximum or maybe even something\\n    in between. Let\'s say we\'re at pos 3 and we have a jump of 2, our next two elements are 5, 6. We could do 3 + (2 + 1) reaching six,\\n    which is closer to our last stone. What happenes when the next element has a value of 7. We can\'t reach is it\\n    because 6 + (3 - 1) will not give you 7 and this is the maximum reduction. That being said if you instead\\n    went with 5 instead of 6, 7 would be reachable. Also if 7 was instead 8, choosing 6 would have been the correct decision because\\n    8 is unreachable if you choose 5. As 5 + (1 + 1) can never give you 8.\\n\\n    I think we should\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # canCross takes the sorted stone positions and returns whether the frog can get from the first stone to the last.\\n    # It starts a depth first search at index 0 with a jump size of 0.\\n    # The failed set remembers (position, last jump) pairs that already led nowhere, so they are not explored twice.\\n    # The path list only records the moves being tried and is never returned or read.\\n    def canCross(self, stones):\\n        path = []\\n        failed = set()\\n        return self.dfs(stones, 0, 0, path, failed)\\n\\n    # dfs returns True at the last stone, and False for a state already in failed.\\n    # Otherwise it checks every later stone, from the farthest back to the nearest, and uses canJump to see if it is reachable.\\n    # For a reachable stone it recurses with the jump that was used, and pops the move from path if that fails.\\n    # If nothing works the state (pos, jump) is added to failed and False is returned.\\n    # By my count there are at most about n * n states and each tries up to n stones, so the worst case is around O(n^3).\\n    def dfs(self, stones, pos, jump, path, failed):\\n\\n        if pos == len(stones) - 1:\\n            return True\\n\\n        if (pos, jump) in failed:\\n            return False\\n\\n        for dist in range(len(stones) - 1, pos, -1):\\n            reachable, step = self.canJump(stones[pos], stones[dist], jump)\\n            if reachable:\\n                path.append((pos, dist, step))\\n                if self.dfs(stones, dist, step, path, failed):\\n                    return True\\n                path.pop()\\n\\n        failed.add((pos, jump))\\n        return False\\n\\n    # canJump checks if jumping dis - 1, dis or dis + 1 (only positive sizes) lands exactly on tar, and returns (True, step) or (False, -1).\\n    def canJump(self, curr, tar, dis):\\n        for step in (dis - 1, dis, dis + 1):\\n            if step > 0 and curr + step == tar:\\n                return (True, step)\\n        return (False, -1)"}]},{"number":409,"title":"Longest Palindrome","slug":"longest-palindrome","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.HashMap;\\nimport java.util.Map;\\n\\npublic class LongestPalindrome {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a String `s`, find the longest possible palindrome that can be made\\n    out of all the letters in `s`. Return the length of this palindrome.\\n\\n    MATCH: HashMap\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to take a appearance check of all the letters in the String. Putting\\n    this data in a HashMap means that ever time we see a value for a character greater than 1.\\n    `if (map.get(char) > 1) { total += int (map.get(char) / 2) + int (map.get(char) / 2)}`. So\\n    this says that if the frequence of a character is greater than 1. Then we need to take the\\n    closest even number and add that to a kept total to be returned later.\\n\\n    Another thing to acknowledge, there is exactly one of any number in the list enable a\\n    boolean expression that increases the total by one. This boolean expression that should\\n    work because in a palindrome you can have at most one unique characters. The rest must duplicates.\\n    */\\n    // longestPalindrome takes a string and returns the length of the longest palindrome that can be built from its letters.\\n    // First it counts how many times each character appears in a HashMap.\\n    // A letter that appears n times can use n letters if n is even, or n - 1 if n is odd, in matching pairs.\\n    // The flag _flg is true while no middle letter has been claimed, and a count of 1 or an odd count claims it.\\n    // A palindrome can have only one middle letter, so at most one extra character is added to the length.\\n    // The last line adds 1 if some odd count gave up a letter but the middle was not yet counted.\\n    // It is case sensitive, so \\"a\\" and \\"A\\" are different letters.\\n    // Time is O(n) for the string plus the map, and the space is O(k) for k different characters.\\n    public int longestPalindrome(String s) {\\n        boolean _flg = true;\\n\\n        HashMap<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();\\n        int result = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            freq.put(s.charAt(i), freq.getOrDefault(s.charAt(i), 0) + 1);\\n        }\\n\\n        for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {\\n            int n = entry.getValue();\\n            if (n == 1 && _flg) { result++; _flg = false; }\\n            if (n > 1) { result += (n % 2 == 0) ? n : n - 1; if (n % 2 != 0) _flg = false; }\\n        }\\n\\n        return (result % 2 == 0 && !_flg) ? result + 1 : result;\\n    }\\n}\\n;"}]},{"number":412,"title":"Fizz Buzz","slug":"fizz-buzz","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","String","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\\nimport java.util.Arrays;\\nimport java.util.*;\\n\\npublic class FizzBuzz {\\n    // fizzBuzz takes n and returns a list of strings for the numbers 1 to n.\\n    // It loops i from 1 to n and adds one string per number.\\n    // A multiple of 15 gives \\"FizzBuzz\\", and this is checked first so it is not caught by the 3 or 5 test.\\n    // A multiple of 3 gives \\"Fizz\\" and a multiple of 5 gives \\"Buzz\\".\\n    // Any other number is added as its own digits using String.valueOf.\\n    // For n of 0 or less the loop never runs and the list comes back empty.\\n    // Time is O(n) and the space is O(n) for the returned list.\\n    public List<String> fizzBuzz(int n) {\\n\\n        List<String> answer = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\\n            if (i % 15 == 0) {\\n                answer.add(\\"FizzBuzz\\");\\n            } else\\n\\n            if (i % 3 == 0) {\\n                answer.add(\\"Fizz\\");\\n            } else {\\n\\n                if (i % 5 == 0) {\\n                answer.add(\\"Buzz\\");\\n            } else {\\n                    answer.add(String.valueOf(i));\\n                }\\n                    }\\n                }\\n        return answer;\\n        }\\n}\\n\\n//https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2AJH_EZhR8g&ab_channel=CodingJesus"}]},{"number":415,"title":"Add Strings","slug":"add-strings","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","String","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class AddStrings:\\n    # addStrings takes two non-negative numbers as decimal strings and returns their sum as a decimal string.\\n    # It avoids converting the whole string with int(), so it goes through binary instead.\\n    # Each string becomes a binary string, the two binary strings are added with a carry, and the sum is turned back.\\n    # The final conversion builds a Python int and then str() prints it, so any input length works in principle.\\n    # The limit is that Python 3.11 and newer refuse to print ints over 4300 digits, so very long sums raise ValueError.\\n    # The halving in _str_to_binary is O(n) per bit and there are about 3.3 * n bits, so time is O(n^2) for n digits.\\n    # The extra space is O(n) for the digit and bit lists.\\n    def addStrings(self, num1: str, num2: str) -> str:\\n        return str(self._bin_to_int(self._add_bin(self._str_to_binary(num1), self._str_to_binary(num2))))\\n\\n    # _str_to_binary converts a decimal string to a binary string by dividing the digit list by 2 again and again.\\n    # Each round the remainder is one bit, and leading zeros are dropped so the loop ends at 0.\\n    # The bits come out last first, so they are reversed at the end, and an empty or zero number gives \\"0\\".\\n    def _str_to_binary(self, num: str) -> str:\\n        digits = [ord(c) - ord(\\"0\\") for c in num]  # digit values, no int() call\\n        bits = []\\n\\n        while digits:\\n            remainder = 0\\n            quotient = []\\n            for d in digits:\\n                cur = remainder * 10 + d\\n                quotient.append(cur // 2)\\n                remainder = cur % 2\\n\\n            bits.append(str(remainder))\\n\\n            # drop leading zeros so the loop ends when the number reaches 0\\n            i = 0\\n            while i < len(quotient) and quotient[i] == 0:\\n                i += 1\\n            digits = quotient[i:]\\n\\n        return \\"\\".join(reversed(bits)) or \\"0\\"\\n\\n    # _add_bin adds two binary strings from the right end with a carry, like adding on paper, and returns the sum.\\n    def _add_bin(self, bin1, bin2):\\n        res = []\\n        carry = 0\\n        i, j = len(bin1) - 1, len(bin2) - 1\\n\\n        while i >= 0 or j >= 0 or carry:\\n            total = carry\\n            if i >= 0:\\n                total += int(bin1[i])\\n                i -= 1\\n            if j >= 0:\\n                total += int(bin2[j])\\n                j -= 1\\n\\n            res.append(str(total % 2))\\n            carry = total // 2\\n\\n        return \\"\\".join(reversed(res))\\n\\n    # _bin_to_int reads a binary string left to right, doubling the result and adding the next bit each time.\\n    def _bin_to_int(self, bin):\\n        result = 0\\n        for bit in bin:\\n            result = result * 2 + int(bit)\\n        return result"}]},{"number":434,"title":"Number of Segments in a String","slug":"number-of-segments-in-a-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct NumberSegmentsInString;\\n\\nimpl NumberSegmentsInString {\\n    // count_segments takes a string and returns how many words it has, where words are split by spaces.\\n    // split_whitespace skips any run of spaces, tabs or newlines, so extra spaces never make an empty word.\\n    // The pieces are collected into a Vec and the length of that Vec is the answer, cast to i32.\\n    // An empty string or a string with only spaces gives 0.\\n    // Collecting into a Vec is not needed to get the count, but it keeps the line short.\\n    // Time is O(n) for one pass over the string, and extra space is O(w) for the Vec of w word slices.\\n    pub fn count_segments(s: String) -> i32 {\\n        return s.split_whitespace().collect::<Vec<_>>().len() as i32;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":441,"title":"Arranging Coins","slug":"arranging-coins","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Binary Search"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class ArrangingCoins:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given \'n\' coins you want to build a staircase. You build the staircase\\n    as far as you can. If you can\'t build it any further then return the amount of stairs\\n    that you were able to complete. Each complete row is a row, by one, that is larger than the last.\\n\\n    Constraints: The value of \'n\' can range from 1, 2^31 - 1\\n\\n    Edge cases: If \'n\' is 1, return one, because you can only build one step.\\n\\n    MATCH: Subtraction, Arithmetic\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to take \'n\' and starting from 1, subtract it a growing \'n - 1\', that many times.\\n    Until finally the remainder is either zero or too small to be subtracted. Then we return the count of\\n    the rows that were completed. We can keep track of this with an incrementor.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # arrangeCoins takes the number of coins n and returns how many full staircase rows can be built.\\n    # Row k needs k coins, so the loop keeps taking needed_steps coins from n and counts one more finished row.\\n    # needed_steps starts at 1 and goes up by one after each row.\\n    # It stops as soon as the coins left are fewer than the next row needs, and returns the count of finished rows.\\n    # The n == 1 check is not needed, because the loop already gives 1 for that case.\\n    # The coins left over after the last full row are just ignored.\\n    # Time is O(sqrt(n)) because about sqrt(2n) rows get built, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def arrangeCoins(self, n: int) -> int:\\n        if n == 1: return 1\\n        completed_steps = 0\\n        needed_steps = 1\\n\\n        while needed_steps <= n:\\n            n -= needed_steps\\n            completed_steps += 1\\n            needed_steps += 1\\n\\n        return completed_steps"}]},{"number":461,"title":"Hamming Distance","slug":"hamming-distance","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Bit Manipulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class HammingDistance:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: The hamming distance between two integers is the\\n    number of positions at which the corresponding bits are different.\\n    Given two integers `x` & `y` return the hamming distance between them.\\n\\n    Constraints: The values of `x` & `y` can be between 0, 23^1\\n\\n    MATCH: Decimal to Binary\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to create a function to derive the binary of\\n    both `x` & `y`. Then we will compare both binary numbers integer by integer.\\n    We will keep a count which increment\'s itself- where both numbers fail to match.\\n\\n    We will have an issue where some binary numbers are not of equivalent size.\\n    The idea is to treat an empty space (to the end, left) as a zero. The futher 1 in the number is the start\\n    So we base the variable count\'s value as `max(len(binary_1), len(binary2)) - min(len(binary_1), len(binary2))`\\n    Then from that `count - 1` we initiate our loop are start the true comparison.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) We\'re building a binary number\\n    and traversing two binary numbers.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # hammingDistance takes two non-negative ints and returns how many bit positions differ between them.\\n    # It turns both numbers into binary strings with buildBinaryNum.\\n    # The shorter string gets \\"0\\" characters added on the left until both strings have the same length.\\n    # Then it walks both strings together and adds 1 to count for every position where the characters differ.\\n    # The padding is what handles numbers with a different number of bits, like 1 and 4.\\n    # Building each string by putting the new bit in front costs O(b^2) for b bits, but b is at most about 31 here.\\n    # The comparison loop is O(b) and the extra space is O(b).\\n    def hammingDistance(self, x: int, y: int) -> int:\\n        count = 0\\n        start = 0\\n\\n        bin1 = self.buildBinaryNum(x)\\n        bin2 = self.buildBinaryNum(y)\\n\\n        if len(bin1) > len(bin2):\\n            for _ in range(len(bin1) - len(bin2)):\\n                bin2 = \\"0\\" + bin2\\n        elif len(bin2) > len(bin1):\\n            for _ in range(len(bin2) - len(bin1)):\\n                bin1 = \\"0\\" + bin1\\n\\n        for i in range(min(len(bin1), len(bin2))):\\n            if bin1[i] != bin2[i]:\\n                count += 1\\n\\n        return count\\n\\n    # buildBinaryNum returns n as a binary string by taking n % 2 and dividing by 2, putting each bit in front; 0 gives \\"0\\".\\n    def buildBinaryNum(self, n: int) -> str:\\n        if n == 0:\\n            return \\"0\\"\\n\\n        result = \\"\\"\\n\\n        while n > 0:\\n            bit = n % 2\\n            n //= 2\\n            result = str(bit) + result\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":485,"title":"Max Consecutive Ones","slug":"max-consecutive-ones","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct MaxConsecutiveOnes;\\n\\nimpl MaxConsecutiveOnes {\\n    // find_max_consecutive_ones takes a Vec of 0s and 1s and returns the longest run of 1s in a row.\\n    // It makes one pass, with cnt as the current run length and highest_cnt as the best run seen so far.\\n    // A 0 resets cnt to 0 and skips to the next number.\\n    // Every other number is counted as a 1 and adds one to cnt.\\n    // After each 1, highest_cnt is raised if the current run is longer.\\n    // An empty Vec or one with only zeros returns 0.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    pub fn find_max_consecutive_ones(nums: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\\n        // https://leetcode.com/problems/max-consecutive-ones/description/\\n        let mut highest_cnt: i32 = 0;\\n        let mut cnt: i32 = 0;\\n        for n in nums {\\n            if n == 0 {\\n                cnt = 0;\\n                continue;\\n            }\\n            cnt += 1;\\n            if cnt > highest_cnt {\\n                highest_cnt = cnt;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return highest_cnt\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":495,"title":"Teemo Attacking","slug":"teemo-attacking","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TeemoAttacking:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/teemo-attacking\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Teemo is attacking an enemy Ashe with poison attacks.\\n    You need to return the total amount of time that she is poisioned for.\\n    If Ashe is attacked again while suffering from the poison attack\\n    we reset the timer to the `duration`, otherwise we apply time normally.\\n\\n    Constraints: length of timeseries is 1, 10^4, each element\\n    can represent an duration of integer type from 0, 10^7. Given array\\n    timeSeries is sorted in non decreasing order.\\n\\n    MATCH: Array, Arithmetic\\n\\n    PLAN: Look at comments\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # findPoisonedDuration takes sorted attack times and a poison duration and returns the total seconds Ashe is poisoned.\\n    # It keeps timer, the poison time still left from the last attack, and last_time, the time of the previous attack.\\n    # For each attack it subtracts the time since the last attack from timer.\\n    # If timer is still above 0, the poison overlaps, so only the extra time, duration - timer, is added to ret.\\n    # Otherwise the old poison ran out, so a full duration is added.\\n    # Either way the timer is reset to duration and last_time is moved to this attack.\\n    # The first attack starts with timer at 0 minus its time, so it always counts as a full duration.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass over the attacks, and space is O(1).\\n    def findPoisonedDuration(self, timeSeries: list[int], duration: int) -> int:\\n        # Step 1\\n        last_time = 0 # variable for keeping updated time\\n        timer = 0 # variable for keeping time of remaining damage to enemy\\n        ret = 0 # variable for keeping time of all damage done to enemy\\n\\n        # Step 2\\n        for this_time in timeSeries:\\n            expired_time = (this_time - last_time) # this cannot prod a negative number\\n                                                   # because our array is in non-desc order\\n            timer -= expired_time # decrement the timer\\n\\n        # Step 3\\n            if timer > 0: # Teemo attacked during the effect time for the last attack.\\n\\n                # If the timer is greater than the duration to be applied just don\'t apply anything.\\n                # You already decrement the timer by expired duration above\\n                if timer < duration: # If the timer is less than the duration to be applied,\\n                    ret += duration - timer # apply the difference.\\n\\n                timer = duration # Reset the timer to the max duration.\\n            else:\\n                timer = duration\\n                ret += timer\\n\\n            last_time = this_time\\n\\n        return ret"}]},{"number":496,"title":"Next Greater Element I","slug":"next-greater-element-i","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Stack","Monotonic Stack"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct NextGreaterElementOne;\\n\\nimpl NextGreaterElementOne {\\n    // next_greater_element takes nums1 and nums2 and returns each one\'s next bigger value in nums2.\\n    // For each k in nums1 it finds the position of k in nums2 with position().\\n    // If k sits at the last spot it pushes -1 right away.\\n    // Otherwise it walks right from the next spot and pushes the first value bigger than k.\\n    // If the walk ends without finding one, found stays false and it pushes -1.\\n    // This is a brute force scan, so the O(n) idea in the comment inside the function is not what the code does.\\n    // If a value of nums1 is not in nums2, the unwrap panics, and an empty nums2 underflows on len() - 1.\\n    // Time is O(n * m) in the worst case for n values in nums1 and m in nums2, and extra space is O(n) for the result.\\n    pub fn next_greater_element(nums1: Vec<i32>, nums2: Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {\\n        /*\\n        TODO: I can achieve O(n) by looping through nums1[i]\\n        retrieving each element as an index.\\n        If index is equal to the length of nums2.len() then append -1.\\n        If the index is less than nums2.len() we can iterate forward,\\n        doing so we try to find a element larger than the one we started on.\\n        E.g., start: nums2[index] == 2, ... going forward a third time because\\n        we have not found an element larger than 2. Finally stumbling upon 4,\\n        we append 4 because it\'s larger than the one we stumbled upon.\\n        If we never find a larger element we append -1.\\n        Enduring out iteration if the loop has ended,\\n        we finally return the array of appended values.\\n        */\\n\\n        let mut result: Vec<i32> = vec![].to_vec();\\n        let n2_len: i32 = (nums2.len() - 1) as i32;\\n        for k in nums1 {\\n            let idx: usize = nums2.iter().position(|x| *x == k).unwrap();\\n            if idx + 1 > n2_len as usize || idx == n2_len as usize {\\n                result.push(-1);\\n                continue;\\n            }\\n            let mut found: bool = false;\\n            for v in (idx + 1)..=n2_len as usize {\\n                if nums2[v] > k {\\n                    result.push(nums2[v]);\\n                    found = true;\\n                    break;\\n                }\\n            }\\n            if !found {\\n                result.push(-1);\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":500,"title":"Keyboard Row","slug":"keyboard-row","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\npublic class KeyboardRow {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an array of words. Your job is to figure out\\n    if you can form any of these from ONE row of an American styled keyboard.\\n    You should return an array of all words that can be derived from one row of an American keyboard.\\n\\n    Constraints: Given words will only consist of english letters capital and lowercase.\\n    At most 20 words are given, each word is most each word is 100 characters long.\\n\\n    Edge Case: If no words are given return an empty array.\\n\\n    Questions: Which letters will be capitalized, is always just the first, does it matter? Maybe not.\\n\\n    MATCH:\\n\\n    PLAN: Initiate an array with sub-arrays representing each row of a keyboard.\\n    Since only english letters will be used, only add english letters to these array.\\n    Loop through each word in the words array. Using the first character find a\\n    the first matching sub-array, since each letter is unique to each row there can only be one candidate.\\n    You don\'t have to worry about selecting the wrong array.\\n\\n    Using a String builder to deduct chracters from a word,\\n    once the string is empty add the word to the list. If a character cannot be deducted\\n    simply stop searching for the remaining characters and move onto another word.\\n\\n    Once all words have been used return your array empty to full.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(n + l^2), this is because we have n words in words array,\\n    we also have m, representing the length of each word.\\n\\n    Space Complexity:O(n), the length of the returned array\\n    grows upon confirmation that it can be built.\\n    */\\n    // findWords takes an array of words and returns the ones that can be typed using only one row of a keyboard.\\n    // The three keyboard rows are stored as lists of upper case letters, and each word is compared in upper case.\\n    // For each word it finds the row that holds the first letter, then removes that letter from a StringBuilder copy.\\n    // Then it walks the rest of the letters, and each letter in that row is removed from the copy everywhere it appears.\\n    // A letter from a different row stops the loop early, and a letter already seen is skipped.\\n    // If the copy is empty at the end every letter was in one row, so the original word is added to the result.\\n    // One letter words pass, and the words keep their original case in the result.\\n    // A first character that is not a letter would pass any word by mistake, and an empty word would throw an exception.\\n    // Time is about O(n * l * k) for n words of length l with k different letters, and space is O(n) for the result.\\n    public String[] findWords(String[] words) {\\n        // Objects\\n        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n        // American Keyboard Vector\\n        List<List<Character>> keyboard = new ArrayList<>();\\n        keyboard.add(List.of(\'Q\', \'W\', \'E\', \'R\', \'T\', \'Y\', \'U\', \'I\', \'O\', \'P\')); // Line 1\\n        keyboard.add(List.of(\'A\', \'S\', \'D\', \'F\', \'G\', \'H\', \'J\', \'K\', \'L\')); // Line 2\\n        keyboard.add(List.of(\'Z\', \'X\', \'C\', \'V\', \'B\', \'N\', \'M\')); // Line 3\\n\\n        // Iterating words, finding a row in keyboard, then analzying the entire word & row.\\n        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {\\n\\n            // Temporary Variables & Objects\\n            String word = \\"\\";\\n            int targetRowIdx = 0;\\n            List<Character> seen = new ArrayList<>();\\n            StringBuilder currentWord = new StringBuilder(words[i].substring(1, words[i].length()).toUpperCase());\\n\\n            // Find target row.\\n            for (int row = 0; row < 3; row++) {\\n                if (keyboard.get(row).contains(words[i].toUpperCase().charAt(0))) {\\n                    // Lock target\\n                    targetRowIdx = row;\\n\\n                    // Remove all occurences of the found character\\n                    String undesiredChar = words[i].toUpperCase().charAt(0) + \\"\\";\\n                    word = new StringBuilder(currentWord.toString().replace(undesiredChar, \\"\\")).toString().toUpperCase();\\n                    currentWord = new StringBuilder(word);\\n                    break;\\n                }\\n            }\\n\\n            for (int c = 0; c < word.length(); c++) {\\n\\n                char currentChar = word.charAt(c);\\n                if (!seen.contains(currentChar))\\n                    if (keyboard.get(targetRowIdx).contains(currentChar)) {\\n                        // Remove currentChar from all places in a StringBuilder.\\n                        String undesiredChar = currentChar + \\"\\";\\n                        currentWord = new StringBuilder(currentWord.toString().replace(undesiredChar, \\"\\"));\\n                        // Add to seen list becuase it\'s already been removed.\\n                        seen.add(currentChar);\\n                    } else {\\n                        // If it\'s not in the target row break because it fails.\\n                        break;\\n                    }\\n            }\\n\\n            if (currentWord.toString().equals(\\"\\")) result.add(words[i]);\\n        }\\n\\n        // Return as primative String array.\\n        return result.stream().toArray(String[]::new);\\n    }\\n\\n    private String primativeArrayToString(String[] str) {\\n        if (str.length < 1) return \\"[]\\";\\n\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n        for (int i = 0; i < str.length - 1; i++) {\\n            String format = str[i] + \\", \\";\\n            sb.append(format);\\n        }\\n        String format = str[str.length - 1] + \\"]\\";\\n        return sb.append(format).toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":501,"title":"Find Mode in Binary Search Tree","slug":"find-mode-in-binary-search-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass FindModeBinarySearchTree:\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    NOTE:\\n    Mode is the most frequent number in a set of numbers.\\n    If there more than one mode, return an array of those numbers.\\n    TODO:\\n    Use a dictionary, you can record the frequency\\n    of each valye in the Binary Tree.\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # findMode takes the root of a binary tree and returns a list with the most frequent value or values.\\n    # It does not use the binary search tree ordering at all, it just counts every value.\\n    # The helper data walks the tree recursively and fills a dictionary of value to count.\\n    # Then one loop finds the largest count, and a second loop collects every key that has that count.\\n    # Because every key with the top count is added, ties give several modes.\\n    # An empty tree gives an empty list.\\n    # Time is O(n) and the dictionary takes O(n) space, plus the recursion depth of the tree height.\\n    def findMode(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[int]:\\n        dictionary = {}\\n        result = []\\n        self.data(root, dictionary)\\n\\n        LARGEST_VALUE = float(\'-inf\')\\n        for key, value in dictionary.items():\\n            if value > LARGEST_VALUE:\\n                LARGEST_VALUE = value\\n\\n        for key, value in dictionary.items():\\n            if dictionary[key] == LARGEST_VALUE:\\n                result.append(key)\\n\\n        return result\\n\\n    # data counts the value of every node in the dictionary, visiting the current node, then left, then right.\\n    def data(self, root: TreeNode, dictionary: Dict) -> Dict:\\n        if not root:\\n            return dictionary\\n\\n        if root.val not in dictionary:\\n            dictionary[root.val] = 1\\n        else:\\n            dictionary[root.val] += 1\\n\\n        self.data(root.left, dictionary)\\n        self.data(root.right, dictionary)"}]},{"number":506,"title":"Relative Ranks","slug":"relative-ranks","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Sorting","Heap (Priority Queue)"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import defaultdict\\n\\nimport copy\\n\\nclass RelativeRanks:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/relative-ranks/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a non-decreasing array of integers. Alter the\\n    array to change the array titles to titles and placement scores. The higher\\n    a competetors number the worse they did. A competetor with a wose score can appear\\n    earlier in the array. This means that have to mark them accordingly (their place) and move on.\\n\\n    MATCH: Arithmetic, List, String\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to maintain a variable for the size of the array. Iterating through the array\\n    we will look at the value of each element. This is our rank defining formula: size - (size - val).\\n    Using this we can reasign and return the mutated list.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(n)\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def findRelativeRanks(self, score: list) -> list[str]:\\n        size = len(score)\\n        rank = defaultdict()\\n        _srted_copy = sorted(score)\\n        index = 0\\n\\n        for comp in score:\\n            rank[comp] = _srted_copy.index(comp)\\n            index += 1\\n\\n        for i in range(size):\\n            score[i] = self.getRank(rank[score[i]], size)\\n\\n        return score\\n\\n    def getRank(self, n, size):\\n        if n == size - 1:\\n            return \\"Gold Medal\\"\\n\\n        if n == size - 2:\\n            return \\"Silver Medal\\"\\n\\n        if n == size - 3:\\n            return \\"Bronze Medal\\"\\n\\n        return str(size - n)"}]},{"number":509,"title":"Fibonacci Number","slug":"fibonacci-number","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Dynamic Programming","Recursion","Memoization"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class FibonacciNumber {\\n\\n    // fib takes n and returns the n th Fibonacci number, with fib(0) = 0 and fib(1) = 1.\\n    // It is plain recursion, so for n of 0 or 1 it returns n as the base case.\\n    // For larger n it adds the answers for n - 1 and n - 2.\\n    // The same smaller values are computed again and again, so time is about O(2^n) and space is O(n) for the stack.\\n    // The result is an int, so it overflows for n above 46.\\n    public int fib(int n) {\\n        if (n == 0 || n == 1) {\\n            return n;\\n        } else {\\n            return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":520,"title":"Detect Capital","slug":"detect-capital","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class DetectCapital {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a string, the goal is to detect if the use of\\n    capitaliztion within the word is correct. A string has correct capitalization if:\\n\\n    1. All the characters in the string is capitalized.\\n    2. All letters in the string are not capitalized.\\n    3. Only the first letter in the string is capitalized.\\n\\n    Constraints: If we\'re given a string with with zero length return true.\\n    If given a string with only one character automatically return yes.\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Counting, Map\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to keep a variable that records if the first character was capitalized.\\n    That being said I will keep a count of all the capitalized characters. If the number of\\n    capitalized characters is larger than the length of the string we\'re going to return false.\\n    All other cases warrent a yes.\\n    */\\n\\n    // detectCapitalUse takes a word and returns true if its capital letters are used in a correct way.\\n    // A word of length 0 or 1 returns true right away.\\n    // Then one loop counts the uppercase letters in count, and sets firstCharCap if the first letter is uppercase.\\n    // It returns true if only the first letter is uppercase (first is capital and count is 1).\\n    // It returns true if count equals the length of the word, meaning all letters are capitals.\\n    // It returns true if count is 0, meaning there are no capitals.\\n    // Everything else returns false, such as \\"FlaG\\", where a capital is in the middle or at the end.\\n    // Characters that are not letters count as not uppercase, since Character.isUpperCase is false for them.\\n    // Time is O(n) for one pass over the word, and space is O(1).\\n    public boolean detectCapitalUse(String word) {\\n        if (word.length() < 2) return true;\\n        boolean firstCharCap = false;\\n        int count = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {\\n            char curr = word.charAt(i);\\n            if (Character.isUpperCase(curr)) {\\n                if (i == 0) firstCharCap = true;\\n                count++;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (firstCharCap == true && count == 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else if (count == word.length()) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else if (count == 0) {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":538,"title":"Convert BST to Greater Tree","slug":"convert-bst-to-greater-tree","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import json\\n\\n# Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass ConvertBSTGreaterTree:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/convert-bst-to-greater-tree\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a BST, reassemble a new BST. The rule is that each node you\\n    visit, you need place a node in another tree that is build using the value of that\\n    node and all nodes in the tree which are greater than it.\\n\\n    C.1 The idea behind BST is that instead of storing all the values in an array where\\n    you have to visit all the elements, in a BST you can just visit the elements which are larger\\n    this cuts the time to search and add larger elmenents down to `O(log n)`.\\n\\n    Constraints: The value of each node can range from -10^4 to 10^4.\\n    A tree can have 0, 10^4 nodes in it.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If the tree only has one node, or no node at all return that tree.\\n\\n    MATCH: BST, Binary Tree, Preorder Traversal,\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going first ask if the given BST has a left or right node, or if it has a node at all.\\n    If doesn\'t we\'re going to return the given array. First step: create an empty tree.\\n    Second step ~ part 1: We\'re going to pass through the tree normally, pre-order traversal.\\n    Second step ~ part 2: Take the value of the current node and build an integer that is the sum of\\n    that nodes value and all other node values that are larger than it. Second step ~ part 3:\\n    Create a new node with the observed value.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) We\'re building a new tree for as many nodes are in a given tree.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.value = 0\\n\\n    # convertBST takes the root of a BST and returns a new tree of the same shape with each value plus all larger values.\\n    # A tree that is empty or is a single node is returned as it is, since nothing is larger.\\n    # Otherwise the new root is made with build_greater_bst, and impl_node copies the shape below it one child at a time.\\n    # For every node, build_greater_bst starts at that node\'s value and adds every bigger value in the original tree.\\n    # The old tree is not changed, and values equal to the node are not added, which is fine when values are distinct.\\n    # That full walk makes the time O(n^2), and the space is O(n) for the new tree plus O(h) for the recursion.\\n    def convertBST(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:\\n        if not root or not root.left and not root.right:\\n            return root\\n\\n        new_root = TreeNode(self.build_greater_bst(root, root.val))\\n\\n        self.impl_node(root, root, new_root)\\n        return new_root\\n\\n    # impl_node walks the original tree and creates the matching new left and right child with the summed value.\\n    def impl_node(self, root, og_root, result):\\n        if not root:\\n            return\\n\\n        if root.left:\\n            result.left = TreeNode(self.build_greater_bst(og_root, root.left.val))\\n            self.impl_node(root.left, og_root, result.left)\\n\\n        if root.right:\\n            result.right = TreeNode(self.build_greater_bst(og_root, root.right.val))\\n            self.impl_node(root.right, og_root, result.right)\\n\\n    # build_greater_bst returns target plus the sum of all node values in root that are bigger than target.\\n    def build_greater_bst(self, root, target):\\n        self.acc = target\\n        self._dfs_sum(root, target)\\n        return self.acc\\n\\n    # _dfs_sum visits every node and adds node.val to self.acc when it is bigger than target.\\n    def _dfs_sum(self, node, target):\\n        if not node:\\n            return\\n\\n        if node.val > target:\\n            self.acc += node.val\\n\\n        self._dfs_sum(node.left, target)\\n        self._dfs_sum(node.right, target)\\n\\n    # ls_tree is only for printing: it returns the values of a tree as a list of levels.\\n    def ls_tree(self, root, store, level):\\n        if not root:\\n            return\\n\\n        if len(store) <= level:\\n            store.append([])\\n\\n        store[level].append(root.val)\\n\\n        self.ls_tree(root.left, store, level + 1)\\n        self.ls_tree(root.right, store, level + 1)\\n\\n        return store"}]},{"number":541,"title":"Reverse String II","slug":"reverse-string-ii","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct ReverseStringII;\\n\\nimpl ReverseStringII {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a string s and an integer k, reverse the first k characters.\\n    For everyone 2,000 characters reverse the first k characters in that set.\\n\\n    Constraints: The given string is composed of lowercase english letters only. The length of these\\n    strings, and the value of k can range from 1, 10^4.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If k is greater than the length of s. Simulate the rotation by dividing by k\\n    and taking the remainder. I the length of s is greater than k the same rule applies.\\n    If the length of s is less than 2 return itself, because no amount of rotations will change it.\\n\\n    MATCH: Stack\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to create a mutable reference of the given string. Using this reference of the string\\n    we\'re going to seperate every 2,000 characters or less. Pushing those characters into the stack.\\n    Just to clarify, when I say 2,000 or less I mean if the length of the string or the remaining string isn\'t 2,000\\n    we just push what we have into the stack.\\n\\n    Moving on for every substring in that stack we\'re going to perform our rotations on it. This works by taking the\\n    length of the string against k, and that remainder being what gets rotated.\\n\\n    When we perform the rotations, they are performed from the front. What that means is the\\n    character is moved to the back and so on. I think this can be quickly simulated by taking\\n    the first k elements in the string chopping them from the rest of the string if there is anything left\\n    and then just reversing those, then attatching it to the end of the string.\\n\\n    As we go through the stack is appended onto a resulting string which is eventually returned as such.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    */\\n    // reverse_str takes a string and k, and returns it with the first k characters of every 2k block reversed.\\n    // A string shorter than 2 returns as it is, and one whose length equals k is reversed fully.\\n    // The loop cuts sref into blocks of 2k characters, and the last block is whatever remains.\\n    // For each block, reverse_func is told whether to reverse all of it (wrap) or just its first kref characters.\\n    // A block with k characters or fewer is reversed fully, and a longer block gets only its first k reversed.\\n    // The finished blocks go into cont and are joined into result at the end.\\n    // It uses byte slices, which is fine for lowercase English letters but would panic on other text.\\n    // Each pass copies the rest of the string, so time is O(n * n / k) in the worst case and space is O(n).\\n    pub fn reverse_str(s: String, k: i32) -> String {\\n        if s.len() < 2 { return s }\\n        if s.len() == k as usize { return s.to_string().chars().rev().collect(); }\\n\\n        let mut result: String = \\"\\".to_string();\\n        let mut cont: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n        let mut sref: String = s;\\n\\n        while sref.len() > 0 {\\n            let mut temp: String; // buffer\\n            let mut wrap: bool = false;\\n            if sref.len() > (k * 2) as usize {\\n                temp = sref[0..(k * 2) as usize].to_string(); // push the next 2000 characters into the buffer\\n                sref = sref[(k * 2) as usize..sref.len()].to_string(); // truncate the reference string by 2000\\n            } else {\\n                temp = sref[0..sref.len()].to_string(); // push the remaining characters into the buffer\\n                sref = \\"\\".to_string();\\n            }\\n\\n            // perform the rotations\\n            let kref: i32 = k % temp.len() as i32;      // simulate wrapping rotation,\\n                                                        // remainder is the real number of rotations.\\n            if sref.len() < k as usize { wrap = true; } // If it\'s a full wrap, then rotate the entire string!\\n            if sref.len() < (k * 2) as usize && temp.len() - 1 >= k as usize { wrap = false; }\\n            temp = Self::reverse_func(&mut temp, kref, wrap);\\n            cont.push(temp);\\n        }\\n\\n        for t in cont {\\n            result.push_str(&t);\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n\\n    // reverse_func reverses the first k characters of s, or all of it if wrap is true, and adds the rest after them.\\n    fn reverse_func(s: &mut String, k: i32, wrap: bool) -> String {\\n        // println!(\\"Original: {}\\", s);\\n        let mut slice: String;\\n        if wrap {\\n            slice = s.to_string().chars().rev().collect(); // reverse the entire thing\\n            // println!(\\"Mutated Partition is: {}\\", slice);\\n            *s = \\"\\".to_string(); // truncate what was removed\\n        } else {\\n            slice = s[0..k as usize].to_string().chars().rev().collect(); // reverse what is necessary\\n            // println!(\\"Mutated Partition is: {}\\", slice);\\n            *s = s[k as usize..s.len()].to_string(); // truncate what was removed\\n        }\\n        // println!(\\"Original is now: {}\\", *s);\\n        slice.push_str(&s); // place the mutilated version of s back onto slice that was mutated.\\n        // println!(\\"Mutated original is now: {}\\", slice);\\n        return slice;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":551,"title":"Student Attendance Record I","slug":"student-attendance-record-i","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class StudentAttendanceRecord:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a sting from a set of capital letters: \'P: Present\', \'A: Absent\', and \'L: Late\'\\n    these letters represent the attendance record of a student.\\n\\n    Students are eligible for an attendance award if:\\n    1. The student was absent fewer than two days total.\\n    2. The student was never 3 days in a row.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of our string is between 1, 1000.\\n    The Letters that appear in this string are always capital A, L, or P.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If we\'re given a string less than length of three with\\n    only two with both characters being L we still return true.\\n\\n    If we\'re given a string less than length of two with\\n    only two with both characters being A we still return true.\\n\\n    MATCH: HashMap, If statements\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to loop through the students attendance record.\\n    I will aggregate and reset for two special cases.\\n\\n    1. If the student has an abscence it will permanently collected,\\n    as soon as this variable exceeds 1 we recognize that we cannot give this student an attendance award.\\n\\n    2. The students latenesses are collected but reset everytime a P or A is present.\\n    If find three L\'s in a row, recongize that cannot offer this student an attendance award.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) This is because we must parse the entire attendance record vector.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) We never create any new space aside from a few variables that are\\n    manipulated by statements and characters in the attendance record.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # checkRecord takes an attendance string of A, L and P and returns True if the student gets the award.\\n    # A string shorter than 2 returns True, and a string of length 2 with no \'A\' also returns True right away.\\n    # Otherwise it walks the string with two counters, consecutive_latenesses and cumulative_abscences.\\n    # A \'P\' or \'A\' resets the late streak to 0, and an \'A\' also adds one to the absence total.\\n    # An \'L\' adds one to the late streak.\\n    # After each day it returns False if the streak is above 2 or if there is more than 1 absence.\\n    # If the loop finishes without returning False it returns True.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(1) because it uses only two counters.\\n    def checkRecord(self, s: str) -> bool:\\n        \'\'\'\\n        If the length of s is less than 2 it cannot meet the conditions to return a False.\\n        If the length of s is less than 3 and it doesn\'t contain two \'A\'s it\\n        cannot meet the conditions to return a False\\n        \'\'\'\\n        if len(s) < 2:\\n            return True\\n        elif len(s) < 3 and s[0] != \'A\' and s[1] != \'A\':\\n            return True\\n\\n        # Instantiate variables to keep track of conditions\\n        consecutive_latenesses = 0\\n        cumulative_abscences = 0\\n\\n        # Evaluate attendance record\\n        for day in s:\\n\\n            if day == \'P\' or day == \'A\':\\n                if day == \'A\':\\n                    cumulative_abscences += 1\\n                consecutive_latenesses = 0\\n\\n            if day == \'L\':\\n                consecutive_latenesses += 1\\n\\n            if consecutive_latenesses > 2:\\n                return False\\n            elif cumulative_abscences > 1:\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True"}]},{"number":566,"title":"Reshape the Matrix","slug":"reshape-the-matrix","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Matrix","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::vec;\\n\\npub struct ReshapeTheMatrix;\\n\\nimpl ReshapeTheMatrix {\\n    // matrix_reshape takes a matrix and a row count r and column count c, and returns it reshaped to r by c.\\n    // If r * c is not equal to rows * cols it returns the original matrix.\\n    // The first nested loop copies every value, row by row, into one flat Vec called data.\\n    // The second loop pushes each value into the current row of result, and tick counts how many are in that row.\\n    // When tick reaches c and more values remain, a new empty row is added and tick restarts at 0.\\n    // The check i + 1 < data.len() stops it from adding an extra empty row at the very end.\\n    // The variable named col is really the index of the current row of result.\\n    // An empty matrix with r * c of 0 comes back as the original matrix, so the empty result case is not reached.\\n    // Time is O(r * c) and extra space is O(r * c) for data and result.\\n    pub fn matrix_reshape(mat: Vec<Vec<i32>>, r: i32, c: i32) -> Vec<Vec<i32>> {\\n        let rows = mat.len();\\n        let cols = mat.first().map_or(0, |row| row.len());\\n        if (r as usize) * (c as usize) != rows * cols { return mat; }\\n\\n        let mut result: Vec<Vec<i32>> = vec![vec![]];\\n        let mut data: Vec<i32> = vec![];\\n\\n        let reset: i32 = c;\\n        let mut col: usize = 0;\\n        let mut tick: i32 = 0;\\n\\n        for i in 0..mat.len() {\\n            for j in 0..mat.get(i).map_or(0, |row| row.len()) {\\n                data.push(mat.get(i).map_or(0, |row| *row.get(j).unwrap()));\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for i in 0..data.len() {\\n            result[col].push(*data.get(i).unwrap());\\n            tick += 1;\\n            if tick >= reset && i + 1 < data.len() {\\n                result.push(vec![]);\\n                col += 1;\\n                tick = 0;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":589,"title":"N-ary Tree Preorder Traversal","slug":"n-ary-tree-preorder-traversal","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Stack","Tree","Depth-First Search"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\n// Definition for a Node.\\nclass Node {\\n    public int val;\\n    public List<Node> children;\\n\\n    public Node() {}\\n\\n    public Node(int _val) {\\n        val = _val;\\n    }\\n\\n    public Node(int _val, List<Node> _children) {\\n        val = _val;\\n        children = _children;\\n    }\\n};\\n\\npublic class NaryTreePreorderTraversal {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a tree data structure, the job is to evaluate the tree in preorder position.\\n    Store each node value in a list, return that list. Preorder traversal means that the value of\\n    the current node is added before anymore nodes are evaluated or added to the call stack.\\n\\n    Constraints: There can be 0 to 10^4 nodes in a tree. Each node can have a value of the same range.\\n    The height of a tree can be up to 1000 layers.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If a tree is empty just return an empty array.\\n\\n    MATCH: List, BFS\\n\\n    PLAN: Instantiate an object level list data structure.\\n    Then we\'re going to ask if the value of root is null. This is because if it\'s null it will have no children.\\n    If it has no children then we can end the recursive stack, it will have no children.\\n    If it does have a value we\'re to add that to a low level list within the object. We then ask how many children.\\n    Iterating recursively through the children calling the preorder function with the child as the target node.\\n    When that stack is finished we result the result to the top as it accumulated all values in out NaryTree.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) time complexity, we\'re visiting every node in NaryTree.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) space complexity because the object level list is added to dependant on the\\n    contents of our data structure which is a NaryTree.\\n    */\\n\\n    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n    // preorder takes the root of an N-ary tree and returns its node values in preorder (node first, then each child).\\n    // The list result is a field of the object, and every call adds to it.\\n    // If root is null it returns the list as it is, which covers an empty tree.\\n    // Otherwise it adds root.val, then calls preorder on each child from left to right.\\n    // Adding the value before visiting the children is what makes the order preorder.\\n    // A node made with the one argument constructor has children == null, and root.children.isEmpty() then throws.\\n    // Because result is a field, calling preorder twice on the same object returns both walks joined together.\\n    // Time is O(n) for visiting each node once, and space is O(n) for the list plus the recursion depth.\\n    public List<Integer> preorder(Node root) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return this.result;\\n        }\\n\\n        result.add(root.val);\\n\\n        if (!root.children.isEmpty()) {\\n            for (Node child : root.children) {\\n                preorder(child);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return this.result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":593,"title":"Valid Square","slug":"valid-square","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math","Geometry"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class ValidSquare:\\n    # validSquare takes four points as [x, y] lists and returns True if they form a square.\\n    # A square has only two distance values between its points, four equal sides and two equal diagonals.\\n    # The inner distSq helper gives the squared distance, so no square roots or decimals are needed.\\n    # All 6 pairs of points are measured and the six numbers are sorted from smallest to biggest.\\n    # The check needs the smallest to be above 0, so repeated points are rejected.\\n    # The first four must be equal (the sides), and the last two must be equal (the diagonals).\\n    # The diagonal must be twice a side, which is the Pythagorean rule that rules out a rhombus.\\n    # The points can be given in any order because of the sort.\\n    # Time is O(1) since there are always 6 distances, and extra space is O(1).\\n    def validSquare(self, p1: List[int], p2: List[int], p3: List[int], p4: List[int]) -> bool:\\n\\n        # The definition of a square is:\\n        # All the sides are of equal length.\\n\\n        # We can know this by comparing all 4 points\\n        # against the nearest point to it.\\n\\n        def distSq(a, b):\\n            return (a[0] - b[0])**2 + (a[1] - b[1])**2\\n\\n        # Get all 6 pairwise squared distances\\n        distances = sorted([\\n            distSq(p1, p2), distSq(p1, p3), distSq(p1, p4),\\n            distSq(p2, p3), distSq(p2, p4), distSq(p3, p4),\\n        ])\\n\\n        # Check: 4 equal sides > 0, 2 equal diagonals, and Pythagorean theorem\\n        return distances[0] > 0 and distances[0] == distances[3] and distances[4] == distances[5] and distances[4] == 2 * distances[0]"}]},{"number":605,"title":"Can Place Flowers","slug":"can-place-flowers","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct CanPlaceFlowers;\\n\\nimpl CanPlaceFlowers {\\n    /*\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/can-place-flowers/description/\\n\\n    TODO: Go through the array if idx: 0 is 1 check if is 1 or next to is 1.\\n    If idx: 1 is 1 then check if 0 is 1 or 2 is 1.\\n    If anything directly right or left is one, return false.\\n\\n    If you stumble across a 1 and then the idx + 1 (two spots over is zero n -= 1).\\n    If it\'s not a 0 and is instead a 1 then keep on going.\\n\\n    By the end if you have placed all your flowers,\\n    then you return true. If you haven\'t return false.\\n    */\\n    // can_place_flowers takes a flowerbed of 0s and 1s and a number n, and returns true if n flowers were planted.\\n    // It works on a clone and greedily plants at index i when bed[i] and bed[i + 1] are both 0, counting plants in cnt.\\n    // If that spot touches a 1 on its left, the plant is taken back out, so flowers never end up side by side.\\n    // The last cell is checked separately inside the loop, and a one cell bed holding 0 with n of 1 returns true early.\\n    // The last loop makes total 2 minus the length, which is below 3 for any bed, so cnt == n decides the result.\\n    // The loop breaks once cnt reaches n, so the answer is true when the greedy planting can reach n flowers.\\n    // An empty flowerbed would panic on 0..b_len - 1, and a lone 0 with n of 2 returns false.\\n    // The logic is tangled and only traced by hand on small beds, so treat it as unproven.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n) for the clone.\\n    pub fn can_place_flowers(flowerbed: Vec<i32>, n: i32) -> bool {\\n        if flowerbed.len() == 1 && flowerbed[0] == 0 && n == 1 {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n        let mut bed  = flowerbed.clone();\\n        let mut flip = false;\\n        let b_len = bed.len();\\n        let mut cnt  = 0;\\n\\n        for i in 0..b_len - 1 {\\n            if cnt == n {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            if bed[i] == 0 && bed[i + 1] == 0 {\\n                bed[i] = 1;\\n                flip = true;\\n                cnt += 1;\\n            }\\n            if i > 0 {\\n                if bed[i] == 1 && bed[i - 1] == 1 && flip == true {\\n                    bed[i] = 0;\\n                    flip = false;\\n                    cnt -= 1;\\n                }\\n            }\\n\\n            if bed[b_len - 1] == 0 && bed[b_len - 2] == 0 && cnt < n {\\n                bed[b_len - 1] = 1;\\n                cnt += 1;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        let mut total = 0;\\n        for i in 0..bed.len() {\\n            if i == 1 {\\n                total += 1;\\n            } else {\\n                total -= 1;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if cnt == n && total < 3 {\\n            return true;\\n        } else {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":628,"title":"Maximum Product of Three Numbers","slug":"maximum-product-of-three-numbers","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Math","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class MaximumProductThreeNumbers:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an array of integers named nums.\\n    You need to find the three numbers in the list\\n    that returns the maximum product.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of our given array can range from 3 to 10^4.\\n    The value of elements in our integer array can range from -1000 to 1000.\\n\\n    Edge Case: Were going to assume that the maximum product will\\n    not be larger than the greatest integer. We just can\'t return it.\\n\\n    MATCH: Sorting, Max, Vector\\n\\n    PLAN: I think we should iterate through the array keeping three variables.\\n    This way we can perform this function in the shortest amount of time.\\n    If we sort the entire array it will be O(n log n) which is stupid considering\\n    we only need the three largest elements.\\n\\n    Just go through the array once, keeping three elements, everytime we find\\n    a larger elements trickle the last largest element down to the second\\n    and third variable.\\n\\n    We also need two variables to collect the smallest numbers which might be negative.\\n    If they are both negative or less than zero AND our list does contain a positive integer\\n    multiply them (two negative numbers) and multiply\\n    said positive number against the largest positive number in the given list.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) only going through our given array once.\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) we aren\'t truly creating any new objects,\\n    only using three pointers for storing data.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # maximumProduct takes a list of at least three ints and returns the largest product of any three of them.\\n    # It walks the list once and keeps the three largest values and the two smallest values seen so far.\\n    # For each number it shifts the largest values down when the number is bigger than one of them, and does the same for the two smallest.\\n    # The best product is either the three largest multiplied, or the two smallest times the largest.\\n    # The second case is for two big negative numbers, which multiply to a positive number.\\n    # Both products are computed and the larger one is returned.\\n    # Time is O(n) with a single pass, and the space is O(1), just five variables.\\n    # With fewer than three numbers the infinity starting values would end up in the product, so the code assumes at least three.\\n    def maximumProduct(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\\n        first_largest = second_largest = third_largest = float(\'-inf\')\\n        first_smallest = second_smallest = float(\'inf\')\\n\\n        for n in nums:\\n            if n > first_largest:\\n                third_largest, second_largest, first_largest = second_largest, first_largest, n\\n            elif n > second_largest:\\n                third_largest, second_largest = second_largest, n\\n            elif n > third_largest:\\n                third_largest = n\\n\\n            if n < first_smallest:\\n                second_smallest, first_smallest = first_smallest, n\\n            elif n < second_smallest:\\n                second_smallest = n\\n\\n        return max(\\n            first_largest * second_largest * third_largest,\\n            first_smallest * second_smallest * first_largest\\n        )"}]},{"number":645,"title":"Set Mismatch","slug":"set-mismatch","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Bit Manipulation","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.HashMap;\\n\\npublic class SetMismatch {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an array of integers that contains an error defined\\n    as a mismatch between two numbers where one number is duplicated and one number is missing.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of every array ranges from 2, 10^4.\\n    The value of each element can range from 1, 10^4. Only one mismatch per array.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Hashmap\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to maintain a HashMap iterated to the length of nums. Where the appearance of a number\\n    is greater than one, we set that as result[0]. Then continue searching for the second number which doesn\'t exist.\\n    Once the expected iteration is not found in the proper index of nums - 1, i we know that\'s the missing\\n    index; setting that as result[1]. Then we break out of the loop and return the result array.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    */\\n    // findErrorNums takes an array that should hold 1 to n but has one number twice and one number missing,\\n    // and returns {duplicate, missing}.\\n    // It counts how often each number shows up in a HashMap.\\n    // The first loop puts each number in map with its count, using getOrDefault to start from 0.\\n    // The second loop goes through every number i from 1 to n.\\n    // If i is in the map with a count above 1, that is the duplicate and goes in result[0].\\n    // If i is not in the map at all, that is the missing number and goes in result[1].\\n    // It is right when the input follows the problem, with exactly one duplicate and one missing number.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n) for the map.\\n    public int[] findErrorNums(int[] nums) {\\n        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\\n        int[] result = new int[2];\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            // Count numbers, should be one of each\\n            map.put(nums[i], map.getOrDefault(nums[i], 0) + 1);\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length + 1; i++) {\\n            if (map.containsKey(i)) {\\n                if (map.get(i) > 1) {\\n                    result[0] = i;\\n                }\\n            } else {\\n                result[1] = i;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n\\n    private static String stringifyPrimIntegerArr(int[] arr) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\\"[\\");\\n        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {\\n            sb.append(arr[i]).append(\\", \\");\\n        }\\n\\n        return sb.append(arr[arr.length - 1]).append(\\"]\\").toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":653,"title":"Two Sum IV - Input is a BST","slug":"two-sum-iv-input-is-a-bst","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","Two Pointers","Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Search Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.HashMap;\\nimport java.util.Map;\\n\\n // Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n/*\\nUNDERSTAND: The job is to search a BST to find the\\nvalue of two nodes that can equal the given int variable k.\\nThis problem is somewhat like 3Sum. You\'re job it return a\\nboolean if these two nodes exist.\\n\\nConstraints:\\nEdge Cases:\\n\\nMATCH: In order traversal, HashMap.\\n\\nPLAN: I\'m going to store the value and amount of said value in a HashMap,\\nsince we\'re looking for two nodes we can discern pretty quickly if our matchs can be found.\\n\\nE.g., We\'re given a BST [10, 2, 3, 2, null, 7] k = 4\\n    if (map.key(2) == 2 & 2 * 2 == 4) {\\n        return true\\n    }\\n\\n    if k = 12, x = (10 - 12) = 2 if map.contains(x) return true\\nOnce we\'ve gone through the entire map, we just return false.\\nWhat we\'re looking for isnt there.\\n\\nTime Complexity: O(N) We have to discover every node in the BST.\\nSpace Complixty: O(N) map grows with the size of the BST.\\nIMPLEMENT:\\n*/\\n\\npublic class TwoSumIVBST {\\n        // findTarget takes the root of a binary search tree and an int k, and returns true if two nodes add up to k.\\n        // First mapBst walks the tree in order and stores each value with how many times it appears in a HashMap.\\n        // Then it loops over every entry (key and its count) in the map.\\n        // If key + key equals k, it needs the value to appear at least twice, since one node cannot be used two times.\\n        // Otherwise it returns true when the map has k - key and that partner is a different value from key.\\n        // If no entry works it returns false, and an empty tree also gives false since the map is empty.\\n        // It does not use the search tree order, so it would work on any binary tree.\\n        // Time is O(n) for the walk and the loop, and space is O(n) for the map plus the recursion depth.\\n        public boolean findTarget(TreeNode root, int k) {\\n            // Create map Object\\n            HashMap<Integer, Integer> mpx = new HashMap<>();\\n            // Fill map\\n            mapBst(root, mpx);\\n            // Search map for k\\n            for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : mpx.entrySet()) {\\n                // Temp Variables\\n                int key = entry.getKey();\\n                int val = entry.getValue();\\n\\n                /*\\n                If our map contains two values that equal k return true,\\n                or perhaps our maps contains the difference of\\n                k minus a nodes value return true.\\n                */\\n                if (val >= 2 && key + key == k) {\\n                    return true;\\n                } else if (mpx.containsKey(k - key) && k - key != key) {\\n                    return true;\\n                }\\n            }\\n\\n            // Never found a combination, return false.\\n            return false;\\n    }\\n\\n    // mapBst is an in order DFS that counts how many times each value appears in the tree.\\n    private static void mapBst(TreeNode root, HashMap<Integer, Integer> mpx) {\\n        if (root == null) return;\\n\\n        mapBst(root.left, mpx);\\n        mpx.put(root.val, mpx.getOrDefault(root.val, 0) + 1);\\n        mapBst(root.right, mpx);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":682,"title":"Baseball Game","slug":"baseball-game","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Stack","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class BaseballGame:\\n    # calPoints takes a list of strings (a number, \\"+\\", \\"D\\" or \\"C\\") and returns the total score after all of them.\\n    # It has no self argument, so it only works when called on the class, like BaseballGame.calPoints(ops).\\n    # score is a list of zeros as long as the input, and index is the slot for the current record, starting at -1.\\n    # A number is stored as a string, \\"+\\" stores the sum of the last two slots, and \\"D\\" stores double the last slot.\\n    # For \\"C\\" the previous slot is set to 0 and index goes back by 2, so the next record overwrites that removed slot.\\n    # The last loop adds every slot with int(), and the zeroed or unused slots add nothing.\\n    # The code assumes valid input, so a \\"+\\" or \\"D\\" with too few earlier scores would read the wrong slot.\\n    # Time is O(n), since calCommand is a constant lookup, and the extra space is O(n) for the score list.\\n    def calPoints(operations: List[str]) -> int:\\n\\n        index = -1\\n        score = [0] * len(operations)\\n\\n        for n in operations:\\n\\n            index = index + 1\\n\\n            if (BaseballGame.calCommand(n) == 404):\\n                score[index] = n\\n\\n            elif(BaseballGame.calCommand(n) == 1):\\n                score[index] = int(score[index - 1]) + int(score[index - 2])\\n\\n            elif(BaseballGame.calCommand(n) == 0):\\n                score[index] = int(score[index - 1]) * 2\\n\\n            elif(BaseballGame.calCommand(n) == -1):\\n                score[index - 1] = 0\\n                index = index - 2\\n\\n        total = 0\\n        for n in score:\\n            total = total + int(n)\\n\\n        return total\\n\\n    # calCommand maps \\"+\\" to 1, \\"D\\" to 0, \\"C\\" to -1, and anything else (a number) to 404.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def calCommand(dir):\\n        match dir:\\n            case \\"+\\":\\n                return 1\\n            case \\"D\\":\\n                return 0\\n            case \\"C\\":\\n                return -1\\n            case _:\\n                return 404"}]},{"number":692,"title":"Top K Frequent Words","slug":"top-k-frequent-words","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","String","Trie","Sorting","Heap (Priority Queue)","Bucket Sort","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TreeNode:\\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\\n        self.val = val\\n        self.left = left\\n        self.right = right\\n\\nclass TopKFrenquentWords:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a list of words and an integer. You need to find the\\n    k most appearing words. If there are more than one words with the same frequencies\\n    then return both words in a list. If you have more than one \\"most frequent word\\" to return,\\n    return it in lexicographical order.\\n\\n    Constraints: The list can have a length of 1, 500 words.\\n    Each word can be composed of 1, 10 characters. Words is only made of lower case english letters.\\n    Given k\'s value can be in the range of 1, the number of unique words in a list (500 words).\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If k is one return a unique set of the the given list in alphabetical order.\\n\\n    MATCH: HashMap, Dict\\n\\n    PLAN: Build a BST keyed by word so each word gets exactly one node; increment\\n    its count in place on repeats (no removal needed). Then collect all (word, count)\\n    pairs via in-order traversal, sort by (-count, word) for frequency desc / alpha asc,\\n    and take the first k.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # topKFrequent takes a list of words and k and returns the k most frequent words, ties in alphabetical order.\\n    # Instead of a dictionary it builds a binary search tree keyed by the word, where each node holds [word, count].\\n    # The nested insert function walks left or right by string order and adds one to the count when the word is found.\\n    # The nested collect function walks the tree in order and gathers (word, count) pairs.\\n    # The pairs are sorted with the key (-count, word), so the highest count comes first and ties go alphabetically.\\n    # The first k words of that sorted list are returned.\\n    # The tree can become a long chain for sorted input, so insert can be slow, but the limit of 500 words keeps it safe.\\n    # Time is about O(n log u) on average for u unique words, O(n * u) worst, plus O(u log u) to sort, and space is O(u).\\n    def topKFrequent(self, words: List[str], k: int) -> List[str]:\\n        # Insert word into tree, or bump its count if it\'s already there\\n        def insert(root: Optional[TreeNode], word: str) -> TreeNode:\\n            if not root:\\n                return TreeNode([word, 1])\\n\\n            if word == root.val[0]:\\n                root.val[1] += 1\\n            elif word < root.val[0]:\\n                root.left = insert(root.left, word)\\n            else:\\n                root.right = insert(root.right, word)\\n\\n            return root\\n\\n        # Collect all (word, count) pairs in-order\\n        def collect(root: Optional[TreeNode], result: List[tuple]) -> None:\\n            if not root:\\n                return\\n            collect(root.left, result)\\n            result.append((root.val[0], root.val[1]))\\n            collect(root.right, result)\\n\\n        tree = None\\n        for word in words:\\n            tree = insert(tree, word)\\n\\n        all_words = []\\n        collect(tree, all_words)\\n\\n        # Highest frequency first; alphabetical for ties\\n        all_words.sort(key=lambda w: (-w[1], w[0]))\\n\\n        return [w for w, _ in all_words[:k]]"}]},{"number":704,"title":"Binary Search","slug":"binary-search","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Binary Search"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class BinarySearch {\\n    // search takes a sorted int array and a target, and returns the index of the target or -1 if it is missing.\\n    // It is a standard binary search, so it only works if nums is sorted in ascending order.\\n    // low and high are the ends of the part of the array that can still hold the target.\\n    // mid is the middle, written as low + (high - low) / 2 so that low + high cannot overflow an int.\\n    // If nums[mid] equals the target it returns mid, if it is smaller the search moves right, else left.\\n    // Each step cuts the range in half, and when low goes past high the target is not there, so it returns -1.\\n    // With duplicates it returns the index of one of them, not necessarily the first.\\n    // Time is O(log n) and space is O(1).\\n    public int search(int[] nums, int target) {\\n        int low = 0;\\n        int high = nums.length - 1;\\n\\n        while (low <= high) {\\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\\n            if (nums[mid] == target) {\\n                return mid;\\n            } else if (nums[mid] < target) {\\n                low = mid + 1;\\n            } else {\\n                high = mid - 1;\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return -1;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":709,"title":"To Lower Case","slug":"to-lower-case","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct ToLowerCase;\\n\\nimpl ToLowerCase {\\n    // to_lower_case takes a string and returns it with the letters A to Z changed to lowercase.\\n    // It loops over every char of s and appends the lowercase version to the new string sb.\\n    // to_ascii_lowercase only changes A to Z and leaves digits, symbols and non English letters as they are.\\n    // sb.insert at the end position works like a push, so the order of the characters stays the same.\\n    // An empty string gives an empty string.\\n    // Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n) for the new string.\\n    pub fn to_lower_case(s: String) -> String {\\n        let mut sb: String = \\"\\".to_string();\\n        for v in s.chars() {\\n            sb.insert(sb.len(), v.to_ascii_lowercase());\\n        }\\n        return sb;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":728,"title":"Self Dividing Numbers","slug":"self-dividing-numbers","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"pub struct SelfDividingNumbers;\\n\\nimpl SelfDividingNumbers {\\n    // _is_composed_divisibles returns true if every digit of n is non zero and divides n evenly.\\n    // It fails fast when n ends in 0, then eval walks the digits using % 10 and / 10.\\n    pub fn _is_composed_divisibles(n: i32) -> bool {\\n\\n        if n % 10 == 0 { return false; } // Fast-failure: Any nmber 10 or greater ending in 0 cannot be self-divisible.\\n\\n        let mut temp_n = n;\\n\\n        fn eval(n: &mut i32) -> bool {\\n            let mut tmp_n: i32 = *n;\\n\\n            while tmp_n > 0 {\\n                if tmp_n % 10 == 0 || *n % (tmp_n % 10) != 0 { return false }\\n                tmp_n = tmp_n / 10;\\n            }\\n\\n            return true\\n        }\\n\\n        return eval(&mut temp_n)\\n    }\\n\\n    // self_divding_numbers takes left and right and returns a Vec of the self dividing numbers from left to right.\\n    // A self dividing number is divisible by each of its own digits, and none of the digits may be 0.\\n    // When left is below 10, it copies the fixed list 1 to 9 starting at left, since one digit numbers all qualify.\\n    // That copy never checks right, so a call like (1, 5) returns 1 to 9, and left of 0 underflows and panics.\\n    // After that it sets _left to 10 and tests each number up to right with _is_composed_divisibles.\\n    // Passing numbers are pushed into result in increasing order.\\n    // Time is O((right - left) * d) where d is the number of digits, and extra space is O(1) besides the output.\\n    pub fn self_divding_numbers(left: i32, right: i32) -> Vec<i32> {\\n\\n        let mut result: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\\n        let mut _left: i32 = left;\\n\\n        // We should skip numbers that are guarenteed to be divisible by themself as in 1..9\\n        if _left < 10 {\\n            let init: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9];\\n            result.extend_from_slice(&init[(left as usize - 1)..]);\\n            _left = 10;\\n        }\\n\\n        for n in _left..=right {\\n            if Self::_is_composed_divisibles(n) {\\n                result.push(n);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return result\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":743,"title":"Network Delay Time","slug":"network-delay-time","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Graph Theory","Heap (Priority Queue)","Shortest Path","Dijkstra\'s Algorithm"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import heapq\\n\\nfrom collections import defaultdict\\n\\nclass Solution:\\n    # networkDelayTime takes the edges as (u, v, w), the node count n and a start node k, and returns the time for all nodes to get the signal, or -1.\\n    # It builds an adjacency dictionary from each node to its (neighbour, weight) pairs.\\n    # Then it runs Dijkstra with a min heap that holds (time so far, node), starting from (0, k).\\n    # When a node is popped for the first time its time is final and is saved in dist, and later pops of the same node are skipped.\\n    # Each neighbour that is not finished yet is pushed with time + weight.\\n    # At the end, if dist has all n nodes it returns the largest time, which is when the last node hears the signal, otherwise -1.\\n    # Time is O(E log E) for E edges because of the heap, and the space is O(n + E).\\n    def networkDelayTime(self, times: list[list[int]], n: int, k: int) -> int:\\n        graph = defaultdict(list)\\n        for u, v, w in times:\\n            graph[u].append((v, w))\\n\\n        heap = [(0, k)]\\n        dist = {}\\n\\n        while heap:\\n            time_taken, node = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n\\n            if node in dist:\\n                continue\\n\\n            dist[node] = time_taken\\n\\n            for nei, w in graph[node]:\\n                if nei not in dist:\\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (time_taken + w, nei))\\n\\n        return max(dist.values()) if len(dist) == n else -1"}]},{"number":771,"title":"Jewels and Stones","slug":"jewels-and-stones","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class JewelsStones:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given substring targets representing different types of jewels, you\'re\\n    also given stones representing an assortment of `stones` of string type. You\'re job is to\\n    return the count of jewels which are characters that can be found witin `jewels` of string type.\\n    found within the given assortment(string).\\n\\n    MATCH: HashMap\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to iterate through every character in `stones` and ask if it can be found in\\n    `jewels` if so, we increment the `count` of int type, returning it after all characters in\\n    `stones` have been visited.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # numJewelsInStones takes a string of jewel types and a string of stones and returns how many stones are jewels.\\n    # It goes through every character of stones and adds 1 to count if the character is in the jewels string.\\n    # The check is a substring search on a string, so there is no hash map here despite the notes above.\\n    # Letters are case sensitive, so \\"a\\" and \\"A\\" are different types.\\n    # Time is O(s * j) for s stones and j jewel types, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def numJewelsInStones(self, jewels: str, stones: str) -> int:\\n        count = 0\\n        for ch in stones:\\n            if ch in jewels:\\n                count += 1\\n\\n        return count"}]},{"number":788,"title":"Rotated Digits","slug":"rotated-digits","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math","Dynamic Programming"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class RotatedDigits:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an integer \'x\' count the amount of digits that can be rotated\\n    180 degrees. Those numbers are 2, 5, 6, and 9. From range 1, n count all the\\n    numbers that are or within said number, the numbers that can be rotated, return\\n    that number.\\n\\n    Constraints: The value of \'n\' can be from 1, 10^4.\\n\\n    MATCH: Matching, Modulos, Division\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to set a loop to run from 1, \'n\'. We\'re going to perform a test\\n    on each number using a helper function. Each number will be evaluated, number by number.\\n    We\'re going to ask if a set of numbers, considered \\"good numbers\\" contain the current\\n    number or sub number that we are then currently evaluating.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    swap_to = (\\"0\\", \\"1\\", \\"5\\", \\"\\", \\"\\", \\"2\\", \\"9\\", \\"\\", \\"8\\", \\"6\\")\\n\\n    # rotatedDigits takes n and returns how many numbers from 1 to n give a different valid number when rotated 180 degrees.\\n    # swap_to maps each digit to its rotated digit, and 3, 4 and 7 have an empty string because they cannot rotate.\\n    # The loop tests every number from 1 to n with the nested testGoodCount function and adds up the results.\\n    # The membership test num in self.swap_to is true for the digits 0, 1, 2, 5, 6, 8 and 9 and false for 3, 4 and 7.\\n    # So a number with a 3, 4 or 7 scores 0, and every other number is rotated digit by digit into the string temp.\\n    # A number is good only if temp differs from the original, so numbers made of 0, 1 and 8 alone are not counted.\\n    # Time is O(n log n) because each number has about log n digits, and space is O(1) besides the short temp string.\\n    def rotatedDigits(self, n: int) -> int:\\n        # testGoodCount returns 1 if the number rotates into a different valid number and 0 otherwise.\\n        def testGoodCount(self, current_num: int) -> bool:\\n            goodness = 0\\n            temp = \\"\\"\\n            for num in str(current_num):\\n                if num in self.swap_to:\\n                    temp += self.swap_to[int(num)]\\n                else:\\n                    return 0\\n\\n            if temp != str(current_num):\\n                goodness += 1\\n\\n            return goodness\\n\\n        total_goodness = 0\\n\\n        for num in range(1, n + 1):\\n            total_goodness += testGoodCount(self, num)\\n\\n        return total_goodness"}]},{"number":796,"title":"Rotate String","slug":"rotate-string","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","String Matching"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RotateString {\\n    // rotateString takes two strings and returns true if goal can be made by moving characters from the front\\n    // of s to the back.\\n    // The idea is to try every rotation of s and compare each one with goal.\\n    // mutable is a StringBuilder copy of s.\\n    // Each loop step moves the last character to the front, by inserting it at index 0 and deleting it from\\n    // the end, so it turns the text one place to the right.\\n    // After n steps every possible rotation has been compared, and the last step (i = n - 1) gives s itself\\n    // back.\\n    // If any rotation equals goal it returns true, and it returns false after n tries.\\n    // Strings of different lengths never match, so they return false, and two empty strings also return false\\n    // because the loop does not run.\\n    // Time is O(n^2) because each step edits and compares a string of length n, and extra space is O(n).\\n    public boolean rotateString(String s, String goal) {\\n\\n        StringBuilder mutable = new StringBuilder(s);\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            mutable.insert(0, mutable.substring(mutable.length() - 1));\\n            mutable.deleteCharAt(mutable.length() - 1);\\n\\n            if (mutable.toString().equals(goal)) {\\n                return true;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":832,"title":"Flipping an Image","slug":"flipping-an-image","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","Bit Manipulation","Matrix","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class FlippingImage {\\n\\n    // flipAndInvertImage takes a square 0/1 image, reverses every row, flips every bit, and returns the same array.\\n    // It goes through the rows one at a time and first calls flipArrayOrder to reverse that row.\\n    // Then it walks the row and turns every 1 into a 0 and every 0 into a 1.\\n    // The image is changed in place, since each row is replaced by its reversed copy inside the original outer array.\\n    // Reversing and inverting in two separate steps gives the same result as doing them together.\\n    // Time is O(n * m) for n rows of m values, and the extra space is O(m) for the temporary row copy.\\n    public int[][] flipAndInvertImage(int[][] image) {\\n        for (int i = 0; i < image.length; i++) {\\n\\n            //flip order\\n            flipArrayOrder(image, i);\\n\\n            for (int j = 0; j < image[i].length; j++) {\\n\\n                //flip bits\\n                if (image[i][j] == 1) {\\n                    image[i][j] = 0;\\n                } else {\\n                    image[i][j] = 1;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return image;\\n    }\\n\\n    // flipArrayOrder copies row i into a new array backwards and puts that array back as row i.\\n    public void flipArrayOrder(int[][] image, int i) {\\n        int[] temp = new int[image[i].length];\\n\\n        for (int j = image[i].length - 1; j >= 0 ; j--) {\\n            temp[image[i].length - j - 1] = image[i][j];\\n        }\\n\\n        image[i] = temp;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":836,"title":"Rectangle Overlap","slug":"rectangle-overlap","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Geometry"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ListResourceBundle;\\n\\npublic class RectangleOverlap {\\n\\n    // isRectangleOverlap takes two rectangles as {x1, y1, x2, y2} arrays (bottom left and top right) and\\n    // returns true if their areas overlap.\\n    // It builds a Rectangle object for each array and asks the first one whether it intersects the second.\\n    // The real check is in Rectangle.intersects, which looks for a way the rectangles are completely apart.\\n    // Rectangles that only touch on an edge or a corner are not counted as overlapping.\\n    // The method does a fixed number of comparisons, so time and space are O(1).\\n    public static boolean isRectangleOverlap(int[] rec1, int[] rec2) {\\n        Rectangle main = new Rectangle(rec1[0], rec1[1], rec1[2], rec1[3]);\\n        Rectangle other = new Rectangle(rec2[0], rec2[1], rec2[2], rec2[3]);\\n\\n        return main.intersects(other);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Rectangle {\\n    //known corners\\n    int x1, y1, x2, y2;\\n    //unknown corners\\n    int x3, y3, x4, y4;\\n\\n    public Rectangle(int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2) {\\n        // known corners\\n        /*Bottom Left*/\\n        this.x1 = x1;\\n        this.y1 = y1;\\n        /*Top Right*/\\n        this.x2 = x2;\\n        this.y2 = y2;\\n\\n        //unknown corners\\n        /*Bottom Right*/\\n        this.x3 = x2;\\n        this.y3 = y1;\\n        /*Top Left*/\\n        this.x4 = x1;\\n        this.y4 = y2;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Returns false if this rectangle is entirely left of, right of, below or above the other one, using <=\\n    // and >= so touching edges count as apart.\\n    // If none of those four cases is true the rectangles share some area, so it returns true.\\n    public boolean intersects(Rectangle other) {\\n\\n        if (this.x2 <= other.x1) return false;\\n        if (this.x1 >= other.x2) return false;\\n        if (this.y2 <= other.y1) return false;\\n        if (this.y1 >= other.y2) return false;\\n\\n        return true;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":841,"title":"Keys and Rooms","slug":"keys-and-rooms","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Graph Theory"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class KeysRooms:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/keys-and-rooms/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an array of rooms where inside each room the element is a\\n    number to another room which grants you access. The goal is to visit every room,\\n    if it\'s possible return True, otherwise, return False.\\n\\n    I think no one room can visited more than once. If you find a room that points to a room\\n    that was already visited you must return False. It\'s sort of like a linked list where\\n    each node should have another address. So if you visit a room and it doesn\'t contain a key\\n    you can stop iterating, or if it has already been visited, return False.\\n\\n    MATCH: List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to maintain a variable key. We\'re going to start from room index zero.\\n    Looking inside that room we judge two things, if the room is empty, stop the iteration. If\\n    the room has a string value inside: \\"visited\\" then return False.\\n\\n    What happens if there are two nodes where each node points to each other.\\n\\n    How about we collect keys, then iterate through collected keys and see if the matches the length\\n    of the array - 1. Which is also every room\'s key.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(n + h)\\n    Space Complexity: O(n)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # canVisitAllRooms takes a list where rooms[i] holds the keys found in room i and returns whether every room can be opened.\\n    # keychain is a set of the rooms reached so far, and it starts with room 0 because that room is open.\\n    # The helper does a depth first search, adding each new key to the set and then going into that room.\\n    # After the search, the loop checks that every room number from 0 to n - 1 is in the set.\\n    # A room already in the set is never visited again, so loops between rooms do not cause endless recursion.\\n    # Time is O(n + total number of keys) and the set takes O(n) space.\\n    # The recursion can go as deep as the number of rooms, which could pass Python\'s default limit of about 1000 for long chains.\\n    def canVisitAllRooms(self, rooms: list[list[int]]) -> bool:\\n        keychain = set([0])\\n        keychain = self.helper(rooms, 0, keychain)\\n\\n        for key_name in range(len(rooms)):\\n            if not key_name in keychain:\\n                return False\\n\\n        return True\\n\\n    # helper visits curr_room, adds every key that is new to keychain and recurses into that room, then returns keychain.\\n    def helper(self,\\n               rooms,\\n               curr_room,\\n               keychain):\\n\\n        for key in rooms[curr_room]:\\n            if key != curr_room and key not in keychain:\\n                keychain.add(key)\\n                self.helper(rooms, key, keychain)\\n\\n        return keychain"}]},{"number":856,"title":"Score of Parentheses","slug":"score-of-parentheses","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String","Stack","Bracket Sequences"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import Counter\\n\\nclass ScoreParentheses:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/score-of-parentheses\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a string of parentheses which are balanced.\\n    Return how many complete pairs of parentheses compose the string.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of a given string can be 2, 50 characters long.\\n    The given String will only consist of open/closed parentheses.\\n    The given String is guarenteed to be a balanced set of parentheses configuration.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Stack, Counter\\n\\n    PLAN: We can use a stack, or we can just keep a count of how many open parentheses we see.\\n    The string is guarenteed to be balanced, so why not just count.\\n\\n    The score of the parentheses inside is multiplied by how many outside complete\\n    parentheses are wrapping it. So we can use a stack, determine if anything else is\\n    inside the track and for each opening parentheses we multiply the inside parentheses keeping a score.\\n\\n    Keep a running total and add that total * 2 when you\'re at the end of the stack.\\n\\n    Time Complexity:\\n\\n    Space Complexity:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # scoreOfParentheses takes a balanced parentheses string and returns its score by the LeetCode rules.\\n    # It uses a stack of running scores, with one starting entry 0 for the top level.\\n    # Every \'(\' pushes a new 0, which is the score of whatever will be inside that pair.\\n    # Every \')\' pops the inside score, and if it is 0 the pair was empty \\"()\\" so it is worth 1, else it is worth double.\\n    # That score is added to the new top of the stack, which is the pair around it, so neighbours add up.\\n    # At the end the only entry left is the total, which is popped and returned.\\n    # This relies on the input being balanced, since an unbalanced string could pop the starting entry and fail.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(n) for the stack when the nesting is deep.\\n    def scoreOfParentheses(self, s: str) -> int:\\n        stack = [0]\\n\\n        for ch in s:\\n            if ch == \'(\':\\n                stack.append(0)\\n\\n            else:\\n                inside_score = stack.pop()\\n\\n                if inside_score == 0:\\n                    score = 1\\n                else:\\n                    score = inside_score * 2\\n\\n                stack[-1] += score\\n\\n        return stack.pop()"}]},{"number":876,"title":"Middle of the Linked List","slug":"middle-of-the-linked-list","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Linked List","Two Pointers"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class MiddleOfTheLinkedList {\\n\\n    /**\\n     * Definition for singly-linked list.\\n     * public class ListNode {\\n     * int val;\\n     * ListNode next;\\n     * ListNode() {}\\n     * ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n     * ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }\\n     * }\\n     */\\n\\n    // middleNode takes the head of a linked list and returns the middle node (the second one if the length is even).\\n    // A single node is returned right away.\\n    // Otherwise it finds the length with findLen and sets midLen to len / 2 (both sides of the if/else are the same).\\n    // Then it moves head forward one node at a time and returns the node it lands on when the step count is midLen.\\n    // Length 5 moves 2 steps and gives the 3rd node, and length 6 moves 3 steps and gives the 4th node.\\n    // It works but uses two passes, where a slow and fast pointer would need only one.\\n    // The recursive findLen uses stack space equal to the list length, so time is O(n) and space is O(n).\\n    public ListNode middleNode(ListNode head) {\\n        if (head.next == null) return head;\\n\\n        int len = findLen(head), currLen = 0, midLen = 0;\\n        if (len % 2 == 0) midLen = len / 2; else midLen = len / 2;\\n\\n        while (head != null) {\\n            head = head.next;\\n            currLen++;\\n            if (currLen == midLen) return head;\\n        }\\n        return null;\\n    }\\n\\n    // findLen counts the nodes recursively, adding 1 per node, so its stack depth equals the length.\\n    public int findLen(ListNode head) {\\n        if (head == null) return 0;\\n        return 1 + findLen(head.next);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":896,"title":"Monotonic Array","slug":"monotonic-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class MonotonicArray {\\n\\n    /*\\n    only making it this complicated to work on recursion,\\n    If order is true, then increasing. If order is false, then decreasing.\\n    If triv is true, then the final result is true because the order is trivial.\\n    This is because only the last element of the array is different.\\n    */\\n    // isMonotonic takes an int array and returns true if it never goes up or never goes down.\\n    // It is written with recursion on purpose, as practice, instead of one loop.\\n    // isIncOrDec looks at the first pair that differs to decide if the array should be increasing or decreasing.\\n    // If that gives true it runs funcInc, which fails on any later drop, otherwise funcDec, which fails on any rise.\\n    // Equal neighbors are allowed in both checks, and a single value or all equal values give true.\\n    // An empty array crashes with an index error, because nums[0] is read.\\n    // Time is O(n), but recursion depth can reach n, so a very long array could overflow the stack.\\n    public boolean isMonotonic(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        if (isIncOrDec(nums, 0)) {\\n            return funcInc(nums, 0);\\n        } else {\\n            return funcDec(nums, 0);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    /*\\n    determine if the array is increasing or decreasing order\\n    */\\n    // isIncOrDec returns true if the first differing pair rises or none differ, false if it falls.\\n    public boolean isIncOrDec(int[] nums, int index) {\\n\\n        if (index == nums.length - 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (nums[index] < nums[index + 1]) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else if (nums[index] > nums[index + 1]) {\\n            return false;\\n        } else {\\n            return isIncOrDec(nums, index + 1);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // funcInc returns false if any later neighbor pair goes down, otherwise true.\\n    public boolean funcInc(int[] nums, int start) {\\n\\n        if (start == nums.length - 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (nums[start] > nums[start + 1]) {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n\\n        return funcInc(nums, start + 1);\\n    }\\n\\n    // funcDec returns false if any later neighbor pair goes up, otherwise true.\\n    public boolean funcDec(int[] nums, int start) {\\n\\n        if (start == nums.length - 1) {\\n            return true;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (nums[start] < nums[start + 1]) {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n\\n        return funcDec(nums, start + 1);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":966,"title":"Vowel Spellchecker","slug":"vowel-spellchecker","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.HashMap;\\nimport java.util.Map;\\n\\npublic class VowelSpellchecker {\\n\\n    // spellchecker takes a word list and a list of queries and returns one answer string per query.\\n    // Each answer is the exact match first, then the first case-insensitive match, then the first vowel-blind match.\\n    // Two HashMaps are built once: one keyed by the lowercase word, one keyed by the lowercase word with vowels as \'*\'.\\n    // putIfAbsent keeps only the first word for each key, so earlier words in the list win.\\n    // For each query it first scans the word list for an exact match, which keeps the original capitalization.\\n    // If that fails it looks up the lowercase query, then the devowelled lowercase query, and otherwise stores \\"\\".\\n    // The order of the three checks is what gives the right priority between match types.\\n    // The exact match is a linear scan, so time is O(q * w * L) for q queries, w words and word length L.\\n    // The maps use O(w * L) extra space, plus the result array.\\n    public static String[] spellchecker(String[] wordlist, String[] queries) {\\n        String[] result_list = new String[queries.length];\\n\\n        // Preprocess maps\\n        Map<String, String> lowerMap = new HashMap<>();\\n        Map<String, String> vowelMap = new HashMap<>();\\n\\n        for (String word : wordlist) {\\n            String lower = word.toLowerCase();\\n            String vowelKey = devowel(lower);\\n\\n            lowerMap.putIfAbsent(lower, word);      // first lowercase occurrence\\n            vowelMap.putIfAbsent(vowelKey, word);   // first vowel-error occurrence\\n        }\\n\\n        // Process queries\\n        for (int p = 0; p < queries.length; p++) {\\n            String q = queries[p];\\n\\n            // exact match\\n            for (String w : wordlist) {\\n                if (q.equals(w)) {\\n                    result_list[p] = w;\\n                    break;\\n                }\\n            }\\n            if (result_list[p] != null) continue;\\n\\n            // case-insensitive\\n            String qLower = q.toLowerCase();\\n            if (lowerMap.containsKey(qLower)) {\\n                result_list[p] = lowerMap.get(qLower);\\n                continue;\\n            }\\n\\n            // vowel-error\\n            String qVowel = devowel(qLower);\\n            if (vowelMap.containsKey(qVowel)) {\\n                result_list[p] = vowelMap.get(qVowel);\\n                continue;\\n            }\\n\\n            // no match\\n            result_list[p] = \\"\\";\\n        }\\n\\n        return result_list;\\n    }\\n\\n    // devowel replaces every vowel with \'*\', so words that differ only in vowels give the same key.\\n    // replace vowels with *\\n    private static String devowel(String word) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\n        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {\\n            if (isVowel(c)) sb.append(\'*\');\\n            else sb.append(c);\\n        }\\n        return sb.toString();\\n    }\\n\\n    // your original vowel check\\n    public static boolean isVowel(char let) {\\n        let = Character.toLowerCase(let);\\n        return let == \'a\' || let == \'i\' || let == \'e\' || let == \'o\' || let == \'u\';\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":993,"title":"Cousins in Binary Tree","slug":"cousins-in-binary-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\n// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nrecord relationshipData(int parent, int child) {\\n    int getParent() {\\n        return this.parent;\\n    }\\n\\n    int getChild() {\\n        return this.child;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class CousinsBinaryTree {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: To find the cousin in a binary\\n    tree you have to evaluate two nodes that\\n    are on the same level as one another. A strinct conditions to be considered\\n    cousins is that two nodes have the same depth with different parents.\\n    To clear any confusions these two nodes don\'t have to have the same value.\\n\\n    Constraints: A tree can have 2, 100 nodes. Value of such nodes range from 1, 100.\\n    Each node is unique, that being said x & y cannot have the same value.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Breadth First Search because you\'re searching level by\\n    level to see if two nodes are cousins.\\n\\n    PLAN: Search each level for two nodes that are equal\\n    to either given primative integers x and y.\\n    To do this we\'re going to section the levels by\\n    sublist of integers into a list. Then search each sublist for\\n    the existance of given variables x & y.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) becuase you might have to search the entire binary tree because\\n    the cousins can be on the most bottom level.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(M) traversing elements and storing it in queue,\\n    but width of binary tree can varry.\\n\\n    IMPLEMENT:\\n    */\\n\\n    // isCousins takes the root of a binary tree and two values x and y, and returns true if the nodes are cousins.\\n    // Cousins are nodes at the same depth with different parents, and the code assumes every value is unique.\\n    // First storeLevelOrder fills collectedTree, with one list per level and one relationshipData per node.\\n    // Each entry holds the parent\'s value and the node\'s own value, and the root is stored as its own parent.\\n    // Then it scans every level, and corx and cory remember the entry whose child value is x and the one for y.\\n    // As soon as both were found on the same level and their parents are different, it returns true.\\n    // If the loop ends without that, it returns false, which covers siblings and nodes on different levels.\\n    // Time is O(n) and space is O(n), since every node is stored once, plus the recursion depth.\\n    public boolean isCousins(TreeNode root, int x, int y) {\\n        List<ArrayList<relationshipData>> collectedTree = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n        storeLevelOrder(root, root, 0, collectedTree);\\n\\n        for (ArrayList<relationshipData> level : collectedTree) {\\n\\n            // Search for the data of two nodes within one level.\\n            relationshipData corx = null, cory = null;\\n            for (relationshipData data : level) {\\n\\n                if (data.getChild() == x) {\\n                    corx = data;\\n                } else if (data.getChild() == y) {\\n                    cory = data;\\n                }\\n\\n                // Ask about relationship if x and y were found.\\n                // They must be cousins meaning they can\'t have the same parents.\\n                if (corx != null && cory != null && corx.getParent() != cory.getParent()) {\\n                    return true;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Function to perform level order traversal\\n    // storeLevelOrder is named level order, but it is really a depth first walk that tracks the level number.\\n    // For each node it makes sure the list for that level exists, and then adds (parent value, node value) to it.\\n    private static void storeLevelOrder(TreeNode root, TreeNode parent, int level, List<ArrayList<relationshipData>> storage) {\\n\\n        // Nothing left to see here, folks.\\n        if (root == null)\\n            return;\\n\\n        // Add a new level to the result if needed\\n        if (storage.size() <= level)\\n            storage.add(new ArrayList<>());\\n\\n        // add node data to the correct sublist which represents the level of the tree\\n        storage.get(level).add(new relationshipData(parent == null ? -1 : parent.val, root.val));\\n\\n        // Recur for left and right children\\n        storeLevelOrder(root.left, root, level + 1, storage);\\n        storeLevelOrder(root.right, root, level + 1, storage);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1021,"title":"Remove Outermost Parentheses","slug":"remove-outermost-parentheses","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","Stack","Bracket Sequences"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class RemoveOutermostParentheses:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: We need to remove all outer parentheses from a given string of valid parentheses.\\n    Outer parentheses means that there is an inner set of complete parentheses.\\n    MATCH: Stack, Vect,\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to turn the string into a vector and use a stack. We\'re going to take the outer most\\n    left parenthesis `\'(\'`, we should continue adding to the stack until we meet the next closing parentheses `\')\'`.\\n    So we\'re going to maintain a string which starts off empty. Every parentheses after the left most,\\n    until the equivalent closing if the string isn\'t done we start over, even if the stack is empty.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # removeOuterParentheses takes a valid parentheses string and returns it with the outermost pair of each part removed.\\n    # It keeps a stack of open parentheses, and the stack size is the current nesting depth.\\n    # When a \'(\' arrives on an empty stack it is an outer one, so left is set to the index just after it.\\n    # When a \')\' arrives and the stack holds one item, it closes an outer pair, so s[left:i] is added to the result.\\n    # All other parentheses are only pushed or popped, since they are inner and stay inside the slice.\\n    # Empty pairs like \\"()\\" add nothing because left is not less than i, so \\"()()\\" gives an empty string.\\n    # This is right for valid input, and an invalid string could pop an empty stack and raise an error.\\n    # Time is O(n) and space is O(n) for the stack and the result.\\n    def removeOuterParentheses(self, s: str) -> str:\\n        result = \\"\\"\\n        stack  = []\\n        left   = 0\\n\\n        for i, paren in enumerate(s):\\n            if len(stack) == 0 and paren == \'(\':\\n                left = i + 1\\n                stack.append(\'(\')\\n            elif paren == \'(\':\\n                stack.append(\'(\')\\n\\n            if len(stack) < 2 and paren == \')\':\\n                if left < i:\\n                    result += \\"\\".join(s[left:i])\\n                stack.pop()\\n            elif paren == \')\':\\n                stack.pop()\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":1022,"title":"Sum of Root To Leaf Binary Numbers","slug":"sum-of-root-to-leaf-binary-numbers","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\n// Definition for a binary tree node.\\nclass TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class SumRootToLeafBinaryNumbers {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a binary tree,\\n    the concept is that you must attend to each root,\\n    leaf combination as a standalone binary number. Then you must take\\n    all binary numbers add them and return the sum they represent via summation.\\n\\n    Constrains:\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Depth First Search, List Organization\\n\\n    PLAN: I plan to do a DFS of all the root, leaf combinations.\\n\\n    Each of these combinations will be stored in a vect. Traversing each\\n    index we will pick apart a sublist, finding it\'s binary representation.\\n    Simply adding up these representations and returning the result/total.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N^2)\\n    Space Complexity: O(N^2) This is because each\\n\\n    IMPLEMENT:\\n    */\\n\\n    @FunctionalInterface\\n    interface BinaryOperations {\\n        int BinaryListToInteger(ArrayList<Integer> sublist);\\n    }\\n\\n    // sumRootToLeaf takes the root of a tree whose nodes are 0 or 1 and returns the sum of all root to leaf\\n    // paths read as binary numbers.\\n    // The idea is to collect every root to leaf path as a list of digits first, then convert each list to a\\n    // number.\\n    // The lambda BinaryListToInteger goes through a path from the last digit to the first and adds binPlace\\n    // for each 1, doubling binPlace each step.\\n    // The helper does a depth first search and fills lsx with one list per leaf.\\n    // The loop at the end converts each path with the lambda and adds the numbers into final_result.\\n    // For example the tree in main has paths 100, 101, 110 and 111, which are 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 = 22.\\n    // Paths longer than 31 digits would overflow the int, since binPlace and the sum are ints.\\n    // Time and space are O(L * h) for L leaves and height h, because every path is copied into its own list,\\n    // so it is O(n^2) in the worst case.\\n    private int sumRootToLeaf(TreeNode root) {\\n\\n        int final_result = 0;\\n\\n        // Intake list of binary digits, return integer representation.\\n        BinaryOperations BinaryListToInteger = (ArrayList<Integer> sublist) -> {\\n\\n            // Temporary Variables\\n            int temp_result = 0;\\n            int binPlace = 1;\\n\\n            for (int digit : sublist.reversed()) {\\n                if (digit == 1) {\\n                    temp_result += binPlace;\\n                }\\n\\n                binPlace *= 2;\\n             }\\n\\n             return temp_result;\\n        };\\n\\n        // Objects\\n        List<ArrayList<Integer>> lsx = new ArrayList<>();\\n        helper(root, lsx, new ArrayList<>());\\n\\n        // Get the decimal representation of ever binary list and sum them.\\n        for (ArrayList<Integer> sub : lsx) {\\n            final_result += BinaryListToInteger.BinaryListToInteger(sub);\\n        }\\n\\n        return final_result;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Depth first search that keeps the current path in sub, copies it into lsx at a leaf, and removes the\\n    // last digit when it backs up.\\n    private static void helper(TreeNode root, List<ArrayList<Integer>> lsx, List<Integer> sub) {\\n        if (root == null) {\\n            return;\\n        }\\n\\n        sub.add(root.val);\\n\\n        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {\\n            lsx.add(new ArrayList<>(sub));\\n        } else {\\n            helper(root.left, lsx, sub);\\n            helper(root.right, lsx, sub);\\n        }\\n\\n        sub.remove(sub.size() - 1);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1046,"title":"Last Stone Weight","slug":"last-stone-weight","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Heap (Priority Queue)"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::cmp::max;\\n\\npub struct LastStoneWeight;\\n\\nimpl LastStoneWeight {\\n    // last_stone_weight takes a Vec of stone weights and returns the weight of the last stone, or 0 if none is left.\\n    // It repeatedly smashes the two heaviest stones, using a Vec that is searched for the maximum each time.\\n    // A single stone is returned right away.\\n    // Each round finds the max r1 and removes it, then finds the next max r2 in the rest and removes it.\\n    // The closure smash_and_add puts the difference r1 - r2 back at the front, and equal stones leave a 0.\\n    // Each round takes out two stones and puts one back, so the length drops by one until a single stone is left.\\n    // A leftover 0 is a stone like any other, so if everything cancels out the answer is 0.\\n    // An empty Vec would panic on unwrap, since max returns None.\\n    // Time is O(n * n) because each of about n rounds scans and removes in the Vec, and extra space is O(n).\\n    pub fn last_stone_weight(stones: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\\n\\n        /*\\n        https://leetcode.com/problems/last-stone-weight\\n\\n        TODO:\\n        Edge Cases: What if the vector is uneven, smashing an uneven amount of rocks together.\\n\\n        Step 1: Find two maximums rocks, store them in a variable,\\n        and remove them from the vector.\\n        Step 2: Get the maximum of both rocks. Subtract the smaller rock from the larget one, and store the result in a variable.\\n        Step 2.1: Put the variable resultant of subtraction back into the vector if the rock value is greater than zero.\\n        Step 3: Repeat from \\"Step: 1\\" until there is only 1 rock left.\\n        */\\n\\n        if stones.len() == 1 {\\n            return stones[0];\\n        }\\n\\n        let mut vect: Vec<i32> = stones.clone();\\n\\n            let smash_and_add= |x: i32, y: i32, vect: Vec<i32>| -> Vec<i32> {\\n                let mut inner_vect: Vec<i32> = vect.clone();\\n                let reduction: i32 = if max(x, y) == x { x - y } else { y - x };\\n                if reduction >= 0 {\\n                    inner_vect.insert(0, reduction);\\n                }\\n                return inner_vect\\n            };\\n\\n        while vect.len() != 1 {\\n            // Step 1:\\n            let r1: i32 = *vect.iter().max().unwrap();\\n            let idx1 = vect.iter().position(|x| *x == r1).unwrap();\\n            vect.remove(idx1);\\n            let r2: i32 = *vect.iter().max().unwrap();\\n            let idx2 = vect.iter().position(|x| *x == r2).unwrap();\\n            vect.remove(idx2);\\n\\n            // Step 2:\\n            vect = smash_and_add(r1, r2, vect);\\n        }\\n\\n        return vect[0]\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1051,"title":"Height Checker","slug":"height-checker","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Sorting","Counting Sort","Bubble Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class HeightChecker {\\n\\n    // heightChecker takes the array of student heights and returns how many positions differ from the sorted order.\\n    // It first copies the array into comparable, because the sort will rearrange the original.\\n    // Then it sorts heights with myMergeSort, a hand written merge sort.\\n    // It walks both arrays and counts every index where the original order and the sorted order disagree.\\n    // A side effect is that the array passed in by the caller ends up sorted.\\n    // Time is O(n log n) because of the merge sort, and the space is O(n) for the copy and the merge pieces.\\n    public int heightChecker(int[] heights) {\\n\\n        int count = 0;\\n        int[] comparable = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(heights, heights.length);\\n        myMergeSort(heights);\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < comparable.length; i++) {\\n            if (comparable[i] != heights[i]) count++;\\n        }\\n        return count;\\n    }\\n\\n    // myMergeSort splits the array in two halves, sorts each by recursion, then merges them back into the original.\\n    public int[] myMergeSort(int[] heights) {\\n\\n        if (heights.length <= 1) return heights;\\n\\n        int[] left = java.util.Arrays.copyOfRange(heights, 0, heights.length / 2);\\n        int[] right = java.util.Arrays.copyOfRange(heights, heights.length / 2, heights.length);\\n\\n        myMergeSort(left);\\n        myMergeSort(right);\\n\\n        merge(left, right, heights);\\n\\n        return heights;\\n    }\\n\\n    // merge takes two sorted arrays and fills heights with their values in order, using one index for each input.\\n    private void merge(int[] left, int[] right, int[] heights) {\\n\\n        int id1 = 0, id2 = 0, id3 = 0;\\n\\n        while (id1 < left.length && id2 < right.length) {\\n            if (left[id1] < right[id2]) {\\n                heights[id3++] = left[id1++];\\n            } else {\\n                heights[id3++] = right[id2++];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        while (id1 < left.length) {\\n            heights[id3++] = left[id1++];\\n        }\\n\\n        while (id2 < right.length) {\\n            heights[id3++] = right[id2++];\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1080,"title":"Insufficient Nodes in Root to Leaf Paths","slug":"insufficient-nodes-in-root-to-leaf-paths","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"class TreeNode {\\n    int val;\\n    TreeNode left;\\n    TreeNode right;\\n    TreeNode() {}\\n    TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }\\n    TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {\\n        this.val = val;\\n        this.left = left;\\n        this.right = right;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npublic class InsufficientNodesRootLeafPaths {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Gardening simulator: Given a binary tree and an integer value \\"limit.\\" You need to cull\\n    the branches that have sum less than the limit. A root is insufficient if every node to leaf path\\n    has a sum less than the limit.\\n\\n    Constraints: The number of nodes in a tree can range from 1, 5000. The value of each node\\n    can range from -10^5, 10^5. Each limit can have a value of -10^9, 10^9.\\n    Constraints:\\n\\n    MATCH: Depth First Search\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to do a depth first search on the binary tree maintaining a temporary sum along the way.\\n    If a node has two branches that fail to meet the limit, we\'re going to set the address to null,\\n    essentially truncating that branch from the rest of the tree.\\n    */\\n\\n    // sufficientSubset takes the root of a tree and a limit and returns the tree after removing insufficient nodes.\\n    // It just starts the helper with a running sum of 0.\\n    // The helper adds the node value to the sum, so the sum is the total from the root down to this node.\\n    // At a leaf it returns null if the sum is below the limit, and keeps the leaf otherwise.\\n    // At an inner node it sets left and right to what the recursive calls return, so cut branches become null.\\n    // If both children ended up null, this node has no good path through it, so it returns null too.\\n    // The tree is changed in place, and the root can come back as null if every path is too small.\\n    // Time is O(n) because each node is visited once, and space is O(h) for the recursion (h is tree height).\\n    public TreeNode sufficientSubset(TreeNode root, int limit) {\\n        return helper(root, limit, 0);\\n    }\\n\\n    private TreeNode helper(TreeNode node, int limit, int sum) {\\n        if (node == null) return null;\\n\\n        sum += node.val;\\n\\n        if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {\\n            return sum < limit ? null : node;\\n        }\\n\\n        node.left = helper(node.left, limit, sum);\\n        node.right = helper(node.right, limit, sum);\\n\\n        return (node.left == null && node.right == null) ? null : node;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1122,"title":"Relative Sort Array","slug":"relative-sort-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Sorting","Counting Sort","Quicksort","Bubble Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class RelativeSortArray:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given two arrays you need to arr1 and arr2. The given arr2 serves as a\\n    guide for the expected order of arr1. Any elements that don\'t appear in arr2\\n    need to be towards the end of arr1 in non-decreasing order.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of given arr1 & arr2 can be from 1, 1000 elements long.\\n    Elements in arr1 & arr2 can store elements that have integer values from 0, 1000.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If we\'re given an arr2 that is empty, meaning we\'re given no directions,\\n    return a sorted array. If we\'re given an array1 that is empty, return an empty array.\\n    If there are more elements in arr2 than in arr1 vice versa, use what\'s\\n    given respectfully to the rules explained above.\\n\\n    MATCH: HashMap\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to check for an empty arr1, if it\'s empty just return it.\\n    Then we check for arr2 being empty, if it\'s empty then we just binary sort.\\n    We\'re going to collect all of the elements in arr1 in dictionary. Then we\'re\\n    going to loop through arr2 dumping the elements per value into a new list.\\n    If any remaining numbers we\'re going binary sort them and append them to the returning list.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # relativeSortArray takes arr1 and arr2 and returns arr1 reordered to follow arr2, with the leftovers sorted at the end.\\n    # If arr1 has fewer than two items it is returned as is.\\n    # A Counter counts how many times each value appears in arr1.\\n    # For each value in arr2, in order, it appends that value as many times as the Counter says, which can be zero.\\n    # Then it collects the values of arr1 that are not in arr2 into unpurposed, once per copy, and sorts them.\\n    # The sorted leftovers are appended, so an empty arr2 simply gives a sorted arr1.\\n    # There is a leftover debug line, print(dict[23, None]), that prints 0 every call, and dict shadows the built-in name.\\n    # The in checks scan lists, so time is O(n * (n + m)) in the worst case plus the sort, and space is O(n).\\n    def relativeSortArray(self, arr1: List[int], arr2: List[int]) -> List[int]:\\n\\n        # Initital conditional checks\\n        if len(arr1) < 2:\\n            return arr1\\n\\n        # Objects\\n        result = []\\n        unpurposed = []\\n\\n        # Collect in dictionary\\n        dict = Counter(arr1)\\n\\n        print(dict[23, None])\\n\\n        # Following arr2 directions and place\\n        for num in arr2:\\n            for _ in range(0, dict[num]):\\n                result.append(num)\\n\\n        # Collecting all the nums without explicit direction.\\n        for num in arr1:\\n            if not num in arr2 and num not in unpurposed:\\n                for _ in range(0, dict[num]):\\n                    unpurposed.append(num)\\n\\n        # Sort unalocated numbers from the unpurposed integers\\n        unpurposed = sorted(unpurposed)\\n\\n        # Append and return\\n        for num in unpurposed:\\n            result.append(num)\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":1290,"title":"Convert Binary Number in a Linked List to Integer","slug":"convert-binary-number-in-a-linked-list-to-integer","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Linked List","Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.List;\\n\\npublic class ConvertBinaryLinkedListInteger {\\n\\n    // getDecimalValue takes the head of a linked list of 0s and 1s, a binary number, and returns its int value.\\n    // A list has no length until it is walked, so it first calls determineNodeLength to count the nodes.\\n    // Then it calls toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros with 2 to the power of the length, which is one place too high.\\n    // That helper gives the first node that value, then halves it for each next node.\\n    // A node with value 1 adds the current value to the sum, and a node with value 0 adds nothing.\\n    // The sum is one factor of 2 too big, which is why the final result is divided by 2.\\n    // Math.pow gives a double that is cast to int, so lists of 31 or more nodes would overflow.\\n    // Time is O(n) and space is O(n), since both helpers recurse once per node.\\n    public int getDecimalValue(ListNode head) {\\n        return toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros(head, (int) Math.pow(2, ((double) determineNodeLength(head)))) / 2;\\n    }\\n\\n    // toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros adds up the place values of the nodes that are 1, and halves numerator at each node.\\n    public int toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros(ListNode head, int numerator) {\\n        if (head == null) return 0;\\n        if (head.val == 1) return numerator + toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros(head.next, numerator / 2);\\n        return toBinaryOnlyOnesAndZeros(head.next, numerator / 2);\\n    }\\n\\n    // determineNodeLength counts the nodes of the list recursively.\\n    public int determineNodeLength(ListNode head) {\\n        if (head == null) return 0;\\n        return 1 + determineNodeLength(head.next);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1331,"title":"Rank Transform of an Array","slug":"rank-transform-of-an-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\\n\\npub struct RankTransformArray;\\n\\nimpl RankTransformArray {\\n    //https://leetcode.com/problems/rank-transform-of-an-array/\\n\\n    // array_rank_transform takes a Vec of numbers and returns a Vec of their ranks, smallest is 1.\\n    // It sorts a clone of the input, and the map stores the rank for every distinct value.\\n    // Walking the sorted clone, a value already in the map is a duplicate, so dec goes up by one and it is skipped.\\n    // A new value gets the index of its first spot in the sorted clone, plus 1, minus dec.\\n    // Taking away dec closes the gaps that duplicates leave, so equal numbers share a rank and ranks go up by 1.\\n    // The last loop looks each original number up in the map and pushes its rank in the original order.\\n    // The position call scans from the start for every distinct value, which makes the worst case slow.\\n    // Time is O(n * n) in the worst case (the sort alone is O(n log n)), and extra space is O(n).\\n    pub fn array_rank_transform(arr: Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {\\n        let mut result: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\\n        let mut map: HashMap<i32,usize> = HashMap::new();\\n        let mut arr_clone: Vec<i32> = arr.clone();\\n        let mut dec: usize = 0;\\n        arr_clone.sort();\\n        for elm in &arr_clone {\\n            if map.contains_key(elm) {\\n                dec += 1;\\n                continue;\\n            } else {\\n                map.insert(*elm, (arr_clone.iter().position(|&x| x == *elm).unwrap() + 1) - dec);\\n        }\\n            }\\n        for elm in arr{\\n            let val: &usize = map.get(&elm).unwrap();\\n            result.push(*val as i32);\\n        }\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1379,"title":"Find a Corresponding Node of a Binary Tree in a Clone of That Tree","slug":"find-a-corresponding-node-of-a-binary-tree-in-a-clone-of-that-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class FindCorrespondingNodeBinaryTreeCloneThatTree {\\n\\n    // getTargetCopy takes an original tree, its clone and a target node, and returns the matching clone node.\\n    // It walks both trees at the same time, so the clone node it is on always sits at the same place as the original node.\\n    // If the original node is null there is nothing here, so it returns null.\\n    // If the original node is the target by reference (==), it returns the cloned node at the same position.\\n    // Otherwise it searches the left side first, and returns that answer if it found something.\\n    // If the left side found nothing it returns whatever the right side search gives.\\n    // It assumes the clone has exactly the same shape as the original, or cloned.left could hit a null pointer.\\n    // Time is O(n) since each node is visited at most once, and space is O(h) for the recursion (h is tree height).\\n    public final TreeNode getTargetCopy(final TreeNode original, final TreeNode cloned, final TreeNode target) {\\n        if (original == null) {\\n            return null;\\n        }\\n        if (original == target) {\\n            return cloned;\\n        }\\n\\n        TreeNode left = getTargetCopy(original.left, cloned.left, target);\\n        if (left != null) {\\n            return left;\\n        }\\n\\n        return getTargetCopy(original.right, cloned.right, target);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1394,"title":"Find Lucky Integer in an Array","slug":"find-lucky-integer-in-an-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindLuckyIntegerArray:\\n    # findLucky takes a list of ints and returns the largest value whose count in the list equals the value, or -1.\\n    # It first builds a dictionary inv that maps every number to how many times it appears.\\n    # Then it goes through the keys and keeps the biggest key n where n == inv[n] in largest_recorded.\\n    # It starts from -1, so -1 comes back when there is no lucky number.\\n    # The method has no self parameter, which works here because it is only called through the class name.\\n    # Time is O(n) and the extra space is O(k) for k different values.\\n    def findLucky(arr: List[int]) -> int:\\n\\n        inv = {}\\n\\n        for n in arr:\\n\\n            if n in inv:\\n                inv[n] += 1\\n\\n            else:\\n                inv[n] = 1\\n\\n        largest_recorded =  -1\\n\\n        for n in inv:\\n            if (n == inv[n]):\\n                if (n > largest_recorded):\\n                    largest_recorded = n\\n\\n        return largest_recorded"}]},{"number":1464,"title":"Maximum Product of Two Elements in an Array","slug":"maximum-product-of-two-elements-in-an-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Sorting","Heap (Priority Queue)"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class MaximumProductOfTwoElementsInAnArray {\\n    // maxProduct takes an int array and returns the product of (largest - 1) and (second largest - 1).\\n    // The first loop finds the largest value max1 and remembers its index in crit.\\n    // The second loop finds the largest value max2 among the other indexes, so a repeated maximum still counts twice.\\n    // Both start at Integer.MIN_VALUE, and subtracting 1 from each before multiplying matches the problem formula.\\n    // With fewer than two elements max2 stays at Integer.MIN_VALUE and the result is meaningless.\\n    // The product is an int, which is fine for the small values the problem allows but would overflow for big ones.\\n    // Time is O(n) with two passes and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        int max1 = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\\n        int max2 = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\\n\\n        int crit = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            if (nums[i] > max1) {\\n                max1 = nums[i];\\n                crit = i;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\\n            if (nums[i] > max2 && i != crit) {\\n                max2 = nums[i];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return (max1 - 1) * (max2 - 1);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1477,"title":"Find Two Non-overlapping Sub-arrays Each With Target Sum","slug":"find-two-non-overlapping-sub-arrays-each-with-target-sum","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Binary Search","Dynamic Programming","Sliding Window"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class NotOverlappingSubArraysTargetSum:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/find-two-non-overlapping-sub-arrays-each-with-target-sum\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an array of integers find two subarrays that contain\\n    the same target sum. Both arrays must be non overlapping as in self contained.\\n    If such two subarrays do exist return the minimum sum of their lengths, if they don\'t return `-1`.\\n    The idea is that you\'re looking for two arrays with the smallest lengths, that are\\n    not intersecting. There can be more than one array.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of an array is from 1, 10^5.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Sliding window\\n\\n    PLAN:\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # minSumOfLengths takes a list and a target and returns the smallest total length of two non-overlapping subarrays that each sum to target, or -1.\\n    # It keeps a running prefix sum s, and pos maps each prefix sum to the last index where it was seen, starting with {0: -1}.\\n    # At index i, if s - target is in pos, then the subarray after j = pos[s - target] up to i sums to target, with length i - j.\\n    # It reuses arr itself as storage, so arr[i] ends up as the shortest target subarray found ending at or before i.\\n    # That means arr[j] is the best shorter subarray that ends before the current one starts, so the pair length is length + arr[j].\\n    # When j is -1 there is nothing before it, so n is added, which is too big to be an answer.\\n    # The result is -1 if ans never dropped below n + 1.\\n    # It overwrites the input list, and the time is O(n) with O(n) space for pos.\\n    def minSumOfLengths(self, arr: list[int], target: int) -> int:\\n        pos = {0: -1}\\n        n = len(arr)\\n        s = 0\\n        ans = n + 1\\n        min_l = n\\n        for i, x in enumerate(arr):\\n            s += x\\n            if s - target in pos:\\n                j = pos[s - target]\\n                length = i - j\\n                ans = min(ans, length + (n if j == -1 else arr[j]))\\n                min_l = min(min_l, length)\\n            arr[i] = min_l\\n            pos[s] = i\\n        return -1 if ans == n + 1 else ans"}]},{"number":1518,"title":"Water Bottles","slug":"water-bottles","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class WaterBottles {\\n\\n    // numWaterBottles takes the starting full bottles and how many empties buy one new bottle, and returns total drunk.\\n    // fullBottles is the running total and starts as numBottles, because all the first bottles get drunk.\\n    // variable is the number of empty bottles in hand at the start of each round.\\n    // Each round trades variable / numExchange empties for new bottles and adds them to the total.\\n    // The leftover empties (variable % numExchange) plus the empties from the new bottles become the next variable.\\n    // The loop stops when there are fewer empties than numExchange, so no more trades are possible.\\n    // If numExchange were 1 the loop would never end, but the problem says it is at least 2.\\n    // The remainder is stored in a double and cast back to int, which works but is not needed.\\n    // Time is O(log n) because the empties shrink by a factor of numExchange each round, and space is O(1).\\n    public static int numWaterBottles(int numBottles, int numExchange) {\\n\\n        int variable = numBottles;\\n        double remainder = 0;\\n        int fullBottles = numBottles;\\n\\n        while (variable >= numExchange) {\\n            int newBottles = variable / numExchange;\\n            remainder = variable % numExchange;\\n            fullBottles += newBottles;\\n            variable = newBottles + (int) remainder;\\n        }\\n\\n        return fullBottles;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1672,"title":"Richest Customer Wealth","slug":"richest-customer-wealth","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Matrix"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RichestCustomerWealth {\\n\\n    // maximumWealth takes a grid where each row is one customer\'s bank accounts and returns the biggest row\\n    // total.\\n    // wealthiest starts at 0 and the loop goes through each customer i.\\n    // accountTotal adds up that row, and wealthiest is replaced when the new total is bigger.\\n    // It works because every row is checked and the largest total is kept.\\n    // Starting at 0 is fine as long as balances are not negative, which the problem promises.\\n    // For m customers with n accounts each, time is O(m * n) and extra space is O(1).\\n    public int maximumWealth(int[][] accounts) {\\n        int wealthiest = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < accounts.length; i++) {\\n            int val = accountTotal(accounts, i);\\n            if (val > wealthiest) {\\n                wealthiest = val;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return wealthiest;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Adds up every amount in row i of accounts and returns the total.\\n    public int accountTotal(int[][] accounts, int i) {\\n\\n        int total = 0;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < accounts[i].length; j++) {\\n\\n            total += accounts[i][j];\\n        }\\n\\n        return total;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1716,"title":"Calculate Money in Leetcode Bank","slug":"calculate-money-in-leetcode-bank","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class CalculateMoneyInLeetcodeBank {\\n\\n    // totalMoney takes the number of days n and returns the total money saved in those days.\\n    // It simulates one day per loop, with days counting the days done so far and sum as the running total.\\n    // money is today\'s deposit, and it goes up by 1 at the start of every loop.\\n    // When days % 7 == 0 it is a Monday, so money is set to reset, and reset goes up by 1 for next week.\\n    // After that days is increased and money is added to sum, so the first day gets 1, then 2, 3, and so on.\\n    // The next Monday gets 2, then 3, and so on, because reset is bigger each week.\\n    // n = 0 returns 0 because the loop never runs. Time is O(n) and space is O(1).\\n    public int totalMoney(int n) {\\n        int days = 0;\\n        int money = 1;\\n        int sum = 0;\\n        int reset = 1;\\n\\n        while (days < n) {\\n\\n            money++;\\n\\n            if (days % 7 == 0) {\\n                money = reset;\\n                reset++;\\n            }\\n\\n            days++;\\n            sum += money;\\n        }\\n\\n        return sum;\\n    }\\n}"},{"language":"C#","code":"namespace LeetCodePractice;\\n\\npublic class CalculatMoneyLeetcodeBank\\n{\\n    // TotalMoney takes the number of days n and returns the total money saved after n days.\\n    // It simply simulates the savings plan one day at a time.\\n    // cash_input is today\'s deposit and starts at 1, and money_banked is the running total.\\n    // Each loop adds today\'s deposit to the total and then raises the next deposit by 1.\\n    // dollar_more is the Monday amount, and after every 7th day it goes up by 1.\\n    // Then cash_input is reset to that new Monday amount, so each week starts $1 higher than the last.\\n    // For n = 0 or less the loop never runs and the result is 0.\\n    // Time is O(n) because of the single loop and space is O(1) because it only uses three ints.\\n    public static int TotalMoney(int n)\\n    {\\n        int money_banked = 0;\\n        int cash_input = 1;\\n        int dollar_more = 1;\\n\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)\\n        {\\n            money_banked += cash_input;\\n            cash_input++;\\n\\n            if (i % 7 == 0)\\n            {\\n                dollar_more++;\\n                cash_input = dollar_more;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return money_banked;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1732,"title":"Find the Highest Altitude","slug":"find-the-highest-altitude","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Prefix Sum"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\\n\\npublic class FindHighestAltitude {\\n    // largestAltitude takes the array of altitude gains between points and returns the highest altitude reached.\\n    // The trip starts at altitude 0, so var holds the current altitude and largest holds the best seen so far.\\n    // For every gain it adds the gain to var, which is the altitude at the next point.\\n    // If var is higher than largest, largest is updated.\\n    // Because largest starts at 0, the starting point counts, so the answer is never below 0.\\n    // Time is O(n) for one pass over the gains, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public int largestAltitude(int[] gain) {\\n\\n        int largest = 0;\\n        int var = 0;\\n\\n        for (int x : gain) {\\n            var += x;\\n\\n            if (var > largest) {\\n                largest = var;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return largest;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1816,"title":"Truncate Sentence","slug":"truncate-sentence","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\\n\\npublic class TruncateSentence {\\n\\n    // truncateSentence takes a sentence of words separated by spaces and a number k, and returns its first k words.\\n    // A Scanner on the string hands out one word at a time, splitting on any whitespace.\\n    // The loop runs k times, appending each word followed by a space to the StringBuilder.\\n    // After the loop the final extra space is removed with deleteCharAt and the text is returned.\\n    // If k is larger than the number of words, sc.next() throws NoSuchElementException.\\n    // If k is 0 the builder is empty and deleteCharAt(-1) throws an exception, so k must be at least 1.\\n    // Time is O(n) over the characters read, and the extra space is O(n) for the result.\\n    public String truncateSentence(String s, int k) {\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(s);\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\\n            sb.append(sc.next()).append(\\" \\");\\n        }\\n\\n        return sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1).toString();\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":1945,"title":"Sum of Digits of String After Convert","slug":"sum-of-digits-of-string-after-convert","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SumDigitsStringAfterConvert:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a string s you must parse it and join\\n    together the index value of its place in the alphabet.\\n    Then you must perform k number of transform on the integer\\n    value of this string. A tranformation is to take the\\n    number apart and add it up. This might need to be\\n    done multiple times.\\n\\n    Constraints: The given string s\'s length can range from 1, 100.\\n\\n    Given variable k representing the number of transformations to be can\\n    range from 1, 10.\\n\\n    Only lower cases.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: You are given 100 z\'s which would be the number 26 sided to sided 100 times,\\n    this gives a 200 digit number, larger than an integer can be. If there are\\n    0 transformations to be performed on this number we cannot return it.\\n    If there is one transformation at least we can.\\n\\n    MATCH: Switch Case, Set\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to initalize a function to perform the transofrmation.\\n    Aggregating each integer against one another.\\n\\n    Before that transform the string into a number,\\n    we will join only 200 at a time, if our string is\\n    larger than 200 we must stop, evaluate the transform and then go again.\\n    If we are given no transformation, we have to return null or zero because it cannot be processed.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # getLucky takes a lowercase string s and a count k, and returns the result of k digit sum steps on the letter positions.\\n    # It turns every letter into its place in the alphabet (a is 1, z is 26) and joins the numbers into s_idx.\\n    # Then transform adds up the digits of a number, and that step is repeated k times on the digit string.\\n    # The first transform works on the long digit string, and later ones work on small ints.\\n    # For strings up to 100 letters the digit string is at most 200 long, so the int_limit branch inside the loop never runs.\\n    # If it did run, s_idx is never reset (the bare line s_idx does nothing), so those digits would be counted twice.\\n    # A character outside a to z makes alphabet.index raise an error, and k = 0 would return the digit string, not an int.\\n    # Time is O(26 * n) for the lookups plus O(n) per transform step, and space is O(n) for the digit string.\\n    def getLucky(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:\\n\\n        # Alphabet set: 1, 26\\n        alphabet = (\'a\', \'b\', \'c\', \'d\', \'e\', \'f\', \'g\',\\n                    \'h\', \'i\', \'j\', \'k\', \'l\', \'m\', \'n\',\\n                    \'o\', \'p\', \'q\', \'r\', \'s\', \'t\', \'u\',\\n                    \'v\', \'w\', \'x\', \'y\', \'z\')\\n\\n        # Transformation function\\n        # transform adds up the digits of a number and returns that sum.\\n        def transform(num) -> int:\\n            temp_total = 0\\n            for n in str(num):\\n                temp_total += int(n)\\n\\n            return temp_total\\n\\n        # Join string to unsummed integer\\n\\n        # Variables\\n        int_limit = False\\n        pre_transform = False\\n        result = 0\\n        s_idx = \\"\\"\\n\\n        for char in s:\\n\\n            if int_limit == True:\\n                # Perform transformation\\n                result += transform(s_idx)\\n\\n                # Reset variables\\n                int_limit = False\\n                s_idx\\n                k -= 1\\n\\n                # Set deduction case to true\\n                pre_transform = True\\n\\n            s_idx += str(alphabet.index(char) + 1)\\n\\n            # Stop before it\'s possible to add another double digit number\\n            if len(s_idx) > 198:\\n                int_limit = True\\n\\n        # Finish the previous aggregation that was forced to happen\\n        if pre_transform == True:\\n            result += transform(s_idx)\\n\\n            for i in range(k):\\n                result = transform(result)\\n            return result\\n\\n        # Assign result the role of s_idx\\n        result = s_idx\\n\\n        for i in range(k):\\n            result = transform(result)\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":2089,"title":"Find Target Indices After Sorting Array","slug":"find-target-indices-after-sorting-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Binary Search","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindTargetIndicesAfterSortingArray:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/find-target-indices-after-sorting-array\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: We need to sort an array are return the indices of a target element value. That\\n    means if more than one indicies has the value of the given targer return it.\\n\\n    MATCH: Quick Sort, Two Pointer.\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to use quick sort to sort a given array, then we\'re going to use a two\\n    pointer to find the target indices and return a list of the ones that have the target value.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N log n)\\n    Space Complexity: O(N)\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # targetIndices takes a list of ints and a target and returns the indices where target sits after sorting the list.\\n    # It first sorts nums in place with the quicksort helper, so the caller\'s list ends up sorted.\\n    # Then find_segment scans the sorted list and collects the indices of the target.\\n    # Equal values are next to each other after sorting, so the answer is one run of consecutive indices.\\n    # The answer is in increasing order, and it is empty when the target is not in the list.\\n    # Quicksort picks the last element as pivot, so a list that is already sorted takes O(n^2) time and deep recursion.\\n    # On average the sort takes O(n log n), and the scan after it is O(n).\\n    def targetIndices(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\\n        self.quicksort(nums, 0, len(nums) - 1)\\n        return self.find_segment(nums, target)\\n\\n    # quicksort sorts arr[low..high] in place by partitioning around a pivot and recursing on both sides.\\n    def quicksort(self, arr: list[int], low: int, high: int):\\n        if low < high:\\n            pi = self.partition(arr, low, high)\\n            self.quicksort(arr, low, pi - 1)\\n            self.quicksort(arr, pi + 1, high)\\n\\n    # partition uses the last element as pivot, moves smaller or equal values left and returns the pivot index.\\n    def partition(self, arr: list[int], low: int, high: int):\\n        pivot = arr[high]\\n        i = low - 1\\n        for j in range(low, high):\\n            if arr[j] <= pivot:\\n                i += 1\\n                arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]\\n\\n        arr[i + 1], arr[high] = arr[high], arr[i + 1]\\n\\n        return i + 1\\n\\n    # find_segment moves left to the first target, then right collects every index holding the target.\\n    def find_segment(self, arr: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\\n\\n        left, right, lock, result = 0, 0, True, []\\n\\n        while left < len(arr) and right < len(arr):\\n            if lock and arr[left] == target:\\n                right, lock = left, False\\n\\n            if not lock and arr[right] == target:\\n                result.append(right)\\n                right += 1\\n                continue\\n\\n            left += 1\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":2091,"title":"Removing Minimum and Maximum From Array","slug":"removing-minimum-and-maximum-from-array","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Greedy"],"solutions":[{"language":"Rust","code":"use std::cmp::{min, max};\\n\\n// https://leetcode.com/problems/removing-minimum-and-maximum-from-array\\n\\npub struct RemovingMinimumMaximumFromArray;\\n\\nimpl RemovingMinimumMaximumFromArray {\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a zero indexed array nums that contains a distinct set of numbers\\n    find the element with the lower & highest integer value. You need to return the number of deletions it will take to\\n    remove both the minimum and maximum element from the array. The minimum number of elements that\\n    need to be removed is the (\'idx of max\' + 1 - \'idx of min\' + 1).\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of the array of numbers can be from 1, 10^5. Each element in the array will\\n    have a distinct value. The range of each value can be between -10^5, 10^5.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given an array with one or two elements, return the length of itself.\\n    This is because the minimum and maximum cannot be dynamic under these cirumstances.\\n\\n    MATCH: Subtraction\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to store two arrays with a value [0] and it\'s index [1] in the given array. One will\\n    represent max and another will represnt minimum. We will check for if \'max[0] > array[i] { \\"update the value of max, and change\\n    the index to match the new occurrence.\\" }, doing the inverse to track the min. Once we\'ve evaluated the entire array, we ask a few questions:\\n\\n    1. Is there less deletion removing the elements from 0, the smallest index and removing the elements from \'nums.len() - 1\' to the largest index?\\n    2. Is there less deletion removing the next k elements after 0, smallest index to the largest index?\\n    3. Is there less deletion removing the next k elements before \'nums.len() - 1\' to the largest index to the smallest index?\\n\\n    We can find the dinstance between two indexes as \\"(\'largest idx\' + 1 - \'smallest idx\' + 1)\\"\\n\\n    Return the smallest possible deletion.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) One pass through the array.\\n    Space Complexity: O(1) The size of our objects never grow, but instead are overwritten at most.\\n    */\\n    // minimum_deletions takes a Vec of distinct numbers and returns the fewest end removals that delete min and max.\\n    // If there are fewer than 3 numbers it returns the length, since both must go anyway.\\n    // One pass finds the smallest and the largest value, each stored as a value and index pair.\\n    // Then it tries three ways, and keeps the smallest count in result.\\n    // The first removes from the front to the earlier index and from the back to the later index.\\n    // The second removes everything from the front up to the later index, which is max index + 1.\\n    // The third should remove everything from the back down to the earlier index, which is len - min index.\\n    // The code uses len - min index + 1, which is one too many, so [2, 3, 4, 1, 5] returns 3 where 2 is right.\\n    // Time is O(n) for one pass and extra space is O(1).\\n    pub fn minimum_deletions(nums: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\\n        if nums.len() < 3 { return nums.len() as i32; }\\n        let mut result: i32 = i32::MAX;\\n\\n        let mut smallest: Vec<i32> = vec![i32::MAX, 0];\\n        let mut largest: Vec<i32> = vec![i32::MIN, 0];\\n\\n        for i in 0..nums.len() {\\n            if smallest[0] > nums[i] { smallest[0] = nums[i]; smallest[1] = i as i32; }\\n            if largest[0] < nums[i] { largest[0] = nums[i]; largest[1] = i as i32; }\\n        }\\n\\n        // println!(\\"smallest: {:?}\\", smallest);\\n        // println!(\\"largest: {:?}\\", largest);\\n\\n        // If result is more than deleting the first n elements to the minimum index needed plus/and doing the\\n        // same in reverse for the maxiumum, starting from the beggining.\\n\\n        // println!(\\"from each end: {}\\", (min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1) + (nums.len() as i32 - (max(smallest[1], largest[1]))));\\n        // println!(\\"removing \'{}\' on the left and \'{}\' on the right\\", min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1, nums.len() as i32 - max(smallest[1], largest[1]));\\n        if result > (min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1) + (nums.len() as i32 - (max(smallest[1], largest[1]))) {\\n            result = (min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1) + (nums.len() as i32 - (max(smallest[1], largest[1])));\\n        }\\n\\n        // If the result is more than deleting the first n elements to the maximum index.\\n        // println!(\\"from the start: {}\\", max(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1);\\n\\n        if result > max(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1 {\\n            result = max(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1;\\n        }\\n\\n        // println!(\\"from the end: {}\\", nums.len() as i32 - min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1);\\n        // If the result is more than deleting the last n elements to the minimum index.\\n        if result > (nums.len() as i32 - min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1) {\\n            result = nums.len() as i32 - min(smallest[1], largest[1]) + 1;\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2108,"title":"Find First Palindromic String in the Array","slug":"find-first-palindromic-string-in-the-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"C#","code":"namespace LeetCodePractice;\\n\\npublic class FindFirstPalindromicStringtheArray\\n{\\n\\n    // FirstPalindrome takes an array of words and returns the first word that is a palindrome, or \\"\\" if none is.\\n    // It checks every word in order and compares characters from both ends moving inward.\\n    // current_element_length is really the last index of the word (Length - 1), not the length.\\n    // A word with length 0 or 1 gives an index below 1, so it is returned right away because it is a palindrome.\\n    // half_current_element_length is the middle index, and the loop compares element[i] with element[last - i].\\n    // A mismatch breaks out of the loop so the next word is tried.\\n    // tracker counts the matches, and at the middle index the whole word matched, so it is returned.\\n    // Time is O(total number of characters) in the worst case and space is O(1).\\n    public static string FirstPalindrome(string[] words)\\n    {\\n        foreach (String element in words)\\n        {\\n            int current_element_length = element.Length - 1;\\n\\n            if (current_element_length < 1)\\n            {\\n                return element;\\n            }\\n\\n            int half_current_element_length = current_element_length / 2;\\n\\n            int tracker = 0;\\n\\n            for (int i = 0; i <= half_current_element_length; i++)\\n            {\\n                //string isn\'t a palindrome\\n                if (element[current_element_length - i] != element[i])\\n                {\\n                    break;\\n                }\\n\\n                //the entire string is matching\\n                if (tracker == half_current_element_length)\\n                {\\n                    return element;\\n                }\\n\\n                tracker++;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return \\"\\";\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2109,"title":"Adding Spaces to a String","slug":"adding-spaces-to-a-string","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","String","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Arrays;\\n\\npublic class AddingSpacesString {\\n\\n    // addSpaces takes a string s and an int array of indexes, and returns s with a space put before each index.\\n    // It builds the answer in a StringBuilder by walking through s one character at a time.\\n    // For every index i it asks the helper contains(i, spaces) if i is one of the space positions.\\n    // If it is, it appends a space and then the character, otherwise it appends only the character.\\n    // This works for any order of positions, because contains looks through the whole array.\\n    // If spaces is empty, contains reads spaces[0] and throws an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.\\n    // It is slow, since contains copies the array on each recursive call.\\n    // With k positions, each lookup can cost O(k^2), so the total time is O(n * k^2) for n characters.\\n    public String addSpaces(String s, int[] spaces) {\\n\\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n            if (contains(i, spaces)) {\\n                sb.append(\\" \\").append(s.charAt(i));\\n            } else {\\n                sb.append(s.charAt(i));\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return sb.toString();\\n    }\\n\\n    // contains checks recursively whether i is in the array, by comparing it with the first element.\\n    // Each recursive call passes a copy of the array without its first element, so it is wasteful.\\n    public boolean contains(int i, int[] spaces) {\\n\\n        if (spaces[0] != i && spaces.length - 1 == 0) {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (spaces[0] == i) {\\n            return true;\\n        } else {\\n            return contains(i, Arrays.copyOfRange(spaces, 1, spaces.length));\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2144,"title":"Minimum Cost of Buying Candies With Discount","slug":"minimum-cost-of-buying-candies-with-discount","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Greedy","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class MinimumCostBuyingCandlesWithDiscount:\\n    # minimumCost takes the list of candy prices and returns the least total you pay when every third candy is free.\\n    # With fewer than three candies there is no free one, so it returns the sum.\\n    # Otherwise, each round it removes the most expensive candy and then the next most expensive, and adds both to real_sum.\\n    # Then it removes the most expensive one that is left, which is the free candy, so it is never added.\\n    # The free one is always no more expensive than the two bought, which is the rule of the offer.\\n    # When two or fewer candies are left they are paid for, so sum(real_sum + cost) is the answer.\\n    # It changes the caller\'s list, because remove is used on it, and max plus remove make each round O(n), so O(n^2) overall.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def minimumCost(cost: List[int]) -> int:\\n\\n        # If len(cost) doesn\'t warrent a discount return the sum of the items in the cart.\\n        if len(cost) < 3:\\n            return sum(cost)\\n\\n        real_sum = []\\n\\n        while len(cost) > 2:\\n            first_largest = max(cost)\\n            cost.remove(first_largest)\\n            second_largest = max(cost)\\n            cost.remove(second_largest)\\n\\n            # The next remove is the discounted candy.\\n            if len(cost) > 0:\\n                cost.remove(max(cost))\\n\\n            # Add the candy that cannot be discounted to the real_sum.\\n            real_sum.append(first_largest)\\n            real_sum.append(second_largest)\\n\\n        return sum(real_sum + cost)"}]},{"number":2236,"title":"Root Equals Sum of Children","slug":"root-equals-sum-of-children","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class RootEqualsSumChildren {\\n    // checkTree takes the root of a tree and returns true if the root\'s value equals its left child\'s value\\n    // plus its right child\'s value.\\n    // It does one comparison and nothing else.\\n    // It relies on the root having exactly two children, which is what the problem promises.\\n    // If either child were null it would throw a NullPointerException.\\n    // Time and space are O(1).\\n    public boolean checkTree(TreeNode root) {\\n        return (root.val == root.left.val + root.right.val);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2331,"title":"Evaluate Boolean Binary Tree","slug":"evaluate-boolean-binary-tree","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Tree","Depth-First Search","Binary Tree"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class EvaluateBooleanBinaryTree {\\n\\n    /* 0 is false, 1 is true, 2 is OR, 3 is AND */\\n\\n    // evaluateTree takes the root of a tree and returns true or false for the whole boolean expression.\\n    // Leaves hold 0 for false or 1 for true, and inner nodes hold 2 for OR or 3 for AND.\\n    // A node with no left and no right child is a leaf, so it returns whether its value is 1.\\n    // Otherwise it evaluates the left subtree and the right subtree by recursion first.\\n    // Then it combines the two answers with OR if the value is 2, or with AND if the value is 3.\\n    // It assumes every inner node has two children, a node with only one child would hit a null pointer.\\n    // Any other inner value returns false.\\n    // Time is O(n) because each node is visited once, and the space is O(h) for the recursion (h is the tree height).\\n    public static boolean evaluateTree(TreeNode root) {\\n\\n        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {\\n            return root.val == 1; // leaf: true if 1, false if 0\\n        }\\n\\n        boolean leftEval = evaluateTree(root.left);\\n        boolean rightEval = evaluateTree(root.right);\\n\\n        if (root.val == 2) return leftEval || rightEval;\\n        if (root.val == 3) return leftEval && rightEval;\\n\\n        return false;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2469,"title":"Convert the Temperature","slug":"convert-the-temperature","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ConvertTheTemperatures {\\n\\n    // convertTemperature takes a temperature in Celsius and returns a double array with Kelvin and Fahrenheit.\\n    // Index 0 holds Kelvin, which is celsius + 273.15.\\n    // Index 1 holds Fahrenheit, which is celsius * 1.80 + 32.00.\\n    // Both values are made with double arithmetic, so tiny rounding differences can show up in the last digits.\\n    // There are no loops or branches, so time and space are O(1).\\n    public double[] convertTemperature(double celsius) {\\n        return new double[] {celsius + 273.15, celsius * 1.80 + 32.00};\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2544,"title":"Alternating Digit Sum","slug":"alternating-digit-sum","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.*;\\n\\npublic class AlternatingDigitSum {\\n\\n    // alternateDigitSum takes an int n and returns its digits added and subtracted in turn.\\n    // The most significant digit is added, the next is subtracted, the next added, and so on.\\n    // It first calls breakInt to get the digits as a list, with the most significant digit first.\\n    // signature is a boolean flag that says if the current digit is added (true) or subtracted (false).\\n    // The loop adds or subtracts each digit and flips the flag, so 521 gives 5 - 2 + 1 = 4.\\n    // For n = 0 the list is empty and the answer is 0, which is correct.\\n    // Negative numbers are not handled, since n % 10 would give negative digits.\\n    // Time and space are O(d) for d digits, because of the digit list.\\n    public int alternateDigitSum(int n) {\\n\\n        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<Integer>();\\n        list = breakInt(n);\\n\\n        boolean signature = true;\\n        Integer digitSum = 0;\\n\\n        for (Integer i : list) {\\n\\n            if (signature) {\\n                digitSum += i;\\n                signature = false;\\n            } else {\\n                digitSum -= i;\\n                signature = true;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return digitSum;\\n    }\\n\\n    // breakInt splits n into its digits, most significant first.\\n    // The loop pushes the last digit first, so it takes them back out from the end to get the order right.\\n    public ArrayList<Integer> breakInt(int n) {\\n        Deque<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();\\n        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n        while (n != 0) {\\n            queue.add(n % 10);\\n            n /= 10;\\n        }\\n\\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\\n            list.add(queue.removeLast());\\n        }\\n\\n        return list;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2582,"title":"Pass the Pillow","slug":"pass-the-pillow","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class PassThePillow {\\n\\n    // passThePillow takes n people in a line and a number of seconds, and returns who holds the pillow after that time.\\n    // The pillow starts at person 1 and moves one person each second, bouncing back at both ends.\\n    // It simulates this with two helpers that call each other, right going up to n and left going down to 1.\\n    // Both count the seconds used in passes and stop as soon as passes equals time.\\n    // If time is left over when an end is reached, the other helper is called to carry on in the other direction.\\n    // It works by walking one second at a time, so time is O(time) and there is one recursive call per bounce.\\n    // A direct formula using time % (n - 1) would avoid the simulation.\\n    public int passThePillow(int n, int time) {\\n        return right(1, 0, n, time);\\n    }\\n\\n    // right moves the pillow up toward n, and hands over to left if there is still time left.\\n    public int right(int pillow, int passes, int n, int time) {\\n        while (pillow < n) {\\n            if (passes == time) {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n            pillow++;\\n            passes++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (passes < time) {\\n            return left(n, passes, n, time);\\n        } else {\\n            return pillow;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // left moves the pillow down toward 1, and hands over to right if there is still time left.\\n    public int left(int pillow, int passes, int n, int time) {\\n        while (pillow > 1) {\\n            if (passes == time) {\\n                break;\\n            }\\n\\n            pillow--;\\n            passes++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (passes < time) {\\n            return right(1, passes, n, time);\\n        } else {\\n            return pillow;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2678,"title":"Number of Senior Citizens","slug":"number-of-senior-citizens","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","String"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Arrays;\\n\\npublic class NumberSeniorCitizens {\\n\\n    // countSeniors takes the array of detail strings and returns how many passengers are older than 60.\\n    // It starts the recursive helper func at index 0.\\n    // Each string has the age in characters 11 and 12, so the code reads both digits and makes a number.\\n    // The helper adds 1 for a person over 60 and then recurses on the next index, stopping at the end of the array.\\n    // An age of exactly 60 does not count, which matches the strict greater than test.\\n    // It assumes every string is at least 13 characters, and it throws an index error on a shorter one.\\n    // Time is O(n), and the recursion depth is n, so the stack space is O(n).\\n    public static int countSeniors(String[] details) {\\n        return func(details, 0);\\n    }\\n\\n    // func is the recursive helper: it counts seniors from index to the end of the array.\\n    public static int func(String[] details, int index) {\\n        if (index == details.length)\\n            return 0;\\n\\n        if ((((details[index].charAt(11) - \'0\' ) * 10) + (details[index].charAt(12) - \'0\')) > 60) {\\n            return 1 + func(details, index + 1);\\n        } else {\\n            return func(details, index + 1);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2706,"title":"Buy Two Chocolates","slug":"buy-two-chocolates","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Greedy","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class BuyTwoChocolates {\\n    // buyChoco takes an array of prices and some money, and returns the money left after buying the two cheapest.\\n    // If the two cheapest cost more than money, it returns money unchanged, because it buys nothing.\\n    // The first loop finds the smallest price, called max1 (the name is misleading), and its index in crit.\\n    // The second loop finds the smallest price among the other indexes, skipping crit, and stores it in max2.\\n    // Skipping by index (not by value) means two chocolates with the same price are both counted.\\n    // diff is money - (max1 + max2), and it is returned only if it is zero or more.\\n    // With fewer than 2 prices, max2 stays Integer.MAX_VALUE and the sum overflows, so the result would be wrong.\\n    // Time is O(n) with two passes, and space is O(1).\\n    public int buyChoco(int[] prices, int money) {\\n\\n        int max1 = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\\n        int max2 = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\\n\\n        int crit = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\\n            if (prices[i] < max1) {\\n                max1 = prices[i];\\n                crit = i;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\\n            if (prices[i] < max2 && i != crit) {\\n                max2 = prices[i];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        int diff = (money - (max1 + max2));\\n        if (diff >= 0) return diff;\\n\\n    return money;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2733,"title":"Neither Minimum nor Maximum","slug":"neither-minimum-nor-maximum","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class NeitherMinimumNorMaximum {\\n\\n    // findNonMinOrMax takes an int array and returns any value that is neither the smallest nor the largest, or -1.\\n    // With fewer than 3 elements it returns -1 at once, since two numbers are always the min and the max.\\n    // The first loop finds the minimum and maximum values.\\n    // The second loop returns the first value that equals neither of them.\\n    // If every value equals the min or the max (for example {1, 1, 2}), it returns -1.\\n    // It returns the first match, not a particular one such as the median, which the problem allows.\\n    // Time is O(n) with two passes and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public int findNonMinOrMax(int[] nums) {\\n        int n = nums.length;\\n        if (n < 3) return -1; // need at least 3 distinct numbers\\n\\n        int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\\n        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\\n\\n        // find global min and max\\n        for (int x : nums) {\\n            if (x < min) min = x;\\n            if (x > max) max = x;\\n        }\\n\\n        // find any element that is neither min nor max\\n        for (int x : nums) {\\n            if (x != min && x != max) {\\n                return x;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        return -1;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":2778,"title":"Sum of Squares of Special Elements ","slug":"sum-of-squares-of-special-elements","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Enumeration"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SumSquaresSpecialElements:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given a 1-indexed int array of unknown length represented\\n    by n return the sum of the special squares of all elements of the given\\n    array. This means that if `n % i == 0` the Math.pow(nums[i], 2) is add\\n    to a tracked sum, which later will be returned.\\n\\n    Constraints: 1-indexed integer array. Numbers are positive, each owning a value\\n    ranging from 1, 50. There can be 1, 50 elements in each array.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Modulo\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to use the function: `n % (i + 1)`, to see if a number is\\n    evenly divided by `n` which represents the length of the given array.\\n    If it is considered a special element if it is evenly divisible, then we add it\\n    to a sum later to be returned.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N)\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # sumOfSquares takes a list nums and returns the sum of squares of the special elements.\\n    # An element is special when its 1-based position i divides the length n evenly.\\n    # The loop uses enumerate, so the 0-based index is shifted with i + 1 to get the 1-based position.\\n    # For each position where n % (i + 1) == 0, the value is squared and added to s.\\n    # Position 1 and position n always qualify, so a one element list returns its own square.\\n    # Time is O(n) for one pass, and space is O(1) because it keeps one running sum.\\n    def sumOfSquares(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\\n        n = len(nums)\\n        s = 0\\n\\n        for i, value in enumerate(nums):\\n            if n % (i + 1) == 0:\\n                s += value * value\\n\\n        return s"}]},{"number":3005,"title":"Count Elements With Maximum Frequency","slug":"count-elements-with-maximum-frequency","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"import java.util.Arrays;\\n\\npublic class CountElementsWithMaximumFrequency2 {\\n\\n    // maxFrequencyElements takes an int array and returns how many elements have the highest frequency.\\n    // Unlike CountElementsWithMaximumFrequency, this version sorts and then counts each run of equal values.\\n    // After sorting, equal values sit next to each other, so count is the length of the current run.\\n    // When the next value is different, the run is over, and count is compared with maxFreq.\\n    // A bigger run replaces result with count, and a run that ties adds count to result.\\n    // The last run is never followed by a different value, so the same check is repeated after the loop.\\n    // For {1, 2, 2, 3, 3} it returns 4, and it works for one element, but an empty array would return 1.\\n    // The sort changes the caller\'s array, and time is O(n log n) for the sort, with the loop being O(n).\\n    public static int maxFrequencyElements(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        Arrays.sort(nums);\\n        int maxFreq = 0;\\n        int count = 1;\\n        int result = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {\\n            if (nums[i] == nums[i + 1]) {\\n                count++;\\n            } else {\\n                if (count > maxFreq) {\\n                    maxFreq = count;\\n                    result = count;\\n                } else if (count == maxFreq) {\\n                    result += count;\\n                }\\n                count = 1;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (count > maxFreq) {\\n            result = count;\\n        } else if (count == maxFreq) {\\n            result += count;\\n        }\\n\\n        return result;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3024,"title":"Type of Triangle","slug":"type-of-triangle","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Math","Sorting","Polygons"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class TypeOfTriangle {\\n\\n    /*Tests for some trangle properties:\\n    The sum of two sides are greater than one side. (this is represented by the String return \\"none\\".)\\n    If no two sides are the same then the triangle is scalene.\\n    If all sides are the same the triangle is equilateral.\\n    */\\n    // triangleType takes an array of 3 side lengths and returns \\"none\\", \\"scalene\\", \\"isosceles\\" or \\"equilateral\\".\\n    // First it checks the triangle rule: each pair of sides must add up to more than the third, or it returns \\"none\\".\\n    // If all three sides are different it returns \\"scalene\\".\\n    // Otherwise two or more sides match, and triLogic is used to tell if all three match.\\n    // triLogic drops the first side when it equals the second, and an empty result means all three sides are equal.\\n    // An empty result gives \\"equilateral\\", and anything else gives \\"isosceles\\".\\n    // It reads nums[0] to nums[2] only, so an array shorter than 3 throws an index error, and time is O(1).\\n    public String triangleType(int[] nums) {\\n\\n        if (nums[0] + nums[1] > nums[2] && nums[1] + nums[2] > nums[0] && nums[2] + nums[0] > nums[1]) {\\n            if (nums[0] != nums[2] && nums[0] != nums[1] && nums[1] != nums[2]) {\\n                return \\"scalene\\";\\n            }\\n        } else {\\n            return \\"none\\";\\n        }\\n\\n        nums = triLogic(nums);\\n\\n        if (nums.length == 0) {\\n            return \\"equilateral\\";\\n        }\\n\\n        return \\"isosceles\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    // triLogic returns an empty array when all three sides are equal, and otherwise a non empty array.\\n    public int[] triLogic(int[] nums) {\\n        if (nums[0] == nums[1] && nums.length == 3) {\\n            return triLogic(new int[]{nums[1], nums[2]});\\n        } else if (nums[0] == nums[1] && nums.length == 2) {\\n            return new int[]{};\\n        } else {\\n            return nums;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3043,"title":"Find the Length of the Longest Common Prefix","slug":"find-the-length-of-the-longest-common-prefix","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Hash Table","String","Trie"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindLengthLongestCommonPrefix:\\n    # longestCommonPrefix takes two lists of integers and returns the length (an int, not a str) of the longest common prefix.\\n    # It tries every number of arr1 against every number of arr2.\\n    # For a pair it compares the digits from the left, one by one, with count as the number of matching digits so far.\\n    # The comparison stops at the first digit that differs, or when all the digits of the arr1 number are used up.\\n    # Every time count grows past the best length seen, long is updated, so long ends as the maximum over all pairs.\\n    # Because digits are compared from the left, 8 and 48 give 0, since 8 is inside 48 but not a prefix of it.\\n    # If no pair shares a first digit it returns 0.\\n    # Time is O(n * m * d), where d is the number of digits, and the extra space is O(d) for the digit list.\\n    def longestCommonPrefix(self, arr1: List[int], arr2: List[int]) -> str:\\n\\n        # This program will go through the second\\n        # vector for as many elements exist in the first vector.\\n        # Each numerical element from the first vector is\\n        # compared to all elements in the second vector.\\n        # Taking the modulo of 123 for example against 123, and 1234 will return 0.\\n        # Returning the examined length of 123 as 3 digits long.\\n        # Suppose the integer after 123 is 1234, the longest recorded matching length will be 1234\\n\\n        # Edge case:\\n        # if 1500 and 1505 are compared 3 is returned because 150 is the largest common prefix.\\n        # There needs to be a way to\\n        # Just because 8 is in 48 it doesn\'t mean it\'s a prefix.\\n        # Prefix must match from 0.\\n\\n        long = 0\\n\\n        for elms1 in arr1:\\n            for elms2 in arr2:\\n                count = 0\\n                a = list(str(elms2))\\n                for elms3 in a:\\n                    if len(str(elms1)) > count:\\n                        if str(elms1)[count] == elms3 + \\"\\":\\n                            count += 1\\n                        else:\\n                            break\\n\\n                        if count > long:\\n                            long = count\\n\\n        return long"}]},{"number":3093,"title":"Longest Common Suffix Queries","slug":"longest-common-suffix-queries","difficulty":"Hard","topics":["Array","String","Trie"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"import json\\n\\nclass LongestCommonSuffixQueries:\\n\\n    # If a preix cannot be found in any of the words\\n    # return the index of the word with\\n    # the shortest length\\n\\n    # stringIndices takes a list of container words and a list of query words and returns, for each query, an index into the container.\\n    # For every query it scores every container word with count_matching_char, the number of equal characters at the end.\\n    # It stores (word length, match count, index) per word, and separately remembers the first shortest word as default_index.\\n    # Then it picks the word with the biggest match count, and on equal counts the shorter word, and on a full tie the earlier one.\\n    # If the best match count is 0, no word shares even the last letter, so the shortest word\'s index is used instead.\\n    # That follows the rules of the problem for ties and for no match.\\n    # It compares every query with every word, so the time is O(q * w * L), which is what the TLE note at the top is about.\\n    # The extra space is O(w) for the dictionary built in each round.\\n    def stringIndices(self, wordsContainer: List[str], wordsQuery: List[str]) -> List[int]:\\n\\n        result = []\\n\\n        # Loop through all the suffix\'s\\n        # in wordsQuery list.\\n        # Loop and compare against every\\n        # word in the wordsContainer list.\\n\\n        # O(n^2)\\n\\n        # TODO:\\n        # Find the shortest word.\\n        # Handle suffixes without any matches.\\n\\n        # If multiple words share a suffix:\\n        #   Priority top, down:\\n        #   Return the word with the most matching characters in descending order\\n        #   Return the shortest word\\n\\n        # If suffix doesn\'t have a match return shortest word\'s index\\n        default_index = None\\n        shortest_word = None\\n\\n        for suffix in wordsQuery:\\n\\n            # Store a word and how much alike a prefix is it\\n            # e.g., {word: 3}\\n\\n            key_value_index = {}\\n\\n            # Store the matching word and record.\\n            # Put index of said word in result\'s List.\\n\\n            for i, word in enumerate(wordsContainer):\\n\\n                # Count how many char the word has in common\\n                # with the suffix in descending order.\\n\\n                # Store word length, matching chars, word index\\n\\n                match = self.count_matching_char(word, suffix)\\n                key_value_index[i] = len(word), match, i\\n\\n                if shortest_word is None or len(word) < len(shortest_word):\\n                    shortest_word = word\\n                    default_index = i\\n\\n            # If all values are the same per word return the shortest word.\\n            # If multiple words have the same matching\\n            # value and the same length return the first word in the list.\\n\\n            golden_word = None\\n\\n            for word in key_value_index:\\n\\n                if golden_word is None:\\n                    golden_word = word\\n                else:\\n                    current_match = key_value_index[word][1]\\n                    best_match = key_value_index[golden_word][1]\\n                    current_length = key_value_index[word][0]\\n                    best_length = key_value_index[golden_word][0]\\n\\n                    if current_match > best_match:\\n                        golden_word = word # better match, always wins\\n                    elif current_match == best_match and current_length < best_length:\\n                        golden_word = word # same match, shorter word wins\\n\\n            best_match = key_value_index[golden_word][1]\\n            if best_match == 0:\\n                result.append(default_index)\\n            else:\\n                result.append(key_value_index[golden_word][2])\\n\\n        return result\\n\\n    # <---- ---- NOT USED BELOW ---- ----\x3e\\n\\n    # Helper function to compare\\n    # a word against target suffix.\\n\\n    # Works by taking the length n of the suffix comparing character.\\n    # Starts from the last char in the word, and the last\\n    # char in the suffix up to n.\\n\\n    # Function return false if conditions above aren\'t correct.\\n\\n    def has_correct_suffix(self, word, suffix) -> bool:\\n\\n        # If suffix length is zero or less\\n        # than the word\'s length return false\\n\\n        if len(suffix) > len(word):\\n            return False\\n\\n        suffix_length = len(suffix)\\n        index_location = suffix_length\\n        word_location = len(word)\\n\\n        # Traverse for the length of the suffix\\n        # Each iteration the index point goes down.\\n\\n        while (index_location > 0):\\n\\n            # Function stops if there is a mismatch, returning false,\\n            # else keeps going until finishing and returns true.\\n\\n            if word[word_location - 1] != suffix[index_location - 1]:\\n                return False\\n\\n            index_location -= 1\\n            word_location -= 1\\n\\n        return True\\n\\n    # <---- ---- NOT USED ABOVE ---- ----\x3e\\n\\n    # count matching char between word and suffix\\n\\n    # count_matching_char compares word and suffix from their last characters and returns how many match before the first difference.\\n    def count_matching_char(self, word, suffix) -> int:\\n\\n        suffix_length = len(suffix)\\n        index_location = suffix_length\\n        word_location = len(word)\\n        match_count = 0\\n\\n        # Traverse for the length of the suffix\\n        # Each iteration the index point goes down.\\n\\n        while (index_location > 0):\\n\\n            # Function stops if there is a mismatch, returning false,\\n            # else keeps going until finishing and returns true.\\n\\n            # TODO:\\n            # If word is longer than suffix return value\\n            # If suffix is longer than word return value\\n\\n            if word_location == 0 or index_location == 0:\\n                return match_count\\n\\n            if word[word_location - 1] != suffix[index_location - 1]:\\n                return match_count\\n\\n            index_location -= 1\\n            word_location -= 1\\n            match_count += 1\\n\\n        return match_count\\n\\n    # <---- ---- NOT USED BELOW ---- ----\x3e\\n\\n    # Determine the shortest word\'s index in a list\\n\\n    def shortest_word_in_list(self, wordsContainer: List[str]) -> int:\\n        shortest_word = None\\n        shortest_index = None\\n\\n        for i, word in enumerate(wordsContainer):\\n            if shortest_word is None or len(word) < len(shortest_word):\\n                shortest_word = word\\n                shortest_index = i\\n\\n        return shortest_index"}]},{"number":3099,"title":"Harshad Number","slug":"harshad-number","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"C#","code":"namespace LeetCodePractice;\\n\\npublic class HashardNumber\\n{\\n    // SumOfTheDigitsOfHarshadNumber takes an int x and returns its digit sum if x is a Harshad number, else -1.\\n    // A Harshad number is one that can be divided evenly by its own digit sum.\\n    // The while loop takes the last digit with % 10 and adds it to sum_of_digits, then drops it with / 10.\\n    // A copy of x is used so the original x is still there for the final check.\\n    // After the loop, x % sum_of_digits == 0 decides between returning the sum or -1.\\n    // If x is 0 the digit sum is 0 and the % operator throws a DivideByZeroException.\\n    // Negative inputs are not handled properly, since their digits come out negative.\\n    // Time is O(number of digits of x) and space is O(1).\\n    public static int SumOfTheDigitsOfHarshadNumber(int x)\\n    {\\n        int sum_of_digits = 0;\\n        int auxilary_number = x;\\n\\n        while (auxilary_number != 0)\\n        {\\n            sum_of_digits += auxilary_number % 10;\\n            auxilary_number /= 10;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (x % sum_of_digits == 0)\\n        {\\n            return sum_of_digits;\\n        }\\n        else\\n        {\\n            return -1;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3174,"title":"Clear Digits","slug":"clear-digits","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["String","Stack","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class ClearDigits {\\n\\n    // clearDigits takes a string and returns it after deleting every digit together with the letter left of it.\\n    // It is recursive and works on the first digit it finds.\\n    // The loop scans from the left, and isNumber tells if the character at i is a digit.\\n    // At the first digit it copies s into a StringBuilder and deletes the digit at i and the character at i - 1.\\n    // Then it calls itself on the shorter string, and with no digit left it returns s as is.\\n    // Since the first digit has only non-digits before it, the character at i - 1 is always the closest letter.\\n    // If the first character is a digit, i - 1 is -1 and deleteCharAt throws an exception.\\n    // Time is O(n^2), because each removal rescans and copies the string, and the recursion goes up to n / 2 deep.\\n    public String clearDigits(String s) {\\n\\n        int len = s.length();\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {\\n            if (isNumber(s.charAt(i))) {\\n                StringBuilder adjust = new StringBuilder(s);\\n                adjust.deleteCharAt(i).deleteCharAt(i - 1);\\n                return clearDigits(adjust.toString());\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return s;\\n    }\\n\\n    // isNumber says if a char is a digit by trying Integer.parseInt on it and catching the NumberFormatException.\\n    // It works, but using Character.isDigit would be simpler and faster than using an exception.\\n    public boolean isNumber(char ch) {\\n        try {\\n            Integer.parseInt(ch + \\"\\");\\n            return true;\\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3379,"title":"Transformed Array","slug":"transformed-array","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Simulation"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class TransformedArray:\\n    # constructTransformedArray takes a circular list of ints and returns a new list of the same length.\\n    # For each value, a positive number means move that many steps to the right and a negative one means move left.\\n    # The result at that position is the number found after moving, and a zero stays 0 from the starting zeros.\\n    # The loop reduces the step count with abs(element) % length, since a full lap lands on the same spot.\\n    # Two helper functions walk one index at a time and wrap around the ends of the list to find the landing index.\\n    # Walking step by step is the part that costs time, a plain modulo would do the same job in one operation.\\n    # Values are read from the original nums and answers go into a separate result list, so nothing is overwritten.\\n    # Time is O(n * n) worst case, since each element can walk up to n - 1 steps, and extra space is O(n) for result.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def constructTransformedArray(nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\\n\\n        length_of_array = len(nums)\\n        result = [0] * length_of_array\\n        index = 0\\n\\n        for element in nums:\\n\\n            remainder = abs(element) % length_of_array\\n\\n            if element < 0:\\n                result[index] = nums[TransformedArray.backwards_traverse(length_of_array, index, remainder)]\\n            elif element > 0:\\n                result[index] = nums[TransformedArray.fowards_traverse(length_of_array, index, remainder)]\\n            else:\\n                index += 1\\n                continue\\n\\n            index += 1\\n\\n        return result\\n\\n    # backwards_traverse moves the index left by amount_shifted steps, jumping to the last slot when it falls below 0.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def backwards_traverse(nums_length, index, amount_shifted) -> int:\\n\\n        for _ in range(amount_shifted):\\n\\n            index -= 1\\n\\n            if index < 0:\\n                index = nums_length - 1\\n\\n        return index\\n\\n        # return (index - shift) % n\\n\\n    # fowards_traverse moves the index right by amount_shifted steps, jumping back to slot 0 when it passes the end.\\n    @staticmethod\\n    def fowards_traverse(nums_length, index, amount_shifted) -> int:\\n\\n        for _ in range(amount_shifted):\\n\\n            index += 1\\n\\n            if index > nums_length - 1:\\n                index = 0\\n\\n        return index"}]},{"number":3517,"title":"Smallest Palindromic Rearrangement I","slug":"smallest-palindromic-rearrangement-i","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["String","Sorting","Counting Sort"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SmallestPalindromicRearrangementI:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: We\'re given a palindrome, we need to return the\\n    smallest lexicographical permutation. In plain english this means\\n    each character in the given palindrome should appear in non-decreasing to decreasing order.\\n\\n    Constraints: the length of the given palindrome can range from 1, 10^5. This is palindrome is only\\n    made of lowercase characters. Every string is a guarenteed palindrome.\\n\\n    Edge Cases: If you\'re given a string < 2 return said string. If you\'re given a\\n    string < 3 return said string in non-decreasing order.\\n\\n    MATCH: Map, Dict, Set\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to take a map of the palindrome.\\n\\n    Formatting the lexicographically correct palindrome:\\n    Singly appearing or unevenly valued characters will be added to our\\n    variable for such character presence.\\n\\n    After handling unevenly valued characters, we treat them as even valued by\\n    flooring them by the division of two. We then simply append the target character\\n    for as many times as the arthimetic deserves it.\\n\\n    To order the palindrome lexicographically, we\'re going to\\n    iterate through a list that represents the alphabet. We ask along the way if there is\\n    the current character in that list also exists in the map. Then appending that character n many times\\n    where n = map[character] to a string.\\n\\n    This string is our intended to be returned as the formatted palindrome.\\n\\n    We\'re putting together the string variable of normally valued characters, singly valued characters,\\n    and then the reversal of normally valued characters. It is to be done so and returned in that order.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) because we\'re going to iterate over a dict which is dependent on n.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) This is because we\'re populating a dict,\\n    please remember that our alphabetical list is treated as a constant.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # smallestPalindrome takes a string that is already a palindrome and returns the smallest palindrome from its letters.\\n    # Strings of length 1 are returned as is, and a length 2 string is two equal letters, so it comes back the same.\\n    # A Counter counts every letter, then the loop goes through the alphabet from a to z so letters are handled in order.\\n    # For each letter, half of its count (rounded down) is added to result, which becomes the sorted left half.\\n    # A letter with an odd count adds one copy to sub_result, which is the middle character.\\n    # The answer is result (left half), then the middle, then the reversed left half.\\n    # It assumes the input really is a palindrome, so at most one letter has an odd count.\\n    # Time is O(n) plus a fixed 26 letter loop, and space is O(n) for the counts and the built strings.\\n    def smallestPalindrome(self, s: str) -> str:\\n\\n        # Quicky handle strings of length 1\\n        if len(s) < 2:\\n            return s\\n\\n        # Quicky handle strings of length 2\\n        if len(s) < 3:\\n            if s[0] > s[1]:\\n                return s\\n            else:\\n                return s[1] + s[0]\\n\\n        alpha = [\'a\', \'b\', \'c\', \'d\', \'e\', \'f\', \'g\', \'h\', \'i\', \'j\', \'k\',\\n                 \'l\', \'m\', \'n\', \'o\', \'p\', \'q\', \'r\', \'s\', \'t\', \'u\', \'v\',\\n                 \'w\', \'x\', \'y\', \'z\']\\n\\n        # Lexicographically correct palindrome\\n        result = \\"\\"\\n\\n        # Standalone for non duplicate characters\\n        sub_result = \\"\\"\\n\\n        # Place the string key(char), vale into the dictionary\\n        dict = Counter(s)\\n\\n        for let in alpha:\\n            if let in dict:\\n                if dict[let] == 1:\\n                    sub_result += let\\n                else:\\n                    if dict[let] % 2 != 0:\\n                        sub_result += let\\n                    for _ in range(0, (dict[let] // 2)):\\n                        result += let\\n\\n        result_reversed = \\"\\".join(reversed(result))\\n        sub_result += result_reversed\\n        return result + sub_result"}]},{"number":3541,"title":"Find Most Frequent Vowel and Consonant","slug":"find-most-frequent-vowel-and-consonant","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Hash Table","String","Counting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class FindMostFrequentVowelAndConsonant {\\n\\n    // maxFreqSum takes a string and returns the highest vowel count plus the highest consonant count.\\n    // It does this by adding the results of the two helpers, vowel and consonant.\\n    // Each helper counts every letter with a switch that treats upper and lower case the same.\\n    // Then each helper picks the largest of its counts, so only the most frequent vowel and consonant matter.\\n    // Characters that are not letters, like spaces, are ignored by both helpers.\\n    // A string with no vowels or no consonants just adds 0 for that part.\\n    // Time is O(n) for the two passes over the string, and the space is O(1) because the counters have a fixed size.\\n    public static int maxFreqSum(String s) {\\n\\n        return vowel(s) + consonant(s);\\n    }\\n\\n    // vowel counts a, e, i, o and u in either case and returns the biggest of those five counts.\\n    private static int vowel(String s) {\\n\\n        int aCount = 0, eCount = 0, iCount = 0, oCount = 0, uCount = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n\\n            char c = s.charAt(i);\\n            switch (c) {\\n                case \'a\':\\n                case \'A\':\\n                    aCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'e\':\\n                case \'E\':\\n                    eCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'i\':\\n                case \'I\':\\n                    iCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'o\':\\n                case \'O\':\\n                    oCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'u\':\\n                case \'U\':\\n                    uCount++;\\n                    break;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        int[] vowelArray = new int[]{aCount, eCount, iCount, oCount, uCount};\\n\\n        int largestVowel = 0;\\n        int count = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < vowelArray.length; i++) {\\n            if (vowelArray[i] > count) {\\n                largestVowel = vowelArray[i];\\n            count = vowelArray[i];\\n        }\\n            }\\n        count = 0;\\n\\n        if (largestVowel == 1) {\\n            return 1;\\n        }\\n        return largestVowel;\\n    }\\n\\n    // consonant does the same for the 21 consonant letters, and y is counted as a consonant.\\n    // The check for largest being 1 is not needed, since it returns 1 either way.\\n    private static int consonant(String s) {\\n\\n        int bCount = 0, cCount = 0, dCount = 0, fCount = 0, gCount = 0, hCount = 0, jCount = 0, kCount = 0, lCount = 0, mCount = 0,\\n                nCount = 0, pCount = 0, qCount = 0, rCount = 0, sCount = 0, tCount = 0, vCount = 0, wCount = 0, xCount = 0, yCount = 0, zCount = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {\\n\\n            char c = s.charAt(i);\\n            switch (c) {\\n                case \'b\':\\n                case \'B\':\\n                    bCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'c\':\\n                case \'C\':\\n                    cCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'d\':\\n                case \'D\':\\n                    dCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'f\':\\n                case \'F\':\\n                    fCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'g\':\\n                case \'G\':\\n                    gCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'h\':\\n                case \'H\':\\n                    hCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'j\':\\n                case \'J\':\\n                    jCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'k\':\\n                case \'K\':\\n                    kCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'l\':\\n                case \'L\':\\n                    lCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'m\':\\n                case \'M\':\\n                    mCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'n\':\\n                case \'N\':\\n                    nCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'p\':\\n                case \'P\':\\n                    pCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'q\':\\n                case \'Q\':\\n                    qCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'r\':\\n                case \'R\':\\n                    rCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'s\':\\n                case \'S\':\\n                    sCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'t\':\\n                case \'T\':\\n                    tCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'v\':\\n                case \'V\':\\n                    vCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'w\':\\n                case \'W\':\\n                    wCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'x\':\\n                case \'X\':\\n                    xCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'y\':\\n                case \'Y\':\\n                    yCount++;\\n                    break;\\n                case \'z\':\\n                case \'Z\':\\n                    zCount++;\\n                    break;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        int[] consonantArray = new int[]{bCount, cCount, dCount, fCount, gCount, hCount, jCount, kCount, lCount, mCount,\\n                nCount, pCount, qCount, rCount, sCount, tCount, vCount, wCount, xCount, yCount, zCount};\\n\\n        int largestConsonant = 0;\\n        int count = 0;\\n\\n        for (int i = 0; i < consonantArray.length; i++) {\\n            if (consonantArray[i] > count) {\\n                largestConsonant = consonantArray[i];\\n                count = consonantArray[i];\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        count = 0;\\n\\n        if (largestConsonant == 1) {\\n            return 1;\\n        }\\n        return largestConsonant;\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3619,"title":"Count Islands With Total Value Divisible by K","slug":"count-islands-with-total-value-divisible-by-k","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Depth-First Search","Breadth-First Search","Union-Find","Matrix"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class CountIslandsWithTotalValueDivisibleK:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    https://leetcode.com/problems/count-islands-with-total-value-divisible-by-k/\\n\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an m x n grid of positive integers count all islands with a total divisible by k.\\n    An island is a group of positive integers. Seperated by zero. We don\'t need to return the islanders just...\\n\\n    MATCH: Dict, Tuple, Counter, Division\\n\\n    PLAN: I need a way to see what cells with positive numbers are next to what other cells. We can implement a directional\\n    array: up, down, left, right. We can store the x and y values in a tuple. We can ask if the x or y values are one index\\n    less or more than another known x and y value of positive number.\\n\\n    Something like, `if isConnected((x, y), positiveCells)` first we verify that the cell contains a positive number, then\\n    we can find out of the number is connected. `isConnected(tuple, list[tuple])` This should work by taking the x in the\\n    new tuple and iterating through the list of tuples.\\n\\n    Then if the tuple is actually connected I think we should maintain a dynamic list of lists that hold the addresses of each\\n    cell per island. I think it\'s wise to contain an identifier. If you find a positive number that has no connections increase\\n    an index and create new sublist of it\'s own islands.\\n\\n    What happens if you find a positive cell that in theory does belong to an island but there is no way to provide it because\\n    the connection hasn\'t been made yet.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]\\n        self.reset()\\n\\n    def reset(self):\\n        self.color_islands = []   # list of lists of (row, col); merged-away islands become []\\n        self.visited       = set()\\n        self.island_values = {}   # (row, col) -> value\\n        self.cell_island   = {}   # (row, col) -> island id\\n\\n    # countIslands takes a grid of non-negative ints and a number k, and returns how many islands have a total divisible by k.\\n    # An island is a group of cells above 0 that touch up, down, left or right, and 0 cells separate islands.\\n    # It clears the saved state, then foo walks the whole grid and builds the islands cell by cell.\\n    # After that each island that is not empty is summed using island_values, and it counts when the sum % k == 0.\\n    # Islands that were merged into another one were emptied, so the if island check skips them.\\n    # An empty grid would fail, because foo starts at grid[0][0] without checking that it exists.\\n    # Time is about O(m * n) for the walk, plus the cost of moving cells when islands merge, and space is O(m * n).\\n    def countIslands(self, grid: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\\n        self.reset()\\n        self.foo(grid, (0, 0))\\n\\n        count = 0\\n        for island in self.color_islands:\\n            # skip islands that were merged into another one\\n            if island and sum(self.island_values[cell] for cell in island) % k == 0:\\n                count += 1\\n        return count\\n\\n    # foo walks every cell with a stack (not recursion) from start, and calls add_cell for each cell above 0.\\n    # The visited set makes sure each cell is pushed only once.\\n    def foo(self, grid, start):\\n        # Iterative traversal of the whole grid (avoids RecursionError)\\n        stack = [start]\\n        self.visited.add(start)\\n\\n        while stack:\\n            r, c = stack.pop()\\n\\n            if grid[r][c] > 0:\\n                self.add_cell(r, c, grid[r][c])\\n\\n            for dr, dc in self.directions:\\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\\n                if 0 <= nr < len(grid) and 0 <= nc < len(grid[0]) and (nr, nc) not in self.visited:\\n                    self.visited.add((nr, nc))\\n                    stack.append((nr, nc))\\n\\n    # add_cell puts one positive cell into an island: it looks at the ids of the neighbours already placed.\\n    # With no neighbours it starts a new island, else it joins the smallest id and merges other touching islands.\\n    def add_cell(self, r, c, value):\\n        # Island ids of every already-placed positive neighbor\\n        ids = {\\n            self.cell_island[(r + dr, c + dc)]\\n            for dr, dc in self.directions\\n            if (r + dr, c + dc) in self.cell_island\\n        }\\n\\n        if not ids:\\n            island_id = len(self.color_islands)\\n            self.color_islands.append([])\\n        else:\\n            island_id = min(ids)\\n            # Merge any other touching islands into island_id\\n            for other in ids - {island_id}:\\n                for cell in self.color_islands[other]:\\n                    self.cell_island[cell] = island_id\\n                self.color_islands[island_id].extend(self.color_islands[other])\\n                self.color_islands[other] = []\\n\\n        self.color_islands[island_id].append((r, c))\\n        self.cell_island[(r, c)] = island_id\\n        self.island_values[(r, c)] = value"}]},{"number":3633,"title":"Earliest Finish Time for Land and Water Rides I","slug":"earliest-finish-time-for-land-and-water-rides-i","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Two Pointers","Binary Search","Greedy","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class EarliestFinishTimeforLandWaterRidesI:\\n    # earliestFinishTime takes start times and durations of land and water rides and returns the earliest time both are done.\\n    # It tries every pair of one land ride k and one water ride v, which is a brute force over all pairs.\\n    # For each pair it works out two orders: land first then water, and water first then land.\\n    # The second ride starts at the later of the first ride\'s end and its own start time, then adds its own duration.\\n    # The smallest finish time seen over all pairs and both orders is returned.\\n    # The UNSOLVED note at the top of the file is out of date, since this loop does the whole task.\\n    # It has no self argument, so it is called on the class, as the examples at the bottom do.\\n    # Time is O(L * W) for L land rides and W water rides, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def earliestFinishTime(landStartTime: List[int],\\n                           landDuration: List[int],\\n                           waterStartTime: List[int],\\n                           waterDuration: List[int]) -> int:\\n\\n        least_time = float(\'inf\')\\n\\n        for k in range(len(landStartTime)):\\n            land_end = landStartTime[k] + landDuration[k]\\n            for v in range(len(waterStartTime)):\\n                water_end = waterStartTime[v] + waterDuration[v]\\n\\n                finish_land_first = max(land_end, waterStartTime[v]) + waterDuration[v]\\n                finish_water_first = max(water_end, landStartTime[k]) + landDuration[k]\\n\\n                least_time = min(least_time, finish_land_first, finish_water_first)\\n\\n        return least_time"}]},{"number":3658,"title":"GCD of Odd and Even Sums","slug":"gcd-of-odd-and-even-sums","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Math","Number Theory"],"solutions":[{"language":"Java","code":"public class GCDOddEvenSums {\\n\\n    /*\\n    UNDERSTAND: Sum the first n odd and sum the first n even numbers.\\n    Then find the GCD of both those numbers.\\n\\n    Constraints: n is between 1 & 1000\\n    Edge Cases: What if n == 1, do we return 1?\\n\\n    MATCH: GCD\\n\\n    PLAN: Sum the first n odd and sum the first n even numbers.\\n    Then find the GCD of both those numbers. Using the euclidean method,\\n    finding the GCD requires taking the modulous of evenSum & oddSum.\\n    Do this until the evenSum or oddSum is zero.\\n    We return the variable which isn\'t zero.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) because we\'re looping for while condition\\n    involving size given by a dynamically given integer.\\n\\n    Space Complexity: O(N) because the larger n is the more\\n    variables increase in size.\\n    */\\n\\n    @FunctionalInterface\\n    interface MathematicalOperations {\\n        int gcd(int larger, int lesser);\\n    }\\n\\n    // gcdOfOddEvenSums takes n and returns the gcd of the sum of the first n odd and the first n even numbers.\\n    // If n is 1 or less it returns 1 right away, which is correct for 1 because the sums are 1 and 2.\\n    // It defines Euclid\'s gcd as a lambda that keeps replacing the pair with (remainder, divisor) until it hits 0.\\n    // A loop runs i from 1 to 2n and adds even i to evenSum and odd i to oddSum.\\n    // The two sums are int, which is safe for n up to 1000 since they stay near one million.\\n    // Finally it returns the gcd of evenSum and oddSum.\\n    // Time is O(n) for the loop plus O(log n) for the gcd, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    public int gcdOfOddEvenSums(int n) {\\n\\n        if (n <= 1) return 1;\\n\\n        MathematicalOperations gcd = (int larger, int lesser) -> {\\n            while (larger != 0) {\\n                int temp = larger;\\n                larger = lesser % larger;\\n                lesser = temp;\\n            }\\n            return lesser;\\n        };\\n\\n        int evenSum = 0, oddSum = 0;\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n * 2; i++) {\\n            if (i % 2 == 0) evenSum += i; else oddSum += i;\\n        }\\n\\n    return gcd.gcd(evenSum, oddSum);\\n    }\\n}"}]},{"number":3718,"title":"Smallest Missing Multiple of K","slug":"smallest-missing-multiple-of-k","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class SmallestMissingMultipleK:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: Given an array of integers and an integer\\n    k find the smallest mulitple of k that is not inside the array.\\n    Return said multiple integer.\\n\\n    Constraints: We\'re looking for a positive integer.\\n    The length of the given array can be from 1, 100. Each value\\n    inside can be 1, 100. K can also be from 1, 100.\\n\\n    MATCH: List\\n\\n    PLAN: We\'re going to use a loop i represented from \'1, 101\' Take \'i * k\'\\n    if it\'s not inside we return that integer as our result.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N)\\n    Space Complexity: O(1)\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # missingMultiple takes a list and k and returns the smallest positive multiple of k that is not in the list.\\n    # It tries i from 1 to 101, and checks whether i * k is in nums.\\n    # The first multiple that is not in the list is returned right away, so the smallest one is found.\\n    # With at most 100 numbers in the list, one of the first 101 multiples must be missing, so the return always happens.\\n    # If the list were longer than that, the loop could end with no return and the function would give back None.\\n    # The variable result is set to -1 but never used.\\n    # Each in check scans the list, so time is O(n) times at most 101 tries, and space is O(1).\\n    def missingMultiple(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\\n        result = -1\\n        for i in range(1, 102):\\n            if not i * k in nums:\\n                return i * k"}]},{"number":3731,"title":"Find Missing Elements","slug":"find-missing-elements","difficulty":"Easy","topics":["Array","Hash Table","Sorting"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class FindMissingElements:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given a list of integers, evaluate all element values and\\n    find elements which are missing between the smallest and largest element in the list.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of the given list of integers can range between 2, 100.\\n    Each element can have a value betwen 1, 100. It doesn\'t seem as if element values are duplicated.\\n\\n    Edge Cases:\\n\\n    MATCH: Counting\\n\\n    PLAN: I\'m going to determine which elements are missing by following non-decreasing order.\\n    Simply, find the minimum/maximum element in the array. Create a loop that iterates to the max from the min.\\n    if there are any iterations missing along the way add it to the list\\n    Any elements that are contained in the originally given list are added to the resulting list,\\n    which is finally returned.\\n\\n    Time Complexity: O(N) N is determined by the largest element in the list. Said\\n    element can at worst be at the end of the list.\\n    Space Complexity: We\'re going to maintain a list of missing elements and return it,\\n    it will grow by N for as many elements that are missing.\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # findMissingElements takes a list of ints and returns the numbers between the smallest and the largest that are not in it.\\n    # It finds the smallest and largest value with min and max.\\n    # Then it loops i from the smallest up to, but not including, the largest, since the largest is in the list anyway.\\n    # If i is not in nums it is appended to result, so the result comes out in increasing order.\\n    # An empty list would fail, because min of an empty list raises ValueError.\\n    # The \\"in\\" check scans the list each time, so the time is O((max - min) * n) and the extra space is O(number missing).\\n    # The variable largest_elm is never used.\\n    def findMissingElements(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\\n        result = []\\n        largest_elm = 1\\n\\n        # Find smallest, largest element\\n        smallest, largest = min(nums), max(nums)\\n\\n        # Loop until the largest element is met.\\n        # Add each element that cannot be found in the given integer array.\\n        for i in range(smallest, largest):\\n            if not i in nums:\\n                result.append(i)\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":3871,"title":"Count Commas in Range II","slug":"count-commas-in-range-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"class CountCommasRangeII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an integer \'n\' you need to return the amount of commas\\n    it will take to build each number with the correct fromat from \'1, n\'.\\n\\n    Constraints: The value of \'n\' can range from 1, 10^5.\\n\\n    Edge cases: Anything below or equal to 1000, return zero.\\n\\n    MATCH:\\n\\n    PLAN: Everytime number increases by 3 `(3..6..9..12)` digits after `n` is the value of 1000\\n    the insertion of another comma is expected, `commas += 1`. The pattern is multiplying by 10^3\\n    each time an upper bound is met. The bounds are determined by starting at 10^ or 1000, the next bound 10^6 - 1,\\n    and then multiplying `(upper_bound - lower_bound) * vairable_comma` each where the variable for commas is\\n    increased by one each time a bound is met. If we change the value of lower by multiping it by 10^3 each iterations\\n    and increasing the amount of commas by one each time, we will get to our max bound. If `n` is not an exact\\n    multiple of 10^3 you should just take it\'s value subtract is against the closest lower bound, take it\'s remainder\\n    and multyping it against the appropriate number of commas for that iteration. e.g, `n = 10050` is 9051\\n    commas because `10050 - 1000 + 1` is 9051. This can be scaled to accomodate numbers in the bill..trillions.\\n\\n    New plan:\\n    \'\'\'\\n\\n    # countCommas takes n and returns the total number of commas needed to write every number from 1 to n.\\n    # Below 1000 there are no commas, so it returns 0 right away.\\n    # Otherwise it works through the ranges 1000..999999, 1000000..999999999, and so on, where each range adds one more comma.\\n    # lower starts the current range, upper ends it (or is n if n stops earlier), and commas is the comma count.\\n    # Each range adds (upper - lower + 1) * commas to result, then lower is multiplied by 1000 and commas goes up by 1.\\n    # The loop ends when lower passes n, so unlike the first version it also works for numbers above 999999.\\n    # Time is O(log n) because there is one loop pass per three digits, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def countCommas(self, n: int) -> int:\\n        if n < 1000: return 0\\n\\n        result = 0\\n\\n        lower = 1000 # `10^3, 10^6 - 1, 10^9 - 1, 10^12 - 1`\\n        commas = 1\\n\\n        while lower <= n:\\n            upper = lower * 1000 - 1\\n            if upper > n:\\n                upper = n\\n\\n            result += (upper - lower + 1) * commas\\n\\n            lower *= 1000\\n            commas += 1\\n\\n        return result"}]},{"number":3876,"title":"Construct Uniform Parity Array II","slug":"construct-uniform-parity-array-ii","difficulty":"Medium","topics":["Array","Math"],"solutions":[{"language":"Python","code":"from collections import Counter\\n\\nimport json\\n\\nclass Node:\\n    def __init__(self, value):\\n        self.data = value\\n        self.next = None\\n\\nclass ConstructUniformParityArrayII:\\n    \'\'\'\\n    UNDERSTAND: You\'re given an array of n distinct integers- you need to create\\n    another array that respects rules of a parody. This means that all numbers are either\\n    odd or even. You should construct this following array by following the rules:\\n    In any order:\\n\\n                For each index of `i` you must choose exactly one of the following\\n                1. list2[i] should be equal to list1[i]\\n                2.1. list2[i] should be equal to list1[i] - list1[j].\\n\\n                2.1. Explanation: `j != i` such that nums1[i] - nums1[j] >= 1.\\n\\n    Constraints: The length of our array can range from 1, 10^5. The value of an element in\\n    the given array can range from 1, 10^9. Every integer in the array is unique.\\n\\n    Edge Case:\\n\\n    MATCH: Array traversal, Hash Map\\n\\n    PLAN: 1. I think we should create a frquency map of numbers from our array. We need to know\\n    how many numbers are even vs odd. If there are move even or odd, that is the direction we lean.\\n    We\'re either going to create a second array which is completely even or odd, remember.\\n    2. We\'re going to ask the frequncy map if there are more even or odd numbers. We\'re keep to use a\\n    flag to keep track of this.\\n    3. We loop the original array, we check the flag is the even/odd depending on the flag we continue.\\n    When we meet a element that is not of the required condition, we pull out the explanation found\\n    in the UNDERSTAND sections 2.1 explanation.\\n    4. We use this formula to tell if the element can be changed to to conform our second\\n    array. E.g., [3, 2, 7], 2 is not odd, 7 - 2 is 5. So long as the different is greater than or\\n    equal to 1 we\'re okay to insert it into the new array. So our second array should read at\\n    this point: [3, 5, 7]. And, it should be mentioned that at this point in the program `i` is at\\n    7 which is odd, and all memebers of the second array follow the parity of being negative.\\n    We return true if we\'ve gotten to this point.\\n    5. If we failed to through step four it would have been because it was impossible to construct the\\n    proper parity array.\\n\\n    NOTE: Instead of using a frequency map we should check the value of the last integer in the array.\\n    The last integer can\'t be changed because there will never be a number after it that can aid in it\'s\\n    transformation.\\n\\n    Each element has one life. Once it\'s used in a computation it cannot be reused again, not evne\\n    the changed version. Say 5 became 2... we still cannot reference 2.\\n\\n    Any element can be used against any other element so long as the following conditions are met.\\n    `if elm < nums1[i] and nums1[i] % 2 != elm % 2 and elm not in seen` but the problem that causes\\n    TLE is having to search every element `for elm in nums1:` I have an idea, maybe I should keep a\\n    linked list. The same conditions that would add element to seen: `if nums1[i] % 2 != par_f` maybe\\n    we should also remove it from a data structure like a `doubly linked list` where you can remove\\n    an element from it in O(1) time. This will speed up efficiency:\\n\\n        1. We won\'t have to shift elements after changing a pointer from the end of a.\\n        2. We will have to search through that many\\n        less elements when doing:\\n                        `for elm in nums1:`\\n                            `if elm < nums1[i] and nums1[i] % 2 != elm % 2 and elm not in seen:`\\n    \'\'\'\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.ll_1 = Node(-1)\\n        self.ll_2 = Node(-1)\\n\\n    # This first uniformArray is a linked list attempt, and it is never used, because the second uniformArray below\\n    # has the same name and replaces it in the class. I did not check that this version is correct.\\n    # It splits the numbers into odd and even lists and runs _isConstruct for each parity, scanning the other list each time.\\n    # That scan makes the worst case O(n^2), which is the slowness the notes in the docstring talk about.\\n    def uniformArray(self, nums1: list[int]) -> bool:\\n        if len(nums1) < 2:\\n            return True\\n\\n        self.ll_1 = Node(-1)\\n        self.ll_2 = Node(-1)\\n\\n        self._createModeLinkedListFromList(nums1, True)\\n\\n        return (self._isConstruct(self.ll_2.next, self.ll_1.next, True)\\n                or self._isConstruct(self.ll_1.next, self.ll_2.next, False))\\n\\n    # _createModeLinkedListFromList puts the numbers with elm % 2 == md into ll_1 and all the others into ll_2.\\n    def _createModeLinkedListFromList(self, nums1, md) -> None:\\n        tail1 = self.ll_1\\n        while tail1.next:\\n            tail1 = tail1.next\\n\\n        tail2 = self.ll_2\\n        while tail2.next:\\n            tail2 = tail2.next\\n\\n        for elm in nums1:\\n            if elm % 2 == md:\\n                tail1.next = Node(elm)\\n                tail1 = tail1.next\\n            else:\\n                tail2.next = Node(elm)\\n                tail2 = tail2.next\\n\\n    # _ll_search_tar looks through list ll for a smaller number of the other parity whose difference with tar has parity flag.\\n    def _ll_search_tar(self, ll, tar, flag) -> bool:\\n        if not ll:\\n            return False\\n\\n        while ll:\\n            if ll.data < tar and tar % 2 != ll.data % 2 and ll.data:\\n                if (tar - ll.data) % 2 == flag:\\n                    return True\\n\\n            ll = ll.next\\n\\n        return False\\n\\n    # _isConstruct checks each node of nums1 with the wrong parity and needs _ll_search_tar to find a helper for it.\\n    def _isConstruct(self, nums1, ll, par_f) -> bool:\\n        while nums1:\\n            if nums1.data % 2 != par_f:\\n                if not self._ll_search_tar(ll, nums1.data, par_f):\\n                    return False\\n            nums1 = nums1.next\\n\\n        return True\\n\\n    \'\'\'\\n    So the answer is True if there are no odds, or no evens, or the smallest odd is less than\\n    the smallest even. `min_odd` and `min_even` record the smallest of value of each taken from nums1,\\n    an array of integers. if the value of either of these is None, this problem is solvable. If the number of odd\\n    is less than the number of even this problem is also solvable.\\n    \'\'\'\\n    # uniformArray takes a list of distinct ints and returns True or False for whether all of them can end up the same parity.\\n    # This is the version that is actually used, because it is defined after the linked list one with the same name.\\n    # It finds min_odd and min_even, the smallest odd and smallest even number, and uses None when a parity is missing.\\n    # If there are no odd numbers or no even numbers, the list is already uniform, so it returns True.\\n    # Otherwise it returns True only when min_odd < min_even, so every even number has a smaller odd to subtract.\\n    # Subtracting an odd number from an even number gives an odd result, so all values can then be made odd.\\n    # It only checks for making everything odd, since the smallest odd number can never become even.\\n    # Time is O(n) for the two passes over the list, and the extra space is O(1).\\n    def uniformArray(self, nums1: List[int]) -> bool:\\n        min_odd = min((x for x in nums1 if x % 2), default=None)\\n        min_even = min((x for x in nums1 if x % 2 == 0), default=None)\\n        if min_odd is None or min_even is None:\\n            return True\\n        return min_odd < min_even"}]}]')}}]);